<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="Life Assurance">
  <data key="d0">Life Assurance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of insurance contract covering a person's life, wherein incorrect particulars or misstatements regarding the age of the life assured do not render the assurance void unless the true age exceeds the limit specified in the table of rates; the insurer who pays the sum assured is not entitled to subrogation against the person who caused the insured event.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fire Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Fire Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of insurance in which the insurer is liable for all damage resulting from fire, including damage from salvage measures, measures to prevent extension of fire, and loss or disappearance of insured things during fire, unless proven to be caused by theft; the insurer is liable even if fire originated from a defect in the insured thing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Suretyship">
  <data key="d0">Suretyship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Suretyship encompasses real and personal guarantees as well as joint and several suretyship, which are transferred to the new obligation only with the consent of the sureties and co-debtors.&lt;SEP&gt;A contract whereby a person guarantees the performance of an obligation by giving an undertaking to the creditor to fulfil the obligation should the debtor fail to do so; it must be established in writing and is valid only if the underlying obligation is valid.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal arrangement in which a surety guarantees the debt of a debtor, governed by Articles 788–801 of the Egyptian Civil Code, with specific rules on proceedings, multiple sureties, and subrogation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insurer">
  <data key="d0">Insurer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Insurer is the party that undertakes, in consideration of a premium, to pay the assured or the beneficiary a sum of money, annuity, or other pecuniary prestation upon the occurrence of a specified risk.&lt;SEP&gt;The party providing insurance coverage who is liable for damages as specified in the insurance contract, entitled to subrogation rights in fire insurance but not in life assurance, and required to refund excess premiums without interest in life assurance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insured">
  <data key="d0">Insured</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The party covered by insurance who is entitled to compensation for insured events, responsible for providing correct particulars, and whose unintentional fault is covered by the insurer while deliberate or fraudulent acts are excluded from coverage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surety">
  <data key="d0">Surety</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who provides a guarantee, whether real or personal, for a debt, and who does not remain bound to the creditor in an assignment of debt unless he has agreed to the assignment.&lt;SEP&gt;A Surety is a guarantor of the debt who is released from obligation when the Debtor's tender is accepted or declared valid by judgment, but is not freed if the Debtor retracts the tender beforehand.&lt;SEP&gt;The surety is a person who provides a personal or real guarantee for the lessee's obligations. The suretyship does not transfer to a tacitly renewed lease unless the surety consents.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who guarantees the performance of a debtor's obligation, must be solvent and reside in Egypt, is discharged simultaneously with the debtor, and is entitled to avail himself of all defenses open to the debtor except the debtor's lack of legal capacity if the surety was aware of it at the time of contracting.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who guarantees the debt of a debtor, with legal rights including demanding that the debtor's property be distrained first and being subrogated to the creditor's rights upon payment of the debt.&lt;SEP&gt;A surety is a guarantor of an inscribed debt who has the right to apply for the sale of property that a third party holder wishes to purge.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beneficiary">
  <data key="d0">Beneficiary</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A third party in whose favor a stipulation is made; may be a future person or institution not yet identified at the date of the contract, provided identification is possible when the contract takes effect.&lt;SEP&gt;The third party who acquires direct rights under a stipulation for their benefit and may demand performance from the promisor; the beneficiary's consent is effective only from the time it is given unless retroactive effect is indicated.&lt;SEP&gt;The person entitled to benefit from wakf property who cannot independently grant a lease without authorization from the Nazir or constituent.&lt;SEP&gt;The Beneficiary is the person for whose benefit the insurance is contracted and who is entitled to receive payments such as the sum assured, annuity, or other pecuniary prestation from the insurer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Beneficiary is the person entitled to receive life annuity payments, who may demand due performance or resiliation of the contract if the grantor fails to fulfil the obligation.&lt;SEP&gt;The person designated to receive the sum assured in a life assurance policy, whose rights against the person causing the insured event cannot be subrogated by the insurer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Premium">
  <data key="d0">Premium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Premium is a pecuniary payment made by the assured to the insurer as consideration for the insurance coverage provided under the contract.&lt;SEP&gt;The payment agreed upon for insurance coverage, which may be adjusted based on the true age of the life assured; if the agreed premium is less than what should have been paid, the sum assured is reduced proportionally, and if higher, the insurer must refund the excess without interest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sum Assured">
  <data key="d0">Sum Assured</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sum Assured is the monetary amount the insurer undertakes to pay to the assured or beneficiary under a life insurance policy upon occurrence of the insured event or maturity.&lt;SEP&gt;The amount payable under an insurance contract, which may be reduced in proportion to the ratio between the agreed premium and the premium that should have been paid on the basis of the true age of the life assured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Egypt">
  <data key="d0">Egypt</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Egypt is the sovereign state whose law serves as the primary reference for characterizing legal relationships in conflicts of law and governs various civil matters within its territory.&lt;SEP&gt;Egypt is a country where the legal provisions described in the text apply, including standards of public policy and morality.&lt;SEP&gt;The country where a surety must reside when a debtor undertakes to offer a surety, as specified in the suretyship provisions of the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table of Rates">
  <data key="d0">Table of Rates</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A schedule specifying age limits and premium rates for life assurance; if the true age of the life assured exceeds the limit specified in the table, the assurance may be rendered void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledge">
  <data key="d0">Pledge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Pledge is a security mechanism whereby a debtor who leaves a pledge in the hand of his creditor as security for his debt is deemed to have tacitly acknowledged the debt, thereby interrupting prescription.&lt;SEP&gt;A security mechanism whose preservation rules apply by analogy to a person exercising the right of retention over a thing.&lt;SEP&gt;A real security or warranty that may encumber an insured thing; when the insured thing is encumbered, rights transfer to the insurance compensation due to the debtor.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pledge is a contract by which a person undertakes, as security for a debt, to hand over a thing to the creditor or a third party, granting the creditor a real right to retain the thing until repayment and to obtain payment from its sale proceeds in preference to other creditors.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pledge (الرهن الحيازة) is a possessory security right. Privileges are extinguished in the same way and in accordance with the same rules as a pledge, as stated in Article 1136.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A real security encumbering property, whose rights transfer to the insurance compensation due to the debtor by virtue of the insurance contract, and whose transfer formalities are borne by the surety in suretyship arrangements.&lt;SEP&gt;A contract by which a creditor acquires a real right over an immovable property appropriated to the payment of a debt, granting preference over ordinary creditors in satisfaction of the claim from the price of the property.&lt;SEP&gt;A mortgage is a real right security over property that ranks from the date of its inscription and secures debts including conditional, future, or contingent obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal security mechanism over immovable property to secure debt repayment. A mortgage may be granted to secure a conditional, future, or contingent debt, and may also secure an opened credit or current account. The mortgage cannot be separated from the debt it secures and depends on it for validity and extinction.&lt;SEP&gt;A Mortgage is a legal right over immovable property as security for a debt, which is extinguished when the secured debt is extinguished or when purge formalities are completed.&lt;SEP&gt;A Mortgage is a legal security right over property whose provisions on inscription, renewal, radiation, indivisibility, effect, and extinguishment apply equally to the judgment charge and pledge.&lt;SEP&gt;A Mortgage (الرهن الرسمي) is a formal security right over immovable property. Provisions relating to mortgages apply to privileged rights over immovable property where not incompatible with the nature of those rights. Privileges are extinguished in the same way as a mortgage, as stated in Articles 1134, 1135, and 1136.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subrogation">
  <data key="d0">Subrogation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal substitution of a third party into the rights of a creditor who has been paid off, including all attributes, accessories, securities, and defenses attached to the debt, arising by law or by agreement.&lt;SEP&gt;Subrogation is the right of a joint and several debtor who has paid the full debt to exercise the creditor's action against the other co-debtors, though recovery is limited to each co-debtor's respective share.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal principle under Article 494 whereby a donee, in case of dispossession, is subrogated into the rights and actions of the donor.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal right of the insurer to step into the position of the insured to pursue claims against third parties; applicable in fire insurance but expressly excluded in life assurance.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal right of a surety who has paid the debt to step into the position of the creditor and exercise all rights against the debtor.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal or contractual substitution of one creditor into the rights of another creditor holding a right secured by an inscription. Subrogation is enforceable against third parties only if inscribed in the margin of the original inscription.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal mechanism by which the Third Party Holder is substituted into the rights of creditors discharged by payment, including guarantees furnished by the Debtor but excluding those furnished by a party other than the Debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495921</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bankruptcy">
  <data key="d0">Bankruptcy</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Bankruptcy is a legal declaration that causes a debtor to forfeit the benefit of the term on obligations, as referenced in Articles 273 and 274.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal condition of the debtor, along with insolvency, that constitutes grounds under Article 547 for compelling the debtor to redeem an annuity.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal proceeding involving a debtor that requires the creditor to prove the debt in the bankruptcy estate, failing which the creditor loses the remedy against the surety to the extent of the loss suffered.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal status subject to special provisions that affect the enforceability and priority of mortgages against third parties. The effectiveness of a mortgage against third parties is subject to the provisions laid down for bankruptcy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commercial Debt">
  <data key="d0">Commercial Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A debt arising from commercial transactions; suretyship in respect of a commercial debt is deemed a civil act even if the surety is a trader, except when arising from guarantees or endorsements on negotiable instruments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Salvage Measures">
  <data key="d0">Salvage Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Actions taken to rescue insured property or prevent the extension of fire; damage caused to insured things by such measures is included in the insurer's liability under fire insurance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force Majeure">
  <data key="d0">Force Majeure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unforeseeable circumstances or irresistible force beyond a person's control that exempt liability for injury, alongside unforeseen circumstances, the fault of the victim, or the fault of a third party.&lt;SEP&gt;Unforeseen events beyond the control of the debtor that may excuse non-performance. A debtor may by agreement accept liability for force majeure.&lt;SEP&gt;Unforeseen circumstances beyond the control of either party that may excuse performance, reduce rent, or relieve obligations under a lease.&lt;SEP&gt;Force Majeure is an unforeseeable and unavoidable event not imputable to the operation of a service that may excuse the concessionaire from responsibility for abnormal service interruptions.&lt;SEP&gt;Force Majeure is a cause beyond the control of the worker that prevents the continuation of work and serves as grounds for dissolution of the contract of service, alongside death or prolonged illness of the worker.&lt;SEP&gt;An unforeseeable and unavoidable event; the insurer is liable for damage resulting from a fortuitous event of force majeure under fire insurance provisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Force Majeure is an unforeseeable and uncontrollable event for which the pledgor is held responsible if the thing pledged is lost or deteriorated as a result.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Future Debt">
  <data key="d0">Future Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A debt that has not yet been created; suretyship may be entered into for a future debt if the guaranteed amount is fixed beforehand, and the surety who has not fixed the duration of the guarantee may revoke it at any time provided the guaranteed debt has not yet arisen.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Liability">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Liability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A liability dependent on the occurrence of a specified condition; suretyship may be entered into in respect of a conditional liability as provided under Article 778.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Negotiable Instruments">
  <data key="d0">Negotiable Instruments</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Commercial papers whose guarantee by backing signatures or endorsements is always deemed a commercial act, as distinguished from the general rule that suretyship for a commercial debt is deemed a civil act.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Real Security">
  <data key="d0">Real Security</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property assigned by law or agreement as a guarantee for a debt, which must be realized before the surety's property can be seized when both real security and suretyship exist for the same debt.&lt;SEP&gt;An adequate security interest in property that a debtor may offer as an alternative to producing a surety, and which includes securities assigned to guarantee a debt even if provided after the suretyship was entered into.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Retention">
  <data key="d0">Right of Retention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal means of security allowing a possessor or holder of a thing to refuse returning it until the amount due is repaid, subject to specific conditions and extinguishment rules.&lt;SEP&gt;Right of Retention is the vendor's legal right to withhold delivery of the thing sold until the purchaser pays the amount due, even if the purchaser offers a mortgage or security. The vendor may also exercise this right before the payment term falls due if the purchaser loses the benefit of the term under Article 273. If the thing sold perishes during retention, the purchaser bears the loss unless caused by the vendor's act.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal right allowing the creditor to retain possession of a movable property as security for a debt; the creditor must surrender such right to the surety upon discharge of the debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495922</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judicial Deposit">
  <data key="d0">Judicial Deposit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Judicial Deposit is a court-supervised procedure allowing the Debtor to deposit the thing due when the Creditor refuses payment, is unknown, incapable, or when the debt is disputed.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal measure whereby an insured thing is placed under court supervision; when the insurer is duly notified of such deposit, the insurer shall not pay any sum due to the insured.&lt;SEP&gt;Judicial Deposit is a legal remedy available to the pledgor, allowing the thing pledged to be placed under court-supervised custody when the pledgee mismanages or commits gross negligence, as stated in Article 1106.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Theft">
  <data key="d0">Theft</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The unlawful taking of property; the insurer under fire insurance is not liable for loss or disappearance of insured things during a fire if the insurer can establish that the loss was occasioned by theft.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accessories of Debt">
  <data key="d0">Accessories of Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplementary obligations attached to a principal debt, including expenses of the first demand for payment and expenses incurred after notice has been given to the surety, which are covered by suretyship in the absence of an express agreement to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Depositor">
  <data key="d0">Depositor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Depositor is a person who entrusts property to another (the Depository) for safekeeping, and who may be required to pay remuneration and reimburse preservation expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Depository">
  <data key="d0">Depository</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Depository is a person who receives and undertakes to safeguard property entrusted by the Depositor, and who must return it upon demand, may not appoint a substitute without authority, and is entitled to reimbursement of preservation expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deposit">
  <data key="d0">Deposit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Deposit is a contract whereby one person entrusts a thing to another for safekeeping; it is presumed gratuitous unless remuneration is stipulated, and it is governed by Articles 721 through 728.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heir of Depository">
  <data key="d0">Heir of Depository</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Heir of Depository is a successor of a depository who may sell or dispose of the deposited thing and is liable to refund the price received or pay its value depending on whether the alienation was for consideration or gratuitous.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Owner">
  <data key="d0">Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The owner is the person who holds title to the thing sold; if the owner ratifies a sale made by a non-owner, the contract becomes binding and valid as regards the purchaser.&lt;SEP&gt;The owner of an animal, building, or thing, who may be distinct from the person in charge and may be called upon to take preventive measures or indemnify third parties.&lt;SEP&gt;The Owner is the rightful proprietor of the deposited thing, entitled to reclaim it or receive compensation from the heir of a depository who has disposed of it.&lt;SEP&gt;A person holding full ownership rights over property, including the right to compel neighbors to place boundary marks, use party walls, heighten party walls, and exercise rights of way.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who holds legal title to property, who is entitled to restitution and bears obligations toward the possessor for certain expenditures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hotel Proprietor">
  <data key="d0">Hotel Proprietor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hotel Proprietor is the owner or operator of a hotel, inn, or similar establishment, responsible for safeguarding effects brought by travelers, with liability limited to L.E. 50 for money, securities, and valuables unless special conditions apply.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hotel Proprietor (صاحب الفندق) is the owner of a hotel who has a privilege over the effects brought into the hotel by travellers for sums due for accommodation, provisions, and expenses incurred on the traveller's account, as established in Articles 1133 and 1144.&lt;SEP&gt;A hotel proprietor who is owed sums for accommodation, food, and expenses by a traveller and holds a privilege over the effects brought by the traveller to the hotel or its annexes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traveller">
  <data key="d0">Traveller</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Traveller is a person who stays at a hotel or inn and brings effects into the establishment, and who must promptly report theft, loss, or damage to the proprietor under penalty of forfeiting rights.&lt;SEP&gt;The Traveller (المسافر) is a guest at a hotel whose effects brought into the hotel are subject to the hotel proprietor's privilege for unpaid accommodation and related charges, as referenced in Articles 1133 and 1144.&lt;SEP&gt;A guest at a hotel who owes sums for accommodation, food, and expenses, and whose effects brought to the hotel are subject to the hotel proprietor's privilege.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Receiver">
  <data key="d0">Receiver</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Receiver (also called Custodian or "Harees") is a person appointed either by agreement of all interested parties or by judicial order to safeguard, manage, and return property under judicial custody, with obligations including keeping regular books of account and rendering annual accounts.&lt;SEP&gt;A Receiver is a person appointed by the Judge Des Referes to manage abandoned or mortgaged property during expropriation proceedings; the third party holder may be appointed as receiver.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judicial Custody">
  <data key="d0">Judicial Custody</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Judicial Custody is a court-ordered safekeeping arrangement for immovable property or things intended to remain in place, requested by the Debtor when the Creditor fails to take delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Judicial Custody is a contract whereby parties entrust a third party with movable or immovable property that is subject to litigation or unestablished legal rights, for safeguarding, management, and return to the person whose right is established, as governed by Articles 729 through 738.&lt;SEP&gt;Judicial Custody (الحراسة) is a legal procedure whereby movables charged with a privilege may be placed under court supervision. A creditor who has reasonable grounds to fear misappropriation of such movables may apply for them to be placed in judicial custody, as provided in Article 1133.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nazir">
  <data key="d0">Nazir</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The administrator or supervisor of wakf property who holds the exclusive right to grant leases and collect rent for such property.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nazir is the administrator of wakf property who cannot lease wakf for more than three years without the judge's authority, unless the Nazir is also the founder or sole beneficiary.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nazir is the administrator of Wakf property whose vacancy, disputed appointment, removal proceedings, wrongful administration, or bad faith may trigger the imposition of judicial custody over the Wakf.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wakf Property">
  <data key="d0">Wakf Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Islamic religious endowment property governed by special lease rules, administered by a Nazir, with restrictions on rent adequacy and lease duration.&lt;SEP&gt;Wakf property is property held in Islamic trust whose lease is governed by special rules, including a three-year lease limit without judicial authorization and the applicability of general lease provisions where not incompatible.&lt;SEP&gt;Wakf Property is endowed property that may be placed under judicial custody in cases such as vacancy of the Nazir, indebtedness of the Wakf, or insolvency of a beneficiary.&lt;SEP&gt;Wakf property is a category of property that requires thirty-three years of possession for acquisition by prescription under Article 970.&lt;SEP&gt;Wakf Property is religious endowment property on which discovered treasure belongs specifically to the founder of the wakf or to his heirs.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of endowed property for whose benefit a servitude is established, subject to an extended thirty-three-year non-user period for extinguishment of the servitude.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gaming and Betting">
  <data key="d0">Gaming and Betting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gaming and Betting are agreements relating to games of chance or bets that are declared void under the law; a loser may reclaim payments within three years, with exceptions for sports bets between participants and legally authorized lotteries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Life Annuity">
  <data key="d0">Life Annuity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Life Annuity is a periodic payment obligation settled for the lifetime of a designated person, created by contract or will, either gratuitously or for valuable consideration, and valid only if made in writing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grantor">
  <data key="d0">Grantor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Grantor is the party obligated under a contract who must fulfil his obligation; if he fails, the beneficiary may demand due performance or resiliation of the contract with damages.&lt;SEP&gt;The Grantor is a person who binds themselves to pay a life annuity to another person, and who must fulfil the obligation or face demands for performance or contract resiliation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Loan for Consumption">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Loan for Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Contract of Loan for Consumption is the legal characterization that a deposit takes when its object is consumable property and the depository has been authorized to make use of it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495923</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Contract of Insurance is referenced as the subject of Section III under Chapter IV on Aleatory Contracts in the civil code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aleatory Contracts">
  <data key="d0">Aleatory Contracts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Aleatory Contracts form Chapter IV of the civil code, encompassing three categories of speculative agreements: Gaming and Betting, Life Annuities, and Contracts of Insurance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Creditors">
  <data key="d0">Creditors</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Creditors are persons whose rights must be protected against the wrongful administration or bad faith of the Nazir of a Wakf, and judicial custody may be ordered as the sole means of safeguarding their claims.&lt;SEP&gt;Creditors are persons to whom the estate owes debts, entitled to payment from estate assets, who may hold security on estate property, and whose approval is required for non-auction sales when the estate is insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hotel Staff">
  <data key="d0">Hotel Staff</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Hotel Staff are employees of a hotel, inn, or similar establishment whose gross negligence can trigger the full liability of the Hotel Proprietor for loss of a Traveller's valuables beyond the standard L.E. 50 limit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interested Parties">
  <data key="d0">Interested Parties</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Interested Parties are all persons with a legal stake in property under judicial custody, whose unanimous consent is required for the appointment of a Receiver and for certain acts beyond routine administration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sports Participants">
  <data key="d0">Sports Participants</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Sports Participants are persons who take part personally in athletic competitions and are permitted under the law to make bets among themselves, though the Judge may reduce excessive stakes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Will">
  <data key="d0">Will</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Will (الوصية) is a legal disposition taking effect after death, governed by the national law of the testator at the time of death, while its form is governed by the national law of the testator at the time of making the will or the law of the country where the will is made, as stated in Article 17.&lt;SEP&gt;A will is a testamentary disposition whose execution by a person placed under interdiction for prodigality or imbecility is valid if duly authorized by the court, as provided in Article 116.&lt;SEP&gt;A Will is a testamentary instrument that may create a Life Annuity obligation, alongside contracts, as an alternative mode of establishing periodic payment commitments.&lt;SEP&gt;A testamentary document that may contain an inalienability clause restricting the disposition of property, subject to validity requirements of legitimate reason and reasonable duration.&lt;SEP&gt;A Will (Wasiya) is a testamentary disposition by which a testator may divide estate property among heirs, setting out the share of each heir, which is revocable during the testator's lifetime and becomes irrevocable upon death.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal instrument by which the right of usufruct may be bequeathed to successive persons alive at the time of the bequest or to an unborn child.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Gift">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Gift</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Contract of Gift is a form of gratuitous transfer referenced in Article 743, whose special formal requirements under the law must be satisfied in addition to the writing requirement for annuity contracts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Usufruct">
  <data key="d0">Usufruct</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A real right to use and enjoy the property of another, mentioned in Article 511 as a type of contribution a partner may make to a partnership, governed by sale warranty provisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Usufruct is a legal right that terminates at the end of the fixed time or, if no time is fixed, upon the death of the usufructuary. It is extinguished by loss of the property, though it may transfer to property obtained in lieu of the destroyed property, and is extinguished by non-user during a period of fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of User and Occupation">
  <data key="d0">Right of User and Occupation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Right of User and Occupation is a legal right whose extent is determined by the personal requirements of the beneficiary and his family, subject to conditions laid down in the creating deed. It may only be transferred to third parties by virtue of a formal provision or for serious reasons.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hekr">
  <data key="d0">Hekr</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hekr is a legal right related to bare property where the bare owner holds preemption rights if the hekr is sold, and the beneficiary of the hekr holds preemption rights if the bare property is sold.&lt;SEP&gt;Hekr is a legal right over land that cannot be concluded for a period exceeding sixty years. It may only be established for reasons of necessity or expediency with court permission and must be formalized by a deed. The grantee may dispose of the right, which is transmissible by inheritance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Idjaratein">
  <data key="d0">Idjaratein</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Idjaratein is a contract by which a wakf creates a hekr on land with buildings in need of repair, in consideration of an immediate payment equal to the value of the buildings and an annual rent equal to the rental value of similar lands.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kholou-el-intifaa">
  <data key="d0">Kholou-el-intifaa</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kholou-el-intifaa is a contract by which a wakf grants a lease of a property even without the permission of the Judge, in consideration of a fixed rent for an indefinite period. The lessee must render the property fit for exploitation, and the wakf may resiliate the contract at any time with due notice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servitudes">
  <data key="d0">Servitudes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A servitude is a right which limits the enjoyment of a property for the benefit of another property belonging to another owner. It may be imposed on State property if not incompatible with its intended use. Servitudes are acquired by legal disposition or inheritance, and only apparent servitudes including rights of way can be acquired by prescription.&lt;SEP&gt;Servitudes are easement rights and other real rights that the third party holder had on the property before acquiring it, which are re-vested in the third party holder after expropriation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wakf">
  <data key="d0">Wakf</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A wakf is an Islamic endowment whose constitution by a person placed under interdiction for prodigality or imbecility is valid if duly authorized by the court, as provided in Article 116.&lt;SEP&gt;A Wakf is a religious endowment or trust that is explicitly prohibited from exercising the right of preemption under Article 939.&lt;SEP&gt;Wakf is a religious endowment property status. Land constituted in wakf is a prerequisite for establishing hekr, and the cessation of wakf status on land may cause termination of hekr rights, unless the cessation results from revocation by the founder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Charie Court of First Instance">
  <data key="d0">Charie Court of First Instance</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Charie Court of First Instance is the court that must grant permission for hekr to be concluded, specifically the court in the district where the land or its most valuable part is situated. The President of the Court or a delegated Judge or notary must draw up the hekr deed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Usufructuary">
  <data key="d0">Usufructuary</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person holding a right of usufruct who may grant leases that end when the usufruct is extinguished unless ratified by the bare owner.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who holds the right of usufruct, entitled to use property and collect its fruits while being bound by duties of preservation, good management, and maintenance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Usufructuary is the holder of usufruct rights who holds the right of preemption in case of a sale of all or part of the bare property which produces the usufruct.&lt;SEP&gt;The usufructuary is the holder of the usufruct right. Upon termination or death, the usufructuary or his heirs may retain possession of the land until standing crops are ripe for harvesting, provided rent is paid for that period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grantee of Hekr">
  <data key="d0">Grantee of Hekr</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The grantee of the hekr holds the right to dispose of his right, owns constructions and plantations absolutely, must pay agreed rent to the grantor, and must take necessary measures to make the land suitable for exploitation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grantor of Hekr">
  <data key="d0">Grantor of Hekr</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The grantor of the hekr receives rent payments from the grantee and may demand resiliation of the contract if rent is not paid for three consecutive years. Upon termination, the grantor may claim removal or maintenance of buildings and plantations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 989">
  <data key="d0">Article 989</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 989 is a referenced legal provision whose paragraph 2 applies when the owner restores destroyed property and the usufruct is re-created in favor of the usufructuary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 179">
  <data key="d0">Article 179</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes that a person, even one lacking discretion, who enriches himself without just cause to the detriment of another is liable to compensate for the loss sustained, and this obligation persists even if the profit later disappears.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 179 is a referenced legal provision governing the compensation of a lessee for expenses when a Kholou-el-intifaa contract is resiliated by the wakf.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1008">
  <data key="d0">Article 1008</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1008 governs the termination of hekr rights, including termination at the end of the fixed period, upon death of the grantee before building or planting, and upon cessation of wakf status on the land.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lessee of Kholou-el-intifaa">
  <data key="d0">Lessee of Kholou-el-intifaa</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The lessee under a Kholou-el-intifaa contract undertakes to render the property fit for exploitation and is entitled to compensation for expenses if the wakf resiliates the contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Original Owner">
  <data key="d0">Original Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The original owner of two separate properties may create apparent servitudes between them by establishing an apparent distinction indicating a relationship of subordination.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495924</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 993">
  <data key="d0">Article 993</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 993 governs the termination of usufruct at the end of the fixed time or upon the death of the usufructuary, and provides that standing crops on the land shall be left with the usufructuary or heirs until ripe for harvesting, provided rent is paid for that period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 994">
  <data key="d0">Article 994</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 994 provides that usufruct is extinguished by loss of the property but is transmitted to any property obtained in lieu of the destroyed property, and addresses re-creation of usufruct if the owner restores the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 995">
  <data key="d0">Article 995</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 995 states that the right of usufruct is extinguished by non-user during a period of fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 999">
  <data key="d0">Article 999</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 999 provides that hekr cannot be concluded for a period exceeding sixty years, and if a longer period is fixed or no period is fixed, the hekr is deemed concluded for sixty years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1000">
  <data key="d0">Article 1000</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1000 provides that hekr may only be concluded for reasons of necessity or expediency with permission of the Charie Court of First Instance, and must be established by deed drawn up by the President of the Court or a delegated official, and published under the law relating to the publication of real rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1003">
  <data key="d0">Article 1003</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1003 states that the grantee of the hekr must pay the agreed rent to the grantor, and in the absence of a contrary provision, the rent is payable at the end of each year.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1004">
  <data key="d0">Article 1004</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1004 provides that hekr cannot be concluded at a rent less than that paid for similar lands, and the rent is adjusted when the rental value of similar lands changes by more than one fifth, provided eight years have passed since the last valuation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1005">
  <data key="d0">Article 1005</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1005 governs the estimation of rent increases or decreases based on the rental value of the land at the time of valuation, taking into account its marketable value and demand, while disregarding improvements or deteriorations caused by the grantee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1007">
  <data key="d0">Article 1007</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1007 requires the grantee of the hekr to take necessary measures to make the land suitable for exploitation, considering agreed conditions, the nature of the soil, intended use, and local custom.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1009">
  <data key="d0">Article 1009</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1009 provides that the grantor of the hekr may demand resiliation of the contract if the rent is not paid for three consecutive years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1010">
  <data key="d0">Article 1010</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1010 provides that upon resiliation or termination of the hekr contract, the grantor may claim removal or maintenance of buildings and plantations against payment of the lesser of their existing value or removal value, and the Court may grant time for payment in exceptional circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1011">
  <data key="d0">Article 1011</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1011 states that the right of hekr is extinguished by non-user during fifteen years, or thirty-three years if the hekr is constituted in wakf.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1012">
  <data key="d0">Article 1012</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1012 provides that no hekr may be established on non-wakf land from the date the law comes into force, while existing hekrs on non-wakf land remain subject to the preceding articles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1015">
  <data key="d0">Article 1015</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1015 defines a servitude as a right limiting the enjoyment of a property for the benefit of another property belonging to another owner, and provides that a servitude may be imposed on State property if not incompatible with its intended use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1016">
  <data key="d0">Article 1016</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1016 provides that the right to a servitude is acquired by legal disposition or inheritance, and that only apparent servitudes including rights of way can be acquired by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1018">
  <data key="d0">Article 1018</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1018 provides that restrictions limiting an owner's right to build on a property, such as height or area limitations, constitute servitude rights on that property for the benefit of the properties for which the restrictions were imposed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="President of the Court">
  <data key="d0">President of the Court</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The judicial officer of the court competent to declare insolvency, empowered to grant alimentary allowances to debtors from attached revenues upon application.&lt;SEP&gt;The President of the Court is the judge who reviews applications, considers the amount of the debt and value of properties, and issues orders authorizing judgment charges.&lt;SEP&gt;The President of the Court is the official who may draw up the hekr deed, or delegate a Judge or notary for this purpose, as provided under Article 1000.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Notary">
  <data key="d0">Notary</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A notary may be delegated by the President of the Court to draw up the hekr deed, as provided under Article 1000.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State Property">
  <data key="d0">State Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">State property and public entity property cannot be acquired by prescription, and any encroachment may be removed administratively by the competent Minister.&lt;SEP&gt;State property refers to public property belonging to the State upon which a servitude may be imposed provided it is not incompatible with the use for which such property is intended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Apparent Servitudes">
  <data key="d0">Apparent Servitudes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Apparent servitudes are servitudes that are visible or manifest, which may be created by the intention of the original owner and are the only type of servitudes that can be acquired by prescription, including rights of way.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rights of Way">
  <data key="d0">Rights of Way</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rights of way are a type of apparent servitude that can be acquired by prescription, as referenced in Article 1016.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Founder of Wakf">
  <data key="d0">Founder of Wakf</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The founder of a wakf is the person who established the wakf endowment and may revoke or reduce its period, as referenced in Article 1008.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Law Relating to the Publication of Real Rights">
  <data key="d0">Law Relating to the Publication of Real Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The law relating to the publication of real rights governs the publication requirements for hekr deeds, as referenced in Article 1000.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heirs of Grantee of Hekr">
  <data key="d0">Heirs of Grantee of Hekr</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The heirs of the grantee of the hekr may request the maintenance of the hekr if the grantee dies before building on or planting the land, as provided under Article 1008.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Worker (العامل)">
  <data key="d0">Worker (العامل)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Worker is a person employed under a contract of service who must personally perform the work, exercise the care of a reasonable person, obey the master's orders, preserve entrusted things, safeguard industrial or commercial secrets, and carry out obligations imposed by special laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Master (رب العمل)">
  <data key="d0">Master (رب العمل)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Master (employer) is a person who employs a worker under a contract of service, owes obligations including paying the worker's salary at the agreed time and place, rendering accounts of profit-sharing or commission, and complying with obligations imposed by special laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Service">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Service</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Contract of Service is a legal agreement between a worker and a master that ends at the expiration of its fixed term or upon completion of the work, and may be dissolved by the death of the worker but not necessarily by the death of the master.&lt;SEP&gt;A Contract of Service is a legal arrangement whereby one party undertakes to work under the supervision or control of another party in exchange for remuneration, which may be fixed-term, indefinite, or for specific work.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tips">
  <data key="d0">Tips</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tips are amounts given by customers of a commercial establishment to employees, collected in a common fund for distribution to workers by or under the supervision of the employer; in some industries a worker's salary may consist solely of tips and food.&lt;SEP&gt;Tips are gratuities given by customers to employees that may be deemed part of salary in industries where customary and subject to regulation, particularly when collected in a common fund and distributed under employer supervision.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Competition Agreement">
  <data key="d0">Non-Competition Agreement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Non-Competition Agreement is a contractual stipulation preventing a worker, after termination of the contract, from competing with the master or participating in a competitive undertaking, valid only if the worker has attained majority and the restriction is limited as to time, place, and kind of work.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Penalty Clause">
  <data key="d0">Penalty Clause</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Penalty Clause is a contractual provision applicable in the event of breach of a non-competition condition; it is void if so onerous as to compel the worker to remain in the master's service longer than agreed, and its voidness extends to the entire non-competition condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Invention">
  <data key="d0">Invention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Invention is a new discovery made by a worker during service; the master generally has no rights to it unless the nature of the worker's duties requires inventive effort or the contract expressly reserves such rights to the master, in which cases the worker may claim special remuneration if the invention has serious economic importance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trade Secrets">
  <data key="d0">Trade Secrets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Trade Secrets are the industrial or commercial secrets of the work that the worker is obligated to safeguard even after the end of the contract; actions relating to their disclosure or enforcement of protective conditions are not subject to the special one-year prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Laws">
  <data key="d0">Special Laws</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Special Laws are laws that impose additional obligations on both the worker and the master beyond those laid down in the contract of service articles, including provisions on salary payment, notice periods, and dissolution of contracts.&lt;SEP&gt;Special Laws are legal enactments that regulate provisions relating to insurance contracts not specifically mentioned in the Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Salary">
  <data key="d0">Salary</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Salary is the payment owed by the master to the worker at the time and place agreed upon in the contract or established by custom, and may include profit-sharing, a percentage of gross receipts or production, or other remuneration of a similar nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compensation for Dismissal">
  <data key="d0">Compensation for Dismissal</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation for Dismissal is a remedy that may be granted to a worker even if the master has not directly dismissed the worker, particularly when the master's vexatious treatment or breach of contract conditions forces the worker to appear to have terminated the contract; it also applies to unjustified transfers to less suitable posts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescription Period">
  <data key="d0">Prescription Period</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescription Period is a one-year limitation period from the time of delivery of the thing sold within which a warranty action must be brought, even if the defect is discovered after the period expires. The vendor cannot invoke this prescription if fraudulent concealment of the defect is proven.&lt;SEP&gt;The Prescription Period is a one-year limitation period from the termination of the contract for actions arising out of a contract of service, except for commission and profit-sharing actions which begin when the master provides a statement, and for trade secrets-related actions which are not subject to this limitation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mandate (الوكالة)">
  <data key="d0">Mandate (الوكالة)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mandate is a contract whereby a mandatary binds himself to perform a judicial act on behalf of a mandator; it must be executed in the same form as that required for the juridical act it concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal contract whereby one person (the mandator) authorizes another (the mandatary) to perform acts on their behalf. A general mandate confers only the power to perform acts of management, while a special mandate is required for acts beyond management such as sale, mortgage, gifts, arbitration, and court representation. A mandate is presumed gratuitous unless agreed otherwise, and it terminates by completion of work, expiration of period, or death of either party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mandatary (الوكيل)">
  <data key="d0">Mandatary (الوكيل)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Mandatary is the person who undertakes to perform a judicial act on behalf of the mandator under a mandate contract; under a general mandate, the mandatary is only conferred the power to perform acts of management.&lt;SEP&gt;The person appointed to perform a mandate. The mandatary is bound to execute the mandate without exceeding its limits, must exercise due care (varying by whether the mandate is gratuitous or remunerated), must inform the mandator of progress, render accounts, and may not use the mandator's property for personal benefit. Multiple mandataries may be jointly and severally liable under certain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mandator (الموكل)">
  <data key="d0">Mandator (الموكل)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Mandator is the person on whose behalf the mandatary performs a judicial act under a mandate contract.&lt;SEP&gt;The person who gives a mandate to another. The mandator may revoke or restrict the mandate at any time, must repay expenses incurred by the mandatary with interest, is responsible for injury sustained by the mandatary in normal performance, and when several mandators appoint a sole mandatary for a common purpose, they are jointly and severally liable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acts of Management">
  <data key="d0">Acts of Management</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acts of Management are acts carried out by a creditor that retain their validity notwithstanding the realization of a resolutory condition, as stated in Article 269.&lt;SEP&gt;Acts of management are legal acts related to the administration of property, which are valid when performed by a minor possessing discretion who has attained the age of eighteen and has been authorized to take possession of his property, or by a person under interdiction for prodigality who has been authorized to take possession of his property, within the limits provided by law, as set out in Articles 112 and 116.&lt;SEP&gt;Acts of Management are the acts that a mandatary is authorized to perform under a general mandate, including granting leases of no more than three years, acts of preservation and maintenance, recovery of rights, payment of debts, and disposal actions necessary for management such as selling crops or goods.&lt;SEP&gt;Acts that a mandatary may perform under a general mandate without special authorization, including granting leases of no more than three years, acts of preservation and maintenance, recovery of rights, discharge of debts, sale of perishable crops, goods or movables, and purchase of things necessary for preservation and exploitation of the mandate object.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Fund">
  <data key="d0">Common Fund</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Common Fund is a collection mechanism where tips given by customers of a commercial establishment are pooled for distribution to workers by or under the supervision of the employer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Notice Period">
  <data key="d0">Notice Period</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Notice Period is the required delay that must precede termination of an indefinite contract of service; its manner and duration are defined by special laws, and failure to observe it obligates the terminating party to compensate the other.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Profit Sharing">
  <data key="d0">Profit Sharing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Profit Sharing is a contractual arrangement whereby the worker is entitled, in addition to or in lieu of agreed salary, to a share of the master's profits, a percentage of gross receipts, production amount, value of savings effected, or other remuneration of a similar nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Balance Sheet">
  <data key="d0">Balance Sheet</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The Balance Sheet is an accounting document after each rendition of which the master is bound to render the worker an account of the profit-sharing amount payable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simulated Contract">
  <data key="d0">Simulated Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A simulated contract is a legal concept referenced in Article 244, involving an ostensible contract that conceals a hidden contract, which creditors and good faith successors may challenge.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal arrangement where parties create an apparent (ostensible) contract to conceal a genuine agreement between them, governed by specific rules regarding creditor and successor rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ostensible Contract">
  <data key="d0">Ostensible Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The visible or apparent contract used to conceal the real agreement between contracting parties; in conflicts of interest, parties relying on the ostensible contract have preference.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hidden Contract">
  <data key="d0">Hidden Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The genuine or real agreement between parties concealed behind an ostensible contract, which binds the contracting parties and their universal successors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insolvency">
  <data key="d0">Insolvency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insolvency refers to the state where a debtor's liabilities exceed assets, triggering special protections for creditors including the right to challenge alienations and exercise the debtor's rights.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal state declared when a debtor's assets are insufficient to pay due debts, governed by court procedures, registration requirements, and specific effects on debts and creditor rights.&lt;SEP&gt;Insolvency is a legal state that may be declared by judgment and automatically ceases upon the expiration of five years from the date of registration of the judgment declaring insolvency. Cessation does not prevent creditors from attacking acts of the debtor.&lt;SEP&gt;Insolvency of the lessee does not render immediately payable rents that have not become due, but the lessor may claim resiliation of the lease unless adequate securities are provided within a reasonable time.&lt;SEP&gt;Insolvency is the financial inability of a joint and several debtor to pay his share of the debt; the insolvent debtor's share is borne pro rata by the co-debtor who effected payment and all other solvent co-debtors.&lt;SEP&gt;The inability of a debtor to satisfy debts, for which the creditor becomes responsible to the surety if the creditor fails to take necessary proceedings in due time under Article 790.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Court of First Instance">
  <data key="d0">Court of First Instance</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The judicial court situated in the district of the debtor's domicile that has authority to declare insolvency and adjudicate related proceedings.&lt;SEP&gt;The Court of First Instance is the judicial body to which the Summary Court refers disputes that fall outside its competence during partition proceedings.&lt;SEP&gt;The Court of First Instance is a court with jurisdiction over mortgage and property matters, receiving declarations of abandonment and applications for judgment charges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Minister of Justice">
  <data key="d0">Minister of Justice</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Minister of Justice (وزير العدل) is the official responsible for executing and implementing the Egyptian Civil Code, which came into force on October 15, 1949.&lt;SEP&gt;The government official who issues regulations (Arrêté) governing the organization of the general register of insolvency declarations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cairo Court">
  <data key="d0">Cairo Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The court that maintains a general register of insolvency registrations and marginal entries received from all courts for centralized record-keeping.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cairo Court is a judicial body whose registrar receives a copy of the judgment ending insolvency for the purpose of making a similar registration entry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Greffier">
  <data key="d0">Greffier</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The court registrar responsible for registering insolvency suits, recording judgments, making marginal entries, and transmitting copies to the Cairo Court.&lt;SEP&gt;The Greffier is a court clerk or registrar responsible for recording judgment charge orders, notifying the debtor, endorsing copies of judgments, and informing relevant court offices on the same day the order is rendered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1119">
  <data key="d0">Article 1119</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A referenced legal provision governing the judicial sale procedure for perishable or deteriorating things.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal provision authorizing the pledgee or pledgor to apply to the Judge for authority to sell the pledged thing by public auction or at market value if it is in danger of perishing, deteriorating, or diminishing in value, with the creditor's right transferring to the sale price.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 244">
  <data key="d0">Article 244</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 244 provides that creditors and particular successors in good faith may invoke a hidden contract and establish the simulation of a prejudicial contract by any means; in case of conflict, those relying on the ostensible contract have preference.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal provision allowing creditors and particular successors in good faith to avail themselves of the hidden contract in simulated contracts and establish simulation by any means.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 245">
  <data key="d0">Article 245</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that the genuine contract binds contracting parties and their universal successors when a genuine contract is hidden behind an ostensible contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 246">
  <data key="d0">Article 246</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision establishing the right of retention, allowing a person to refrain from performing obligations until the creditor performs connected obligations or provides adequate security.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 247">
  <data key="d0">Article 247</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that the right of retention does not imply a privilege, requiring the retainer to preserve the thing according to pledge rules and to account for its fruits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 248">
  <data key="d0">Article 248</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that the right of retention is extinguished when the thing ceases to be in the hands of the possessor or holder, with a 30-day claim period for restitution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 249">
  <data key="d0">Article 249</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing a debtor to be declared insolvent when assets are insufficient to pay due debts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495926</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 250">
  <data key="d0">Article 250</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that insolvency is declared by judgment of the Court of First Instance upon petition of the debtor or a creditor, heard as a matter of urgency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 251">
  <data key="d0">Article 251</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring the court to consider all circumstances surrounding the debtor, including future resources, personal ability, and creditor interests, before declaring insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 252">
  <data key="d0">Article 252</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision setting an eight-day period for opposition and a fifteen-day period for appeal in insolvency judgments, calculated from the date of notification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 253">
  <data key="d0">Article 253</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring the greffier to register insolvency suits in a special register, record judgments and marginal entries, and send copies to the Cairo Court for a general register.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 254">
  <data key="d0">Article 254</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring a debtor who changes domicile to notify the greffier of the former court, which must transmit records to the new court at the debtor's expense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 255">
  <data key="d0">Article 255</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision rendering all debts immediately due upon insolvency declaration, with allowance for the judge to maintain or extend payment deadlines in the interests of both debtor and creditors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 256">
  <data key="d0">Article 256</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that insolvency does not bar individual creditor proceedings, but charges on debtor property registered after the insolvency petition are void against prior creditors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 257">
  <data key="d0">Article 257</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision rendering void, as against creditors, any debtor acts after insolvency registration that diminish assets or increase liabilities, including debt payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 258">
  <data key="d0">Article 258</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing a debtor to dispose of property at normal price with proceeds deposited at the court treasury; sales below normal price require the purchaser to deposit the difference.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 259">
  <data key="d0">Article 259</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing the court president to grant an alimentary allowance to the debtor from attached revenues, subject to three-day opposition periods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 260">
  <data key="d0">Article 260</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision imposing misappropriation penalties on debtors who willfully become insolvent in fraud of creditors or who conceal property or fabricate fictitious debts after insolvency judgment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 261">
  <data key="d0">Article 261</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision governing cessation of insolvency by court judgment when liabilities no longer exceed assets or when the debtor pays debts that were due before the declaration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 262">
  <data key="d0">Article 262</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision providing for automatic cessation of insolvency upon the expiration of five years from the date of registration of the insolvency judgment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 263">
  <data key="d0">Article 263</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A referenced legal provision governing the restoration of original payment terms for debts after the state of insolvency ceases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Misappropriation">
  <data key="d0">Misappropriation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A penal offense applicable to debtors who willfully render themselves insolvent or conceal property in fraud of creditor rights.&lt;SEP&gt;Misappropriation is a legal penalty applicable to any person, including an heir, who fraudulently appropriates a part of the assets of the estate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Possessor">
  <data key="d0">Possessor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who holds or possesses a thing and may exercise the right of retention, having incurred necessary or useful expenditure on the thing, and is required to preserve it according to pledge rules.&lt;SEP&gt;A possessor is a person who exercises actual control over a right or thing, and whose rights and obligations are governed by these articles.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who holds physical possession of property, whose rights and obligations vary depending on whether possession is held in good faith or bad faith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Caisse du Tribunal">
  <data key="d0">Caisse du Tribunal</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Caisse du Tribunal is the court treasury where proceeds from public auction sales are deposited by order of the judge.&lt;SEP&gt;The court treasury where purchase prices from debtor property dispositions are deposited for distribution to creditors in accordance with the procedure for distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alimentary Allowance">
  <data key="d0">Alimentary Allowance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A living allowance that the President of the Court may grant to a debtor from attached revenues during insolvency proceedings, subject to opposition within three days.&lt;SEP&gt;An Alimentary Allowance is a financial provision authorized by the Juge de Service for heirs who were supported by the deceased, paid from estate assets pending final winding up and deductible from each heir's inheritance share under Article 882.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Particular Successors in Title">
  <data key="d0">Particular Successors in Title</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal successors who, if in good faith, may avail themselves of the hidden contract and establish the simulation of a contract by which they were prejudiced.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Universal Successors in Title">
  <data key="d0">Universal Successors in Title</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal successors who are bound by the genuine (hidden) contract when a genuine contract has been concealed behind an ostensible contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Obligation is a legal duty or commitment that is extinguished through extinctive prescription after fifteen years unless a special provision specifies otherwise.&lt;SEP&gt;An Obligation is a legal bond that is definitely created as a liability of the Debtor, requiring performance, payment, or abstention, and may be extinguished by payment or equivalent means.&lt;SEP&gt;A binding duty that must be performed by a debtor under a contract; its object must be determinate as to kind and quantity, and when the quality is not specified, the debtor must supply an article of average quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract">
  <data key="d0">Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal agreement between parties creating reciprocal obligations; may be rescinded upon non-performance, impossibility of performance, or by mutual agreement, with reinstatement of parties to their former position.&lt;SEP&gt;A binding agreement between parties that constitutes the law governing their relationship; it must be performed in good faith and cannot be revoked or altered except by mutual consent or for reasons provided by law.&lt;SEP&gt;A contract is created, subject to any special formalities required by law, from the moment two persons exchange two concordant intentions. Contracts may be formed in person, between absent parties, by telephone, or through conduct.&lt;SEP&gt;A contract is a legal agreement whose validity may be affected by legal capacity, defects of consent such as mistake, fraud, duress, or lesion, and the nature of its object, as governed throughout Articles 109 to 134.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal instrument that may contain an inalienability clause restricting the disposition of property, subject to validity requirements of legitimate reason and reasonable duration.&lt;SEP&gt;A Contract is the legal mechanism through which ownership of movables and immovables and other real rights are transferred, provided the contract refers to an object belonging to the person disposing of it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Consideration">
  <data key="d0">Consideration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal reason or cause for an obligation; an obligation is presumed to have lawful consideration even if not expressed, and a contract is void when assumed without consideration or with consideration contrary to public policy or morality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nullity">
  <data key="d0">Nullity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal state of invalidity of a void contract; it may be invoked by any interested person and ordered by the court on its own initiative, cannot disappear by ratification, and proceedings are prescribed after fifteen years from the conclusion of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Avoidance">
  <data key="d0">Avoidance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal remedy allowing a contracting party to annul a voidable contract; the right to avoidance belongs exclusively to the designated party, is extinguished by ratification, and is prescribed if not invoked within three years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ratification">
  <data key="d0">Ratification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The act by which an owner confirms a sale made by a non-owner, making the contract binding and valid; ratification also applies in the context of sales during last illness where heirs may ratify a sale exceeding one third of the inheritance.&lt;SEP&gt;Express or implied confirmation of a voidable contract that extinguishes the right to avoidance and is retroactive to the date of the contract without prejudice to the rights of third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Adhesion">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Adhesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract containing leonine conditions, where the judge may modify these conditions or relieve the adhering party of the obligation to perform them in accordance with the principles of equity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Policy">
  <data key="d0">Public Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fundamental legal and societal principles; a contract is void if its object or consideration is contrary to public policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Public policy in Egypt serves as a ground for refusing to apply foreign law provisions that are contrary to it.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental legal principle that constrains natural obligations; no natural obligation can ever be contrary to public policy.&lt;SEP&gt;A fundamental legal principle that constrains the creation of natural obligations; a natural obligation cannot exist if it is contrary to public policy, as stated in Article 200.&lt;SEP&gt;Matters touching the status of individuals or general legal principles on which a compromise cannot be made, though proprietary interests arising from personal status or penal offences may be compromised.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Morality">
  <data key="d0">Morality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Standards of moral conduct recognized by law; a contract is void if its object or consideration is contrary to morality.&lt;SEP&gt;Morality in Egypt serves as a ground, alongside public policy, for refusing to apply foreign law provisions that are contrary to it, as stated in Article 28.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Incapacity">
  <data key="d0">Legal Incapacity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state of lacking legal capacity to contract; it serves as grounds for avoidance of a contract, with the prescription period running from the date of cessation of such incapacity.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal incapacity refers to a condition under which a person may be declared totally or partially incapable of concluding contracts, including minors, persons suffering from insanity, mental derangement, imbecility, and prodigals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mistake">
  <data key="d0">Mistake</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An error affecting contract validity that serves as grounds for avoidance; the right to avoidance must be invoked within three years from the date the mistake is discovered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495927</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fraudulent Misrepresentation">
  <data key="d0">Fraudulent Misrepresentation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deliberate deception that serves as grounds for avoidance of a contract; the right to avoidance must be invoked within three years from the date the misrepresentation is discovered.&lt;SEP&gt;Fraudulent misrepresentation consists of artifices or intentional silence by a party or their representative of such gravity that, but for them, the other party would not have concluded the contract, as defined in Article 125.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Duress">
  <data key="d0">Duress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coercion that serves as grounds for avoidance of a contract; the right to avoidance must be invoked within three years from the date the duress ceases.&lt;SEP&gt;Duress is unlawful pressure whereby a person contracts under the stress of justifiable fear instilled by the other party, involving serious and imminent danger to life, limb, honor, or property, as described in Article 127.&lt;SEP&gt;A circumstance under which a payor makes a payment, which constitutes an exception to the rule barring restitution; a payor who paid under duress may recover the payment even if he knew he was not obligated, as provided in Article 181.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stipulation for Third Party">
  <data key="d0">Stipulation for Third Party</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contractual provision by which a person stipulates that an obligation be performed for the benefit of a third party, giving the beneficiary a direct right against the promisor; the beneficiary may be a future or unspecified person identifiable at the time the contract takes effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stipulator">
  <data key="d0">Stipulator</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A party who makes a stipulation in favor of a third party in a contract; may revoke the stipulation before the beneficiary claims it, substitute a new beneficiary, or retain the benefit for himself.&lt;SEP&gt;The contracting party who stipulates that an obligation be performed for the benefit of a third party; the stipulator may revoke the stipulation before the beneficiary accepts it, substitute a new beneficiary, or retain the benefit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Universal Successor in Title">
  <data key="d0">Universal Successor in Title</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A general successor, such as an heir, who acquires the rights and obligations of a contracting party, subject to the rules relating to successions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Particular Successor in Title">
  <data key="d0">Particular Successor in Title</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A specific successor who acquires property along with associated obligations and rights, provided such obligations constitute essential elements of the property and the successor had knowledge at the time of transfer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leonine Condition">
  <data key="d0">Leonine Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unfair or oppressive terms found in contracts of adhesion that the judge may modify or relieve the adhering party from performing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Promisor">
  <data key="d0">Promisor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The person who undertakes to perform an obligation for the benefit of a third party; the promisor may set up against the beneficiary the defenses arising out of the contract and is liable to indemnify the other contracting party if the third party refuses to perform.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equity">
  <data key="d0">Equity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal principle referenced as a standard for contract performance and for the modification of leonine conditions in contracts of adhesion, requiring fairness and just outcomes between the parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract Interpretation">
  <data key="d0">Contract Interpretation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of ascertaining the common intention of the contracting parties; when contract wording is clear it cannot be deviated from, but when interpretation is needed, the search goes beyond the literal meaning of words to account for the nature of the transaction and commercial usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reinstatement">
  <data key="d0">Reinstatement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal remedy by which parties to a void or annulled contract are restored to their prior positions; if reinstatement is impossible, equivalent damages may be awarded.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Damages">
  <data key="d0">Damages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation for injury or loss awarded by a judge; may consist of a money payment paid in installments, a regular periodical payment, restoration of the original position, or performance of a specific act connected to the unlawful act.&lt;SEP&gt;Compensation awarded to a party when reinstatement to the position prior to a void or annulled contract is impossible, equivalent to the loss suffered.&lt;SEP&gt;Monetary compensation awarded for non-performance or delayed performance of obligations, including losses suffered by the creditor, profits deprived, and moral prejudice, assessed by the judge if not fixed by contract or law.&lt;SEP&gt;Financial compensation payable by the lessor to the lessee for losses caused by defects, disturbances, or government authority acts imputable to the lessor, or payable by the lessee for unauthorized alterations or unlawful retention of the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contracting Party">
  <data key="d0">Contracting Party</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who enters into a contract; the effects of a contract apply to the contracting parties, and a contract binds them not only to expressed conditions but also to everything deemed a necessary sequel according to law, usage, and equity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Property Owner">
  <data key="d0">Property Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who holds the exclusive right to use, enjoy, and dispose of property within the limits of the law, including rights to all fruits, products, and accessories of the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neighbor">
  <data key="d0">Neighbor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An owner of adjoining property who has rights and obligations related to neighboring properties, including nuisance claims, right of way, and shared costs for canals and boundary marks.&lt;SEP&gt;An adjoining property owner bound by specific obligations regarding shared walls, views, boundary marks, and rights of way, and who may become a co-proprietor of a heightened party wall by contributing to costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint and Several Liability">
  <data key="d0">Joint and Several Liability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A form of liability arising when several persons are responsible for an injurious act; they are jointly and severally obligated to make reparation, with liability shared equally unless the judge fixes individual shares.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of liability where each party is individually responsible for the entire obligation, applicable to sureties either by contract stipulation or by operation of law in judicial and legal suretyship.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Right of Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The exclusive legal right to use, enjoy, and dispose of a thing within the limits of the law, encompassing all essential elements of the thing owned and extending to what is above and below land.&lt;SEP&gt;The fundamental property right from which other real rights such as usufruct, use, and occupation are derived, forming the subject of Chapter II of the legal text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Way">
  <data key="d0">Right of Way</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal easement allowing an owner whose land is cut off from a public road to pass over neighboring land, subject to fair compensation and the condition of least possible damage.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal easement allowing a landowner to pass through neighboring property when the land is cut off from a public road, subject to payment of fair compensation and exercised where it causes the least possible damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Private Canal or Drain">
  <data key="d0">Private Canal or Drain</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A privately constructed waterway or drainage system built in conformity with regulations, granting the builder exclusive usage rights, while neighboring owners may use it for irrigation or drainage after the owner's needs are met.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Marks">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Marks</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical markers placed along the boundaries of adjoining properties, with the cost of delimitation shared between neighboring owners.&lt;SEP&gt;Markers placed along the boundaries of adjoining properties, whose cost of delimitation is shared between neighboring owners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Egyptian Territory">
  <data key="d0">Egyptian Territory</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The geographic boundaries of Egypt; property situated outside Egyptian territory is not taken into account when indicated by a surety under Article 789.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Egyptian Civil Code">
  <data key="d0">Egyptian Civil Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Egyptian Civil Code (القانون المدني المصري) is a comprehensive legislative document governing civil matters in Egypt, including laws, rights, legal capacity, conflicts of law, and various civil obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;The body of civil law containing provisions governing suretyship relationships and real property rights in Egypt, as reflected in Articles 788–814.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Debt">
  <data key="d0">Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A financial obligation owed by a debtor to a creditor, the central subject of the suretyship provisions, including capital, interest, and expenses, as governed by Articles 788–801.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-Surety">
  <data key="d0">Sub-Surety</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who guarantees the obligation of a principal surety, as provided under Article 797; the creditor may not proceed against the sub-surety before acting against the principal surety unless the sub-surety is jointly and severally liable with the principal surety.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Road">
  <data key="d0">Public Road</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A public thoroughfare referenced in Article 812; an owner whose land is cut off from or lacks adequate access to a public road is entitled to a right of way over neighboring land.&lt;SEP&gt;A road accessible to the public, referenced as the access route for which a right of way may be claimed when land is cut off, and as an exception allowing an oblique view prohibition to cease when the view is simultaneously a direct view over a public road.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Land">
  <data key="d0">Land</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A form of property whose ownership extends to what is above and below it to the extent of useful enjoyment, and which is subject to rules on water passage, irrigation, drainage, right of way, and boundary demarcation under Articles 802–814.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fair Compensation">
  <data key="d0">Fair Compensation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation payable to a neighbor when a right of way is exercised over the neighbor's land.&lt;SEP&gt;A payment required by law when an owner is deprived of property under Article 805, when water passage is permitted across land under Article 809, and when a right of way is exercised over neighboring land under Article 812.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bilateral Contract">
  <data key="d0">Bilateral Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract binding both parties to correlative obligations (contrats synallagmatiques); either party may abstain from performing his obligation if the other party does not perform.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rescission">
  <data key="d0">Rescission</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The dissolution of a contract; may be demanded after formal summons, agreed to as automatic (ipso facto) upon non-performance, or occur automatically upon impossibility of performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Formal Summons">
  <data key="d0">Formal Summons</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal notice that must be served on a debtor before demanding performance or rescission of a contract, unless the parties expressly agree to dispense with it.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal procedure to notify a debtor of default, served through the court or by equivalent act, including by post in accordance with the Code de Procedure, or resulting from a stipulation in an agreement.&lt;SEP&gt;A Formal Summons is the official notification issued by the Vendor or Purchaser to the prospective preemptor, which must contain an adequate description of the property, the price, costs, conditions of sale, and the identities of the Vendor and Purchaser, on pain of nullity, as prescribed in Articles 940 and 941.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reward">
  <data key="d0">Reward</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A prize promised to the public in exchange for a specified service; the promisor is bound to pay whoever performs the service, even without knowledge of the promise, and may withdraw the promise by public notice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unlawful Act">
  <data key="d0">Unlawful Act</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fault causing injury to another that imposes an obligation of reparation on the person who committed it; forms the basis for tort liability and may give rise to a damages action prescribed after three years.&lt;SEP&gt;An illegal action for which any contractual clause discharging a person from responsibility is void. Formal summons is not required when the object of the obligation is payment of damages for an unlawful act.&lt;SEP&gt;An act that gives rise to liability regardless of legal capacity; a voluntary agent who is not legally capable is still liable if his liability results from an unlawful act, as stated in Article 196.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legitimate Defense">
  <data key="d0">Legitimate Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Self-defense of one's own person or property, or that of a third party; exempts a person from liability for causing injury, provided the defensive measures do not exceed what is necessary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Official">
  <data key="d0">Public Official</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A government employee who is not liable for causing injury if acting in pursuance of a superior's order that he was obliged to obey, provided he had reasonable grounds to believe the act was lawful and acted with care.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Victim">
  <data key="d0">Victim</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who suffers injury from an unlawful act and may claim damages; the right of action is prescribed after three years from knowledge of the injury and the responsible person, or fifteen years from the unlawful act.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supervisor">
  <data key="d0">Supervisor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person entrusted by law or agreement with the supervision of a person requiring care due to minority or mental or physical condition; liable for injuries caused by the supervised person and may escape liability by proving due diligence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Minor">
  <data key="d0">Minor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person under fifteen years of age or still under the care of a guardian; deemed to require supervision, which transfers to schoolteachers or craft supervisors while under their care.&lt;SEP&gt;A minor is a person who has not attained majority. A minor who has attained eighteen years has a special domicile for acts the law considers the minor capable of performing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Master">
  <data key="d0">Master</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who has actual powers of supervision and control over a servant; liable for damage caused by the servant's unlawful acts performed in the course of or as a result of employment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Master (Employer) commissions works, is obligated to take delivery and pay the price, may terminate the contract before completion provided compensation is paid, and has rights regarding contract resiliation in cases of significant cost overruns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servant">
  <data key="d0">Servant</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person under the employment and control of a master; the master-servant relationship exists when the master has actual powers of supervision and direction over the servant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Animal Keeper">
  <data key="d0">Animal Keeper</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person in charge of an animal, even if not its owner, who is liable for harm caused by the animal even if it strays or escapes, unless the harm resulted from a cause beyond the keeper's control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Animal">
  <data key="d0">Animal</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">A living creature under the care of an animal keeper; harm caused by the animal gives rise to the keeper's strict liability.&lt;SEP&gt;A living creature for whose harm a person in charge is liable even if not the owner, even if the animal strays or escapes, unless the accident was due to a cause beyond control, as established in Article 176.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Building Keeper">
  <data key="d0">Building Keeper</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person in charge of a building, even if not its owner, who is liable for damage caused by the building's collapse.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Penal Offence">
  <data key="d0">Penal Offence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A criminal offense from which a damages claim may arise; when the penal action has not yet prescribed, the civil damages action is only prescribed when the penal action itself is prescribed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Necessity">
  <data key="d0">Necessity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal defense where a person causes injury to another in order to avoid a greater injury threatening himself or a third party; the person is only responsible for such equitable damages as the judge deems appropriate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security">
  <data key="d0">Security</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A guarantee that a debtor may be ordered to provide when damages are paid by installments or in the form of a regular periodical payment, as determined by the judge.&lt;SEP&gt;Security refers to the special guarantee or collateral given to a creditor by a debtor, whether by subsequent act or by virtue of law. The debtor must not diminish the security, and if it is reduced, the debtor may forfeit the benefit of the term unless adequate replacement security is provided.&lt;SEP&gt;Security refers to guarantees on movable or immovable property given to creditors of the estate, which must be published in accordance with provisions for judgment charges on real property if granted on immovable property.&lt;SEP&gt;A guarantee or surety that may be demanded from the possessor or usufructuary to protect the rights of the owner, including in contexts of installment repayments, endangered ownership rights, and movable property subject to usufruct.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Redress">
  <data key="d0">Right of Redress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal right of a person held responsible for the act of another to claim compensation from that other person to the extent that the other person is responsible for reparation of the injury.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Restoration">
  <data key="d0">Restoration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A method of making good damage ordered by the judge upon the demand of the victim, whereby the original position before the unlawful act is restored.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Mandate">
  <data key="d0">Special Mandate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mandate required for any act that is not an act of management, in particular for sale, mortgage, gifts, compromise, admission, arbitration, tendering of an oath, and court representation. It only confers power to act in specified matters and matters necessarily incidental thereto according to prevailing custom.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Substitute (نائب الوكيل)">
  <data key="d0">Substitute (نائب الوكيل)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person nominated by the mandatary to perform the mandate in the mandatary's place. If appointed without authorization, the mandatary is responsible for the substitute's acts as if they were their own, and both are jointly and severally liable. If authorized, the mandatary is only liable for faulty choice or faulty instructions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Party (الغير)">
  <data key="d0">Third Party (الغير)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An external person who deals with the mandatary or in whose interest a mandate may be granted. When a mandate is given in the interest of a third party, the mandator may not revoke or restrict it without the third party's consent. Articles 104 to 107 regarding representation apply to the relationship with third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heirs (ورثة)">
  <data key="d0">Heirs (ورثة)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The heirs of a deceased mandatary who, if they possess legal capacity and knowledge of the mandate, are bound to inform the mandator of the death and take necessary steps to protect the mandator's interests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judge (القاضى)">
  <data key="d0">Judge (القاضى)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The judicial authority who assesses the remuneration agreed for a mandate, unless the remuneration has been voluntarily paid after performance of the mandate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deposit (الوديعة)">
  <data key="d0">Deposit (الوديعة)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract whereby one person (the depository) agrees to take delivery from another person (the depositor) a thing which the depository undertakes to keep in safe custody and return in kind. The depository may not use the deposited thing without authority or appoint a substitute except in cases of urgent necessity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Depository (المودع عنده)">
  <data key="d0">Depository (المودع عنده)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The person who receives the deposited thing and is bound to keep it in safe custody and return it. In a gratuitous deposit, the depository must exercise the care used in their own affairs; in a remunerated deposit, the diligence of a reasonable person is required. The depository must return the thing upon the depositor's request.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Depositor (المودع)">
  <data key="d0">Depositor (المودع)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The person who delivers a thing to the depository for safe custody. The depositor has the right to demand return of the deposited thing at any time, unless the contract specifies a term fixed in the interests of the depository.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Articles 104 to 107">
  <data key="d0">Articles 104 to 107</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provisions regarding representation that apply to the relationship of a mandator and a mandatary with third parties dealing with the mandatary, as referenced in Article 713.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint and Several Liability (التضامن)">
  <data key="d0">Joint and Several Liability (التضامن)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal concept whereby multiple mandataries appointed under the same mandate, or multiple mandators who appoint a sole mandatary for a common purpose, share collective responsibility. Applies when the mandate is indivisible, when damage results from common fault, or when a mandatary appoints an unauthorized substitute. Mandataries are not responsible for acts by co-mandataries exceeding mandate limits or involving wrongful use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reasonable Person (الرجل المعتاد)">
  <data key="d0">Reasonable Person (الرجل المعتاد)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal standard of care and diligence used as a benchmark for obligations under both mandate and deposit contracts. Under a remunerated mandate, the mandatary must always exercise the diligence of a reasonable person; under a remunerated deposit, the depository must exercise the same standard. Under gratuitous arrangements, the standard is the care one gives to their own affairs, without exceeding the reasonable person standard.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Renunciation of Mandate">
  <data key="d0">Renunciation of Mandate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The right of the mandatary to resign from the mandate at any time, even contrary to agreement, by notifying the mandator. In a remunerated mandate, the mandatary must indemnify the mandator for loss from untimely or unjustified renunciation. A mandatary may not renounce a mandate given in the interest of a third party without serious justification and must notify the third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Revocation of Mandate">
  <data key="d0">Revocation of Mandate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The right of the mandator to revoke or restrict the mandate at any time, notwithstanding any agreement to the contrary. In a remunerated mandate, the mandator must indemnify the mandatary for loss from untimely or unjustified revocation. The mandator cannot revoke a mandate granted in the interest of a third party without that party's consent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servitude">
  <data key="d0">Servitude</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Servitude is a type of encumbrance or right affecting the thing sold, which the vendor is presumed not to warrant if it was apparent or disclosed to the purchaser, as provided under Article 445.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal right constituting a relationship of subordination between two properties, where the dominant tenement benefits from rights over the servient tenement, including use, preservation, and specific restrictions on building or land use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dominant Tenement">
  <data key="d0">Dominant Tenement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The property that benefits from a servitude, whose owner is entitled to carry out works necessary to use and preserve the right of servitude and must use it in the least harmful manner possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495929</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servient Tenement">
  <data key="d0">Servient Tenement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The property that bears the burden of a servitude, whose owner may not diminish the use of the servitude or make it more inconvenient, but may demand its transfer or cessation under certain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgagor">
  <data key="d0">Mortgagor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The person who grants a mortgage over property; may be the debtor or a third party, and must be the owner of the mortgaged property with legal capacity to dispose of it.&lt;SEP&gt;The mortgagor is the party who bears the cost of inscription, its renewal, and its radiation of the mortgage, unless an agreement provides otherwise.&lt;SEP&gt;The person who grants a mortgage over their property. The mortgagor may dispose of and manage the mortgaged property and collect its fruits until they become incorporated in the immovable. The mortgagor may be a person other than the debtor, and in such case only the mortgaged property may be proceeded against. The mortgagor is the guarantor of the effectiveness of the mortgage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgagee Creditor">
  <data key="d0">Mortgagee Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The creditor in whose favor a mortgage is constituted, acquiring a real right of preference over ordinary and subordinate creditors for payment of the claim from the proceeds of the mortgaged immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Immovable Property">
  <data key="d0">Immovable Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Immovable Property (العقار) refers to real estate whose possession, ownership, and real rights are governed by the law of the place where the immovable is situated, as stated in Article 18.&lt;SEP&gt;Immovable property includes things fixed in place that cannot be removed without damage, real rights over immovable property including ownership, and movables placed by their owner in an immovable to serve or exploit it.&lt;SEP&gt;Immovable property is real estate belonging to a person, including a legally incapable person, whose value is assessed at the time of sale to determine whether lesion exceeds one fifth.&lt;SEP&gt;Immovable Property refers to real estate whose ownership and other real rights are not transferred unless the rules laid down in the law regulating the publication of real rights are observed, and which may be subject to preemption.&lt;SEP&gt;Property on which a mortgage may only be constituted; it must be marketable, capable of being sold by public auction, and specifically described as to its nature and situation.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of property that is fixed or attached to land, including real estate and buildings, which serves as the subject of special privileges established in Articles 1147 through 1149.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Authentic Document">
  <data key="d0">Authentic Document</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A formal official instrument required for a valid gift of immovable property and for a binding promise to make a gift; absence of such a document renders the gift void unless it is disguised under another contract.&lt;SEP&gt;An official instrument required for the valid constitution of a mortgage, the costs of which are borne by the mortgagor unless otherwise agreed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Book IV: Accessory Rights Or Real Securities">
  <data key="d0">Book IV: Accessory Rights Or Real Securities</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The fourth book of the legal code governing accessory real rights and real securities, including mortgages and related provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter I: Mortgages">
  <data key="d0">Chapter I: Mortgages</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The first chapter of Book IV, governing the constitution, effects, and rules applicable to mortgages over immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1148">
  <data key="d0">Article 1148</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A referenced legal provision granting a privilege to sums due to contractors or architects, preserved without prejudice when a mortgage extends to accessories of the mortgaged property.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 1148 provides that sums due to contractors and architects entrusted with erection, reconstruction, repair, or maintenance of buildings or other works have a privilege over such works limited to the increase in value, and such privilege must be inscribed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-Owners In Common">
  <data key="d0">Co-Owners In Common</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Joint owners of a dominant tenement who share servitude rights; use of the servitude by one co-owner interrupts prescription in favor of all others.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deed Of Creation">
  <data key="d0">Deed Of Creation</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The document by which a servitude is created, containing the rules that govern the servitude as stated in Article 1019.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local Custom">
  <data key="d0">Local Custom</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The customary practices of a locality that serve as one of the governing sources for servitudes alongside the deed of creation and statutory provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescription">
  <data key="d0">Prescription</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescription (التقادم) refers to legal time limits, with new legislative provisions applying from the time they come into force for incomplete prescription periods.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal time limit for bringing claims, generally set at three years from knowledge of the right and fifteen years from when the right arose for enrichment, restitution, and voluntary agency claims.&lt;SEP&gt;Prescription is the extinction of a debt through the passage of a legally prescribed time period; it benefits other co-debtors only to the extent of the contributing debtor's share, and interruption or suspension as to one debtor does not affect others.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal mechanism by which a neighbor may acquire the right to a direct view over another's property at a distance of less than one meter through long-term continuous use, preventing the other neighbor from building within one meter along the length of the building in which the view was opened.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal mechanism by which a right of servitude can be extinguished through non-user over a prescribed period, or by which the manner of exercising the servitude can be modified, as stated in Article 1027.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-User">
  <data key="d0">Non-User</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The failure to exercise a servitude right over a specified period, leading to its extinguishment: fifteen years in general and thirty-three years for servitudes benefiting wakf property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fruits And Revenues">
  <data key="d0">Fruits And Revenues</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The produce and income generated by a mortgaged property, which from the date of transcription of the formal summons to pay are assimilated to the immovable and distributed in the same manner as the price of the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Formal Summons To Pay">
  <data key="d0">Formal Summons To Pay</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An official notice whose transcription triggers the assimilation of the fruits and revenues of the mortgaged property to the immovable, making them distributable in the same manner as the property price.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Future Property">
  <data key="d0">Future Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property that does not yet exist at the time of the gift, a gift of which is declared void under Article 492.&lt;SEP&gt;Property not yet owned or in expectancy; a mortgage on future property is declared void under Article 1033.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section I: The Constitution Of Mortgages">
  <data key="d0">Section I: The Constitution Of Mortgages</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A subdivision of Chapter I within Book IV, specifically addressing the requirements and rules for the valid creation and formation of mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lessor">
  <data key="d0">Lessor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The party in a lease contract who is bound to deliver the leased property, maintain it in good condition, carry out necessary repairs, and abstain from disturbing the lessee's enjoyment.&lt;SEP&gt;The property owner who grants a lease to the lessee. The lessor is obligated to abstain from disturbing the lessee's enjoyment, warrants against disturbances and defects, and has rights including lien on movables, rent collection, and objecting to alterations. The lessor is liable for compensation when defects or disturbances cause damage to the lessee.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessor is the property owner who grants a lease to the lessee. The lessor has rights of retention and recovery over movables, is entitled to receive rent, may agree to or object to improvements, and may terminate the lease under certain conditions such as personal need or the lessee's insolvency.&lt;SEP&gt;The party who grants a lease and owns or controls the leased property, with rights to receive rent and obligations to hand over property and perform major repairs.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lessor (المؤجر) is a person who leases property. The lessor is deemed a holder of furniture in leased premises for purposes of privilege enforcement. The lessor has a privilege over attachable movables and crops on the leased property belonging to the lessee for rent and other sums due under the lease, as governed by Article 1143.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who leases property and holds a lessor's privilege, which has the same rank as a hotel proprietor's privilege.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lessee">
  <data key="d0">Lessee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The party in a lease contract who enjoys the use of the leased property in return for paying rent and may demand resiliation or rent reduction under certain conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The party who rents and occupies the leased property from the lessor. The lessee is obligated to use the property as agreed, carry out lessee's repairs, pay rent at agreed times, notify the lessor of issues requiring intervention, restitute the property upon lease expiration, and is responsible for fire damage and deterioration not resulting from normal use.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessee is the party who rents the leased property from the lessor. The lessee is obligated to restitute the leased property upon expiration, maintain it in good condition, pay rent, and may assign the lease or sublet subject to agreement. The lessee may make improvements and claim reimbursement for them.&lt;SEP&gt;The party who rents and occupies leased property, with obligations including proper cultivation, maintenance of land and equipment, and payment of rent.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lessee is the party who leases wakf property and is bound, under penalty of resiliation of the contract, to make up the rent to the level of rent for similar properties if the agreed rent is grossly inadequate.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lessee (المستأجر) is a person who rents property from a lessor. The lessee's movables and crops on the leased property are subject to the lessor's privilege, as stated in Article 1143.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-Lessee">
  <data key="d0">Sub-Lessee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A sub-lessee is a person who rents the leased property from the principal lessee under a sub-lease arrangement. The sub-lessee is directly answerable to the lessor for amounts owed to the principal lessee after a summons is served.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sub-Lessee (المستأجر من الباطن) is a person who rents property from the principal lessee. The lessor's privilege extends to movables and crops belonging to the sub-lessee if sub-letting was expressly prohibited, or up to the amount due by the sub-lessee to the principal lessee if sub-letting was not prohibited, as governed by Article 1143.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignee">
  <data key="d0">Assignee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The assignee is the person to whom a right is assigned, who may exercise a right of recourse in warranty against the assignor, and whose assignment is preferred if it is first effective as regards third parties in cases of multiple assignments.&lt;SEP&gt;The assignee is the person to whom the lease is transferred by the principal lessee. The principal lessee remains guarantor for the assignee's obligations unless the lessor formally accepts the assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heirs of Lessee">
  <data key="d0">Heirs of Lessee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The heirs of the lessee may claim termination of the lease upon the death of the lessee if the burden of the lease has become too heavy for their resources or exceeds their needs, provided they act within six months of the death.&lt;SEP&gt;The successors of a deceased lessee who may claim reimbursement of expenses or continue cultivation of the land until crops ripen in the case of amodiation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Owner">
  <data key="d0">New Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The new owner is a third party to whom ownership of the leased property is voluntarily or forcibly transferred. The new owner is bound by the lease only if it has an established date prior to the transfer, and may evict the lessee only after providing proper notice and compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leased Property">
  <data key="d0">Leased Property</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The specific thing that is the subject of a lease contract, which must be delivered in a condition suitable for its intended purpose.&lt;SEP&gt;The property subject to the agreement of lease, which the lessee is entitled to use and enjoy. It may be an immovable property such as a house, warehouse, shop, or agricultural land, and may contain defects or be subject to third-party claims and government authority actions.&lt;SEP&gt;The leased property is the subject of the lease contract. It must be restituted by the lessee upon expiration in the condition it was received, subject to permissible loss or deterioration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lease Contract">
  <data key="d0">Lease Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The lease contract is the agreement between the lessor and lessee governing the use of the leased property. It ends at the expiration of the agreed term and may be renewed tacitly, assigned, or sublet subject to conditions established in the applicable articles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495930</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignment of Lease">
  <data key="d0">Assignment of Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Assignment of lease is the transfer of the lessee's rights under the lease to an assignee. The principal lessee remains guarantor for the assignee's obligations unless the lessor formally agrees to the assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-Lease">
  <data key="d0">Sub-Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sub-lease is an arrangement where the lessee rents out the whole or part of the leased property to a sub-lessee. A prohibition of sub-letting implies a prohibition of assignment and vice versa.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compensation">
  <data key="d0">Compensation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation is a payment owed by the lessee to the lessor for unlawful retention of the leased property, for damage caused by removal of unauthorized improvements, or owed by the lessor to the lessee for early eviction or termination of the lease.&lt;SEP&gt;Damages that the lessee may be entitled to claim when the leased property is unfit for use, its usefulness is appreciably diminished, or the lessor fails to fulfill obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Compensation is the legal mechanism by which mutual debts between a creditor and a co-debtor are offset; a joint and several debtor cannot set up compensation with regard to that which a creditor owes to another co-debtor, except in respect of that other co-debtor's share.&lt;SEP&gt;Compensation refers to amounts due on account of the destruction of a building, which shall be set aside for the costs of reconstruction without prejudice to the rights of registered creditors, per Article 868.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Improvements">
  <data key="d0">Improvements</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Improvements include buildings, plantings, or other additions made by the lessee to the leased property that increase its value. The lessor is bound to reimburse the lessee for improvements unless they were made without knowledge or despite objection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rent">
  <data key="d0">Rent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The payment due under a lease, which may consist of money or any other prestation, and is determined by agreement or based on current rent for similar properties.&lt;SEP&gt;The payment owed by the lessee to the lessor for use of the leased property, payable at agreed times or, in the absence of agreement, at times established by local custom. Payment is presumed to cover prior terms unless proven otherwise.&lt;SEP&gt;Rent is the payment owed by the lessee to the lessor for use of the leased property. Advance rent payments cannot be set up against a new owner if the lessee knew of the ownership transfer at the time of payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Notice of Evacuation">
  <data key="d0">Notice of Evacuation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Notice of evacuation is a formal notification given by one party to the other to terminate the lease and vacate the leased property, governed by the periods specified in Article 563.&lt;SEP&gt;The formal notice required to terminate a lease of undetermined duration, with specific notice periods varying by property type: three months for agricultural and uncultivated land, two months for houses, shops, offices, and warehouses, and one month for apartments and furnished rooms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tacit Renewal">
  <data key="d0">Tacit Renewal</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tacit renewal occurs when the lessee continues to enjoy the leased property after expiration with the knowledge and without objection of the lessor. It is deemed a new lease on the same conditions but for an indefinite duration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 563">
  <data key="d0">Article 563</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 563 is a referenced legal provision that prescribes the periods of notice of termination applicable to lease agreements and is cited throughout Articles 599 through 608.&lt;SEP&gt;A referenced legal provision governing notice periods required for lease termination.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Real Securities">
  <data key="d0">Real Securities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Real securities are property-based guarantees provided by the debtor for the original obligation, which may be transferred to the new obligation under specific conditions depending on the type of novation.&lt;SEP&gt;Real securities are guarantees supplied by the lessee in connection with the lease. They transfer to a tacitly renewed lease subject to the rules of publication applicable to real property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Industrial or Commercial Establishment">
  <data key="d0">Industrial or Commercial Establishment</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An industrial or commercial establishment is a business entity created by the lessee on the leased immovable property. Its sale by the lessee may trigger judicial exceptions to sub-letting prohibitions under Article 594.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movables">
  <data key="d0">Movables</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Movables are personal property items present on the leased property over which the lessor holds rights of retention and recovery, except when removed to meet the professional requirements of the lessee in accordance with customary requirements of daily life.&lt;SEP&gt;Furniture, goods, crops, cattle, or implements found in the leased property that serve as security for rent payment and are subject to the lessor's lien, even when they do not belong to the lessee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proces Verbal">
  <data key="d0">Proces Verbal</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A proces verbal is a formal written record or inventory setting out particulars of the leased property drawn at the time of delivery. Its absence creates a legal presumption that the lessee received the property in good condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transfer of Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Transfer of Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Transfer of ownership is the voluntary or forced conveyance of the leased property to a third party, which affects the enforceability of the lease against the new owner depending on whether the lease has an established date prior to the transfer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resiliation of Lease">
  <data key="d0">Resiliation of Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The termination of a lease contract that the lessee may demand if the leased property is unfit for use, poses a serious health danger, or is partially or totally destroyed.&lt;SEP&gt;The termination of the lease agreement, available as a remedy to the lessee when enjoyment is deprived by third-party claims, government authority acts, trespass, or defects warranting such action.&lt;SEP&gt;Resiliation of lease is the judicial cancellation of the lease contract, distinct from normal termination. The lessor may claim resiliation due to the lessee's insolvency unless securities are provided, and the lessee may claim resiliation if not authorized to assign or sublet, upon payment of equitable compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Party Wall">
  <data key="d0">Party Wall</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A wall shared between two adjoining properties that co-proprietors may use for its intended purpose, including supporting roof beams, provided the wall is not overloaded; repairs and reconstruction costs are borne proportionally by co-proprietors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direct View">
  <data key="d0">Direct View</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal concept restricting a neighbor from having a straight-facing opening over a neighbor's property at a distance of less than one meter, measured from the outside face of the wall or the edge of a balcony.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oblique View">
  <data key="d0">Oblique View</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal concept restricting a neighbor from having an angled opening over a neighbor's property at a distance of less than fifty centimeters from the outside edge of the opening, unless the view also constitutes a direct view over a public road.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Light Shaft Opening">
  <data key="d0">Light Shaft Opening</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An opening whose base is above normal human height, intended solely for the passage of air and light, not providing a view over neighboring property, and exempt from distance requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inalienability Clause">
  <data key="d0">Inalienability Clause</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A clause in a contract or will stipulating that a property cannot be alienated; valid only if based on a legitimate reason, such as protecting a lawful interest, and limited to a reasonable duration, which may extend for the life of a relevant party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Joint Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Joint ownership refers to the legal state in which multiple persons hold undivided shares in common property, governed by statutory provisions regarding alienation, partition, and management.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of property ownership in which two or more persons own the same undivided thing, with their respective shares presumed equal in the absence of proof to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-owner">
  <data key="d0">Co-owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who holds an undivided share in jointly owned property, with absolute ownership of their share, rights to alienate it, collect its fruits, participate in management, and take preservation measures even without consent of others.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who holds an undivided share in an immovable property held in common. A co-owner may grant a mortgage on their undivided share or on a divided part of the immovable. If as a result of partition a different property is attributed to the co-owner, the mortgage transfers to an equivalent portion of the new property.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who shares ownership of property with others and has partitioned it, holding a privilege over the partitioned property for remedies and repayment of differences.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Manager">
  <data key="d0">Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person entrusted with directing partnership affairs whose powers cease upon dissolution and who is deemed a liquidator toward third parties until a formal liquidator is appointed.&lt;SEP&gt;A person appointed by majority of co-owners or by the Court to manage property held in common; a co-owner who manages without objection from others is considered a mandatory of the rest.&lt;SEP&gt;A Manager is one or more persons appointed by co-owners holding the majority in value of shares to administer family joint ownership, with authority to introduce changes to improve enjoyment of the property, and who may be discharged by the Court or by the appointing co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Majority Decision">
  <data key="d0">Majority Decision</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A decision binding on all co-owners when taken by a majority calculated on the basis of the value of their shares, applicable to ordinary acts of management of commonly held property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 813">
  <data key="d0">Article 813</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision granting every owner the right to compel neighbors to place boundary marks on adjoining properties, with delimitation costs shared between them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 814">
  <data key="d0">Article 814</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision governing the use of party walls for intended purposes and the proportional sharing of repair and reconstruction costs among co-proprietors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 815">
  <data key="d0">Article 815</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision permitting an owner to heighten a party wall for good reason, bearing all costs, provided no serious prejudice is caused to the co-owner, and requiring full reconstruction at the owner's cost if the wall cannot support heightening.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495931</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 816">
  <data key="d0">Article 816</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing a neighbor who did not contribute to heightening expenses to become a co-proprietor of the heightened part by paying half the cost and half the value of any increased thickness ground area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 817">
  <data key="d0">Article 817</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision establishing the presumption that a wall separating two buildings at the time of construction is a party wall up to the point where it ceases to be common.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 818">
  <data key="d0">Article 818</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision preventing an owner from compelling a neighbor to assign part of a wall or land except as provided in Article 816, and prohibiting demolition of a wall that injures a neighbor without good reason.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 819">
  <data key="d0">Article 819</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring a minimum distance of one meter for direct views over a neighbor's property, with rights preserved by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 820">
  <data key="d0">Article 820</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring a minimum distance of fifty centimeters for oblique views, with the prohibition lifted if the view is simultaneously a direct view over a public road.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 821">
  <data key="d0">Article 821</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision exempting light shaft openings from distance requirements when the opening is above normal human height and intended only for air and light.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 822">
  <data key="d0">Article 822</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring factories, wells, steam engines, and establishments injurious to neighbors to be constructed at distances and under conditions laid down by regulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 823">
  <data key="d0">Article 823</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision governing the validity of inalienability clauses in contracts or wills, requiring a legitimate reason and reasonable duration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 824">
  <data key="d0">Article 824</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision declaring that any alienation contrary to a valid inalienability clause in a contract or will is void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 825">
  <data key="d0">Article 825</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision defining co-ownership as ownership by two or more persons of the same undivided thing, with shares presumed equal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 826">
  <data key="d0">Article 826</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision granting each co-owner absolute ownership of their share including alienation and collection of fruits, with provisions for transfer of rights upon partition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 827">
  <data key="d0">Article 827</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that management of property held in common belongs jointly to all co-owners unless otherwise agreed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 828">
  <data key="d0">Article 828</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision making majority decisions binding on all co-owners for ordinary management, allowing the Court to appoint a manager, and deeming unobjected managing co-owners as mandatories of the others.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 829">
  <data key="d0">Article 829</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing co-owners holding at least three-quarters of common property to make essential modifications exceeding normal management, with dissenting co-owners having two months to challenge in Court.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 830">
  <data key="d0">Article 830</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing any co-owner to take necessary preservation measures for common property without the consent of other co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 831">
  <data key="d0">Article 831</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring all co-owners to bear management, preservation, tax, and other costs of common property proportionally to their shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 832">
  <data key="d0">Article 832</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 832 provides that co-owners possessing at least three-quarters of common property may decide to alienate it based on serious grounds, with notification to other co-owners and a right of action before the Court within two months for dissenting co-owners.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal provision allowing co-owners holding at least three-quarters of common property to decide on its alienation based on serious grounds, with Court determination if partition is contrary to co-owners' interests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Establishments Injurious to Neighbors">
  <data key="d0">Establishments Injurious to Neighbors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A category encompassing factories, wells, steam engines, and all similar establishments that must be constructed at distances and under conditions prescribed by regulations due to their potential to cause harm to neighboring properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Regulations">
  <data key="d0">Regulations</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal rules that prescribe the distances and conditions under which factories, wells, steam engines, and establishments injurious to neighbors must be constructed, as referenced in Article 822.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acquirer">
  <data key="d0">Acquirer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who acquires a share or specific part of property held in common from a co-owner, whose right transfers to the portion that falls to the settler upon partition, or who may demand cancellation of the alienation if unaware the settler lacked ownership of the specific part alienated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partition">
  <data key="d0">Partition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Partition refers to the legal process of dividing property held in common among co-owners into separate parts, which may be carried out in kind, by sale, or by provisional arrangements.&lt;SEP&gt;The division of property held in common among co-owners into separate individual portions, referenced in the context of transferring acquirer rights to the part devolving upon the settler, and as a factor in determining whether alienation of jointly owned property should proceed.&lt;SEP&gt;The division of co-owned immovable property. A mortgage granted by all co-owners remains effective regardless of the result of partition. A mortgage on an individual co-owner's share transfers to equivalent property attributed upon partition, with the portion fixed by judicial order.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Movable Property">
  <data key="d0">Common Movable Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Movable property or a collection of property held in common by co-owners, referenced in Article 833 in the context of a co-owner's right of recovery of a common share before partition is effected.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 833">
  <data key="d0">Article 833</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 833 grants a co-owner of movable property or property consisting of movables and immovables the right to repurchase an undivided share sold by another co-owner to a third person within thirty days of knowledge or notification of the sale.&lt;SEP&gt;A partially visible legal provision concerning a co-owner's right to recover a common share in movable property or a collection of property before partition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solidarity">
  <data key="d0">Solidarity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Solidarity (Joint and Several Obligation) is a relationship between multiple creditors or multiple debtors that is not presumed but created by agreement or by law. Payment by one debtor liberates all others, and a creditor may pay any one of several joint creditors.&lt;SEP&gt;Solidarity is the legal principle under which multiple debtors are jointly bound for the same obligation, such that payment by one debtor liberates all others, and a creditor may take action against all debtors jointly or severally.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint and Several Creditor">
  <data key="d0">Joint and Several Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A joint and several creditor is a creditor in a solidary obligation who receives payment on account of the debt that reverts to all creditors and is divided proportionally or equally among them, subject to contrary agreement or legal provision.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint and Several Debtor">
  <data key="d0">Joint and Several Debtor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A joint and several debtor is a debtor in a solidary obligation who is liable for the entire debt, is only responsible for his own acts in performance, and has a right of recourse against co-debtors for their respective shares after full payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-debtor">
  <data key="d0">Co-debtor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Co-debtor is a person sharing joint responsibility for a debt, whose obligation is not discharged if the Debtor retracts a tender before acceptance or judgment.&lt;SEP&gt;A co-debtor is a fellow debtor in a joint obligation who shares liability and against whom other co-debtors may claim contribution for their respective shares, including shares of insolvent co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Novation">
  <data key="d0">Novation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Novation is the replacement of an existing obligation with a new one, effected by a change of the debt, a change of the Debtor, or a change of the Creditor through agreement of the parties.&lt;SEP&gt;Novation is a legal mechanism that extinguishes an original obligation with its accessories and substitutes a new obligation in its place, requiring that both obligations be free from grounds of nullity.&lt;SEP&gt;Novation is the replacement of an existing debt with a new obligation; when occurring between a creditor and one joint and several debtor, it releases other co-debtors unless the creditor reserves his rights against them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Merger">
  <data key="d0">Merger</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Merger is a legal mechanism whereby the qualities of creditor and debtor in the same debt are united in one person, extinguishing the debt to the extent of the merger, with revival possible if the cause of merger disappears retroactively.&lt;SEP&gt;Merger occurs when the capacities of creditor and debtor unite in the same person, which does not extinguish the debt as regards other co-debtors except to the extent of the merged share of the co-debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Release of Debt">
  <data key="d0">Release of Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Release of debt is the creditor's voluntary discharge of a debtor from obligation, which does not release other co-debtors unless the creditor expressly declares such; the creditor may claim the balance from remaining co-debtors after deducting the released debtor's share.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indivisibility">
  <data key="d0">Indivisibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Indivisibility is a legal principle whereby an obligation cannot be divided, either because its object is naturally indivisible or because the parties intended that performance should not be divided.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignment of a Right">
  <data key="d0">Assignment of a Right</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The assignment of a right is the transfer of a creditor's claim to a third party, valid without the debtor's consent, subject to conditions of notification, attachability, and comprising all associated warranties, securities, privileges, mortgages, and accrued interest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transmission of an Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Transmission of an Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmission of an obligation is the broader legal framework covering the transfer of rights and obligations between parties, organized under Chapter IV of the civil code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter IV">
  <data key="d0">Chapter IV</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter IV, titled "Transmission of an Obligation," is a structural division of the civil code encompassing provisions on the assignment of rights and the transfer of obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;A chapter titled "Privileged Rights" introducing priority rights established by law for certain claims.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495932</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section I">
  <data key="d0">Section I</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section I, titled "The Assignment of a Right," is a subdivision within Chapter IV specifically addressing the legal rules governing the assignment of a creditor's right to a third party, including validity, effectiveness, warranties, and notification requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Section I, titled "Elements of a Pledge," is the first section of Chapter III covering the essential requirements, elements, and conditions for a valid pledge, including the object of the pledge and applicable mortgage provisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A section titled "General Provisions" under Chapter IV, appearing at the end of the text and introducing the rules on privileged rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 298">
  <data key="d0">Article 298</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 298 provides that if a joint and several debtor becomes insolvent, the insolvent debtor's share is borne by the co-debtor who effected payment and all other solvent co-debtors pro rata to their respective shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 300">
  <data key="d0">Article 300</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 300 defines when an obligation is indivisible: when its object is by nature not susceptible to division, or when the parties' intention or the purpose of the obligation requires indivisible performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 303">
  <data key="d0">Article 303</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 303 establishes that a creditor may assign his right to a third party provided the claim is not impossible to assign by law, agreement, or nature, and the assignment is valid without the debtor's consent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction">
  <data key="d0">Transaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A transaction is a settlement entered into between a creditor and one joint and several debtor that benefits other co-debtors if it involves remission of the debt or release of liability, but is only binding on other co-debtors if they consent when it creates or increases obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignor">
  <data key="d0">Assignor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The assignor is the person who transfers or assigns a right to another party, warrants the existence of the right at the moment of assignment, and is responsible for personal acts even if the assignment is without consideration or warranty.&lt;SEP&gt;The assignor is the original creditor who transfers his right to a third party; in an assignment for valuable consideration the assignor warrants the existence of the right at the time of assignment, but does not warrant the debtor's solvency unless specifically stipulated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attachment">
  <data key="d0">Attachment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal seizure served upon the debtor of an assigned debt, which, if served before the assignment becomes effective against third parties, makes the assignment equivalent to an attachment against the distrainer.&lt;SEP&gt;Attachment is a legal constraint on a creditor's right limiting its transferability; a right can only be assigned to the extent to which it can be attached, as provided under Article 304.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Formal Demand">
  <data key="d0">Formal Demand</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A formal demand is a procedural act directed at a debtor; a formal demand by the creditor to one joint and several debtor has no effect against other co-debtors, but a formal demand issued by one co-debtor against the creditor benefits all other co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acknowledgement of Debt">
  <data key="d0">Acknowledgement of Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An acknowledgement of debt is a debtor's recognition of the obligation; when made by one joint and several debtor, it does not bind the other co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oath">
  <data key="d0">Oath</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An oath is a sworn statement used in judicial proceedings; if one joint and several debtor refuses an oath or tenders it to the creditor, it does not prejudice other co-debtors, but if the creditor tenders an oath to one co-debtor who takes it, the oath benefits all other co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judgment">
  <data key="d0">Judgment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A judgment is a court decision that, upon becoming final, replaces a contract when a party who promised to enter into a contract refuses to do so and legal proceedings are brought to enforce the promise, provided the conditions for conclusion of the contract are met.&lt;SEP&gt;A judgment is a court decision in relation to a joint and several obligation; a judgment against one co-debtor has no effect against others, while a judgment in favor of one co-debtor benefits all others unless based on a ground personal to that co-debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgagee">
  <data key="d0">Mortgagee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A mortgagee is a secured creditor who holds a mortgage on property and is paid before unsecured creditors out of the proceeds of sale of the mortgaged property, in the order of the rank of their inscriptions.&lt;SEP&gt;The creditor who holds the mortgage right. The mortgagee may oppose any act or omission that appreciably diminishes their security, take protective measures in emergencies, and claim expenses from the mortgagor. The mortgagee has the right of preference over ordinary creditors and the right of tracing into substituted assets.&lt;SEP&gt;The Mortgagee is a creditor who holds a mortgage on property and has the right to initiate expropriation proceedings against the third party holder or debtor to recover the secured debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d0">Mortgaged Property</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The immovable property subject to a mortgage, which may include constructions erected on third-party land. Every part of the mortgaged property secures the whole of the debt. The mortgaged property may be disposed of by the mortgagor, but such disposal does not affect the right of the mortgagee. If the property perishes or deteriorates, the mortgage transfers to compensation, insurance monies, or expropriation payments.&lt;SEP&gt;Mortgaged Property is immovable property subject to a mortgage that may be sold by auction, purged of inscriptions, abandoned, or expropriated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inscription">
  <data key="d0">Inscription</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Inscription refers to the registration of a mortgage, its renewal, and its radiation, governed by the law regulating the publication of real rights.&lt;SEP&gt;The formal registration of a mortgage deed or judgment to establish its legal effect against third parties. Inscription procedures, renewal, radiation, and annulment are governed by the law regulating the publication of real rights. The cost of inscription, its renewal, and its radiation is borne by the mortgagor unless otherwise agreed.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal requirement for registration of certain privileges over immovables, which determines the rank of the privilege by the date of its registration.&lt;SEP&gt;Inscription (القيد) is a legal registration procedure applicable to privileged rights over immovable property. The provisions relating to inscription, its effects, renewal, and radiation are applicable to privileges over immovables, as stated in Article 1134.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Preference">
  <data key="d0">Right of Preference</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Right of preference is a legal priority right held by certain creditors over others, recognized in Articles 234 and 242, which grants privileged standing in the distribution of the debtor's assets.&lt;SEP&gt;The Right of Preference is the legal principle by which mortgagees are paid before unsecured creditors out of the proceeds of the mortgaged property according to the rank of their inscriptions.&lt;SEP&gt;The right of mortgagees to be paid before ordinary creditors from the proceeds of the mortgaged property or from the property that has replaced it, according to their order of rank.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Tracing">
  <data key="d0">Right of Tracing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Right of Tracing allows mortgagees to pursue their claims against the mortgaged property or any monies obtained in substitution thereof.&lt;SEP&gt;The right of the mortgagee to follow the mortgage into substituted assets such as compensation, insurance monies, or expropriation payments when the mortgaged property perishes or deteriorates.&lt;SEP&gt;The Right of Tracing (حق التتبع) is a legal concept allowing a creditor to follow privileged property into the hands of subsequent holders. General privileges, even over immovables, do not confer a right of tracing, as stated in Article 1134.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code of Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Code of Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Code of Procedure is the legal code prescribing the manner in which deposits are to be made following a tender of the thing due.&lt;SEP&gt;The Code of Procedure is the body of procedural law that governs the manner of sale of common property that cannot be divided in kind.&lt;SEP&gt;The law governing the procedural aspects of expropriation and sale of mortgaged property, prescribing the delays and formalities for such proceedings.&lt;SEP&gt;The Code of Procedure is the procedural law governing expropriation proceedings and legal processes related to mortgage enforcement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights">
  <data key="d0">Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights is the legislation that governs the publication of acts, judgments, and instruments related to immovable property, determining which must be published and the rules for such publication.&lt;SEP&gt;The Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights is the governing legislation that controls inscription, renewal, radiation, and annulment of radiation of mortgages.&lt;SEP&gt;The law governing the procedures for inscription, its renewal, radiation, annulment of radiation, and all effects related to the formal registration of real rights including mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Formal Summons to Pay">
  <data key="d0">Formal Summons to Pay</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A formal notification to the debtor requiring payment. From the date of its transcription, the fruits and revenues of the mortgaged property are assimilated to the immovable. Its transcription also triggers time limits for lease enforceability against the mortgagee.&lt;SEP&gt;A Formal Summons to Pay is a legal notice served by a mortgagee upon the debtor or third party holder demanding payment of the secured debt, and its transcription benefits all other creditors.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal document transcribed and served in connection with mortgage enforcement proceedings, in whose margin the declaration of abandonment by the Third Party Holder must be entered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lease">
  <data key="d0">Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract by which the lessor undertakes to enable the lessee to enjoy a specific thing for a certain time in return of a fixed rent.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal agreement for renting property, including fixed-period leases that may be terminated under serious circumstances with equitable compensation.&lt;SEP&gt;A rental agreement entered into by the mortgagor over the mortgaged property. A lease cannot have effect against the mortgagee unless it has an established date before the transcription of the formal summons to pay. Leases exceeding nine years are enforceable against the mortgagee only for nine years unless transcribed before the inscription of the mortgage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section II: Effects of a Mortgage">
  <data key="d0">Section II: Effects of a Mortgage</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal section covering the effects of mortgage as between the parties with respect to the mortgagor and the mortgagee, and the effects of mortgage as regards third parties, including rights of preference and tracing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pactum Commissorium">
  <data key="d0">Pactum Commissorium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An agreement that authorizes the creditor to acquire the mortgaged property at a fixed price upon non-payment or to sell it without legal formalities. Such agreements are void even if entered into after the constitution of the mortgage. However, the debtor may transfer the property in payment of the debt after it has fallen due.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rules of Accession">
  <data key="d0">Rules of Accession</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal rules governing the right of a landowner to keep constructions erected on their land by a third party, requiring the landowner to pay compensation. Referenced in Article 1038 in connection with the mortgage on constructions built on land belonging to another person.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mortgage Deed">
  <data key="d0">Mortgage Deed</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The formal document in which the mortgage is constituted. The mortgage deed must specify the amount of the secured debt or the maximum amount the debt may attain, particularly when the mortgage secures a conditional, future, or contingent debt, an opened credit, or a current account.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ordinary Creditors">
  <data key="d0">Ordinary Creditors</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Creditors who do not hold a mortgage or preferential right. Mortgagees are paid from the proceeds of the mortgaged property before ordinary creditors according to their order of rank.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignment of Rent">
  <data key="d0">Assignment of Rent</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The advance receipt or transfer of rent from the mortgaged property. An assignment of rent for a period not exceeding three years is not valid against the mortgagee unless it has an established date prior to the transcription of the formal summons to pay. For periods exceeding three years, transcription before the inscription of the mortgage is required.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expropriation">
  <data key="d0">Expropriation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Expropriation is a legal process of forcibly depriving an owner, third party holder, or receiver of ownership of mortgaged property through compulsory sale by public auction.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal process by which the mortgagee, upon summons to the debtor to pay, may proceed with the forced sale of the mortgaged property within the delays and formalities prescribed by the Code of Procedure. Expropriation for public utility also generates payments to which a mortgage may transfer under the right of tracing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abandonment of Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d0">Abandonment of Mortgaged Property</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A procedure by which a mortgagor who is not the debtor may avoid proceedings against them by surrendering the mortgaged property, following the procedures and rules laid down for the abandonment of an immovable by a third party holder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 834">
  <data key="d0">Article 834</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 834 establishes that every co-owner may demand partition of property held in common unless bound by law or agreement, and prohibits agreements preventing partition for more than five years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 835">
  <data key="d0">Article 835</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 835 allows co-owners in unanimous agreement to divide common property in any manner they deem fit, with legal formalities required when a co-owner is subject to legal incapacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 836">
  <data key="d0">Article 836</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 836 provides that if co-owners disagree on partition, a co-owner wishing to withdraw may summon others before the Summary Court, which may delegate experts for valuation and division.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 837">
  <data key="d0">Article 837</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 837 states that the expert will compose separate parts based on the smallest share, and if a co-owner cannot obtain all his share in kind, he shall be compensated by payment equal to the shortage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 838">
  <data key="d0">Article 838</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 838 grants the Summary Court jurisdiction over disputes relating to the composition of separate parts and referral of other disputes to the Court of First Instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 839">
  <data key="d0">Article 839</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 839 provides that once disputes are resolved and lots allocated, the Summary Court gives judgment assigning each co-owner his divided part, with partition by drawing lots if no direct allotment was made.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 840">
  <data key="d0">Article 840</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 840 requires Court ratification of a final partition judgment when one of the co-owners is absent or under legal incapacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 841">
  <data key="d0">Article 841</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 841 provides that when property cannot be divided in kind or would suffer serious diminution in value, it shall be sold in the manner laid down by the Code of Procedure, with auction restricted to co-owners if they unanimously request it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 842">
  <data key="d0">Article 842</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 842 grants personal creditors of any co-owner the right to oppose partition in kind or sale by auction without their intervention, requiring notification to all co-owners and joinder of inscribed creditors before a partition action.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 843">
  <data key="d0">Article 843</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 843 deems each co-partitioner to have been the owner of the part falling to him from the date he became co-owner and never to have owned the other parts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 844">
  <data key="d0">Article 844</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 844 establishes that co-partitioners warrant each other against interference or eviction due to causes existing before partition, with liability proportional to shares, subject to waiver by express agreement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 846">
  <data key="d0">Article 846</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 846 governs provisional partition, whereby co-owners allot enjoyment of divided parts equal to their shares, with a maximum duration of five years and conversion to final partition after fifteen years of continuous possession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 847">
  <data key="d0">Article 847</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 847 provides that a provisional partition may also take place when co-owners agree to take turns enjoying all the common property for periods corresponding to their shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 848">
  <data key="d0">Article 848</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 848 states that provisional partition is governed by provisions relating to contracts of lease insofar as they are not incompatible with its nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 849">
  <data key="d0">Article 849</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 849 allows co-owners to agree on a provisional partition during final partition proceedings, and permits the Summary Judge to order it upon application of one co-owner if agreement cannot be reached.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 850">
  <data key="d0">Article 850</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 850 prohibits co-owners from demanding partition of common property when the purpose for which it is intended requires it to remain permanently in common ownership.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 850 establishes that co-owners of property held in common cannot demand its partition if the intended use requires it to always remain in common.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 851">
  <data key="d0">Article 851</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 851 permits members of the same family with a common occupation or interest to create a family joint ownership in writing, consisting of inherited property or any other property they agree to include.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 851 provides that members of the same family with a common occupation or interest may agree in writing to create a family joint ownership from inherited or other property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-Owner">
  <data key="d0">Co-Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A co-owner is a person who holds an undivided share in property held in common with others, possessing rights to demand partition, repurchase shares, and participate in decisions regarding the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Provisional Partition">
  <data key="d0">Provisional Partition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Provisional partition is a temporary arrangement in which co-owners agree to allot each other the enjoyment of divided parts of common property, which may convert to a final partition after fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obligatory Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Obligatory Joint Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Obligatory joint ownership arises when the purpose of the common property requires it to remain permanently undivided, preventing co-owners from demanding partition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Family Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Family Joint Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family joint ownership is a form of joint ownership created by written agreement among members of the same family who share a common occupation or interest, comprising inherited or other property.&lt;SEP&gt;Family Joint Ownership is a legal arrangement whereby members of the same family who share a common occupation or interest agree in writing to hold property jointly, consisting either of an inheritance left wholly or partly in joint ownership or any other property they agree to place in joint ownership, for a period not exceeding fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Repurchase">
  <data key="d0">Right of Repurchase</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The right of repurchase allows a co-owner to buy back an undivided share sold by another co-owner to a third person within thirty days of knowledge or notification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Summary Court">
  <data key="d0">Summary Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Summary Court is the judicial body with competence to hear partition disputes, appoint experts, allocate divided parts, and refer matters outside its competence to the Court of First Instance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Summary Court is the court within whose jurisdiction the deceased was domiciled, and on whose notice board estate-related announcements must be posted under Article 886.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Summary Judge">
  <data key="d0">Summary Judge</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Summary Judge is the judicial officer empowered to order a provisional partition upon the application of a co-owner when agreement cannot be reached among co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expert">
  <data key="d0">Expert</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The expert is a person delegated by the Court to proceed with the valuation of common property and to divide it into separate parts based on the smallest share.&lt;SEP&gt;An Expert is a person with special experience who may be employed by the Administrator for the preparation of the inventory and the estimation of the value of the estate property.&lt;SEP&gt;The Expert is a person with special knowledge who may be engaged by the administrator to assist in preparing the inventory and estimating the value of estate assets under Article 888.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Action">
  <data key="d0">Right of Action</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The right of action allows a dissenting co-owner to challenge an alienation decision before the Court within two months from the date of notification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warranty Against Eviction">
  <data key="d0">Warranty Against Eviction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The warranty against eviction is an obligation among co-partitioners to indemnify each other against interference or eviction caused by reasons predating the partition, unless waived by express agreement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 845">
  <data key="d0">Article 845</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 845 allows rescission of partition by agreement if a co-partitioner proves injury exceeding one fifth of his share, provided the action is commenced within one year following the partition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Personal Creditor">
  <data key="d0">Personal Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A personal creditor of a co-owner has the right to oppose a partition in kind or a sale by auction without their intervention, and must be notified and joined in all proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Lease">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract of lease is the legal framework referenced in Article 848 as governing provisional partition in terms of validity against third parties, capacity, rights, obligations, and means of proof.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Representative">
  <data key="d0">Representative</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Representative is a person authorized to receive payment on behalf of the Creditor, and payment made to the Representative is deemed valid.&lt;SEP&gt;A representative is a person who enters into a contract on behalf of a principal within the limits of his authority. The representative, not the principal, is the person examined for vices of consent or effects of knowledge of special circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495934</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payment">
  <data key="d0">Payment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The fulfillment of an obligation which may be made by the debtor, his representative, or any interested or uninterested third party, subject to certain conditions including ownership and capacity to dispose, and which extinguishes the obligation.&lt;SEP&gt;Payment is the fulfillment of a debt obligation, which must be made with the very thing due, at the proper time and place, and carries specific rules regarding imputation, partial payment, and expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Receipt">
  <data key="d0">Receipt</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Receipt is a document issued by the Creditor as proof of discharge of payment, which qualifies the bearer to receive payment unless personal payment was agreed upon.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document of Title of Debt">
  <data key="d0">Document of Title of Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Document of Title of Debt is a legal instrument evidencing the existence of a debt, which the Debtor has the right to have annotated, restituted, or cancelled upon payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Giving in Payment">
  <data key="d0">Giving in Payment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Giving in Payment is a method of extinguishing an obligation whereby the Creditor accepts another prestation in place of what is due, governed by provisions of sale and payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Delegation">
  <data key="d0">Delegation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Delegation is the transfer of a debt obligation to a third party, where a third party agrees to take the place of the original Debtor with the Creditor's consent.&lt;SEP&gt;Delegation is a legal mechanism whereby a debtor procures the acceptance by the creditor of a third party who undertakes to pay in the debtor's stead, and does not necessarily require a prior debt between the debtor and the third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Summons">
  <data key="d0">Summons</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A Summons is a formal legal notice served on the Creditor, after which the Creditor becomes responsible for loss or deterioration of the thing and interest ceases to run.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interest">
  <data key="d0">Interest</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interest is a financial charge accruing on the debt that ceases to run from the time a summons has been served on the Creditor.&lt;SEP&gt;The agreed charge payable by the borrower on a loan, falling due at agreed dates; absence of an interest agreement renders the loan gratuitous and without consideration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Actual Tender">
  <data key="d0">Actual Tender</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Actual Tender is the formal offering of the thing due by the Debtor, which is equivalent to payment when followed by a deposit or equivalent measure, provided it is accepted by the Creditor or recognized as valid by a final judgment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Final Judgment">
  <data key="d0">Final Judgment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Final Judgment is a conclusive court decision that recognizes the validity of a tender or deposit, after which the Debtor may not retract the tender without releasing co-debtors and sureties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expenses">
  <data key="d0">Expenses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Expenses are costs in connection with payment that are at the charge of the Debtor unless otherwise agreed, and are the first to be deducted from an insufficient payment before interest and principal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Voidable Contract">
  <data key="d0">Voidable Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A voidable contract is a contract susceptible to annulment, and if the original obligation arises from one, novation is valid only if the new obligation aims to confirm the contract and replace the original.&lt;SEP&gt;A Voidable Contract is a contract susceptible to annulment; novation of an obligation arising from a voidable contract is only valid if the new obligation is intended to ratify the original contract and replace it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal">
  <data key="d0">Principal</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Principal is the main amount of the debt to which payment is imputed last, after expenses and interest have been deducted.&lt;SEP&gt;The principal is the person on whose behalf a representative acts. Rights and obligations from contracts concluded by a representative within authority are binding upon the principal.&lt;SEP&gt;The person on whose behalf a voluntary agent acts, who is bound to carry out obligations, indemnify the agent, reimburse expenses, and compensate for loss when the agent acted with reasonable care.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d0">Third Party Holder</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A third party holder is any person who acquires ownership of a mortgaged property or any real right over it capable of being mortgaged, without being personally responsible for the debt secured by the mortgage.&lt;SEP&gt;The Third Party Holder is a person who holds mortgaged property and has rights and obligations including payment of inscribed debts, purge of the property, abandonment, participation in auctions, warranty claims against preceding owners, and subrogation into the rights of discharged creditors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unsecured Creditor">
  <data key="d0">Unsecured Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An unsecured creditor is an ordinary creditor who is paid after mortgagees from the proceeds of sale of the mortgaged property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Former Owner">
  <data key="d0">Former Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The former owner is the previous owner of the mortgaged property from whom the third party holder acquired the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purchaser by Auction">
  <data key="d0">Purchaser by Auction</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The purchaser by auction is the person who acquires the property at public auction and is liable for the price of adjudication, the cost of formalities for the purge, and refunding costs to the disposed third party holder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inscribed Creditor">
  <data key="d0">Inscribed Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An inscribed creditor is a creditor whose rights are registered on the mortgaged property and who may compel payment or apply for the sale of the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purge">
  <data key="d0">Purge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Purge is the legal process by which a third party holder clears the mortgaged property of all inscriptions, requiring formal summons to inscribed creditors and potentially leading to a public auction.&lt;SEP&gt;Purge is a legal procedure by which a third party holder clears mortgaged property of all inscriptions and encumbrances by paying creditors or depositing funds.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rank Assignment">
  <data key="d0">Rank Assignment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rank Assignment determines the order in which mortgagees are paid and is calculated from the date of inscription of the mortgage, even if it secures a conditional, future, or contingent debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rank Assignment by Subrogation">
  <data key="d0">Rank Assignment by Subrogation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mortgagee may assign his rank in favor of another creditor having a mortgage inscribed on the same property, within the limits of his secured debts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Applicant">
  <data key="d0">Applicant</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The applicant is an inscribed creditor or surety who applies for the sale of the property during the purge process, must deposit a sum sufficient to cover auction costs at the Court, and may not renounce the application without consent of all inscribed creditors and sureties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compulsory Expropriation">
  <data key="d0">Compulsory Expropriation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Compulsory Expropriation is the formal legal process that must be followed when an application is made for the sale of a mortgaged property, as prescribed in the purge and auction procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Greffier of the Court">
  <data key="d0">Greffier of the Court</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Greffier of the Court is the official responsible for recording the judgment that puts an end to the state of insolvency in the margin of the registration and for sending a copy of the judgment to the Registrar of the Cairo Court.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Registrar of the Cairo Court">
  <data key="d0">Registrar of the Cairo Court</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Registrar of the Cairo Court is the official who makes a registration entry similar to that of the greffier upon receiving a copy of the judgment ending insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Conditional Obligation is an obligation whose existence or extinction depends on a future and uncertain event, as defined in Article 265.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Suspensive Condition">
  <data key="d0">Suspensive Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Suspensive Condition is a condition upon which the existence of an obligation depends, and the obligation does not become executory until the condition is realized. The obligation is void if the condition is impossible, contrary to morality or public order, or depends solely on the will of the obligor.&lt;SEP&gt;A suspensive condition is a legal condition under which a sale upon trial is deemed to have been made, requiring acceptance of the thing sold before the sale becomes effective.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resolutory Condition">
  <data key="d0">Resolutory Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Resolutory Condition is a condition upon which the extinction of an obligation depends. When realized, the obligation is extinguished and the creditor must restitute what has been received. Acts of management by the creditor remain valid.&lt;SEP&gt;A resolutory condition is an alternative condition under which a sale upon trial may be made, as opposed to a suspensive condition, unless the agreement or circumstances indicate otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time Clause">
  <data key="d0">Time Clause</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Time Clause (term) is a provision where the performance or extinction of an obligation depends on a future certain event, as defined in Article 271.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alternative Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Alternative Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Alternative Obligation is one whose object includes numerous prestations and the debtor is entirely freed by the performance of one of them. The option belongs to the debtor unless agreed or provided otherwise by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Facultative Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Facultative Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Facultative Obligation is one whose object consists of a single prestation but the debtor may free himself by performing another prestation in its place. The nature of the obligation is determined by the principal prestation promised.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retroactive Effect">
  <data key="d0">Retroactive Effect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Retroactive Effect refers to the legal principle by which the fulfillment of a condition is effective retroactively to the day the obligation was contracted, unless the will of the parties or the nature of the contract indicates otherwise, as defined in Article 270. The extinction of an obligation at the end of a resolutory term has no retroactive effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Restitution">
  <data key="d0">Restitution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal obligation to return what was received without proper cause, governed by rules on good faith, bad faith, legal capacity, and prescription.&lt;SEP&gt;Restitution is the obligation of a creditor to return what has been received upon the realization of a resolutory condition, as stated in Article 269. If restitution is impossible due to a cause for which the creditor is responsible, the creditor is liable in damages.&lt;SEP&gt;The obligation of the lessee to return the leased property to the lessor upon expiration of the lease. Unlawful retention of the property obliges the lessee to pay compensation based on the rental value of the property and any harm suffered by the lessor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protective Measures">
  <data key="d0">Protective Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Protective Measures are legal actions a creditor may take to safeguard his rights before the realization of a suspensive condition or before the expiration of a suspensive term, as referenced in Articles 268 and 274.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vendor">
  <data key="d0">Vendor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Vendor is a party to a sale contract who is bound to transfer ownership of a thing or propriety right to the purchaser and must allow the purchaser to trial the thing sold in applicable cases.&lt;SEP&gt;The seller of mortgaged immovable property who may agree with the purchaser to assign the mortgage debt, triggering the requirement for creditor notification and ratification.&lt;SEP&gt;The vendor is the seller in a sale contract who transfers ownership of goods or property and has the right to demand payment; the vendor may dissolve the sale if the purchaser fails to pay on the due date.&lt;SEP&gt;The vendor is a party to a sale contract who is obligated to transfer ownership of the thing sold, deliver it in its proper state, and provide warranty against disturbance and eviction to the purchaser.&lt;SEP&gt;The Vendor is the seller in a sale contract who bears warranty obligations against dispossession and defects, possesses a right of retention over the thing sold until payment, and may be liable for refunds, compensation, and legal interest. The Vendor remains liable even under warranty exclusion clauses if dispossession results from the Vendor's own acts or fraudulent concealment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Vendor is the seller in a property transaction who is required to issue or receive formal summons regarding preemption and is bound by the effects of preemption.&lt;SEP&gt;A seller of movable or immovable property who is owed the price and accessories and holds a privilege over the property sold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Upon Trial">
  <data key="d0">Sale Upon Trial</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sale upon trial is a type of sale deemed made subject to a suspensive condition of acceptance, where the purchaser's silence after the agreed or reasonable time for trial constitutes acceptance of the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Subject To Tasting">
  <data key="d0">Sale Subject To Tasting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sale subject to tasting allows the purchaser to accept the thing sold at their discretion, but acceptance must be declared within the time fixed by agreement or custom, and the sale is complete only from the date of such declaration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Price Determination">
  <data key="d0">Price Determination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The method of establishing the price may be confined to indicating the basis on which the price will ultimately be fixed, including reference to the market price at the place and time of delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Market Price">
  <data key="d0">Market Price</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The market price is the prevailing trade price used to determine the price of the thing sold when the parties have not fixed a specific price, evaluated at the place and time of delivery or at a place where prices are customarily deemed applicable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lesion">
  <data key="d0">Lesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lesion refers to a disadvantage or loss exceeding one fifth of the value of an immovable property sold by a legally incapable person, giving rise to a right of action to supplement the price to four fifths of the normal price.&lt;SEP&gt;Lesion refers to a situation where the obligations of one contracting party are out of all proportion to the advantages obtained, due to exploitation of obvious levity of character or unbridled passion, allowing the judge to annul the contract or reduce obligations, as set out in Article 129.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legally Incapable Person">
  <data key="d0">Legally Incapable Person</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A legally incapable person is an individual lacking full legal capacity whose immovable property, if sold with lesion exceeding one fifth, entitles the vendor or representative to seek a supplement of the price.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supplement Of Price Action">
  <data key="d0">Supplement Of Price Action</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal action to make up the price to four fifths of the normal value when an immovable belonging to a legally incapable person is sold with lesion exceeding one fifth, prescribed within three years from the cessation of incapacity or the death of the owner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obligations Of The Vendor">
  <data key="d0">Obligations Of The Vendor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vendor is bound to perform everything necessary to transfer the right to the thing sold to the purchaser and to abstain from all acts that might render the transfer impossible or difficult, including proper delivery and warranty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ownership Transfer">
  <data key="d0">Ownership Transfer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ownership transfer is the conveyance of property rights from the vendor to the purchaser, which may occur upon sale in bulk, upon full payment in a credit sale, or upon delivery depending on the terms agreed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale In Bulk">
  <data key="d0">Sale In Bulk</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sale in bulk transfers ownership to the purchaser in the same manner as ownership of a definite and ascertained thing, even when the price depends on the extent, weight, or measure of the goods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Credit Sale">
  <data key="d0">Credit Sale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A credit sale is a deferred-price sale in which the vendor may stipulate that ownership transfer is subject to full integral payment of the price, even after delivery of the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Installment Payment">
  <data key="d0">Installment Payment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Installment payment is a method of paying the price in parts, where the parties may agree that the vendor retains a portion as damages if the sale is cancelled for non-payment, and ownership is deemed transferred from the date of sale when all installments are paid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Delivery">
  <data key="d0">Delivery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Delivery consists in placing the thing sold at the disposal of the purchaser so that the purchaser can take possession and enjoy it without hindrance, including all accessories and permanent appurtenances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deficiency And Excess">
  <data key="d0">Deficiency And Excess</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deficiency or excess in the quantity of the thing sold relative to the contract gives rise to rights of the purchaser to demand price reduction or contract cancellation, or rights of the vendor to claim a supplement of the price, prescribed within one year of actual delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Perishment Before Delivery">
  <data key="d0">Perishment Before Delivery</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Perishment of the thing sold before delivery due to a cause beyond the vendor's control results in the dissolution of the sale and refund of the price to the purchaser, unless the purchaser was summoned to take delivery before the loss.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deterioration Before Delivery">
  <data key="d0">Deterioration Before Delivery</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Deterioration of the thing sold before delivery gives the purchaser the option to cancel the sale if the diminution is substantial or to uphold the sale at a reduced price.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warranty Against Disturbance">
  <data key="d0">Warranty Against Disturbance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vendor warrants the purchaser against disturbance in enjoyment of the thing sold, whether caused by the vendor's own act or by a third party having a right over the thing sold enforceable against the purchaser.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessor's legal obligation to protect the lessee against disturbances caused by the lessor, the lessor's servants, other lessees, or successors in title. Any agreement excluding or limiting this warranty is void if the lessor fraudulently concealed the cause.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Revendication Action">
  <data key="d0">Revendication Action</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An action for revendication is a legal proceeding brought against the purchaser by a third party claiming ownership of the thing sold, requiring the vendor to join as co-defendant or take the purchaser's place upon receiving notice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warranty Of Eviction">
  <data key="d0">Warranty Of Eviction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The vendor's obligation to protect the purchaser against dispossession by a third party, including refunding amounts paid by the purchaser to avoid dispossession, together with legal interest and expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code Of Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Code Of Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Code of Procedure governs the procedural aspects of legal actions, including the vendor's obligation to intervene in a revendication action brought against the purchaser.&lt;SEP&gt;The Code of Procedure (Qanun Almurafa'at) is the body of procedural law referenced as governing the recourse against court orders admitting claims related to inventory disputes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 422">
  <data key="d0">Article 422</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 422 provides that in a sale subject to tasting, the purchaser may accept the thing sold but must declare acceptance within the time fixed by agreement or custom.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 423">
  <data key="d0">Article 423</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 423 allows the method of establishing the price to be confined to indicating the basis on which the price will be fixed, with the market price determined at the place and time of delivery in case of doubt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 424">
  <data key="d0">Article 424</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 424 provides that a sale is not void for lack of a fixed price if the parties intended to adopt the current trade price or the price usually applied in their dealings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 425">
  <data key="d0">Article 425</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 425 grants a right of action to supplement the price to four fifths of the normal price when an immovable belonging to a legally incapable person is sold with lesion exceeding one fifth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 426">
  <data key="d0">Article 426</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 426 prescribes the supplement of price action within three years from the cessation of legal incapacity or the death of the owner, protecting third parties in good faith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 427">
  <data key="d0">Article 427</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 427 prohibits an action for supplement of price on grounds of lesion in sales conducted by public auction in accordance with the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 428">
  <data key="d0">Article 428</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 428 obligates the vendor to perform everything necessary to transfer the right sold and to abstain from acts that might render the transfer impossible or difficult.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 429">
  <data key="d0">Article 429</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 429 states that in a sale in bulk, ownership transfers as with a definite and ascertained thing, even when the price depends on measurement of the goods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 430">
  <data key="d0">Article 430</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 430 allows the vendor in a credit sale to stipulate that ownership transfer is subject to full payment, with provisions for installment payments, retention of damages, and retroactive ownership transfer upon full payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 431">
  <data key="d0">Article 431</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 431 obligates the vendor to deliver the thing sold in the state it was at the time of the sale.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 432">
  <data key="d0">Article 432</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 432 defines delivery as including accessories and everything permanently appropriated for the use of the thing sold, in accordance with the nature of things, local custom, and the intention of the parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 433">
  <data key="d0">Article 433</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 433 establishes the vendor's liability for deficiency and the purchaser's obligations for excess in the quantity of the thing sold, with rights to cancel or adjust the price.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 434">
  <data key="d0">Article 434</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 434 prescribes rights related to deficiency or excess in the thing sold within one year from actual delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 435">
  <data key="d0">Article 435</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 435 defines delivery as placing the thing sold at the disposal of the purchaser and allows delivery by mere agreement when the thing was already in the purchaser's possession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 436">
  <data key="d0">Article 436</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 436 provides that delivery of a thing to be sent to the purchaser is not effective until the thing reaches the purchaser, unless agreed otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 437">
  <data key="d0">Article 437</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 437 provides that if the thing sold perishes before delivery due to a cause beyond the vendor's control, the sale is dissolved and the price refunded.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 438">
  <data key="d0">Article 438</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 438 gives the purchaser the option to cancel the sale or uphold it at a reduced price if the value of the thing sold is diminished by deterioration before delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 439">
  <data key="d0">Article 439</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 439 imposes on the vendor a warranty against disturbance in the purchaser's enjoyment, whether caused by the vendor or a third party with a right derived from the vendor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 440">
  <data key="d0">Article 440</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 440 governs the vendor's obligation to intervene in a revendication action brought against the purchaser, and the consequences of failure to notify or intervene.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 440 is a referenced legal provision regarding the purchaser's obligation to notify the vendor of the action of revendication, referenced in Article 443 as a condition for recovering certain litigation costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 441">
  <data key="d0">Article 441</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 441 preserves the purchaser's warranty right even if the purchaser in good faith acknowledges the third party's claim or compromises with the third party, provided the vendor was duly notified and given the opportunity to intervene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 442">
  <data key="d0">Article 442</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 442 allows the vendor to free himself from warranty consequences by refunding to the purchaser amounts paid to avoid dispossession, including legal interest and expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contracting Parties">
  <data key="d0">Contracting Parties</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The contracting parties are the individuals or entities entering into a sale agreement, including the vendor and purchaser, who may fix the price, agree on installment terms, or describe the contract as a lease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract Of Lease">
  <data key="d0">Contract Of Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract of lease is a description the contracting parties may give to what is substantively a sale, to which the provisions governing credit sales and installment payments still apply.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 224">
  <data key="d0">Article 224</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 224 provides that damages fixed by agreement are not due if the debtor establishes that the creditor has not suffered any loss. The judge may reduce these damages if the amount was grossly exaggerated or the principal obligation was partially performed. Any contrary agreement is void.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 224 is a legal provision referenced in Article 430 paragraph 2, under which the judge may reduce the amount of agreed damages according to circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-owners">
  <data key="d0">Co-owners</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Co-owners are the members of a family or building who jointly hold shares in property held in common, with rights and obligations regarding partition, disposal of shares, management, and cost participation.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-owners are persons who jointly own parts of a building and are bound to conform to the decisions of the syndicate regarding reconstruction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ownership of Storeys in Buildings">
  <data key="d0">Ownership of Storeys in Buildings</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ownership of Storeys in Buildings is a legal framework governing situations where different storeys or apartments of a building belong to different owners, who are considered co-owners of the ground and parts intended for common use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Building">
  <data key="d0">Building</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A structure for whose collapse damage a person in charge is liable even if not the owner, even if the collapse is partial, unless it did not result from negligent maintenance, age, or defect, as established in Article 177.&lt;SEP&gt;A Building is a physical structure divided into storeys or apartments that may belong to different owners, containing common parts and individual parts subject to specific legal rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Parts">
  <data key="d0">Common Parts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common Parts are the parts of a building intended for the common use of all owners, including foundations, main walls, main entrances, yards, roofs, lifts, passages, corridors, floor supports, and pipes of all kinds, which cannot be divided and in which each owner holds a share proportional to the value of his part in the building.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Syndicate of Owners">
  <data key="d0">Syndicate of Owners</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Syndicate of Owners is an organization that may be formed by owners of storeys or apartments in a building to manage common parts, establish rules, take out collective insurance, authorize works, and make binding decisions regarding the building held in common.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Representative of the Syndicate">
  <data key="d0">Representative of the Syndicate</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Representative of the Syndicate is a person appointed by majority vote of owners or by the President of the Court of First Instance to execute the decisions of the syndicate, take preservation measures, and represent the syndicate before the courts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="President of the Court of First Instance">
  <data key="d0">President of the Court of First Instance</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The President of the Court of First Instance is the judicial officer within whose jurisdiction the building is located, with authority to appoint or discharge the representative of the syndicate when the required majority is not obtained.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Upper Storey">
  <data key="d0">Upper Storey</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Upper Storey is the higher storey of a building, whose owner has rights and obligations in relation to the lower storey, including the right to rebuild the lower storey at the lower owner's cost and the obligation not to heighten the building in a way that injures the lower storey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lower Storey">
  <data key="d0">Lower Storey</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Lower Storey is the lower storey of a building, whose owner is bound to execute works and repairs necessary to prevent the upper storey from falling and to rebuild if the building is destroyed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Title Deeds">
  <data key="d0">Title Deeds</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Title Deeds are legal documents that may contain provisions contrary to the default rules regarding co-ownership of the ground and common parts of a building.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 852">
  <data key="d0">Article 852</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 852 stipulates that a family joint ownership may be created for a period not exceeding fifteen years, with provisions for early withdrawal by court authorization or after six months notice if no period is fixed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 853">
  <data key="d0">Article 853</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 853 provides that co-owners cannot demand partition while family joint ownership continues, and no co-owner may dispose of his share to a non-family member without consent of all co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 854">
  <data key="d0">Article 854</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 854 governs the appointment and discharge of managers by co-owners holding the majority in value, and the manager's authority to introduce changes for better enjoyment of the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 855">
  <data key="d0">Article 855</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 855 provides that family joint ownership is governed by the provisions relating to joint property and to mandate, subject to the preceding special provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 856">
  <data key="d0">Article 856</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 856 establishes the rules for ownership of storeys and apartments in a building, defining common parts and proportional shares in relation to the value of each owner's part.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 857">
  <data key="d0">Article 857</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 857 regulates the use of and modifications to common parts, requiring consent of all owners for modifications except those that facilitate use without changing purpose or causing prejudice.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 858">
  <data key="d0">Article 858</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 858 obliges every owner to participate in the costs of preservation, maintenance, management, and reconstruction of common parts proportionally, and prohibits renouncing shares to avoid such costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 859">
  <data key="d0">Article 859</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 859 obliges the owner of a lower storey to execute repairs to prevent the upper storey from falling, and authorizes the Judge to order the sale of the lower storey or urgent repairs if the owner refuses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 860">
  <data key="d0">Article 860</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 860 provides that if a building is destroyed, the owner of the lower storey must rebuild it, and the owner of the upper storey may rebuild it at the lower owner's cost and withhold occupancy until repayment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 861">
  <data key="d0">Article 861</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 861 prohibits the owner of the upper storey from heightening the building in a way that injures the lower storey.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 862">
  <data key="d0">Article 862</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 862 provides that owners of storeys or apartments in a building may form a syndicate, which may also be formed for the purpose of constructing or acquiring buildings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 863">
  <data key="d0">Article 863</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 863 allows a syndicate to establish rules with the consent of all members to ensure better enjoyment and good management of the building held in common.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 864">
  <data key="d0">Article 864</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 864 provides that in the absence of management rules, the syndicate manages common parts through binding decisions taken by majority of owners based on share value, after summoning all interested parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 865">
  <data key="d0">Article 865</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 865 authorizes the syndicate to take out collective insurance against risks and to authorize works or installations that increase the building's value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 866">
  <data key="d0">Article 866</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 866 provides for the appointment of a representative to execute syndicate decisions, either by majority vote or by the President of the Court of First Instance, with duties including preservation and court representation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 867">
  <data key="d0">Article 867</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 867 governs the remuneration and discharge of the representative of the syndicate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 868">
  <data key="d0">Article 868</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 868 provides that if a building is destroyed by fire or otherwise, co-owners are bound to conform to the syndicate's reconstruction decision, with compensation set aside for reconstruction costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint Property">
  <data key="d0">Joint Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Joint Property is the legal concept of property held in common (الملكية الشائعة), the rules of which govern family joint ownership alongside mandate rules per Article 855.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mandate">
  <data key="d0">Mandate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal arrangement whose rules apply to voluntary agency when the principal ratifies the agent's act, as referenced in Articles 190 and 193, and which governs the obligations of the mandatary regarding restitution and accounting.&lt;SEP&gt;Mandate is the legal concept of agency (الوكالة), the rules of which govern family joint ownership alongside joint property rules per Article 855.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-family Member">
  <data key="d0">Non-family Member</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Non-family Member is a person outside the family who may only become a partner in family joint ownership if he and all co-owners consent, as established in Article 853.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inner Walls">
  <data key="d0">Inner Walls</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Inner Walls are walls separating two apartments in a building that are owned as party property by the owners of the two adjacent apartments, as specified in Article 856.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Registered Creditors">
  <data key="d0">Registered Creditors</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Registered Creditors are creditors with registered claims against the building whose rights are preserved when compensation for destruction is allocated for reconstruction costs, as stated in Article 868.&lt;SEP&gt;Registered Creditors are persons with registered claims against property whose rights must be preserved during reconstruction or estate winding up.&lt;SEP&gt;Registered Creditors are creditors whose mortgages or charges are registered against the Purchaser and who retain their rights of preference on that portion of the sale price of the property which reverts to the Purchaser, notwithstanding preemption, as provided in Article 947.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Collective Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Collective Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Collective Insurance is insurance against risks to the building or to the co-owners jointly, which the syndicate may take out with the prescribed majority consent per Article 865.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abandonment">
  <data key="d0">Abandonment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Abandonment is a legal process by which a third party holder declares relinquishment of mortgaged property through a declaration submitted to the Registrar of the competent Court of First Instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Caisse of the Court">
  <data key="d0">Caisse of the Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Caisse of the Court is the court's treasury where the third party holder may deposit sums for valuation, auction prices, or debt payments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judge Des Referes">
  <data key="d0">Judge Des Referes</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Judge Des Referes is a judge handling urgent matters who has the authority to appoint a receiver for managing property during expropriation proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Auction">
  <data key="d0">Auction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An Auction is a public sale process for mortgaged property, in which the third party holder and other persons may participate, subject to conditions on minimum bid prices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judgment Charge">
  <data key="d0">Judgment Charge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Judgment Charge is a legal charge on one or more specific immovable properties belonging to a debtor, obtained by a creditor through an enforceable judgment to secure payment of principal, interest, and costs.&lt;SEP&gt;A Judgment Charge is a legal right that a creditor may obtain to secure a debt, governed by the same provisions as a mortgage, including rules on inscription, renewal, radiation, indivisibility, effect, reduction, and extinguishment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preceding Owner">
  <data key="d0">Preceding Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Preceding Owner is the person from whom the third party holder acquired the property and against whom the third party holder has a right of action for warranty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section III: Extinguishment of the Mortgage">
  <data key="d0">Section III: Extinguishment of the Mortgage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Section III: Extinguishment of the Mortgage is a legal section covering the termination of mortgage rights, including through extinction of debt, completion of purge, or compulsory sale.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter II: Judgment Charges upon Immovable Property">
  <data key="d0">Chapter II: Judgment Charges upon Immovable Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter II: Judgment Charges upon Immovable Property is a legal chapter governing the constitution, requirements, and procedures for obtaining judgment charges on immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Title Deed">
  <data key="d0">Title Deed</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal document establishing ownership of property that the Third Party Holder transcribes, and which serves as the basis for ownership claims during purge and auction proceedings, including the original title deed referred to in Articles 1073 and 1075.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enforceable Judgment">
  <data key="d0">Enforceable Judgment</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A court judgment rendered on the merits of a case condemning the Debtor to a liquidated amount, which serves as a prerequisite for obtaining a Judgment Charge over immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judgment of Adjudication">
  <data key="d0">Judgment of Adjudication</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A court judgment that confers ownership rights on a person other than the Third Party Holder who acquires the mortgaged property at auction, deriving the right from the Third Party Holder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arbitration Award">
  <data key="d0">Arbitration Award</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A decision issued by arbitrators that cannot serve as a basis for a Judgment Charge until it has been made enforceable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Foreign Court">
  <data key="d0">Foreign Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A court outside the jurisdiction whose judgments cannot serve as a basis for a Judgment Charge until the judgment has been made enforceable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fruits of the Property">
  <data key="d0">Fruits of the Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The produce or income generated by the mortgaged property that the Third Party Holder is liable to restitute from the date of being summoned to pay or abandon the property, or from a new summons if proceedings are abandoned for three years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section I: The Constitution of a Judgment Charge">
  <data key="d0">Section I: The Constitution of a Judgment Charge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal section governing the creation, eligibility requirements, and procedural steps for establishing Judgment Charges on immovable property, including the conditions under which judgments may serve as a basis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gift">
  <data key="d0">Gift</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gift is a legal concept whose substantive rules govern the release of an obligation, as stated in Article 372.&lt;SEP&gt;A contract by which the donor disposes of property belonging to him without consideration; the donor may impose upon the donee the performance of a specific obligation while retaining the intention of making a gift.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal mechanism whereby a donor transfers property to a donee, governed by provisions on delivery, warranties against dispossession and defects, consideration, and revocation rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Donor">
  <data key="d0">Donor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The person who makes a gift by disposing of property without consideration; the donor may impose obligations on the donee, must execute the gift through an authentic document for immovables, and cannot demand restitution of voluntarily delivered gifts that are null due to formal defects.&lt;SEP&gt;The person who makes a gift, obligated to deliver the gifted thing, subject to limited liability for defects and dispossession, and entitled to revoke the gift under specified conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Donee">
  <data key="d0">Donee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The recipient of a gift who must accept the gift or have it accepted by a representative for the gift to be complete; the donee may be subject to obligations imposed by the donor.&lt;SEP&gt;The person who receives a gift, entitled to delivery of the gifted thing, potentially bound to perform consideration, and protected by warranty provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 491">
  <data key="d0">Article 491</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a gift of a definite and specific thing not belonging to the donor is governed by the provisions of Articles 466 and 467.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 492">
  <data key="d0">Article 492</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision declaring that a gift of future property is void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 493">
  <data key="d0">Article 493</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision obligating the donor to deliver the gifted thing to the donee, applying the provisions related to the delivery of a sold thing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 494">
  <data key="d0">Article 494</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision addressing the donor's warranty against dispossession, covering intentional concealment and gifts made for valuable consideration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 495">
  <data key="d0">Article 495</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision concerning the donor's warranty for defects in the gifted thing, including liability for intentionally hidden defects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 496">
  <data key="d0">Article 496</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision limiting the donor's liability to intentional acts or gross negligence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 497">
  <data key="d0">Article 497</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision binding the donee to perform any consideration imposed upon him, whether in favor of the donor, a third party, or the public interest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495938</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 498">
  <data key="d0">Article 498</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision limiting the donee's obligation to perform consideration to the extent of the value of the thing donated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 499">
  <data key="d0">Article 499</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision regarding the donee's obligation to discharge the donor's existing debts as consideration for the gift.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Valuable Consideration">
  <data key="d0">Valuable Consideration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation or payment provided by the donee in exchange for a gift, affecting warranty obligations and the donor's liability for dispossession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Revocation of Gift">
  <data key="d0">Revocation of Gift</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal process by which a donor may cancel a gift, either by mutual consent of the donee or through court authority, subject to specific grounds and obstacles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 500">
  <data key="d0">Article 500</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing revocation of a gift with the donee's consent or, if the donee does not consent, through court application based on reasonable grounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 501">
  <data key="d0">Article 501</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision enumerating reasonable grounds for revocation, including serious ingratitude by the donee, the donor's inability to maintain himself, and the birth of a child after the donation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 502">
  <data key="d0">Article 502</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision listing obstacles to revocation of a gift, including inherent increase in value, death of a party, alienation by the donee, spousal gifts, and acts of charity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 503">
  <data key="d0">Article 503</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a revoked gift is deemed null and void, with rules on restitution of fruits and repayment of necessary and useful expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 504">
  <data key="d0">Article 504</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision addressing the donor's liability for loss if the donor takes back the gifted thing without consent or court decision.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partnership">
  <data key="d0">Partnership</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Partnership is a legal business entity formed by partners who contribute capital, services, or other assets, and share in profits and losses according to the partnership deed or statutory provisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A contract by which two or more persons undertake to contribute jointly in a pecuniary undertaking by providing contributions of property or services, with the object of sharing profits or losses.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal business entity formed by partners, subject to dissolution by unanimous agreement or court order, and governed by a partnership deed that defines liquidation and partition procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partner">
  <data key="d0">Partner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Partner is a member of a partnership who contributes capital, services, or other assets and is entitled to a share of profits while being liable for losses and partnership debts in proportion to his contribution, unless otherwise agreed.&lt;SEP&gt;A person who contributes to a partnership through property, services, or other forms of contribution, and shares in the profits or losses of the undertaking.&lt;SEP&gt;A member of a partnership who holds the right to request dissolution, seek the exclusion of another partner, apply for retirement, and share in the distribution of partnership assets or losses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 505">
  <data key="d0">Article 505</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision defining partnership as a contract involving contributions of property or services for sharing in profits or losses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 506">
  <data key="d0">Article 506</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a partnership is deemed a juristic person upon constitution, with publication formalities required for recognition by third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Juristic Person">
  <data key="d0">Juristic Person</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A juristic person enjoys all rights within the limits established by law, except those inherent in the nature of an individual. It has its own patrimonium, legal capacity, the right to sue, and its own domicile.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal personality attributed to a partnership upon its constitution, which is enforceable against third parties only after completion of publication formalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A juristic person is a legal entity whose good or bad faith in possession is determined by the intention of its representative, as established by Article 965.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partnership Deed">
  <data key="d0">Partnership Deed</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Partnership Deed is the contractual agreement establishing the partnership, defining the shares of partners in profits and losses, the method of management, and other terms governing the partnership.&lt;SEP&gt;The written document required to establish a partnership, which must be in writing under penalty of nullity, along with any modifications to it.&lt;SEP&gt;The contractual document governing the partnership that specifies the manner of liquidation, partition of assets, and the valuation of partners' capital contributions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 507">
  <data key="d0">Article 507</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring partnership deeds to be in writing under pain of nullity, and addressing the limited effects of nullity among partners and toward third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 508">
  <data key="d0">Article 508</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision presuming partners' contributions to be equal and to consist of ownership of property, unless agreement or custom provides otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 509">
  <data key="d0">Article 509</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that the influence or credit of a partner cannot alone constitute a contribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 510">
  <data key="d0">Article 510</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision making a partner liable for interest on unpaid money contributions from the date the contribution fell due, plus additional compensation for loss.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 511">
  <data key="d0">Article 511</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision applying sale warranty provisions to property ownership contributions and lease provisions to use-of-property contributions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 512">
  <data key="d0">Article 512</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision governing service contributions, requiring partners to perform promised services and account for profits, while not obligating contribution of patents without agreement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 513">
  <data key="d0">Article 513</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 513 provides that if a partner's contribution consists of debts due by third parties, his obligation to the partnership is only extinguished by recovery of those debts, and he is liable for damages if the debts are not paid when due.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal provision addressing contributions consisting of debts due by third parties, where the contributing partner's obligation is only extinguished upon recovery of such debts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Articles 466 and 467">
  <data key="d0">Articles 466 and 467</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Referenced legal provisions that govern the gift of a definite and specific thing not belonging to the donor, as cited in Article 491.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dispossession">
  <data key="d0">Dispossession</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The loss of ownership or possession of the gifted thing, addressed in Article 494, which governs the donor's warranty obligations against dispossession and the donee's right to equitable compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alimony">
  <data key="d0">Alimony</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Alimony (النفقة) refers to obligations for payment of maintenance between relatives, governed by the national law of the person liable for such payment, as stated in Article 15.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal obligation to provide financial support to another person, mentioned in Article 501 as a ground for revocation of a gift if the donor becomes unable to meet this obligation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fruits">
  <data key="d0">Fruits</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The produce or profits derived from the gifted thing, addressed in Article 503, which states that the donee is liable for restitution of fruits only from the date of agreement on revocation or commencement of legal proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spouse">
  <data key="d0">Spouse</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A marital partner mentioned in Article 502 as a category relating to gifts between spouses, which constitutes an obstacle to the revocation of a gift even after dissolution of the marriage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="National Courts">
  <data key="d0">National Courts</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">National Courts (المحاكم الوطنية) are Egyptian judicial bodies that previously operated under an older civil law issued on October 28, 1883.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mixed Courts">
  <data key="d0">Mixed Courts</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Mixed Courts (المحاكم المختلطة) are Egyptian judicial bodies that previously operated under an older civil law issued on June 28, 1875.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Qasr El-Qubba">
  <data key="d0">Qasr El-Qubba</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Qasr El-Qubba (قصر القبة) is the palace in Cairo where the Egyptian Civil Code was issued on July 16, 1948 (9 Ramadan 1367).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Custom">
  <data key="d0">Custom</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Custom (العرف) is a source of law applied by judges when no legislative provision exists, serving as a secondary legal reference after statutory law.&lt;SEP&gt;Local practice or general usage governing rent payment times, tolerated defects, lessee's repairs, and other aspects of the lease relationship in the absence of express agreement between the parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Islamic Sharia">
  <data key="d0">Islamic Sharia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Islamic Sharia (الشريعة الإسلامية) is a source of law applied by judges in the absence of both legislative provisions and custom, serving as a tertiary legal reference in the Egyptian Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Justice">
  <data key="d0">Natural Justice</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural Justice (القانون الطبيعي) is a legal principle applied by judges as a final source of law when no legislative provision, custom, or Islamic Sharia principle is available.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rules of Equity">
  <data key="d0">Rules of Equity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Rules of Equity (قواعد العدالة) are legal principles applied alongside natural justice when no other legal source is available.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Capacity">
  <data key="d0">Legal Capacity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal Capacity (الأهلية) refers to the legal competence of persons as defined by legislative provisions, applicable to all persons fulfilling the conditions embodied in such provisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal capacity is the ability to exercise civil rights. Full legal capacity is attained at twenty-one years of age completed in accordance with the Gregorian calendar, provided the person is in possession of mental faculties and not under legal disability.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal capacity is the capacity of a person to conclude contracts. Every person who has not been declared under total or partial legal incapacity has legal capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal capacity is the ability of a person who has not been declared to be under total or partial legal incapacity to conclude a contract, as established in Article 109 of the Civil Code.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal ability to enter into contracts; its absence limits the liability of recipients and voluntary agents to the extent of their enrichment.&lt;SEP&gt;The requirement that parties to a compromise must possess the legal ability to dispose for valuable consideration of the rights that are the objects of the compromise contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gregorian Calendar">
  <data key="d0">Gregorian Calendar</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Gregorian Calendar (التقويم الميلادي) is the standard calendar used for calculating periods of limitation under the Egyptian Civil Code unless otherwise specified.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marriage">
  <data key="d0">Marriage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Marriage (الزواج) is a legal institution whose validity conditions are governed by the national law of each spouse, and its effects are regulated by the law of the husband's country at the time of conclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inheritance">
  <data key="d0">Inheritance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inheritance (الميراث) and wills are governed by the national law of the deceased, testator, or person disposing of property at the time of death.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractual Obligations">
  <data key="d0">Contractual Obligations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Contractual Obligations (الالتزامات التعاقدية) are governed by the law of the common domicile of the contracting parties, or by the law of the place where the contract was concluded.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Contractual Obligations">
  <data key="d0">Non-Contractual Obligations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-Contractual Obligations (الالتزامات غير التعاقدية) are governed by the law of the state in whose territory the act giving rise to the obligation took place.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Private International Law">
  <data key="d0">Private International Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Private International Law (القانون الدولي الخاص) applies in cases of conflict of laws for which no specific provision is made in the Egyptian Civil Code.&lt;SEP&gt;Private international law provisions are excluded when a foreign law is deemed applicable; only the internal provisions of such foreign law shall apply.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Official Gazette">
  <data key="d0">Official Gazette</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Official Gazette (الجريدة الرسمية) is the official publication in which Egyptian laws, including the Egyptian Civil Code, are published as a legal requirement for promulgation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Advance Proof">
  <data key="d0">Advance Proof</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Advance Proof (الأدلة التي تعد مقدماً) refers to evidence prepared in advance, governed by the provisions of the law in force at the time when such proof was or should have been established, as stated in Article 9.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Foreign Juristic Person">
  <data key="d0">Foreign Juristic Person</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Foreign Juristic Person (الشخص الاعتباري الأجنبي) such as companies, associations, or foundations whose legal status is governed by the law of the state where its actual principal seat of management is located, with Egyptian law applying if principal activities are carried out in Egypt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Divorce">
  <data key="d0">Divorce</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Divorce (التطليق) is a legal dissolution of marriage governed by the law of the country to which the husband belongs at the time of commencement of legal proceedings, as distinguished from repudiation in Article 13.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Separation">
  <data key="d0">Separation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Separation (الانفصال) is a legal concept distinct from divorce and repudiation, governed by the law of the country to which the husband belongs at the time of commencement of legal proceedings, as stated in Article 13.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Guardianship">
  <data key="d0">Guardianship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Guardianship (الولاية والوصاية والقوامة) encompasses natural and legal guardianship, receivership, and other systems for the protection of persons without legal capacity and absent persons, governed by the national law of the person to be protected, as stated in Article 16.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movable Property">
  <data key="d0">Movable Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Movable Property (المنقول) refers to movable assets whose possession, ownership, and real rights are governed by the law of the place where the movable was situated at the time of the event resulting in acquisition or loss of such rights, as stated in Article 18.&lt;SEP&gt;Movable property includes all things that are not immovables, and all proprietary rights other than real rights over immovable property.&lt;SEP&gt;Property that can be physically moved, subject to special rules for acquisition by possession under Articles 976 and 977.&lt;SEP&gt;Movable Property refers to personal property whose ownership is transferred by contract upon identification, and which may become mingled with other movables belonging to different owners.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of property that can be moved, including personal effects and items sold by vendors, which serves as the subject of special privileges established in Articles 1144 through 1146.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Real Rights">
  <data key="d0">Real Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Real Rights (الحقوق العينية) refer to property rights including possession and ownership, regulated by the law of the location of the property, as stated in Article 18.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tort">
  <data key="d0">Tort</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tort (الفعل الضار) refers to obligations arising from harmful acts, with the exception that acts occurring abroad which are lawful in Egypt are not governed by the law of the place where the act occurred, as stated in Article 21.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="International Convention">
  <data key="d0">International Convention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An International Convention (المعاهدة الدولية) in force in Egypt can override the provisions of the Egyptian Civil Code regarding conflicts of law, as stated in Article 23.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State with Multiple Legal Systems">
  <data key="d0">State with Multiple Legal Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A State with Multiple Legal Systems refers to a country having several coexisting legal systems, where the internal law of that state determines which legal system applies, as stated in Article 26.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 222">
  <data key="d0">Article 222</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 222 provides that a judge may award compensation for moral prejudice only to spouses and relatives up to the second degree, by reason of grief caused by the death of the victim.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 223">
  <data key="d0">Article 223</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 223 states that the parties may fix in advance the amount of damages either in the contract or in a subsequent agreement, subject to the provisions of Articles 215 to 220.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 225">
  <data key="d0">Article 225</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 225 states that when the loss exceeds the contractually fixed amount, the creditor cannot claim an increased sum unless the creditor proves the debtor committed fraud or gross negligence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 226">
  <data key="d0">Article 226</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 226 provides that when the object of an obligation is a sum of money of known amount and the debtor delays payment, the debtor is bound to pay interest at four percent in civil matters and five percent in commercial matters as damages for delay, running from the date of the court claim unless otherwise agreed or provided by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 227">
  <data key="d0">Article 227</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 227 allows the parties to agree on another rate of interest not exceeding seven percent. Any agreed rate exceeding seven percent is reduced to seven percent with surplus refunded. Any commission or consideration exceeding the maximum limit is treated as disguised interest and subject to reduction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Creditor">
  <data key="d0">Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Creditor (الدائن) is a person or party to whom a debt, obligation, or annuity is owed and who is entitled to receive payment or performance from a debtor. The creditor holds a range of rights and powers in relation to the obligation, including the authority to ratify or refuse an assignment of debt, to accept payment, and to demand specific performance of the obligation. Where payment is not due, the creditor may be required to make restitution, with the right to restitution influenced by whether the payment was received in good faith. A creditor may also voluntarily release the debtor or be replaced through novation by a third party.

To protect and enforce their rights, a creditor may take legal proceedings against the debtor, seek court orders for enforcement, and claim damages for losses and lost profits. The creditor may interrupt prescription through legal proceedings, summons, attachment, or other formal acts. A creditor is entitled to require the debtor to provide adequate securities guaranteeing the debt and may take protective measures when there are reasonable grounds to apprehend misappropriation — for instance, under Article 1133, a creditor may apply for movables charged with a privilege in their favor to be placed in judicial custody. The creditor benefits from pledges, mortgages, and other forms of security, and may apply for a judgment charge on the debtor's immovable property, with the right to appeal a rejection of such application to the Court of First Instance. Creditors are ranked by priority for payment from the proceeds of property sales.

In relation to sureties, the creditor receives the guarantee from the surety but must generally first take proceedings against the debtor before pursuing the surety. The creditor is bound to prove the debt in bankruptcy to preserve rights against the surety, and may levy execution on the property of both the debtor and, under certain conditions, the surety.

During insolvency proceedings, creditors retain certain rights, including the ability to bring individual actions against the debtor. These rights are preserved even upon the cessation of insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 228">
  <data key="d0">Article 228</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 228 establishes that moratory interest, whether fixed by law or by agreement, is due without the creditor being obliged to prove loss as a result of delay.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 229">
  <data key="d0">Article 229</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 229 provides that if a creditor in bad faith prolongs litigation, the judge may reduce legal or contractual interest or refuse to allow interest for the period of unjustified prolongation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 230">
  <data key="d0">Article 230</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 230 governs the distribution of the price of expropriated property sold by auction, limiting moratory interest for admitted creditors based on whether the purchaser or the Caisse du Tribunal is bound to pay interest, with interest distributed pro rata among all creditors.&lt;SEP&gt;A referenced legal provision governing interest provisions, cited in Article 1111 as limiting the scope of accrued interest secured by a pledge.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 231">
  <data key="d0">Article 231</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 231 allows a creditor to demand supplementary damages in addition to interest if the creditor establishes that a loss exceeding the interest was caused by the debtor in bad faith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 232">
  <data key="d0">Article 232</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 232 prohibits interest on outstanding interest and caps total interest collected by the creditor at the amount of the capital, subject to commercial rules and practices to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 233">
  <data key="d0">Article 233</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 233 states that the legal rate of commercial interest on current accounts varies according to the applicable local market rate, and capitalization on current accounts is effected according to commercial usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section III">
  <data key="d0">Section III</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section III covers the means of realizing and securing the rights of creditors, including means of execution and means of guarantee.&lt;SEP&gt;A section titled "Extinguishment of a Pledge" encompassing Articles 1112–1113, governing the termination of pledge rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purchaser">
  <data key="d0">Purchaser</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Purchaser is the buyer in a sale contract who acquires ownership of a thing in consideration of a price. As a party to the sale, the Purchaser is obligated to pay the price and take delivery of the thing sold, bearing the costs of the deed of sale, stamp duties, transcription fees, and delivery expenses unless otherwise agreed. The Purchaser must have sufficient acquaintance with the thing sold and is obligated to inspect it for defects upon delivery, giving timely notice of any discovered defects. In trial or tasting sales, the Purchaser has the right to accept or refuse the thing sold.

The Purchaser is entitled to significant protections under the law. These include warranty protection against defects, disturbance, and dispossession. When disturbed in enjoyment or threatened with dispossession, the Purchaser may retain the price. The Purchaser is also entitled to claim compensation, refunds, legal interest, and reimbursement of expenses in cases of dispossession or defects.

In specific contexts, the Purchaser's role carries additional legal implications. When purchasing mortgaged immovable property, the Purchaser may agree with the vendor to assume the mortgage debt through assignment, with the creditor required to ratify or refuse within six months of notification. When a Purchaser buys an industrial or commercial establishment from a lessee on leased property, the court may maintain the lease in the Purchaser's favor despite a prohibition on sub-letting, provided the Purchaser furnishes adequate security and the lessor suffers no real prejudice. A Purchaser who buys a deposited thing from the heir of a depository in good faith is also recognized as a protected party. In property transactions subject to preemption rights, the Purchaser must be notified of any intention to exercise preemption, and the preemptor may assume the Purchaser's rights and obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 234">
  <data key="d0">Article 234</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 234 provides that the debts of a debtor are secured by all of the debtor's property, and all creditors are treated equally with respect to this security, subject to any right of preference acquired in accordance with law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 235">
  <data key="d0">Article 235</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 235 grants every creditor, even if the claim has not fallen due, the right to exercise in the name of the debtor all the debtor's rights of action except those that are purely personal or cannot be attached, provided the creditor proves the debtor has not exercised such rights and that failure to do so results in or increases insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 236">
  <data key="d0">Article 236</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 236 states that a creditor exercising the debtor's rights is deemed to be the debtor's representative, and proceeds from such exercise fall into the patrimonium of the debtor and serve as security to all creditors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 237">
  <data key="d0">Article 237</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 237 allows any creditor whose claim has fallen due to demand that a prejudicial act of alienation by the debtor be declared void if it diminished rights or increased obligations resulting in or increasing insolvency, subject to conditions in the following article.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 238">
  <data key="d0">Article 238</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 238 sets conditions for invalidating debtor acts: acts for valuable consideration require proof of debtor fraud and the other party's knowledge of fraud; gratuitous acts are void against the creditor even without fraud; and subsequent transferees' liability depends on their knowledge of fraud or insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 239">
  <data key="d0">Article 239</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 239 provides that a creditor alleging debtor insolvency need only establish the amount of debts, while the debtor must prove that assets equal or exceed liabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 240">
  <data key="d0">Article 240</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 240 provides that once an act is declared void, the benefits of cancellation shall extend to all creditors prejudiced by the act.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 241">
  <data key="d0">Article 241</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 241 allows a person who acquired a right from an insolvent debtor without paying the price to escape a creditor's action by depositing the normal price at the Caisse du Tribunal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 242">
  <data key="d0">Article 242</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 242 provides that fraud giving unjustified preference to one creditor over another only results in loss of that advantage. Payment by an insolvent debtor to one creditor before the originally fixed date is not valid against other creditors, nor is post-date payment resulting from a fraudulent arrangement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 243">
  <data key="d0">Article 243</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 243 prescribes a three-year limitation period for an action to set aside an act of alienation from the date the creditor learns of the grounds, and a fifteen-year absolute limitation from the date of the contested act.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Judge">
  <data key="d0">The Judge</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The judge is the judicial authority empowered to award compensation, reduce damages, reduce or refuse interest, and declare acts void under various provisions of the law.&lt;SEP&gt;The Judge is the judicial officer who appoints administrators, confirms testamentary executors, may discharge administrators for adequate reasons, and can require periodic accountings of administration.&lt;SEP&gt;The judicial authority who authorizes sales of pledged things, fixes sale conditions, orders deposit of sale prices, and resolves matters related to pledge enforcement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Creditor">
  <data key="d0">The Creditor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The creditor is a party owed an obligation who may claim damages, exercise the debtor's rights, demand voiding of prejudicial acts, and receive interest payments under the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Debtor">
  <data key="d0">The Debtor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The debtor is a party bound by an obligation who owes payment or performance to the creditor, whose property secures debts, and whose acts of alienation may be challenged by creditors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Caisse Du Tribunal">
  <data key="d0">Caisse Du Tribunal</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Caisse du Tribunal is the court treasury where the price of expropriated property may be deposited and which may be bound to pay interest on such deposits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractual Damages">
  <data key="d0">Contractual Damages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Contractual damages refer to compensation fixed in advance by agreement between parties, subject to judicial reduction if grossly exaggerated or if no loss was suffered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moratory Interest">
  <data key="d0">Moratory Interest</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Moratory interest is compensation for delay in payment, fixed either by law or agreement, due without proof of loss, with rates varying for civil and commercial matters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Paulian Action">
  <data key="d0">Paulian Action</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Paulian action allows creditors to challenge and set aside prejudicial acts of alienation by debtors, subject to proof of fraud for valuable consideration acts and a three-year prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fraud">
  <data key="d0">Fraud</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fraud is a legal concept central to creditor-debtor relations, referenced in Articles 225, 238, and 242, encompassing intentional deception by the debtor such as entering into acts with intent to defraud creditors.&lt;SEP&gt;Fraud is a ground on which a purchaser may claim annulment of a sale despite a contractual statement that the purchaser is acquainted with the thing sold, provided fraud by the vendor is proven.&lt;SEP&gt;Intentional deception for which a debtor always remains liable. A debtor cannot be contractually discharged from liability arising from personal fraud.&lt;SEP&gt;Deceptive conduct by the lessor, such as fraudulently concealing the cause of a warranty against disturbance or defects, which renders any agreement excluding or limiting such warranty void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bad Faith">
  <data key="d0">Bad Faith</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bad faith is a legal concept appearing in two distinct contexts: a creditor's bad faith in prolonging litigation (Article 229) and a debtor's bad faith causing loss exceeding interest (Article 231).&lt;SEP&gt;A legal state of mind in which a recipient of undue payment acts dishonestly; a bad faith recipient must also return interest, profits gained, or profits negligently forfeited.&lt;SEP&gt;Bad Faith is a state attributed to the vendor who, knowing of defects or third-party rights, fails to disclose them to the purchaser, resulting in enhanced liability including responsibility for superfluous expenditures that would not otherwise be recoverable.&lt;SEP&gt;Bad faith arises when a possessor knows of infringement or has usurped possession by violence, and it negatively affects the possessor's legal position.&lt;SEP&gt;A state of awareness of the illegitimacy of one's possession, which increases liability for fruits, loss, and deterioration of the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disguised Interest">
  <data key="d0">Disguised Interest</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Disguised interest refers to any commission or consideration stipulated by the creditor that, together with agreed interest, exceeds the maximum legal limit, as defined in Article 227.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Syndicate">
  <data key="d0">The Syndicate</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Syndicate is the body that decides by majority on the reconstruction of destroyed buildings and provides loans to co-owners secured by a privileged charge on their property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Privileged Charge">
  <data key="d0">Privileged Charge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Privileged Charge is a legal security on a co-owner's divided part and undivided share in common parts of a building, ranking from the date of its registration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The State">
  <data key="d0">The State</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The State is identified as a juristic person under Egyptian law, along with its provinces (mudirias), towns, and villages.&lt;SEP&gt;The State is the governing entity that owns uncultivated land without an owner and authorizes its appropriation or possession in accordance with regulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohammedan Law">
  <data key="d0">Mohammedan Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohammedan Law is Islamic law governing the establishment of heirs, their hereditary shares, and the devolution of estate property onto them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Court">
  <data key="d0">The Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Court is the judicial authority empowered to order the dissolution of a partnership upon the demand of any partner for non-performance of obligations or other reasons not attributable to the partners.&lt;SEP&gt;The Court is the judicial body that oversees estate administration, appoints administrators, orders preservation measures, and controls the winding up process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Administrator of an Estate">
  <data key="d0">Administrator of an Estate</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Administrator of an Estate is a person appointed to take possession of estate property, wind up the estate under court control, publish notices to creditors and debtors, and file statements of assets and liabilities within prescribed time limits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Testamentary Executor">
  <data key="d0">Testamentary Executor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Testamentary Executor is a person appointed by the deceased to manage the estate, subject to confirmation by the Judge, and governed by the same rules as an administrator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Greffier">
  <data key="d0">The Greffier</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Greffier is the court clerk responsible for entering day by day the court orders regarding the appointment of administrators and confirmation of testamentary executors in a public register with alphabetical indexes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ministere Public">
  <data key="d0">Ministere Public</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Ministere Public is the public prosecution office that can request the discharge and replacement of an estate administrator or urgent preservation measures for estate property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Juge de Service">
  <data key="d0">Juge de Service</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Juge de Service, or Judge in Chambers, is the judicial officer who authorizes alimentary allowances to heirs supported by the deceased and resolves disputes regarding such allowances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inheritance Certificate">
  <data key="d0">Inheritance Certificate</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Inheritance Certificate is a document that must be delivered to an heir before the heir may dispose of estate assets, recover estate debts, or set off personal debts against estate debts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Omdah">
  <data key="d0">The Omdah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Omdah is a local official whose main residence door serves as a mandatory posting location for estate-related notices in towns or villages where estate property is situated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Estate">
  <data key="d0">Estate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Estate is the total collection of property, assets, rights, and liabilities left by a deceased person, subject to formal administration, debt settlement, and division among heirs according to law.&lt;SEP&gt;The Estate comprises the property, assets, and obligations of a deceased person that are subject to administration, winding up, and distribution to heirs under court supervision.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heirs">
  <data key="d0">Heirs</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The successors in title of a deceased voluntary agent or principal, who are bound by the same obligations as the deceased under Article 194, specifically referencing the obligations of a mandatary's heirs under Article 717.&lt;SEP&gt;Persons entitled to inherit from a deceased person; in the context of sale during last illness, heirs may ratify a sale exceeding one third of the inheritance value or require the purchaser to pay the amount necessary to make up the two thirds.&lt;SEP&gt;Heirs are the successors of a deceased partner who may continue the Partnership in place of the deceased partner by agreement, even if they are minors, as provided under Article 528.&lt;SEP&gt;Heirs are the persons entitled to inherit from the deceased, obligated to inform the administrator of known estate debts and claims, who receive the residue of the estate property proportionally after settlement of all liabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Heirs are persons entitled to inherit from the deceased, receive alimentary allowances during the winding up process, and ultimately receive shares of the estate property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Deceased">
  <data key="d0">The Deceased</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Deceased is the person whose estate is subject to administration, who may have appointed a testamentary executor, and whose burial and funeral expenses must be paid from the estate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buried Treasure">
  <data key="d0">Buried Treasure</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Buried Treasure refers to hidden or buried valuables that no one can establish ownership over, belonging to the owner or bare owner of the property where discovered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uncultivated Land">
  <data key="d0">Uncultivated Land</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uncultivated Land is land without an owner that becomes the property of the State and can only be appropriated with state authority, though cultivation or building confers immediate ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Estate Winding Up">
  <data key="d0">Estate Winding Up</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Estate Winding Up is the legal process of administering and settling a deceased person's estate, including inventory, preservation, payment of debts, publication of notices, and distribution to heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movables Without an Owner">
  <data key="d0">Movables Without an Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Movables Without an Owner refers to property whose owner has abandoned possession with the intention of renouncing ownership, or property that never had an owner, and which can be acquired by taking possession with the intent of appropriation under Article 870.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wild Animals">
  <data key="d0">Wild Animals</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Wild Animals are non-domestic animals deemed to have no owner as long as they remain at liberty, and regain ownerless status if the owner does not immediately seek them or ceases pursuit after they regain freedom.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Domestic Animals">
  <data key="d0">Domestic Animals</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Domestic Animals are animals that are tamed and accustomed to return to a designated place, distinguished from wild animals in that they are not automatically considered ownerless when at liberty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Regulations">
  <data key="d0">Special Regulations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Special Regulations are legal rules governing the rights of fishing, hunting, things found, and antiquities as referenced in Article 873.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Antiquities">
  <data key="d0">Antiquities</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Antiquities are historical objects and artifacts whose ownership and discovery rights are governed by special regulations under Article 873.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Register">
  <data key="d0">Public Register</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Public Register is a court-maintained register recording orders regarding the appointment of administrators and confirmation of testamentary executors, indexed alphabetically by the names of deceased persons under Article 879.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Debtors of the Estate">
  <data key="d0">Debtors of the Estate</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Debtors of the Estate are persons who owe obligations to the estate and must be called upon by the administrator to submit particulars of their debts within three months of the last publication of notice under Article 886.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acquisition by Appropriation">
  <data key="d0">Acquisition by Appropriation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Acquisition by Appropriation is a legal method of acquiring ownership of ownerless movables or immovables by taking possession with the intent of appropriation, as described in Articles 870 through 874.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statement of Assets and Liabilities">
  <data key="d0">Statement of Assets and Liabilities</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Statement of Assets and Liabilities is a document that the administrator must file with the Court registry within four months of appointment, detailing the assets and liabilities of the estate with an estimate of their value under Article 887.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constituent of Wakf">
  <data key="d0">Constituent of Wakf</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The person who established the wakf endowment and may delegate leasing authority to beneficiaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agricultural Land">
  <data key="d0">Agricultural Land</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Land used for agricultural purposes subject to specific lease regulations regarding cultivation methods, crop rotation, and maintenance obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cattle and Agricultural Implements">
  <data key="d0">Cattle and Agricultural Implements</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Items found on agricultural land that may be included in a lease, requiring the lessee to properly care for and maintain them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Amodiation">
  <data key="d0">Amodiation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sharecropping arrangement where agricultural or tree-planted land is granted to a lessee in exchange for a fixed share of the crop as rent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 608">
  <data key="d0">Article 608</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision allowing either contracting party to terminate a fixed-period lease when serious and unforeseen circumstances render performance too burdensome, subject to notice under Article 563 and equitable compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 609">
  <data key="d0">Article 609</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision permitting officials or employees to terminate dwelling leases when their duties require a change of residence, with contrary agreements being void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 612">
  <data key="d0">Article 612</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision stipulating that when an agricultural lease is expressed in years, it is deemed to cover annual crop rotations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 614">
  <data key="d0">Article 614</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision allocating routine repair responsibilities to the lessee and major repairs and building construction to the lessor for agricultural land.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 615">
  <data key="d0">Article 615</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision relieving the lessee from payment of all or part of the rent when force majeure prevents land preparation or sowing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 616">
  <data key="d0">Article 616</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision allowing rent remission for total crop loss and rent reduction for partial loss due to force majeure, unless compensated by other profits or insurance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 619">
  <data key="d0">Article 619</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision defining amodiation as granting agricultural or tree-planted land to a lessee in consideration of the lessor receiving a fixed share of the crop.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 624">
  <data key="d0">Article 624</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision governing equal division of produce in amodiation by default and shared bearing of force majeure losses between both parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 626">
  <data key="d0">Article 626</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision stating that amodiation does not terminate on the death of the lessor but terminates on the death of the lessee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 628">
  <data key="d0">Article 628</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision granting the Nazir the exclusive right to lease wakf property, while beneficiaries cannot lease without authorization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 630">
  <data key="d0">Article 630</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision prohibiting the Nazir from leasing wakf property to himself but permitting leasing to ascendants and descendants at current rent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 633">
  <data key="d0">Article 633</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision limiting the Nazir's authority to lease wakf property to a maximum of three years without judicial authorization.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 633 limits the Nazir's authority to lease wakf property to three years without the judge's permission, with an exception when the Nazir is also the founder or sole beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 365">
  <data key="d0">Article 365</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 365 provides that compensation takes place only when invoked by the interested party, extinguishes both debts to the extent of the smaller amount, and cannot be renounced before the right arises.&lt;SEP&gt;A referenced legal provision governing notice periods for lease termination.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 610">
  <data key="d0">Article 610</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision stating that when the leased property is agricultural land, the lessor is not bound to hand over cattle and agricultural implements to the lessee unless they are included in the lease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 611">
  <data key="d0">Article 611</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision obligating the lessee to take proper care of and maintain cattle and agricultural implements belonging to the lessor in the manner required for their customary use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 613">
  <data key="d0">Article 613</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision requiring the lessee of agricultural land to work the land according to normal agricultural use, maintain it in a good state of production, and prohibiting substantial changes to cultivation methods without the lessor's consent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 617">
  <data key="d0">Article 617</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision allowing the lessee to remain on agricultural land after the lease ends, upon payment of proportional rent, until the harvest ripens for reasons not imputable to the lessee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495942</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 618">
  <data key="d0">Article 618</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision prohibiting an outgoing lessee from diminishing or retarding the enjoyment of the land by an incoming lessee, and requiring the outgoing lessee to allow the incoming lessee to prepare and sow the land.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 620">
  <data key="d0">Article 620</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision stating that in the absence of contrary agreement or custom, the conditions governing leases apply to amodiation subject to specific subsequent provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 621">
  <data key="d0">Article 621</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision establishing that when no term is fixed, an amodiation is deemed to have been granted for one yearly rotation of crops.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 622">
  <data key="d0">Article 622</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision providing that a lease in case of amodiation includes agricultural implements and cattle belonging to the lessor which are on the land at the time of the agreement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 623">
  <data key="d0">Article 623</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision requiring the lessee in amodiation to give the same care to cultivation and crop preservation as to his own affairs, holding him responsible for land deterioration unless he proves reasonable care was exercised.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 625">
  <data key="d0">Article 625</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision prohibiting the lessee in amodiation from assigning the lease or subletting the land without the consent of the lessor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 627">
  <data key="d0">Article 627</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision requiring the lessor to reimburse the lessee or his heirs for expenditures on unripened crops when amodiation ceases before its term, and allowing heirs to continue cultivation until crops ripen if the lessee dies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 629">
  <data key="d0">Article 629</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision designating the Nazir as the person entitled to receive rent for wakf property, prohibiting payment to the beneficiary without the Nazir's consent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 631">
  <data key="d0">Article 631</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision rendering a lease of wakf property invalid if the rent is grossly inadequate, unless the lessor is the sole beneficiary with administrative power, in which case the lease binds only the lessor and not succeeding beneficiaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 632">
  <data key="d0">Article 632</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal provision requiring rent estimation for wakf property leases to be made at the time of contract conclusion, and obligating the lessee to supplement inadequate rent under penalty of contract resiliation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Incoming Lessee">
  <data key="d0">Incoming Lessee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The person who succeeds an outgoing lessee in the use of agricultural land and is entitled to prepare and sow the land before taking possession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Greffier Of The Court">
  <data key="d0">Greffier Of The Court</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Greffier of the Court is the court official who must notify the debtor of the judgment charge order, endorse it on the authenticated copy of the judgment, and inform the greffier of the Court that rendered the judgment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Court Of First Instance">
  <data key="d0">Court Of First Instance</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Court of First Instance is a judicial body before which the debtor or creditor may appeal orders related to judgment charges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="President Of The Court">
  <data key="d0">President Of The Court</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The President of the Court is the judicial officer who decides on applications for judgment charges, and whose rejection of such an application may be appealed by the creditor to the Court of First Instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledgor">
  <data key="d0">Pledgor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Pledgor is the person who undertakes to deliver the thing pledged as security for a debt, guarantees the pledge and its efficacy, and must not diminish the value of the pledged thing.&lt;SEP&gt;The debtor who grants the pledge and may request restitution of the pledged thing, apply to the Judge for authorization of an advantageous sale, and has the right to be informed of collections made on the pledged debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledgee">
  <data key="d0">Pledgee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Pledgee is the creditor who receives the thing pledged, must preserve and maintain it with reasonable care, manage it, and may not derive gratuitous advantage from it; must restitute it upon repayment of the debt.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pledgee is the creditor who holds a pledge and, if unpaid when the pledged debt and secured debt fall due, may collect the pledged debt up to the amount due, demand its sale, or request its allocation in accordance with Article 1121, paragraph 2.&lt;SEP&gt;The creditor who holds the pledge, with rights including collection of interest and periodic payments, enforcement of the pledge through sale or appropriation, and obligations including preservation of the pledged thing, payment of taxes and charges, and protection of the pledged debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Thing Pledged">
  <data key="d0">The Thing Pledged</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Thing Pledged is the movable or immovable property delivered by the pledgor to the pledgee as security for a debt, which must be capable of being sold independently by public auction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter III">
  <data key="d0">Chapter III</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter III, titled "Rights Derived from the Right of Ownership Pledge," is the legal chapter encompassing the rules on pledges including their elements, effects, and the obligations of the pledgor and pledgee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section II">
  <data key="d0">Section II</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section II covers the effects of a judgment charge, its reduction, and extinguishment, as well as the effects of a pledge between contracting parties and as regards third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fruits And Revenue">
  <data key="d0">Fruits And Revenue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fruits and Revenue are the produce or income generated by the thing pledged, which the pledgee must apply in reduction of the secured debt in a specific order: first to preservation expenses and repairs, then to expenses and interest, and then to the capital amount of the debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Person Assuming the Debt">
  <data key="d0">Person Assuming the Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A third party who undertakes to assume a debt in place of the original debtor through an agreement, who is responsible to effect payment to the creditor until ratification or refusal, and who may raise defenses available to the original debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Original Debtor">
  <data key="d0">Original Debtor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The original debtor in an assignment of debt scenario who enters into an agreement with a third party to transfer the debt, and who warrants the solvency of the person assuming the debt at the moment the creditor ratifies the assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distrainer">
  <data key="d0">Distrainer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who serves an attachment upon the debtor of an assigned debt, whose rights are affected by whether the assignment has become effective against third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignment of Right">
  <data key="d0">Assignment of Right</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The transfer of a right from an assignor to an assignee, governed by provisions regarding warranties, solvency of the debtor, effectiveness against third parties, and preference among multiple assignments of the same right.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignment of Debt">
  <data key="d0">Assignment of Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The transfer of a debt obligation from an original debtor to a person assuming the debt, effected by agreement between the parties, requiring creditor ratification to be effective, and governed by provisions on warranties, defenses, and surety obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warranty of Solvency">
  <data key="d0">Warranty of Solvency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A guarantee regarding the financial solvency of the debtor, which the assignor does not provide unless specifically stipulated, and which only applies to the debtor's solvency at the time of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Recourse in Warranty">
  <data key="d0">Right of Recourse in Warranty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The right of an assignee to seek remedy against the assignor, limiting the assignor's liability to restitution of what was received together with interest and expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defense">
  <data key="d0">Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal arguments that a debtor may raise against an assignee, including those previously available against the assignor at the time the assignment became effective, and those arising from the contract of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter V: Extinction of Obligations">
  <data key="d0">Chapter V: Extinction of Obligations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A chapter of the civil code covering the various ways in which obligations are extinguished, including through payment and subrogation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section II: Assignment of Debt">
  <data key="d0">Section II: Assignment of Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A section of the civil code governing the transfer of debt obligations from an original debtor to a person assuming the debt, including provisions on creditor ratification, warranties, and defenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 309">
  <data key="d0">Article 309</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that an assignor does not warrant the solvency of the debtor unless such warranty is specifically stipulated, and if warranted, it only applies to solvency at the time of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 313">
  <data key="d0">Article 313</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision providing that in the event of several assignments relating to the same right, preference is given to the assignment that is first effective as regards third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 316">
  <data key="d0">Article 316</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that an assignment of debt is not effective against the creditor unless ratified by the creditor, and that silence after a reasonable period constitutes refusal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 326">
  <data key="d0">Article 326</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision enumerating the cases in which a third party who makes payment is subrogated into the rights of the creditor, including joint liability, prior creditor status, acquisition of mortgaged property, and statutory subrogation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 322">
  <data key="d0">Article 322</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision governing the sale of mortgaged immovable property, stating that sale does not imply transfer of the mortgage debt unless agreed, and that creditor silence for six months after notification constitutes ratification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 310">
  <data key="d0">Article 310</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that when an assignee exercises the right of recourse in warranty against the assignor in accordance with the two preceding articles, the assignor is only liable to restitute what he has received together with interest and expenses notwithstanding any agreement to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 311">
  <data key="d0">Article 311</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that an assignor is responsible for personal acts, even if the assignment is not for valuable consideration or even if it has been stipulated to be without warranty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495943</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 312">
  <data key="d0">Article 312</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a debtor may raise against the assignee the defenses he was entitled to raise against the assignor at the moment the assignment became effective, and may also raise defenses arising from the contract of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 314">
  <data key="d0">Article 314</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision governing the effect of attachment served upon the debtor before an assignment becomes effective against third parties, making the assignment equivalent to an attachment, and providing for pro rata division of debt among the first distrainer, the assignee, and the second distrainer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 315">
  <data key="d0">Article 315</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that an assignment of debt is effected by an agreement between the debtor and a third party who undertakes to assume the debt in place of the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 317">
  <data key="d0">Article 317</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that until the creditor ratifies or refuses the assignment, the person assuming the debt is responsible to the original debtor to effect payment on due date to the creditor, and that the original debtor cannot compel payment by the person assuming the debt until he has discharged his own obligation under the contract of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 318">
  <data key="d0">Article 318</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that the debt assigned is transmitted with all its warranties, but the surety, whether real or personal, does not remain bound to the creditor unless he has agreed to the assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 319">
  <data key="d0">Article 319</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that in the absence of agreement to the contrary, the original debtor warrants the solvency of the person assuming the debt at the moment the creditor ratifies the assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 320">
  <data key="d0">Article 320</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a person assuming the debt may raise against the creditor the defenses which the original debtor was entitled to raise, as well as defenses arising out of the contract of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 321">
  <data key="d0">Article 321</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that an assignment of debt may also take place between the creditor and the person assuming the debt by an agreement providing that such person replaces the original debtor in his obligation, with Articles 318 and 320 being applicable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 323">
  <data key="d0">Article 323</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that payment may be made by the debtor, his representative, or any interested party, and also by a third party who is not interested, even without the knowledge or against the wish of the debtor, subject to the debtor's right to oppose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 324">
  <data key="d0">Article 324</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a third party who pays off the debt has a remedy against the debtor up to the amount paid, and that the debtor against whose wish payment was made may contest the claim if he shows interest in opposing the payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 325">
  <data key="d0">Article 325</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that payment is only valid if the payer is the owner of that with which he has paid and has the capacity to dispose of it, but payment by a person without legal capacity still extinguishes the obligation provided it does not prejudice the payer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 327">
  <data key="d0">Article 327</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a creditor who receives payment from a third party may by agreement subrogate such third party into his rights even without the debtor's consent, provided the agreement is concluded at the time of payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 328">
  <data key="d0">Article 328</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a debtor who has borrowed money to pay the debt may subrogate the lender into the rights of the creditor even without the creditor's consent, provided the contract of loan states the purpose and the receipt identifies the source of funds.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 329">
  <data key="d0">Article 329</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a third party subrogated by law or agreement in the rights of the creditor is substituted for the creditor as regards the debt to the amount of sums paid, with all attributes, accessories, securities, and defenses attached to the debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 330">
  <data key="d0">Article 330</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision governing partial payment and subrogation, stating that a creditor shall not be prejudiced by partial payment and retains priority over the paying third party, and that a further subrogated party shares pro rata with the first.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 331">
  <data key="d0">Article 331</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a holder of a mortgaged property who has paid all the mortgage debt and been subrogated into the rights of creditors may only claim from the holder of another property mortgaged for the same debt the proportional share based on the value of the immovable held.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 332">
  <data key="d0">Article 332</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that payment shall be made to the creditor or his representative, and that a person producing a receipt in discharge from the creditor is deemed qualified to receive payment unless it has been agreed that payment shall be made to the creditor in person.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 333">
  <data key="d0">Article 333</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that if payment is made to a person other than the creditor or his representative, the debtor is not discharged unless the creditor ratifies the payment or derives benefit from it, to the extent of such benefit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section I: Payment">
  <data key="d0">Section I: Payment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A section of the civil code covering payment as a means of extinguishing obligations, including provisions on who may make payment, conditions of validity, subrogation, and the proper recipient of payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holder of Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d0">Holder of Mortgaged Property</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A third party holder of a mortgaged immovable property who has paid all the mortgage debt and has been subrogated into the rights of the creditors, with the right to claim proportional contributions from holders of other properties mortgaged for the same debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lender">
  <data key="d0">Lender</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who lends money to a debtor for the purpose of paying off a debt, and who may be subrogated into the rights of the creditor who received the payment.&lt;SEP&gt;The party to a loan for consumption who must deliver the thing that is the object of the contract to the borrower and bears the loss if the thing perishes before delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Court">
  <data key="d0">Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Court is the judicial body vested with broad authority across civil, contractual, property, and estate matters. It serves as the primary judicial authority empowered to authorize deposits, public auctions, and judicial custody, as well as to grant reasonable delays for the performance of obligations. In the realm of contracts, the Court decides points of detail in disputes according to the nature of the transaction, the provisions of law, custom, and equity. It may order the nullity of a void contract on its own initiative and, in cases of economic imbalance caused by exceptional events, it has the authority to reduce compensation due for loss of profit, grant price increases, or order the resiliation of the contract.

The Court exercises significant powers over personal status and capacity. It pronounces or raises interdictions, appoints judicial advisers, and ratifies acts of minors and interdicted persons. It also adjudicates avoidance claims and is authorized to grant revocation of a gift and adjudicate on the nullity of partnership deeds. In partnership matters specifically, the Court may order dissolution, appoint liquidators, and authorize a partner's retirement upon application.

In property and lease matters, the Court authorizes preventive measures against building dangers, adjudicates claims, and its involvement triggers obligations such as restitution of profits. It may maintain a lease despite a prohibition on sub-letting when a commercial or industrial establishment is sold, grant the lessor time for settlement of payments for improvements, and authorize the lessee to carry out repairs and deduct costs from rent when the lessor delays maintenance obligations. The Court also grants damages instead of material redress for breaches of servitude restrictions when it considers there are reasons for doing so, and decides matters in accordance with equity when movables belonging to different owners become mingled. It handles actions in preemption before the district court where the property is situated.

Regarding co-ownership and joint property, the Court resolves disputes among co-owners, appoints managers for commonly held property, orders security for dissenting co-owners, and decides on the alienation of jointly owned property. It may authorize a co-owner to withdraw a share from family joint ownership before the agreed term, discharge a manager, order the sale of a lower storey, and appoint a representative of a syndicate.

The Court oversees the entire estate administration process, including receiving inventory filings, investigating disputes, authorizing debt payments, fixing deadlines, ordering the sale or allocation of property, and issuing inheritance certificates. It is also the body before which conditions of sale are filed and where third party holders or applicants deposit sums to cover costs or satisfy creditors. Additionally, the Court renders orders authorizing judgment charges, and its greffier is responsible for notifying and endorsing such orders. The Court orders judicial custody, stamps receiver account books, and receives copies of accounts from court-appointed receivers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Receipt in Discharge">
  <data key="d0">Receipt in Discharge</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A document issued by the creditor that, when produced to the debtor, deems the bearer qualified to receive payment unless it has been agreed that payment shall be made to the creditor in person.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Loan">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Loan</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">An agreement between a debtor and a lender in which the sum borrowed is stated to be for the purpose of effecting payment of a debt, enabling subrogation of the lender into the creditor's rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract of Assignment">
  <data key="d0">Contract of Assignment</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The agreement governing the assignment of a right or debt, from which defenses may be derived and which defines the obligations of the parties involved.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Managing Partner">
  <data key="d0">Managing Partner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Managing Partner is a partner entrusted with the management of the partnership by a special clause in the partnership deed or by subsequent appointment, entitled to perform acts of management and disposition within the objects of the partnership, provided they are not tainted with fraud.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Personal Creditors">
  <data key="d0">Personal Creditors</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Personal Creditors are creditors of an individual partner who may only claim payment from the partner's share in the profits during the continuance of the partnership, and from the partner's share in assets after liquidation and payment of partnership debts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 514">
  <data key="d0">Article 514</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 514 establishes rules for profit and loss sharing when the partnership deed does not fix shares, requiring shares to be proportional to capital contributions, and granting additional shares for partners contributing both services and money or property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 515">
  <data key="d0">Article 515</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 515 declares the partnership deed void if one partner agrees not to participate in profits or losses, and permits a service-only partner to be relieved from sharing losses provided no remuneration is allowed for his services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 516">
  <data key="d0">Article 516</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 516 governs the appointment and removal of managing partners, granting managing partners appointed by the deed authority to act despite objections, while allowing subsequent appointments to be revoked like ordinary mandates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 517">
  <data key="d0">Article 517</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 517 regulates the conduct of multiple managing partners, allowing each to act separately unless unanimity or majority decisions are required, with exceptions only for urgent matters involving serious and irreparable loss.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 518">
  <data key="d0">Article 518</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 518 provides that majority decisions shall be decided by numerical majority in the absence of an agreement to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Auction">
  <data key="d0">Public Auction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Public Auction is a legally governed sale method conducted in accordance with formal statutory provisions and judicial authorization. It serves as the prescribed or required mechanism in several distinct legal contexts, including the liquidation of movable and immovable property belonging to an estate—carried out following the manner and delays established for forced sales unless all heirs unanimously agree to an alternative method—as well as the forced sale of mortgaged immovable property through compulsory expropriation formalities, which may be initiated either by a creditor's application or by a third-party holder's wish to purge the mortgage. A mortgaged property must be marketable and susceptible to sale by public auction for this mechanism to apply.

Public Auction also functions as a court-authorized method for the sale of pledged property, particularly when the pledged item is deteriorating, in danger of perishing, or when there is a default of payment, as referenced in Articles 1119, 1121, and 1129. Only movables or immovables that can be sold independently may become the object of a pledge under this framework, as stated in Article 1097. Additionally, it is the designated sale method for items of a rapidly perishing nature or movables that incur exorbitant storage costs, with the proceeds from such sales deposited in the Caisse du Tribunal.

Several legal consequences attach specifically to sales conducted by Public Auction. A contract is formed only upon acceptance of the final bid, and any prior bid is nullified from the moment a higher bid is made, even if that higher bid is subsequently found to be void. Because a Public Auction is conducted in conformity with legal provisions, an action for supplement of price on grounds of lesion does not lie. Furthermore, preemption rights cannot be exercised when the sale is made by Public Auction, as explicitly stated in Article 939.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495944</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 519">
  <data key="d0">Article 519</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 519 excludes non-managing partners from management but grants them the right to personally examine the books and documents of the partnership, rendering any agreement to the contrary void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 520">
  <data key="d0">Article 520</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 520 provides that in the absence of special provisions on management, each partner is deemed authorized to manage the partnership, subject to the right of other partners to object before completion of any act.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 521">
  <data key="d0">Article 521</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 521 obliges each partner to abstain from activities prejudicial to the partnership and to exercise the same care as for his own interests, with a higher standard of care for remunerated managers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 522">
  <data key="d0">Article 522</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 522 holds a partner liable for interest on partnership money taken or retained, and entitles a partner to interest on private funds advanced to the partnership in good faith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 523">
  <data key="d0">Article 523</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 523 makes partners personally liable for partnership debts not covered by partnership assets, each in proportion to his share in losses, and voids any agreement relieving a partner from such liability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 524">
  <data key="d0">Article 524</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 524 provides that partners are not jointly or severally liable for partnership debts unless otherwise agreed, but if one partner becomes insolvent, his share is apportioned among the others.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 525">
  <data key="d0">Article 525</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 525 restricts personal creditors of a partner from claiming against the partner's capital share during the partnership, limiting them to the partner's profit share, while allowing protective attachment before liquidation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 526">
  <data key="d0">Article 526</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 526 states that a partnership ends upon expiration of its term or achievement of its object, but is extended year by year if partners continue operations, subject to creditor opposition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 527">
  <data key="d0">Article 527</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 527 provides that a partnership ends upon total or substantial loss of its capital, and is dissolved if a specific thing pledged as contribution perishes before delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 528">
  <data key="d0">Article 528</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 528 provides that a partnership is terminated by death, interdiction, insolvency, or bankruptcy of a partner, but may continue with heirs or remaining partners by agreement, with the departing partner's share valued at the date of the event.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 529">
  <data key="d0">Article 529</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 529 permits termination of a partnership by retirement of a partner with no fixed term, provided proper notice is given without fraudulent intent or unsuitable timing, or by unanimous agreement of all partners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 530">
  <data key="d0">Article 530</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 530 empowers the Court to order dissolution of the partnership for non-performance by a partner of his obligations or for other reasons not attributable to the partners, and voids any agreement to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Partner Manager">
  <data key="d0">Non-Partner Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Non-Partner Manager is a manager of the Partnership who is not a partner, and who may be discharged from his post at any time, as distinguished from a managing partner appointed under the Partnership Deed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partnership Capital">
  <data key="d0">Partnership Capital</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Partnership Capital refers to the total contributions made by partners to the Partnership, forming the financial foundation against which profit shares, loss shares, and partnership debts are measured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partnership Liquidation">
  <data key="d0">Partnership Liquidation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Partnership Liquidation is the process of winding up the Partnership's affairs, after which personal creditors of a partner may enforce their claims against the partner's share in the Partnership assets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partnership Books and Documents">
  <data key="d0">Partnership Books and Documents</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Partnership Books and Documents are the financial records of the Partnership that non-managing partners are entitled to personally examine, with any agreement to the contrary being void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agreement of Lease">
  <data key="d0">Agreement of Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The contract between the lessor and lessee establishing their respective rights and obligations, including terms on rent, use of the property, warranties, and conditions for resiliation or alteration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Government Authority">
  <data key="d0">Government Authority</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A governmental body whose lawful acts may appreciably diminish the enjoyment of the leased property, entitling the lessee to claim resiliation of the lease or reduction of rent, and potentially damages if the grounds are imputable to the lessor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warranty Against Defects">
  <data key="d0">Warranty Against Defects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The lessor's obligation to ensure the leased property is free from defects that prevent or appreciably diminish enjoyment, except for defects customarily tolerated or known to the lessee at the time of contract. The lessor is not liable for defects of which the lessee was informed or aware.&lt;SEP&gt;Warranty Against Defects is a legal guarantee obligating the vendor for defects in the thing sold that diminish its value or usefulness at the time of delivery, even if the vendor was ignorant of them. It does not cover defects known to the purchaser, discoverable by reasonable inspection, or customarily tolerated, unless the vendor affirmed the absence of defects or fraudulently concealed them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reduction of Rent">
  <data key="d0">Reduction of Rent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A remedy available to the lessee when the enjoyment of the leased property is appreciably diminished due to third-party claims, government authority acts, trespass, or defects in the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lien">
  <data key="d0">Lien</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The lessor's right to retain and recover attachable movables found in the leased property as security for all amounts due under the lease, even when such movables do not belong to the lessee. This right is limited by professional requirements and customary needs of the lessee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servants of the Lessor">
  <data key="d0">Servants of the Lessor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Individuals acting on behalf of the lessor whose acts of disturbance or damage are covered by the lessor's warranty to the lessee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Successor in Title">
  <data key="d0">Successor in Title</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who acquires rights from the lessor and whose lawful claims against the lessee are covered by the lessor's warranty against disturbance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lessee's Repairs">
  <data key="d0">Lessee's Repairs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Maintenance and repair obligations of the lessee carried out in accordance with general usage, unless otherwise agreed between the parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Other Lessee">
  <data key="d0">Other Lessee</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Another tenant of the same lessor whose disturbances or lawful claims against the primary lessee are covered by the lessor's warranty. When several lessees rent the same property, the one who without fraud first enters into possession has preference.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fire">
  <data key="d0">Fire</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An event causing damage to the leased property for which the lessee is responsible unless the cause is proven not imputable to the lessee. When multiple lessees occupy a building, each is responsible in proportion to the part occupied, unless the fire started in one specific part.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alteration">
  <data key="d0">Alteration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Changes or modifications made by the lessee to the leased property, requiring the lessor's permission unless no damage is thereby occasioned to the lessor. Unauthorized alterations may compel the lessee to reinstate the property and pay compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security for Rent">
  <data key="d0">Security for Rent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The obligation of the lessee of a house, warehouse, shop, or agricultural land to stock the leased property with movables of sufficient value to guarantee rent payment for two years or the lease period if shorter, unless rent is paid in advance or other guarantees are provided.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Administrator">
  <data key="d0">Administrator</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An Administrator is a person appointed to manage the estate of a deceased person, responsible for filing an inventory of assets and liabilities with the Court within four months, informing interested parties, paying estate debts, executing legacies, and distributing remaining property to heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inventory">
  <data key="d0">Inventory</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The Inventory is a formal statement of the assets and liabilities of the estate with an estimate of their value, filed by the administrator with the registry of the Court, and subject to dispute by interested parties within thirty days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deceased">
  <data key="d0">Deceased</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Deceased (also referred to as the testator or Alwarith) is the person whose death gave rise to the estate, whose papers disclose claims and debts, and whose property is subject to administration and division among heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Registry Of The Court">
  <data key="d0">Registry Of The Court</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Registry of the Court is the official office where the administrator files the statement of assets and liabilities of the estate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elam Charei">
  <data key="d0">Elam Charei</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Elam Charei (I'lam Shari'i) is a formal legal document certifying a person's inheritance rights, which an heir must produce to the Court in order to obtain a certificate establishing rights in the inheritance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legacies">
  <data key="d0">Legacies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legacies are testamentary gifts or charges specified in a will that the administrator must execute and pay after the settlement of all debts of the estate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interested Party">
  <data key="d0">Interested Party</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An Interested Party is any person with a legal interest in the estate who must be informed of the filing of the inventory by registered letter and who may petition the Court regarding disputes within thirty days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agricultural Industrial Or Commercial Enterprise">
  <data key="d0">Agricultural Industrial Or Commercial Enterprise</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Agricultural, Industrial or Commercial Enterprise is an economic unit within the estate that constitutes a distinct economic unity and must be allotted as a whole to the most capable heir requesting it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Family Papers">
  <data key="d0">Family Papers</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Family Papers are documents and articles of sentimental value to the heirs owing to their relationship to the deceased, whose division or sale is ordered by the Court in the absence of agreement among the heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Forced Sales">
  <data key="d0">Forced Sales</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Forced Sales refer to the prescribed legal procedure and delays governing the public auction sale of movable and immovable estate property when heirs do not unanimously agree on an alternative method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 554">
  <data key="d0">Article 554</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 554 is a referenced legal provision governing the calculation of amounts payable to creditors when a debt not yet due is made immediately exigible at the request of all heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extinctive Prescription">
  <data key="d0">Extinctive Prescription</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Extinctive Prescription is a legal mechanism that extinguishes obligations through the passage of time, with varying periods of fifteen years, five years, three years, or one year depending on the type of obligation.&lt;SEP&gt;Extinctive prescription is a legal mechanism that extinguishes obligations through the passage of time, with a general term of fifteen years and special shorter terms for certain periodic rights.&lt;SEP&gt;Extinctive prescription refers to rules governing the calculation, suspension, and interruption of prescription periods, which are applied by analogy to acquisitive prescription under Article 973.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal mechanism that extinguishes rights through the passage of time, whose rules are applicable to acquisitive prescription where compatible.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Contract">
  <data key="d0">Sale Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Sale Contract is an agreement whereby the vendor binds himself to transfer to the purchaser the ownership of a thing or any other propriety right in consideration of a price in money.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sale Contract is the legal agreement between the vendor and the purchaser governing the transfer of ownership of the thing sold in exchange for a price. Its costs, stamp duties, transcription fees, and other expenses are borne by the purchaser in the absence of contrary agreement or usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State">
  <data key="d0">State</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The State is the governmental entity to which taxes and dues are owed, with a three-year prescription period applicable to such claims.&lt;SEP&gt;The State is a sovereign entity that owns immovable and movable property which may be allocated for public utility purposes as part of the public domain.&lt;SEP&gt;The governmental entity that acquires ownership of land uncovered by the sea, as stated in Article 919.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Periodic Payments">
  <data key="d0">Periodic Payments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Periodic Payments are sums payable at recurring intervals such as rent of buildings and agricultural land, interest, periodical payments, salaries, wages, and pensions, subject to a five-year prescription period even if the debt is admitted by the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professional Remuneration">
  <data key="d0">Professional Remuneration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Professional Remuneration refers to sums due to physicians, chemists, lawyers, engineers, experts, receivers in bankruptcy, brokers, professors, and teachers for work within the scope of their professions or expenses incurred, subject to a five-year prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taxes and Dues">
  <data key="d0">Taxes and Dues</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Taxes and Dues are financial obligations owed to the State, subject to a three-year prescription period that runs from the end of the year for which they are due or from the date of relevant legal proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescription Interruption">
  <data key="d0">Prescription Interruption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescription Interruption is a legal mechanism that halts and restarts the prescription period, triggered by legal proceedings, summons, attachment, creditor claims in bankruptcy, or express or tacit admission by the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescription Suspension">
  <data key="d0">Prescription Suspension</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescription Suspension is a legal mechanism that halts the running of prescription when there is a bar preventing the creditor from claiming his right, including moral bars, lack of legal representation for incapable persons, absent persons, or convicted criminals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Natural Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Natural Obligation is a residual moral obligation that remains upon the debtor even after the legal obligation has been extinguished by prescription.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of obligation that exists without direct legal enforcement, determined by the judge in the absence of legal provision. A debtor cannot claim restitution of what was voluntarily given to discharge a natural obligation. No natural obligation can be contrary to public policy.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of obligation whose performance cannot be enforced, but which may serve as a valid cause for a civil obligation; a debtor cannot claim restitution of what was voluntarily given to discharge it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale by Sample">
  <data key="d0">Sale by Sample</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sale by Sample is a type of sale where the thing sold must conform to the sample, and the burden of proof lies with the party in whose custody the sample deteriorated or perished.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale upon Trial">
  <data key="d0">Sale upon Trial</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sale upon Trial is a type of sale where the purchaser has the option to accept or refuse the thing sold within an agreed or reasonable time, and silence after the trial period is equivalent to acceptance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Subject to Tasting">
  <data key="d0">Sale Subject to Tasting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sale Subject to Tasting is a type of sale where the purchaser may accept the thing sold and must declare acceptance within a time fixed by agreement or custom, with the sale only concluded upon such declaration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Book II: Specific Contracts">
  <data key="d0">Book II: Specific Contracts</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Book II: Specific Contracts is a section of the civil code dealing with specific named contracts, including contracts regarding ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter VI: Proof of Obligations">
  <data key="d0">Chapter VI: Proof of Obligations</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter VI: Proof of Obligations is a chapter of the civil code covering Articles 389 through 417, all of which have been repealed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One-Year Prescription Rights">
  <data key="d0">One-Year Prescription Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One-Year Prescription Rights refer to claims that are prescribed after one year, including rights of action of merchants and manufacturers for things supplied, hotel and restaurant proprietors for accommodation and food costs, and workmen, servants, and wage earners for their pay and supplies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oath of Payment">
  <data key="d0">Oath of Payment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Oath of Payment is a procedural requirement where a person claiming the one-year prescription must swear that the debt has actually been paid; the Judge directs this oath ex officio, and if the debtor is deceased, it may be directed to the heirs or their guardians.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescription Calculation">
  <data key="d0">Prescription Calculation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescription Calculation refers to the rule that prescription periods are computed in days rather than hours, the first day is not counted, and the period is completed when the last day has ended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescription Commencement">
  <data key="d0">Prescription Commencement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescription Commencement governs when the prescription period begins to run, namely from the day the debt becomes due, from the realization of a suspensive condition, from the date of eviction in warranty claims, from the expiration of a term for future debts, or from when the creditor is in a position to express his will.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Party">
  <data key="d0">Third Party</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">In legal contexts, a Third Party is any person or entity who is not an original party to a contract, agreement, or legal obligation between two primary parties. The concept of a Third Party arises across a wide range of civil and contractual situations, consistently referring to an external participant whose rights, obligations, and interactions with the primary parties are governed by specific legal provisions.

In the realm of obligations and contracts, a Third Party may assume the role of a new debtor or a new creditor through the mechanism of novation, as described in Article 352. A Third Party may also make payment on behalf of a debtor and, upon doing so, may be subrogated into the rights of the creditor. However, while a Third Party may acquire rights under a contract through a stipulation for a Third Party—gaining a direct right against the promisor—they generally do not acquire obligations under a contract to which they are not a party. A Third Party may also serve as a beneficiary of consideration under Article 497 or as a debtor in the contribution of debts under Article 513, and may be affected by partnership publication formalities under Article 506.

In property-related matters, a Third Party may hold an existing right over a thing sold at the time of sale, which is enforceable against the purchaser and may cause disturbance in the purchaser's enjoyment. A Third Party may also acquire possession of property, such as a good-faith purchaser at a market or public sale, and may be subject to restitution claims. Additionally, in the context of property disposition, the lawful interest and lifetime of a Third Party—being a person other than the one disposing of property or the one in whose favor it is disposed—may serve as valid grounds for an inalienability clause under Article 823.

With respect to security interests, a Third Party is a person not party to a mortgage agreement; a mortgage is effective against Third Parties only if the deed or judgment establishing the mortgage has been inscribed before the Third Party acquires real rights on the property, subject to bankruptcy provisions. Similarly, in the context of a pledge, a Third Party may be chosen by the contracting parties to hold the pledged thing, and their rights are preserved under the law even as the pledge confers rights against all persons.

A Third Party may also play a role in matters of lease, where an external party may claim rights over the leased property or commit trespass or disturbance. In such cases, the lessee may take legal action against the Third Party in the lessee's own name for damages and possessory actions. Furthermore, a Third Party who is not a contracting party may practice fraudulent misrepresentation or duress; the victim of such Third Party acts may demand avoidance of the contract only if the other contracting party was aware or should have been aware of the fraud or duress, as specified in Articles 126 and 128.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Renunciation of Prescription">
  <data key="d0">Renunciation of Prescription</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Renunciation of Prescription refers to the rule that a debtor cannot renounce the benefits of prescription before acquiring the right to invoke it, nor agree to a term different from that fixed by law, but may renounce even tacitly after acquiring the right, provided the renunciation does not prejudice creditors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Written Document">
  <data key="d0">Written Document</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A Written Document establishing a right referred to in Articles 376 or 378 extends the prescription period for that right to fifteen years instead of the original shorter period.&lt;SEP&gt;The required form of evidence for establishing a compromise, which can only be proved by a written document or by an official proces-verbal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heirs and Guardians">
  <data key="d0">Heirs and Guardians</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Heirs and Guardians are persons who may be directed to take the oath of payment when the debtor is deceased, declaring either that they do not know of the existence of the debt or that they know it has been paid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 670">
  <data key="d0">Article 670</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 670 is a legal provision requiring the concessionaire of a public utility service who enjoys a de jure or de facto monopoly to observe strict equality between consumers regarding services rendered and rates charged, while permitting special treatment involving rate reduction if granted to all applicants fulfilling general conditions, and prohibiting granting advantages to some consumers while refusing them to others, with liability for compensation for discrimination disturbing fair competition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 671">
  <data key="d0">Article 671</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 671 is a legal provision stipulating that rates laid down by the public authority have force of law with respect to contracts between the concessionaire and consumers, that the parties cannot depart from them by agreement, and that modified rates apply without retroactive effect from the date fixed by the act of ratification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 672">
  <data key="d0">Article 672</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 672 is a legal provision stating that any irregularity or mistake in applying rates to individual contracts is subject to rectification, entitling the consumer to recover excess payments or the concessionaire to collect shortfalls, with the right of recovery barred by prescription after one year from collection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 673">
  <data key="d0">Article 673</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 673 is a legal provision requiring consumers of water, gas, electricity, and power distribution services to tolerate short interruptions necessary for upkeep, while permitting the concessionaire to repudiate responsibility for abnormal interruptions caused by force majeure or fortuitous events, including strikes under certain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 674">
  <data key="d0">Article 674</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 674 is a legal provision defining a contract of service as one whereby one contracting party undertakes to work in the service and under the supervision or control of the other party in consideration for remuneration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 675">
  <data key="d0">Article 675</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 675 is a legal provision stating that the provisions of the Contracts of Service section apply only insofar as they are not inconsistent with special laws relating to service, which define categories of workers excluded from these provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Debtor">
  <data key="d0">Debtor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Debtor (المدين) is the person or party legally obligated to fulfill, perform, or pay a debt or obligation to a creditor. The debtor's core duty is to deliver or perform the object of an obligation, and may be compelled to specific performance. When the degree of quality of the object is unspecified and cannot be determined by usage or circumstances, the debtor must supply an article of average quality. In monetary obligations, the debtor is bound only to the actual figure stated in the contract. The debtor is always liable for fraud or gross negligence and may be held liable for damages upon non-performance or delay. The debtor enjoys several rights, including the designation of which debt to discharge, the right to demand receipts, the right to make a judicial deposit, and the right of retraction of tender under specified conditions. In alternative obligations, the debtor holds election rights. A debtor cannot claim restitution of voluntary payments made to discharge a natural obligation.

The debtor may invoke defenses against an assignee that were available against the assignor, as well as defenses arising from the contract of assignment. The debtor may also invoke prescription as a defense, and may interrupt prescription by expressly or tacitly acknowledging the debt. In the case of a litigious right that has been assigned, the debtor may extinguish the assigned right by paying the assignee the actual price paid, together with expenses and interest. The debtor may be replaced through novation or delegation by a third party. Revocation of a third-party stipulation does not liberate the debtor vis-à-vis the stipulator unless expressly agreed.

In the context of mortgages and secured obligations, the debtor is the person obligated to pay the secured debt underlying the mortgage. The mortgagor may be the debtor or a third party acting in the debtor's interest. If mortgaged property perishes or deteriorates without the mortgagor's fault, the debtor may either furnish adequate security or pay the debt in full before it falls due. After the debt has fallen due, the debtor may transfer the mortgaged property to the creditor in payment of the debt. The debtor's property may also be subject to privileged rights, including general and special privileges covering obligations such as wages, alimony, taxes, and rent, and may be subject to execution to satisfy outstanding debts, expropriation proceedings, and judgment charges. The debtor may lodge an appeal against an order authorizing a judgment charge before the judge who gave the order or before the Court of First Instance.

A debtor who holds the right of redemption under an annuity may be compelled to redeem if payment defaults occur, securities prove insufficient, or insolvency arises. In the context of suretyship, the debtor's failure to perform triggers the surety's obligation, and the debtor may be required to offer a solvent surety residing in Egypt or adequate real security. If the debtor becomes bankrupt, the creditor must prove the debt.

A debtor may be declared bankrupt or insolvent if assets are insufficient to meet obligations, and is subject to penalties for willful insolvency or the concealment of property. Insolvency may result in the forfeiture of term benefits. Upon cessation of insolvency, the debtor may reclaim previously discharged debts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 676">
  <data key="d0">Article 676</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 676 is a legal provision applying contract of service rules to the relationship between masters and canvassers, commercial representatives, commercial travellers, assurance agents, and other intermediaries even if remunerated on commission, and granting departing commercial representatives the right to commission on orders resulting from their efforts during service.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 677">
  <data key="d0">Article 677</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 677 is a legal provision stating that a contract of service does not require any special form unless law or administrative regulations provide otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 678">
  <data key="d0">Article 678</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 678 is a legal provision allowing contracts of service to be concluded for a specific service, a fixed period, or an indefinite duration, and granting a worker the right to resiliate without compensation after five years with six months prior notice for contracts exceeding five years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 679">
  <data key="d0">Article 679</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 679 is a legal provision stating that a fixed-period contract terminates automatically upon expiration of its term, and that continued performance by both parties after expiration deems the contract renewed for an indefinite duration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 680">
  <data key="d0">Article 680</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 680 is a legal provision stating that a contract for a specific work terminates upon completion of the agreed work, and that if the work is renewable by nature and the contract continues, it is deemed impliedly renewed for the period necessary to perform the same work again.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 681">
  <data key="d0">Article 681</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 681 is a legal provision establishing the presumption that the performance of services is made for remuneration when such services are not customarily performed gratuitously or fall within the scope of the performer's profession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 682">
  <data key="d0">Article 682</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 682 is a legal provision governing the determination of salary when not specified in contracts or factory regulations, prescribing a hierarchy of applicable rates for similar work, trade custom, local custom, and judicial determination according to equity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 683">
  <data key="d0">Article 683</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 683 is a legal provision enumerating sums that form an integral part of an employee's salary for purposes of computing the attachable portion, including commissions, percentages on sales, cost of living allowances, gratuities, fidelity bonuses, and family allowances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 684">
  <data key="d0">Article 684</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 684 is a legal provision deeming tips to form part of salary only in industries where customary and subject to regulation, and recognizing tips as salary when collected in a common fund and distributed under employer supervision, including hotels and restaurants where salary may consist solely of tips and food.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 685">
  <data key="d0">Article 685</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 685 is a legal provision outlining the obligations of the worker, including performing work personally with reasonable care and obeying the employer's orders related to the agreed work provided such orders do not violate the contract, law, or public morals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concessionaire of Public Utility Service">
  <data key="d0">Concessionaire of Public Utility Service</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Concessionaire of Public Utility Service is the entity that operates a public utility service, potentially enjoying a de jure or de facto monopoly, and is bound to observe equality among consumers, apply legally prescribed rates, rectify rate irregularities, and may repudiate responsibility for service interruptions caused by force majeure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer">
  <data key="d0">Consumer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Consumer is a customer who receives services from the Concessionaire at rates collected in accordance with the conditions stipulated in the concession contract and applicable laws.&lt;SEP&gt;The Consumer is the user of public utility services who is entitled to strict equality in services and rates, may receive special rate treatment if conditions are met, and must tolerate short service interruptions for maintenance purposes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Authority">
  <data key="d0">Public Authority</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Public Authority is the body that lays down the rates applicable to contracts between the concessionaire and consumers, which have force of law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section II: Contracts of Service">
  <data key="d0">Section II: Contracts of Service</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section II: Contracts of Service is a chapter of the legal code containing provisions governing employment relationships, including contract formation, remuneration, duration, and the obligations of workers and employers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principle of Equality">
  <data key="d0">Principle of Equality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Principle of Equality is a legal doctrine requiring the concessionaire of a public utility to treat all consumers equally in services rendered and rates charged, while permitting general special treatment and prohibiting selective advantages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Worker">
  <data key="d0">Worker</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Worker is a party to a contract of service who undertakes to perform work under the supervision or control of an employer in exchange for remuneration, with obligations including personal performance and obedience to lawful orders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Employer">
  <data key="d0">Employer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Employer, also referred to as the master, is a party to a contract of service who supervises or controls the worker and undertakes to pay remuneration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commercial Representative">
  <data key="d0">Commercial Representative</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Commercial Representative is an intermediary who works under the orders and supervision of an employer, may be remunerated on a commission basis, and retains the right to commission on orders resulting from their efforts even after leaving service.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commercial Traveller">
  <data key="d0">Commercial Traveller</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Commercial Traveller is a type of intermediary working under employer supervision, remunerated potentially on commission, with similar post-employment commission rights as the commercial representative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Canvasser">
  <data key="d0">Canvasser</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Canvasser is an intermediary who works under employer supervision and may be remunerated on a commission basis, to whom contract of service provisions apply.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strike">
  <data key="d0">Strike</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A Strike is considered a fortuitous event under Article 673 if the concessionaire proves it occurred without fault and that replacement of strikers or avoidance of consequences was not possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assurance Agent">
  <data key="d0">Assurance Agent</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Assurance Agent is an intermediary who works under the orders and supervision of a master, to whom contract of service provisions apply even if remunerated on a commission basis or working for multiple employers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fortuitous Event">
  <data key="d0">Fortuitous Event</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Fortuitous Event is an unforeseen occurrence within the operation of a public utility service that could not have been anticipated or whose consequences could not have been avoided by vigilant management, serving as grounds for the concessionaire to repudiate responsibility for abnormal service interruptions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fair Competition">
  <data key="d0">Fair Competition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fair Competition is the natural competitive balance between parties that must not be disturbed by discriminatory practices of the concessionaire, as referenced in Article 670.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Laws Relating to Service">
  <data key="d0">Special Laws Relating to Service</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Special Laws Relating to Service are legislative enactments that take precedence over the general provisions of Section II on Contracts of Service and define the categories of workers to which those provisions do not apply.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract Of Pledge">
  <data key="d0">Contract Of Pledge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A possessory pledge that secures not only the capital of the debt but also, in the same rank, necessary preservation expenses, compensation for defect losses, contract and inscription costs, enforcement costs, and accrued interest, as defined under Article 1111.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extinguishment Of A Pledge">
  <data key="d0">Extinguishment Of A Pledge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The termination of pledge rights, occurring upon extinguishment of the secured debt, renunciation by the pledgee, union of pledge and ownership rights in one person, or loss or extinguishment of the pledged thing or right, as governed by Articles 1112 and 1113.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledge Of An Immovable">
  <data key="d0">Pledge Of An Immovable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of pledge (antichresis) involving immovable property, requiring both delivery of the pledged immovable to the pledgee and inscription of the contract for validity against third parties, governed by Articles 1114–1116.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledge Of A Movable">
  <data key="d0">Pledge Of A Movable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of pledge involving movable property, requiring a written contract with an established date adequately setting out the secured debt amount and pledged object for validity against third parties, governed by Articles 1117–1122.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Good Faith">
  <data key="d0">Good Faith</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Good Faith is a foundational legal principle requiring that parties act honestly, fairly, and without fraud in their legal dealings. At its core, it binds contracting parties not only to the express terms of their agreements but also to everything deemed a necessary sequel according to law, usage, and equity. Good faith operates as both a general standard of conduct and a specific legal state of mind applied across a wide range of civil law contexts.

In the realm of contractual obligations, good faith governs the performance of contracts and the handling of mistakes. A party who has committed a mistake cannot exploit it in a manner contrary to good faith, and the mistaken party remains bound by the intended contract if the other party is prepared to perform it. Good faith also serves as a legal condition enabling creditors and particular successors in title to avail themselves of a hidden contract or to establish the simulation of a contract by which they were prejudiced.

In property and possession law, good faith describes an honest belief in the legitimacy of one's possession at the time of acquisition. A possessor acting in good faith is one who is unaware of infringing upon another's right, and this state is always presumed by law. Good faith possession serves as a basis for acquiring ownership and fruits, limits liability for loss or deterioration, and affects the duration required for acquisitive prescription. Among multiple lessees competing for the same property, a lessee who in good faith transcribes a lease before another takes possession obtains preference. Good faith is also evaluated by courts when resolving disputes over mingled movables in accordance with the rules of equity.

Good faith extends significant protections to third parties and creditors. A third party who acquires a real right on an immovable in good faith is shielded from the supplement of price action. Similarly, a purchaser who acknowledges or compromises with a third party's claim while acting in good faith preserves warranty rights. In mortgage law, a mortgage constituted by an owner whose title is later annulled, resiliated, or abolished remains valid in favor of a mortgagee creditor who acted in good faith at the time of the mortgage's conclusion. Good faith also protects third parties acquiring rights during the interval between the extinguishment and revival of a mortgage, and is a required state of honest belief for a creditor seeking a judgment charge over immovable property.

In the context of unjust enrichment and payment, a good faith recipient of undue payment is bound only to return what was actually received, reflecting the law's recognition that honest conduct should limit the scope of restitutionary liability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledge Of Debts">
  <data key="d0">Pledge Of Debts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of pledge involving debt instruments, requiring notification to or acceptance by the debtor for validity, and possession of the debt title by the pledgee for validity against third parties, governed by Articles 1123–1129.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Privileged Rights">
  <data key="d0">Privileged Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Priority rights established by law for certain claims, introduced as Chapter IV of the legal code, granting a first-ranking priority to specified rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1111">
  <data key="d0">Article 1111</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a contract of pledge secures not only the capital debt but also necessary preservation expenses, compensation for defect losses, contract and inscription costs, enforcement costs, and accrued interest subject to Article 230.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1112">
  <data key="d0">Article 1112</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that a right of pledge is extinguished upon extinguishment of the secured debt and is revived if the cause of extinguishment disappears, without prejudice to third-party rights acquired in good faith during the interval.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1113">
  <data key="d0">Article 1113</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision enumerating causes of pledge extinguishment: renunciation by the pledgee with legal capacity, union of pledge and ownership rights in one person, and loss of the pledged thing or extinguishment of the pledged right.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1114">
  <data key="d0">Article 1114</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring inscription of the pledge contract, in addition to delivery of the immovable, for a pledge of an immovable to be valid against third parties, applying the mortgage inscription rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1115">
  <data key="d0">Article 1115</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision permitting a pledgee of an immovable to lease it to the pledgor while maintaining validity against third parties, with inscription or marginal notation requirements depending on when the lease is agreed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1116">
  <data key="d0">Article 1116</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision obliging the pledgee of an immovable to maintain it, pay necessary preservation expenses, annual taxes, and charges, with the right to deduct these from collected fruits; the pledgee may abandon the pledge to free himself of these obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1117">
  <data key="d0">Article 1117</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring a written contract with an established date, adequately specifying the secured debt amount and pledged object, for a pledge of a movable to be valid against third parties, with creditor rank fixed by the established date.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1118">
  <data key="d0">Article 1118</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision applying the possession rules of material movables and bearer securities to pledges of movables, protecting good faith pledgees even if the pledgor lacked disposal authority, and protecting good faith third-party holders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1120">
  <data key="d0">Article 1120</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision permitting the pledgor to apply to the Judge for authorization to sell the pledged thing at an advantageous price even before debt maturity, with the Judge fixing sale conditions and ordering deposit of the price.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1121">
  <data key="d0">Article 1121</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1121 is a legal provision referenced in Article 1129 that governs the sale or allocation of a pledged debt to the pledgee.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal provision granting the unpaid pledgee the right to apply to the Judge for authority to sell the pledged thing by public auction or at market value, or to appropriate it in payment of the debt at a value estimated by experts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1122">
  <data key="d0">Article 1122</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that the preceding pledge provisions apply unless incompatible with commercial laws, provisions relating to authorized lending institutions, or special laws and regulations governing pledge of movables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1123">
  <data key="d0">Article 1123</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring notification to or acceptance by the debtor for validity of a debt pledge, and possession of the debt title by the pledgee for validity against third parties, with pledge rank fixed by the date of notification or acceptance, as provided for in Article 305.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1124">
  <data key="d0">Article 1124</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing nominative bonds and bonds payable to order to be pledged through the special transfer procedure prescribed by law, provided the transfer is stated to be by way of pledge, without need for notification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1125">
  <data key="d0">Article 1125</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision prohibiting the pledge of debts that cannot be assigned or attached.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1126">
  <data key="d0">Article 1126</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision granting the pledgee the right to collect interest and periodic payments on pledged debts, offsetting collected amounts first against expenses, then interest, then capital, and obliging the pledgee to protect the pledged debt and promptly inform the pledgor of collections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1127">
  <data key="d0">Article 1127</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing the debtor of a pledged debt to raise against the pledgee defenses regarding the validity of the secured debt and defenses against the original creditor, to the same extent as in cases of debt assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1128">
  <data key="d0">Article 1128</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision requiring the debtor of a prematurely due pledged debt to discharge it to both pledgee and pledgor jointly, with the pledge transferring to the deposited amount, and obliging both to cooperate on investment and creation of a new pledge in favor of the pledgee.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1129">
  <data key="d0">Article 1129</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1129 establishes that if the pledged debt and the secured debt fall due, the unpaid pledgee may collect the pledged debt, demand its sale, or request its allocation in accordance with Article 1121, paragraph 2.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal provision permitting the unpaid pledgee, when both the pledged debt and secured debt fall due, to collect from the pledged debt up to the amount owed, or demand its sale or allocation in accordance with Article 1121 paragraph 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 305">
  <data key="d0">Article 305</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A referenced legal provision governing notification or acceptance procedures for debt pledges, cited in Article 1123.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495948</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section IV">
  <data key="d0">Section IV</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A section titled "Certain Kinds of Pledge" covering pledges of immovables (antichresis), movables, and debts, encompassing Articles 1114–1129.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Secured Debt">
  <data key="d0">Secured Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The debt guaranteed by a contract of pledge, whose extinguishment causes the pledge to be extinguished and whose revival revives the pledge, referenced throughout Articles 1111–1129.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Parties In Good Faith">
  <data key="d0">Third Parties In Good Faith</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Persons who acquire rights legally and in good faith over pledged property, whose rights are protected during the interval between extinguishment and revival of a pledge, or through possession of movables, as stated in Articles 1112, 1113, and 1118.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Debtor Of A Pledged Debt">
  <data key="d0">Debtor Of A Pledged Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original debtor of a debt that has been given in pledge, who may raise defenses against the pledgee, must discharge the debt jointly to the pledgee and pledgor if it falls due prematurely, and may be notified of the pledge as per Articles 1127 and 1128.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nominative Bonds">
  <data key="d0">Nominative Bonds</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Registered bonds that may be pledged in accordance with the special legal procedure prescribed for their transfer, provided the transfer is stated to be by way of pledge, as stated in Article 1124.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bonds Payable To Order">
  <data key="d0">Bonds Payable To Order</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Order bonds that may be pledged through the special legal transfer procedure, provided the transfer is stated to be by way of pledge, without need for notification, as stated in Article 1124.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bearer Securities">
  <data key="d0">Bearer Securities</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Securities held by the bearer, whose possession rules apply to the pledge of movables, referenced in Article 1118.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Movables">
  <data key="d0">Material Movables</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Tangible movable property whose possession rules apply to the pledge of movables, referenced in Article 1118.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Institutions Authorized To Lend Money On Pledge">
  <data key="d0">Institutions Authorized To Lend Money On Pledge</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Lending institutions authorized by law to accept pledges, whose special provisions take precedence over general pledge provisions as stated in Article 1122.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledged Thing">
  <data key="d0">Pledged Thing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The property (movable, immovable, or debt instrument) given as security under a contract of pledge, subject to preservation, inscription, and enforcement rules throughout the articles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warranty Against Dispossession">
  <data key="d0">Warranty Against Dispossession</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Warranty Against Dispossession is a legal guarantee obligating the vendor to protect the purchaser against loss of ownership of the thing sold to a person with superior legal title. It may be increased, restricted, or abolished by special agreement between the contracting parties, but any clause restricting it is void if the vendor fraudulently conceals the rights of a third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Dispossession">
  <data key="d0">Total Dispossession</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Total Dispossession is the complete loss of the thing sold to a person legally entitled to it, entitling the purchaser to claim the value of the thing at the time of dispossession, legal interest, the value of restored profits, useful and superfluous expenditures, litigation costs, and general compensation for losses and missed profits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Dispossession">
  <data key="d0">Partial Dispossession</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Partial Dispossession is the partial loss or encumbrance of the thing sold, where the purchaser may claim the same remedies as total dispossession if the loss is severe enough that the purchaser would not have entered the contract, provided the thing sold and its profits are returned; otherwise, the purchaser may only claim compensation for the loss sustained.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Thing Sold">
  <data key="d0">The Thing Sold</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The Thing Sold is the object or property that is the subject of the sale contract between the vendor and the purchaser. It must possess the qualities guaranteed by the vendor and be free of defects diminishing its value or usefulness at the time of delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judicial Sale">
  <data key="d0">Judicial Sale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Judicial Sale is a sale conducted through a judicial process in which no warranty against defects applies to the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Administrative Sale by Auction">
  <data key="d0">Administrative Sale by Auction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Administrative Sale by Auction is a sale conducted through an administrative auction process in which no warranty against defects applies to the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fraudulent Concealment">
  <data key="d0">Fraudulent Concealment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fraudulent Concealment is the intentional and fraudulent hiding of defects in the thing sold or of the rights of a third party by the vendor, which renders void any clause excluding or restricting the warranty and prevents the vendor from invoking the prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Interest">
  <data key="d0">Legal Interest</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal Interest is the statutory interest accruing on amounts owed by the vendor to the purchaser, such as the value of the thing sold at the time of dispossession or the refund of the price paid, calculated from the time of dispossession or from the date of formal summons.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 443">
  <data key="d0">Article 443</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 443 is a legal provision setting forth the claims available to the purchaser in case of total dispossession, including the value of the thing sold, legal interest, value of restored profits, useful and superfluous expenses, litigation costs, and general compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 444">
  <data key="d0">Article 444</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 444 is a legal provision governing the purchaser's remedies in case of partial dispossession or encumbrance of the thing sold, allowing full claims under Article 443 if the loss is severe, or limited compensation otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 447">
  <data key="d0">Article 447</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 447 is a legal provision defining the vendor's warranty liability for defects in the thing sold that diminish its value or usefulness, holding the vendor liable even for unknown defects but excluding defects known or discoverable by the purchaser through reasonable inspection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 459">
  <data key="d0">Article 459</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 459 is a legal provision establishing the vendor's right of retention, allowing the vendor to withhold the thing sold until payment of the price, and extending this right when the purchaser loses the benefit of the payment term under Article 273.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 273">
  <data key="d0">Article 273</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 273 is a referenced legal provision regarding the loss of the benefit of the payment term by the purchaser, referenced in Article 459 as a condition for the vendor's extended right of retention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 445">
  <data key="d0">Article 445</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 445 is a legal provision allowing the contracting parties to increase, restrict, or abolish the warranty against dispossession by special agreement. It presumes the vendor stipulated no warranty against an apparent or disclosed servitude, and renders void any clause restricting warranty if the vendor intentionally conceals the rights of a third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 446">
  <data key="d0">Article 446</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 446 is a legal provision establishing that, notwithstanding a clause excluding warranty, the vendor remains liable for any dispossession resulting from the vendor's own acts, and any contrary agreement is void. The vendor must also refund the value of the thing sold if dispossession results from a third party's act, unless the purchaser knew the grounds or purchased at own risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 448">
  <data key="d0">Article 448</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 448 is a legal provision exempting the vendor from liability for defects that are customarily tolerated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 449">
  <data key="d0">Article 449</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 449 is a legal provision requiring the purchaser to inspect the thing sold upon delivery and give notice of any covered defect to the vendor within a reasonable time, failing which the purchaser is deemed to have accepted the thing sold. For hidden defects, notice must be given immediately upon discovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 450">
  <data key="d0">Article 450</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 450 is a legal provision entitling the purchaser to bring a warranty action in accordance with Article 444 when timely notice of a defect has been given to the vendor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 451">
  <data key="d0">Article 451</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 451 is a legal provision confirming that a warranty action exists even if the thing sold has perished, regardless of the cause of destruction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 452">
  <data key="d0">Article 452</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 452 is a legal provision prescribing a one-year limitation period for warranty actions from the time of delivery, even if the defect is discovered later, unless the vendor agrees to a longer period. The vendor cannot invoke this prescription if fraudulent concealment is proven.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 453">
  <data key="d0">Article 453</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 453 is a legal provision permitting the contracting parties to increase, restrict, or abolish the warranty by specific agreement, while rendering void any clause abolishing or restricting the warranty if the vendor intentionally and fraudulently conceals defects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 455">
  <data key="d0">Article 455</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 455 is a legal provision governing the vendor's warranty of proper working for an agreed period, requiring the purchaser to notify the vendor of a defect within one month of its appearance and to commence an action within six months of notification, on pain of forfeiture of warranty rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 456">
  <data key="d0">Article 456</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 456 is a legal provision stating that, subject to contrary agreement or custom, the price is payable at the place of delivery, and if not payable at delivery, payment must be made at the purchaser's domicile on the due date.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 457">
  <data key="d0">Article 457</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 457 is a legal provision establishing that the price is payable at the time of delivery and granting the purchaser the right to retain the price when disturbed by a third party or threatened with dispossession, while allowing the vendor to demand payment upon supplying security. It also applies when the purchaser discovers a defect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 458">
  <data key="d0">Article 458</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 458 is a legal provision stating that the vendor is not entitled to legal interest on the price unless the purchaser has been placed in default by formal summons or the thing sold is productive of fruits and has been delivered. The purchaser acquires revenues and increases of the thing sold and bears its charges from the time of sale.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 460">
  <data key="d0">Article 460</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 460 is a legal provision assigning the risk of loss of the thing sold to the purchaser while the vendor exercises the right of retention, unless the loss results from an act of the vendor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495949</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 461">
  <data key="d0">Article 461</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 461 is a legal provision establishing that in the sale of commodities or movable effects with an agreed payment term, the sale is automatically dissolved without summons if the price is not paid on the due date, at the vendor's option and subject to contrary agreement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 462">
  <data key="d0">Article 462</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 462 is a legal provision assigning the costs of the deed of sale, stamp duties, transcription fees, and all other expenses to the purchaser in the absence of contrary agreement or usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judge">
  <data key="d0">Judge</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Judge (القاضي) is a judicial authority and officer vested with broad discretionary powers across numerous areas of civil law. As the primary judicial decision-maker, the Judge is responsible for deciding cases using various sources of law and determining the applicable law in matters involving persons of unknown or plural nationality.

In procedural and prescription matters, the Judge may not invoke prescription on his own initiative but may direct oaths in one-year prescription cases and presides over all legal proceedings related to prescription. The Judge also has authority to grant extensions of time, such as allowing administrators additional time to file inventory statements when justified by circumstances.

In the realm of contractual and obligations law, the Judge plays a central role. The Judge may grant additional time to debtors, reject rescission applications for minor breaches, and assess damages in light of prevailing circumstances while determining the appropriate method of compensation. The Judge is empowered to reduce excessively onerous contractual obligations arising from exceptional and unpredictable events, modify leonine conditions in contracts of adhesion, and reduce obligations to reasonable limits. Under Article 224, the Judge may also reduce the amount of damages agreed upon between contracting parties. Furthermore, the Judge fixes reasonable times for the term of obligations, determines the object of obligations when parties fail to elect, and adjudicates matters related to insolvency and conditional obligations.

Regarding damages, penalties, and natural obligations, the Judge determines the existence of natural obligations in the absence of legal provisions, orders specific performance, fixes damages, and authorizes enforcement actions. The Judge may increase penalties or reduce damages based on circumstances, including reducing damages for a voluntary agent's mistakes when justified. In cases of dispossession where the donor intentionally concealed the cause, the Judge may award equitable compensation to the donee.

In partnership and corporate matters, the Judge evaluates the seriousness of reasons for dissolution, appoints liquidators when partners cannot agree, and authorizes a partner's retirement from a partnership. The Judge also oversees wakf (waqf) property matters, specifically authorizing leases exceeding three years and supervising the Nazir's leasing activities, with the Nazir required to obtain the Judge's permission for such long-term leases.

The Judge exercises authority over judicial custody, appointing receivers, reducing excessive betting stakes, and making decisions regarding the termination and administration of custody arrangements. In employment and remuneration matters, the Judge fixes salaries in accordance with equity when no applicable rates or customs exist, determines the nature and extent of work to be performed, and may designate a trustworthy person to receive information necessary to verify the accuracy of a master's profit-sharing account on behalf of a worker.

In property and structural matters, the Judge is empowered under Article 962 to order the suspension or continuance of threatening works and to require adequate guarantees. Under Articles 859 and 860, the Judge may order the sale of a lower storey, order the execution of urgent repairs, and enforce structural obligations between storey owners. The Judge also determines the method of repayment of expenses in usufruct arrangements, may order the removal of property from a usufructuary, and may declare the extinction of a usufruct. In mortgage matters, the Judge fixes the portion of property to which a mortgage is transferred after partition and may order the cessation of acts that could result in loss or deterioration of mortgaged property. Additionally, under Article 1000, the Judge may be delegated by the President of the Court to draw up a hekr deed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Action of Revendication">
  <data key="d0">Action of Revendication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Action of Revendication is a legal action brought to recover ownership of the thing sold, the costs of which the purchaser may claim from the vendor under Article 443, provided the vendor was notified of the action in accordance with Article 440.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dissolution of Sale">
  <data key="d0">Dissolution of Sale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dissolution of Sale is a legal remedy that terminates the sale contract, mentioned in Articles 443 and 461 as an alternative basis for the purchaser's action against the vendor, and as the automatic consequence of non-payment in commodity sales.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Annulment of Sale">
  <data key="d0">Annulment of Sale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Annulment of Sale is a legal remedy that voids the sale contract, mentioned in Article 443 as an alternative basis for the purchaser's action against the vendor, distinct from warranty claims for dispossession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proper Working Warranty">
  <data key="d0">Proper Working Warranty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Proper Working Warranty is a specific type of warranty under Article 455 whereby the vendor guarantees the functionality of the thing sold for a specified period, subject to the purchaser's obligation to notify defects within one month and commence an action within six months of notification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Notice of Defect">
  <data key="d0">Notice of Defect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Notice of Defect is the purchaser's obligation under Articles 449 and 455 to inform the vendor of any defect discovered in the thing sold within a reasonable time or within one month, respectively, failing which the purchaser is deemed to have accepted the thing sold and forfeits warranty rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commodities and Movable Effects">
  <data key="d0">Commodities and Movable Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Commodities and Movable Effects are categories of movable property the sale of which is subject to automatic dissolution without summons if the price is not paid on the due date, as provided under Article 461.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Founder (Waqif)">
  <data key="d0">Founder (Waqif)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Founder is the person who established the wakf and may, if serving as Nazir, lease wakf property for more than three years without the judge's authority.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sole Beneficiary">
  <data key="d0">Sole Beneficiary</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The sole beneficiary is the single person entitled to the benefits of the wakf, who may also serve as Nazir with the privilege of leasing wakf property beyond three years without judicial authorization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loan for Use (Ariyah)">
  <data key="d0">Loan for Use (Ariyah)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A loan for use is a contract by which the lender undertakes to hand over to the borrower, without valuable consideration, a non-consumable thing for use during a specific time or for a specific purpose, to be restituted after use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lender (Mu'ir)">
  <data key="d0">Lender (Mu'ir)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The lender is the party in a loan for use who delivers a non-consumable thing to the borrower and must reimburse necessary preservation expenses, but does not generally warrant against dispossession or hidden defects unless agreed or deliberately concealed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Borrower (Musta'ir)">
  <data key="d0">Borrower (Musta'ir)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The borrower is the party in a loan for use who receives a non-consumable thing, must use it according to the contract or its nature, bears normal maintenance expenses, and must restitute the thing after use with due care.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract for Work (Muqawalah)">
  <data key="d0">Contract for Work (Muqawalah)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract for work is an agreement where one contracting party undertakes to perform a piece of work or service in consideration for remuneration which the other party undertakes to pay.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractor (Muqawil)">
  <data key="d0">Contractor (Muqawil)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The contractor is the party who undertakes to perform work or a service, may supply materials or labor alone, and is responsible for the quality of materials and proper execution of the work.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Master of the Work (Rabb al-Amal)">
  <data key="d0">Master of the Work (Rabb al-Amal)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The master of the work is the party who commissions the work, pays the remuneration, may supply materials, and is entitled to receive the completed work.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architect (Muhandis Mi'mari)">
  <data key="d0">Architect (Muhandis Mi'mari)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The architect is a professional who prepares building plans and may supervise their execution, bearing joint and several liability with the contractor for ten years for structural defects, or limited liability for defects in plans alone if not supervising execution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subcontractor">
  <data key="d0">Subcontractor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The subcontractor is a contractor working under the main contractor, against whom the main contractor may have rights of recourse as stated in Article 651.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 634">
  <data key="d0">Article 634</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 634 provides that general lease provisions apply to wakf leases insofar as they are not incompatible with the preceding special wakf rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 635">
  <data key="d0">Article 635</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 635 defines a loan for use as a contract for gratuitous lending of a non-consumable thing for a specific time or purpose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 636">
  <data key="d0">Article 636</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 636 obligates the lender to deliver the thing lent in its condition at the time of contract conclusion and to leave the borrower in possession during the loan period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 637">
  <data key="d0">Article 637</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 637 requires the lender to reimburse the borrower for necessary preservation expenses, while useful expenses follow the rules for bad-faith possessors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 638">
  <data key="d0">Article 638</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 638 limits the lender's liability for dispossession and hidden defects unless the lender deliberately concealed them or gave an express warranty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 639">
  <data key="d0">Article 639</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 639 restricts the borrower to using the thing lent only in the manner prescribed by contract, nature, or custom, and prohibits assigning use to third parties without the lender's authority.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 640">
  <data key="d0">Article 640</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 640 provides that the borrower bears normal maintenance expenses without right of refund, and may remove additions made to the thing lent if restored to original condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 641">
  <data key="d0">Article 641</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 641 requires the borrower to exercise the same care as for personal property but not less than a reasonable person, and holds the borrower liable for loss even from fortuitous events if personal property was preferentially saved.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 642">
  <data key="d0">Article 642</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 642 requires the borrower to restitute the thing lent at the end of the loan in its current state at the place it was received, absent contrary agreement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 643">
  <data key="d0">Article 643</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 643 governs termination of the loan for use upon expiration of the agreed term, completion of purpose, or at the lender's demand if no term is fixed, and permits early restitution by the borrower unless prejudicial to the lender.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 644">
  <data key="d0">Article 644</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 644 allows the lender to terminate the loan for use at any time if the lender has an urgent unforeseen need, the borrower misuses the thing, or the borrower becomes insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 645">
  <data key="d0">Article 645</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 645 provides that a loan for use ends with the death of the borrower unless otherwise agreed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 646">
  <data key="d0">Article 646</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 646 defines a contract for work as one party's undertaking to perform work or service for remuneration to be paid by the other party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 647">
  <data key="d0">Article 647</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 647 allows the contractor to supply labor only or both labor and materials, with the master responsible for materials in the former case.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 648">
  <data key="d0">Article 648</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 648 makes the contractor responsible for and warrants the good quality of materials when the contractor supplies them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 649">
  <data key="d0">Article 649</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 649 requires the contractor to care for master-supplied materials, use them with technical skill, account for their use, and refund the value of materials rendered unfit due to neglect or lack of skill.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 650">
  <data key="d0">Article 650</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 650 allows the master to demand resiliation of the contract or assign the work to another contractor at the first contractor's cost if defective performance is not corrected within a reasonable period, or immediately if correction is impossible.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 651">
  <data key="d0">Article 651</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 651 imposes joint and several liability on the architect and contractor for ten years for total or partial demolition of constructions or permanent works, including defects endangering solidity, running from the date of delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 652">
  <data key="d0">Article 652</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 652 limits the architect's liability to defects resulting from plans when the architect only prepares plans without supervising execution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 653">
  <data key="d0">Article 653</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 653 renders void any clause seeking to exclude or restrict the warranty of the architect and the contractor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 654">
  <data key="d0">Article 654</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 654 prescribes warranty actions after three years from the date of destruction or discovery of defects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 209">
  <data key="d0">Article 209</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 209 is a referenced legal provision governing the handing over of works to a replacement contractor at the original contractor's cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Year Lease Limit">
  <data key="d0">Three-Year Lease Limit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The three-year lease limit is the maximum duration for which the Nazir may lease wakf property without judicial authorization, applicable even to successive contracts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ten-Year Warranty (Decennial Liability)">
  <data key="d0">Ten-Year Warranty (Decennial Liability)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ten-year warranty is the period during which the architect and contractor are jointly and severally liable for total or partial demolition of constructions and defects endangering solidity, running from delivery of the works.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section II: Loan for Use">
  <data key="d0">Section II: Loan for Use</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section II covers the legal provisions governing loans for use, including obligations of the lender and borrower, and termination rules, spanning Articles 635 through 645.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section I: Contracts for Work">
  <data key="d0">Section I: Contracts for Work</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section I covers contracts for work and concessions of public utility services, including obligations of the contractor and master, spanning Articles 646 through 654.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter III: Contracts for the Hire of Services">
  <data key="d0">Chapter III: Contracts for the Hire of Services</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter III is the legal chapter covering contracts for the hire of services, including contracts for work and concessions of public utility services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 655">
  <data key="d0">Article 655</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 655 provides that once the contractor has completed the work and placed it at the disposal of the master, the master must take delivery of it as soon as possible in accordance with customary practice. If the master refuses without lawful cause to take delivery despite being summoned by formal notice, the work is deemed to have been delivered to the master.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Will (الوصية)">
  <data key="d0">Will (الوصية)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal instrument for transferring property upon death, governed by Mohammedan Law and specific legislation, as established in Articles 915–917 of the civil code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Testator (الموصى)">
  <data key="d0">Testator (الموصى)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who makes a testamentary disposition or will; the division made by the testator becomes irrevocable upon the testator's death.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heirs (الورثة)">
  <data key="d0">Heirs (الورثة)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Persons entitled to inherit from a deceased person's estate under the rules of inheritance, including contingent heirs who may predecease the deceased.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Estate (التركة)">
  <data key="d0">Estate (التركة)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The total property and obligations left by a deceased person, subject to division, debts, and winding-up procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohammedan Law (الشريعة الإسلامية)">
  <data key="d0">Mohammedan Law (الشريعة الإسلامية)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Islamic legal system that governs wills alongside statutory laws on wills, as referenced in Article 915.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Division by Disposition (Mortis Causa)">
  <data key="d0">Division by Disposition (Mortis Causa)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method of dividing property that takes effect upon death, which may always be revoked during the testator's lifetime and becomes irrevocable upon death, as governed by Articles 909–913.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rules of Inheritance (قواعد الميراث)">
  <data key="d0">Rules of Inheritance (قواعد الميراث)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal rules determining how property not included in a division devolves in common upon heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accession (الالتصاق)">
  <data key="d0">Accession (الالتصاق)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal mechanism for acquiring property through natural or artificial attachment, covering both immovable and movable property as addressed in Articles 918–931.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accession to Immovable Property (الالتصاق بالعقار)">
  <data key="d0">Accession to Immovable Property (الالتصاق بالعقار)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rules governing ownership of buildings, plantations, alluvium, and other works on land, including rights and obligations of land owners and third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accession to Movable Property (الالتصاق بالمنقول)">
  <data key="d0">Accession to Movable Property (الالتصاق بالمنقول)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rules governing ownership when movable properties belonging to different owners become mingled and cannot be separated without deterioration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alluvium">
  <data key="d0">Alluvium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Land formed gradually and imperceptibly by a river through sediment deposits, belonging to riparian owners under Article 918.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="River">
  <data key="d0">River</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A natural watercourse that creates alluvium for riparian owners and may displace or uncover land and form islands in its channel.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sea">
  <data key="d0">Sea</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A body of water that may cover or uncover land; land uncovered by the sea belongs to the State under Article 919.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lake and Pond (البحيرات والبرك)">
  <data key="d0">Lake and Pond (البحيرات والبرك)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Bodies of still water whose adjoining land owners do not acquire ownership of land uncovered by the retreat of these waters under Article 920.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Creditors of the Estate (دائنو التركة)">
  <data key="d0">Creditors of the Estate (دائنو التركة)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Persons to whom the estate owes debts; unsecured creditors may take action on alienated immovable property of the estate if their claims are recorded in accordance with the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Party (أجنبي)">
  <data key="d0">Third Party (أجنبي)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person other than the land owner who may carry out works or constructions on land belonging to another, with varying rights depending on good faith and consent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Land Owner (مالك الأرض)">
  <data key="d0">Land Owner (مالك الأرض)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The owner of land who holds presumptive ownership of buildings, plantations, and works above or below the ground, and has specific rights and obligations regarding constructions by third parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Court (المحكمة)">
  <data key="d0">Court (المحكمة)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The judicial body that resolves disputes related to accession, mingled movables, and encroachment, deciding matters according to rules of equity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract (العقد)">
  <data key="d0">Contract (العقد)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method of acquiring ownership and other real rights in movable and immovable property, as referenced in Article 932.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 909">
  <data key="d0">Article 909</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that division by disposition (mortis causa) may always be revoked and becomes irrevocable on the death of the testator.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 910">
  <data key="d0">Article 910</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that property not included in a division devolves in common on the heirs in accordance with the rules of inheritance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 911">
  <data key="d0">Article 911</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that if a contingent heir predeceases the deceased, the divided part allotted to that heir devolves in common on the other heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 912">
  <data key="d0">Article 912</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that general rules of division, except those relating to lesion, apply to division by disposition (mortis causa).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 913">
  <data key="d0">Article 913</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing any heir to call for a division of the estate in accordance with Article 895 when estate debts are not included or creditors do not agree.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 914">
  <data key="d0">Article 914</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision granting unsecured creditors the right to take action on alienated or charged immovable property of the estate if their claims are recorded by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 915">
  <data key="d0">Article 915</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that wills are governed by Mohammedan Law and laws on wills.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 916">
  <data key="d0">Article 916</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision deeming legal dispositions made during a terminal illness with the object of making a gift as testamentary dispositions governed by rules applicable to wills.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 917">
  <data key="d0">Article 917</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision deeming a disposition to an heir as testamentary when the disposer retains possession and enjoyment of the property during his lifetime.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 922">
  <data key="d0">Article 922</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision establishing the presumption that all buildings, plantations, and works on land were carried out by the land owner at the owner's expense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 923">
  <data key="d0">Article 923</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that constructions made with another's materials become the land owner's property if removal would cause serious damage or if recovery proceedings are not commenced within one year.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 924">
  <data key="d0">Article 924</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision granting the land owner the right to demand removal or retention of works erected by a third party who knew the land was not theirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 925">
  <data key="d0">Article 925</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision addressing works carried out by a third party in good faith, limiting the land owner's remedies to payment of value or increased land value.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 928">
  <data key="d0">Article 928</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision allowing a court to compel an adjoining land owner to transfer ownership of encroached land to a neighbor who built in good faith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 931">
  <data key="d0">Article 931</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that when two movables of different owners are mingled inseparably, the court decides the matter according to rules of equity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 932">
  <data key="d0">Article 932</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that ownership and other real rights in movable and immovable property transfer by contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Illness Immediately Preceding Death (مرض الموت)">
  <data key="d0">Illness Immediately Preceding Death (مرض الموت)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A terminal illness during which any legal disposition made with the object of gifting is deemed a testamentary disposition governed by the rules applicable to wills, as established in Article 916.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Good Faith (حسن النية)">
  <data key="d0">Good Faith (حسن النية)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal principle affecting the rights and remedies available to land owners and third parties in construction and accession disputes, referenced in Articles 925, 928, and 931.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Riparian Owners (الملاك المجاورين)">
  <data key="d0">Riparian Owners (الملاك المجاورين)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Owners of land adjoining a river who acquire ownership of alluvium formed gradually and imperceptibly by the river under Article 918.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Islands (الجزر)">
  <data key="d0">Islands (الجزر)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Land formations created in a river's channel whose ownership is regulated by special laws under Article 921.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Light Constructions (المنشآت الصغيرة)">
  <data key="d0">Light Constructions (المنشآت الصغيرة)</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Temporary structures such as sheds, shops, and shelters erected on another's land without the intention of permanent maintenance, which remain the property of the person who erected them under Article 929.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Laws on Wills (القوانين الصادرة في شأنها)">
  <data key="d0">Laws on Wills (القوانين الصادرة في شأنها)</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Statutory legislation governing wills alongside Mohammedan Law, as referenced in Article 915.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Debts of the Estate (ديون التركة)">
  <data key="d0">Debts of the Estate (ديون التركة)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Financial obligations of a deceased person's estate that must be addressed during division; if not included in the division or if creditors do not agree, any heir may call for a new division under Article 913.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Materials (المواد)">
  <data key="d0">Materials (المواد)</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Building materials belonging to a person other than the land owner, used in constructions on the land, whose ownership and recovery rights are governed by Articles 923 and 930.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buildings, Plantations, and Works (بناء أو غراس أو منشآت)">
  <data key="d0">Buildings, Plantations, and Works (بناء أو غراس أو منشآت)</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Constructions, plantations, and other structures existing above or below ground, presumed to have been carried out by the land owner at the owner's expense under Article 922.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 918">
  <data key="d0">Article 918</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that alluvium formed gradually and imperceptibly by a river belongs to the riparian owners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 919">
  <data key="d0">Article 919</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that land uncovered by the sea belongs to the State and no one may encroach upon the sea shore except to restore covered property boundaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 920">
  <data key="d0">Article 920</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that owners of lands adjoining still waters such as lakes and ponds do not acquire ownership of uncovered land nor lose ownership of overflowed land.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 921">
  <data key="d0">Article 921</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that ownership of land displaced or uncovered by a river and of islands formed in its channel is regulated by special laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 926">
  <data key="d0">Article 926</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that when a third party carries out works with permission of the land owner, the land owner cannot demand removal and must pay one of two prescribed amounts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 927">
  <data key="d0">Article 927</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision referring to Article 982 for the payment of compensation addressed in Articles 924, 925, and 926.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 929">
  <data key="d0">Article 929</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that light constructions such as sheds, shops, and shelters erected on another's land without permanent intent belong to the person who erected them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 930">
  <data key="d0">Article 930</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision stating that when a third party carries out works with another's materials, the material owner cannot claim restitution but may seek compensation from both the third party and the land owner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 895">
  <data key="d0">Article 895</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision referenced in Article 913 as the basis for division of an estate when debts are unresolved or creditors do not agree.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 982">
  <data key="d0">Article 982</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal provision referenced in Article 927 governing the payment of compensation for works addressed in the accession provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Egyptian Nationality">
  <data key="d0">Egyptian Nationality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Egyptian nationality is a legal status governed by a special law, determining who is considered a national of Egypt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Personality">
  <data key="d0">Legal Personality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal personality commences from the time a child is born alive and ends at death. The law also determines the rights of a child en ventre de sa mere.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Domicile">
  <data key="d0">Domicile</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A domicile is the place where a person habitually resides. A person may have more than one domicile at the same time or may have none.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moslem Law">
  <data key="d0">Moslem Law</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Moslem law (Islamic law) is applied to cases involving missing persons and absent persons in the absence of special laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Consumable Things">
  <data key="d0">Consumable Things</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Consumable things are those whose utility consists in their consumption or disposal by reason of their destined use, including things destined for sale in commercial establishments.&lt;SEP&gt;Consumable things are those whose utility, by reason of their destined use, consists in their consumption or disposal. All things destined for sale in commercial establishments are deemed consumable.&lt;SEP&gt;Items that are consumed through use, which a usufructuary who has provided security may use provided they are replaced upon termination of the usufruct.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fungibles">
  <data key="d0">Fungibles</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fungibles are things that can be replaced by one another in payment and are customarily estimated in trade by number, measure, volume, or weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungibles are things which can be replaced one by another in payment and which are customarily estimated in trade by number, measure, volume or weight.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wakfs">
  <data key="d0">Wakfs</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Wakfs are recognized as one of the categories of juristic persons under the Egyptian Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Missing Person">
  <data key="d0">Missing Person</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A missing person is subject to provisions contained in special laws, or in the absence of such laws, Moslem law. The domicile of a missing person is the domicile of the legal representative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495952</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absent Person">
  <data key="d0">Absent Person</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An absent person is subject to provisions contained in special laws, or in the absence of such laws, Moslem law. The domicile of an absent person is the domicile of the legal representative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Birth And Death Registers">
  <data key="d0">Birth And Death Registers</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Birth and death registers are official records used to establish birth and death, regulated by a special law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Family">
  <data key="d0">Family</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The family of a person is composed of relatives. Persons having a common ascendant are deemed to be relatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direct Lineal Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Direct Lineal Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Direct lineal relationship is the relationship existing between ascendants and descendants, calculated by ascending to the common ancestor and counting each relative excluding the common ancestor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Collateral Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Collateral Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Collateral relationship is the relationship existing between persons who have a common ascendant without one of them being a descendant of the other, calculated by ascending from one descendant to the common ancestor then descending to the other.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Domicile">
  <data key="d0">Special Domicile</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A special domicile may be elected for the performance of a specific legal act, and the election must be evidenced by writing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Guardianship">
  <data key="d0">Natural Guardianship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural guardianship is one of the legal regimes governing persons deprived of full or partial legal capacity, subject to conditions and rules laid down by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Guardianship">
  <data key="d0">Legal Guardianship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal guardianship is one of the legal regimes governing persons deprived of full or partial legal capacity, subject to conditions and rules laid down by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curatorship">
  <data key="d0">Curatorship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Curatorship is a legal regime governing persons deprived of full or partial legal capacity, subject to conditions and rules laid down by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Religious Groups And Communities">
  <data key="d0">Religious Groups And Communities</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Religious groups and communities that the state has recognized are classified as juristic persons under Egyptian law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commercial And Civil Corporations">
  <data key="d0">Commercial And Civil Corporations</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Commercial and civil corporations are recognized as juristic persons under the Egyptian Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Associations And Foundations">
  <data key="d0">Associations And Foundations</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Associations and foundations created in accordance with the provisions of the Egyptian Civil Code are classified as juristic persons.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First Name And Family Name">
  <data key="d0">First Name And Family Name</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Every person must have a first name and a family name, and the family name of a person is bestowed upon his children. Acquisition and change of family name are governed by special legislation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Personal Liberty">
  <data key="d0">Personal Liberty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Personal liberty is a fundamental right that no person can renounce, as established in Article 49.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Personality Rights">
  <data key="d0">Personality Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Personality rights are rights inherent in a person's personality. A person whose personality rights have been unlawfully infringed shall have the right to demand cessation of the infringement and compensation for any damage sustained.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Name Rights">
  <data key="d0">Name Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Name rights protect a person whose right to the use of his name is unlawfully disputed or whose name is unlawfully used by another, entitling that person to demand cessation and compensation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trade Or Profession Domicile">
  <data key="d0">Trade Or Profession Domicile</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The place where a person exercises a trade or profession is considered as his domicile as regards matters carried on in connection with such trade or profession, as stated in Article 41.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Person Under Legal Disability">
  <data key="d0">Person Under Legal Disability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A person under legal disability is a category alongside minors, missing persons, and absent persons whose domicile is the domicile of the legal representative. A person in a similar legal position to a minor who has attained eighteen years shall have a special domicile for acts the law considers capable of performing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Things Outside The Ambit Of Trade">
  <data key="d0">Things Outside The Ambit Of Trade</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Things outside the ambit of trade by their very nature are things that cannot be objects of exclusive possession. Things outside the ambit of trade by law are things which cannot be objects of proprietary rights, as stated in Article 81.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rights In Non-Material Things">
  <data key="d0">Rights In Non-Material Things</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rights in respect of non-material things are regulated by special laws, as stated in Article 86.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Provinces (Mudirias)">
  <data key="d0">Provinces (Mudirias)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Provinces (mudirias) are administrative divisions of the state recognized as juristic persons under Egyptian law, along with towns and villages, in accordance with conditions fixed by law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proprietary Rights">
  <data key="d0">Proprietary Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Proprietary rights are rights over things that may be the object of such rights, provided the things are not outside the ambit of trade by their nature or by virtue of law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pledged Debt">
  <data key="d0">Pledged Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Pledged Debt is a debt used as security for another debt, which the pledgee may collect, sell, or allocate upon maturity if unpaid, as governed by Article 1129.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter IV: Privileged Rights">
  <data key="d0">Chapter IV: Privileged Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter IV of the legal code deals with privileged rights (حقوق الامتياز), comprising general provisions and the classification of different kinds of privileges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section I: General Provisions">
  <data key="d0">Section I: General Provisions</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section I of Chapter IV covers general provisions governing privileged rights, including the definition, ranking, scope, and enforceability of privileges, as established in Articles 1130 through 1136.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Privileged Right">
  <data key="d0">Privileged Right</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Privileged Right (privilege) is a right of preference granted by law to a particular right by reason of its quality, as defined in Article 1130. No right is privileged except by virtue of a provision of the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="General Privileges">
  <data key="d0">General Privileges</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">General Privileges are privileged rights that extend to all movable and immovable property of the debtor, as stated in Article 1132. They are not subject to publication and do not give a right of tracing property into the hands of subsequent holders, even over immovables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Privileges">
  <data key="d0">Special Privileges</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Special Privileges are privileged rights limited to a specific movable or immovable property only, as defined in Article 1132.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State Treasury">
  <data key="d0">State Treasury</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The State Treasury (الخزانة العامة) is the government financial entity to which sums are due for taxes, duties, and other dues. Privileges securing sums due to the State Treasury over immovables are not subject to publication and rank prior to any other privilege over immovables or mortgages and prior to general privileges, as established in Articles 1134 and 1139.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holder in Good Faith">
  <data key="d0">Holder in Good Faith</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Holder in Good Faith (حائز حسن النية) is a person who possesses a movable without knowledge of any defect in title. A privilege cannot be set up against a holder in good faith of a movable, as established in Article 1133.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Costs of Legal Proceedings">
  <data key="d0">Costs of Legal Proceedings</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Costs of Legal Proceedings (المصروفات القضائية) are expenses incurred in the common interest of all creditors for the preservation and sale of the debtor's property. They have the highest-ranking privilege and are payable in priority to any other claim, including privileged or mortgage-secured claims, as established in Article 1138.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1130">
  <data key="d0">Article 1130</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1130 defines a privilege as a right of preference granted by law to a particular right by reason of its quality, and states that no right is privileged except by virtue of a provision of the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1131">
  <data key="d0">Article 1131</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1131 provides that the rank of a privilege is fixed by law, that unranked privileged rights rank after all privileges provided for in the chapter, and that privileged rights of the same rank are paid rateably.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1132">
  <data key="d0">Article 1132</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1132 states that general privileges extend to all movable and immovable property of the debtor, while special privileges are limited to a specific movable or immovable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1133">
  <data key="d0">Article 1133</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1133 provides that a privilege cannot be set up against a holder in good faith of a movable, deems the lessor and hotel proprietor as holders for purposes of the article, and allows a creditor to apply for judicial custody of movables if misappropriation is feared.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1134">
  <data key="d0">Article 1134</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1134 makes mortgage provisions applicable to privileged rights over immovable property, while providing exceptions for general privileges and State Treasury privileges, which are not subject to publication and rank prior to all other immovable privileges or mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1135">
  <data key="d0">Article 1135</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1135 provides that provisions applying to the loss or deterioration of mortgaged property apply also to privileges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1136">
  <data key="d0">Article 1136</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1136 states that in the absence of a contrary provision, privileges are extinguished in the same way and in accordance with the same rules as a mortgage or a pledge.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section II: Kinds of Privileges">
  <data key="d0">Section II: Kinds of Privileges</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section II of Chapter IV enumerates the specific kinds of privileged rights, including general privileges and special privileges over movables, as detailed in Articles 1137 through 1144.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1138">
  <data key="d0">Article 1138</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1138 grants a privilege over the price of property for costs of legal proceedings incurred in the common interest of all creditors for preservation and sale, payable in priority to any other claim.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1139">
  <data key="d0">Article 1139</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1139 grants a privilege to sums due to the State Treasury for taxes, duties, and other dues, payable out of the proceeds of charged property before all other rights except costs of legal proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1140">
  <data key="d0">Article 1140</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1140 grants a privilege over a movable for expenses incurred for its preservation and necessary repairs, ranking after costs of legal proceedings and sums due to the State Treasury, with inter se ranking in inverse order of dates incurred.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1141">
  <data key="d0">Article 1141</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1141 grants a privilege over all of the debtor's property for wages due to servants, clerks, and workmen for the last six months, sums due for food and clothing supplied to the debtor and dependents for the last six months, and alimony due to family members for the last six months.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1142">
  <data key="d0">Article 1142</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1142 grants a privilege over the crop for sums disbursed for seeds, manure, fertilizers, insecticides, cultivation, and harvesting, all of the same rank, and a similar privilege over agricultural implements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1143">
  <data key="d0">Article 1143</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1143 grants a lessor's privilege over attachable movables and crops on the leased property for rent and other sums due under the lease, enforceable for two years, with provisions for sub-lessees and removal of goods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1144">
  <data key="d0">Article 1144</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1144 grants a hotel proprietor a privilege over the effects brought by a traveller into the hotel for sums due for accommodation, provisions, and expenses, with the same rank as the lessor's privilege.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 1144 establishes that sums due to hotel proprietors by a traveller for accommodation, food, and expenses are secured by a privilege over the effects brought by the traveller to the hotel or its annexes, and that the hotel proprietor may object to removal of such effects if unpaid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1137">
  <data key="d0">Article 1137</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1137 is a transitional provision stating that, in addition to the privileges established by special provisions of the law, the rights enumerated in the following articles are privileged.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compensation (Set Off)">
  <data key="d0">Compensation (Set Off)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Compensation (set off) is a legal mechanism allowing a debtor to extinguish mutual debts with a creditor, provided both debts are for money or fungibles of like nature and quality, are not in dispute, and are due and enforceable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Release Of Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Release Of Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Release of obligation is a voluntary discharge of a debtor by the creditor, completed upon reaching the debtor's knowledge and subject to the rules governing gifts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Impossibility Of Performance">
  <data key="d0">Impossibility Of Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Impossibility of performance is a legal ground for extinguishing an obligation when the debtor establishes that performance has become impossible due to causes beyond the debtor's control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Original Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Original Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The original obligation is the pre-existing obligation that is extinguished and replaced by a new obligation through novation, along with its accessories.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Obligation">
  <data key="d0">New Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The new obligation is the replacement obligation created through novation, which must be free from grounds of nullity and may or may not inherit the securities of the original obligation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Current Account">
  <data key="d0">Current Account</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A current account is a financial arrangement in which mere entry of a debt does not constitute novation, but fixing and agreeing the balance does.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Promissory Note">
  <data key="d0">Promissory Note</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A promissory note is a written debt instrument whose subscription in respect of a pre-existing debt does not, by itself, constitute novation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 353">
  <data key="d0">Article 353</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 353 provides that novation can only be effected if both the original and new obligations are free from grounds of nullity, and if the original obligation arises from a voidable contract, the new obligation must aim to confirm and replace it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 354">
  <data key="d0">Article 354</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 354 states that novation is not presumed and must be expressly agreed or clearly inferred from circumstances, and does not result merely from changes to the date, place, or mode of performance, or from modifications to securities or interest rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 355">
  <data key="d0">Article 355</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 355 provides that mere entry of a debt in current account does not effect novation, but fixing and agreeing the balance does, with special securities maintained unless otherwise agreed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 356">
  <data key="d0">Article 356</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 356 establishes that novation extinguishes the original obligation with its accessories and substitutes a new obligation, with securities not transferred unless provided by law, agreement, or the intention of the parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 357">
  <data key="d0">Article 357</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 357 sets out conditions for transferring real securities to the new obligation, differing based on whether novation results from a change of debt, debtor, or creditor, and requires compliance with registration provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 358">
  <data key="d0">Article 358</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 358 provides that suretyship, whether real, personal, or joint and several, is only transferred to the new obligation with the consent of the sureties and co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 359">
  <data key="d0">Article 359</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 359 defines delegation as occurring when a debtor procures the creditor's acceptance of a third party who undertakes to pay in the debtor's stead, without requiring a prior debt between the debtor and the third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 360">
  <data key="d0">Article 360</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 360 provides that delegation with an agreement to substitute a new obligation constitutes novation by change of debtor, liberating the original debtor provided the new obligation is valid and the new debtor is not insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 361">
  <data key="d0">Article 361</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 361 states that the new debtor's obligation towards the creditor is valid even if the obligation towards the original debtor is void or contestable, subject to a right of recourse against the original debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 362">
  <data key="d0">Article 362</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 362 grants the debtor a right to compensation of mutual debts with the creditor, provided both are for money or fungibles of like nature, are not in dispute, and are due and enforceable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 363">
  <data key="d0">Article 363</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 363 allows compensation even when the places of payment differ, but requires the debtor to compensate the creditor for any resulting loss.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 364">
  <data key="d0">Article 364</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 364 specifies exceptions to compensation, including claims for restitution of unjustly deprived property, deposited or lent items, and rights not liable to attachment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 366">
  <data key="d0">Article 366</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 366 allows compensation even after the prescription period has expired, provided the period had not expired when compensation first became possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 367">
  <data key="d0">Article 367</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 367 prohibits compensation to the detriment of rights acquired by third parties, including after seizure of property by a third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 368">
  <data key="d0">Article 368</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 368 provides that a debtor who consented to assignment of debt without reserve cannot set up compensation against the assignee, while a debtor who merely received notification of assignment may still do so.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 369">
  <data key="d0">Article 369</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 369 provides that a debtor who paid despite having a right to compensation cannot invoke securities to the prejudice of third parties, unless unaware of the right.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 370">
  <data key="d0">Article 370</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 370 establishes that merger extinguishes the debt when creditor and debtor qualities unite in one person, with revival if the cause disappears retroactively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 371">
  <data key="d0">Article 371</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 371 provides that obligations are extinguished by voluntary release of the debtor by the creditor, effective upon the debtor's knowledge and void if refused.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 372">
  <data key="d0">Article 372</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 372 states that release is subject to the rules governing gifts and requires no special form even for obligations originally conditional on a special form.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 373">
  <data key="d0">Article 373</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 373 provides that an obligation is extinguished if the debtor proves performance has become impossible due to causes beyond the debtor's control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 374">
  <data key="d0">Article 374</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 374 sets the general term of extinctive prescription at fifteen years, subject to special provisions and exceptions in the law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 375">
  <data key="d0">Article 375</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 375 prescribes a five-year prescription period for periodic rights such as rent, interest, revenues, wages, and pensions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignment Of Debt">
  <data key="d0">Assignment Of Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Assignment of debt is a legal mechanism whereby a creditor transfers the creditor's right to a third party (assignee), with implications for the debtor's right to set up compensation depending on whether the debtor consented without reserve or merely received notification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right Of Recourse">
  <data key="d0">Right Of Recourse</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Right of recourse is the new debtor's legal right to seek redress against the original debtor when the new debtor's obligation towards the original debtor is void or contestable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Periodic Rights">
  <data key="d0">Periodic Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Periodic rights are rights payable periodically such as rent for buildings and agricultural land, interest, revenues, wages, and pensions, which are subject to a five-year prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Material Object">
  <data key="d0">Non-Material Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A non-material object is an intangible thing; rights in respect of non-material objects are regulated by special laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Domain">
  <data key="d0">Public Domain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The public domain consists of immovable and movable property owned by the State or public juristic persons and allocated for purposes of public utility. Such property is not alienable, not liable to seizure, nor subject to acquisition by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Juristic Persons">
  <data key="d0">Public Juristic Persons</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Public juristic persons are legal entities that may own property allocated for public utility purposes, forming part of the public domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Declaration Of Intention">
  <data key="d0">Declaration Of Intention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A declaration of intention is an expression of will that may be verbal, written, by signs in general use, or by conduct. It becomes effective when it comes to the knowledge of the person for whom it was intended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Consent">
  <data key="d0">Consent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Consent is a fundamental element of contracts, requiring the exchange of concordant intentions between two parties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Offer">
  <data key="d0">Offer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An offer is a proposal made by an offeror that may be accepted to form a contract. An offeror who fixes a time limit for acceptance is bound to maintain the offer until the expiration of that limit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Offeror">
  <data key="d0">Offeror</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The offeror is the person who makes an offer in contract formation and is bound to maintain the offer within any specified time limit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acceptance">
  <data key="d0">Acceptance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acceptance is the agreement to an offer. An acceptance that goes beyond, restricts, or modifies the offer is deemed a rejection comprising a new offer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract Of Adhesion">
  <data key="d0">Contract Of Adhesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract of adhesion is a contract where acceptance is confined to adherence to standard conditions drawn up by the offeror and not subject to discussion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Earnest Money">
  <data key="d0">Earnest Money</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Earnest money is a payment made at the time a contract is concluded, indicating that either party may withdraw from the contract. The party who paid and withdraws forfeits the earnest money; the party who received it and withdraws must repay double.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Promise To Contract">
  <data key="d0">Promise To Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A promise to contract is an agreement by which parties promise to enter into a particular contract in the future. It is only binding if all essential points and the time for conclusion are stated, and any legally required form must also be observed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mandatory">
  <data key="d0">Mandatory</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A mandatory is a representative who acts in accordance with the principal's precise instructions. The principal cannot plead the ignorance of the mandatory of circumstances the principal knew or should have known.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Minor Lacking Discretion">
  <data key="d0">Minor Lacking Discretion</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A minor lacking discretion has no legal capacity to dispose of property; all acts in law by such a person are deemed void.&lt;SEP&gt;A minor lacking discretion is a person who has no legal capacity to dispose of property; all such acts in law are deemed void as per Article 110.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Discerning Minor">
  <data key="d0">Discerning Minor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A discerning minor is a minor with discretion whose purely beneficial financial acts are valid, purely harmful acts are void, and mixed acts are voidable for the benefit of the minor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sources Of Obligations">
  <data key="d0">Sources Of Obligations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sources of obligations are the origins from which legal obligations arise, with contracts being identified as one such source as outlined in Chapter I of Book I on obligations generally.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Personal Rights">
  <data key="d0">Personal Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Personal rights, also referred to as obligations, are rights arising from legal relationships between persons, forming the subject of the First Part of the civil law text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Utility">
  <data key="d0">Public Utility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Public utility is the purpose for which immovable and movable property owned by the State or public juristic persons is allocated, thereby making such property part of the public domain. Cessation of allocation for public utility causes the property to lose its public domain status.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silence As Acceptance">
  <data key="d0">Silence As Acceptance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Silence as acceptance is the legal principle that failure to reply is equivalent to acceptance when the offer relates to existing dealings between the parties or when the offer is solely in the interests of the offeree. A contract may also be deemed concluded if the offer is not refused within a reasonable time when no formal acceptance was anticipated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Dealing">
  <data key="d0">Self-Dealing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Self-dealing is the prohibition on a representative contracting with himself in the name of the person represented, whether for his own benefit or for that of a third party, without authorization from the principal. The principal may ratify such a contract after the fact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Undisclosed Representation">
  <data key="d0">Undisclosed Representation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Undisclosed representation refers to the situation where a contracting party does not disclose at the time of contract conclusion that he is acting as a representative. The contract only operates in favor of or binds the principal if the third party necessarily knew of the representation or was indifferent to contracting with either the principal or the representative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extinction Of Representation">
  <data key="d0">Extinction Of Representation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Extinction of representation occurs when a representative's authority has ended. If both the representative and the third party were unaware of the extinction at the time the contract was concluded, the effects of the contract, whether rights or obligations, revert to the principal or his successor in title.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obligations">
  <data key="d0">Obligations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Obligations are legal duties arising between persons, treated as equivalent to personal rights, and governed generally in Book I of the civil law framework with contracts identified as a primary source.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architect">
  <data key="d0">Architect</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Architect is responsible for preparing plans and specifications and supervising construction work, and is entitled to separate fees for each of these tasks. The Architect is bound by warranty obligations; any clause excluding or restricting the warranty is void.&lt;SEP&gt;A person entrusted with the construction or design of buildings or other works, entitled to a privilege over such works.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractor">
  <data key="d0">Contractor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Contractor executes works under a contract for work, is obligated to notify the master of cost overruns, may entrust work to sub-contractors, and is entitled to payment upon delivery. The Contractor is bound by warranty obligations and is responsible for the sub-contractor's work.&lt;SEP&gt;A person entrusted with the erection, reconstruction, repair, or maintenance of buildings or other works, entitled to a privilege over such works.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-contractor">
  <data key="d0">Sub-contractor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sub-contractor is a party to whom the Contractor may entrust the execution of all or part of the work, unless restricted by contract terms or the personal nature of the work.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workmen">
  <data key="d0">Workmen</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Workmen are workers employed by a Contractor or Sub-contractor in the execution of work who hold direct rights of action against the Master and the main Contractor for sums due.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contract For Work">
  <data key="d0">Contract For Work</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Contract for Work is a legal agreement between a Master and a Contractor for the execution of works, establishing mutual obligations regarding delivery, payment, warranty, and termination, governed by specific legal provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warranty">
  <data key="d0">Warranty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Warranty is a legal obligation binding the Architect and the Contractor for the quality and soundness of works. Actions on warranty are prescribed after three years from destruction or discovery of defects, and any clause excluding or restricting the warranty is void.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sub-contract">
  <data key="d0">Sub-contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Sub-contract is an arrangement permitting a Contractor to entrust the execution of all or part of the work to a Sub-contractor, subject to contractual restrictions and the requirement that the Contractor remains responsible to the Master.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concession Of Public Utility Service">
  <data key="d0">Concession Of Public Utility Service</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Concession of Public Utility Service is a contract for the management of a public utility service of an economic nature, concluded between an Administrative Authority and a private person or company for a fixed period of exploitation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Administrative Authority">
  <data key="d0">Administrative Authority</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Administrative Authority is the governmental body in charge of organizing a public utility service and entering into concession agreements with private persons or companies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concessionaire">
  <data key="d0">Concessionaire</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Concessionaire is a private person or company entrusted with the exploitation of a public utility service for a fixed period, obligated to provide services to consumers in accordance with the concession terms and applicable laws.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractor's Heirs">
  <data key="d0">Contractor's Heirs</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Contractor's Heirs are the successors of a deceased Contractor who may continue the contract if they offer sufficient guarantees for the due performance of the work.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractor's Estate">
  <data key="d0">Contractor's Estate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Contractor's Estate is the legal successor to the deceased Contractor's rights, entitled to receive payment from the Master for the value of work already done and expenses incurred for uncompleted work when the contract is dissolved by death.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unit Price Contract">
  <data key="d0">Unit Price Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Unit Price Contract is a type of contract for work concluded on the basis of an estimate drawn up on a unit price basis, requiring the Contractor to notify the Master of anticipated cost overruns and forfeiting the right to recover excess expenses if notification is not given.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lump Sum Contract">
  <data key="d0">Lump Sum Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Lump Sum Contract is a type of contract for work concluded on a lump sum basis according to a plan agreed with the Master, under which the Contractor has no claim to a price increase even if modifications are made, unless due to the Master's fault or authorized by the Master.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Materials">
  <data key="d0">Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Materials are the supplies used in the execution of works, whose loss in the event of destruction before delivery falls on the party who supplied them, whether the Master or the Contractor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monopoly">
  <data key="d0">Monopoly</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Monopoly refers to a de jure or de facto exclusive right held by a Concessionaire over a public utility service, which obligates the Concessionaire to observe strict equality among all consumers in services and rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preemption">
  <data key="d0">Preemption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Preemption is a legal right that allows a qualified person to substitute themselves in a sale of immovable property in place of the purchaser, subject to specific conditions and procedures laid down in Articles 935 through 948.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal mechanism by which the right of usufruct may be acquired, listed alongside legal disposition and prescription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ownership">
  <data key="d0">Ownership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ownership is a real right in movables and immovables that can be transferred by contract, subject to provisions regarding identification of the object and publication of real rights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preemptor">
  <data key="d0">Preemptor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Preemptor is the person exercising the right of preemption, who is substituted for the purchaser in all rights and obligations vis-a-vis the vendor upon exercising that right.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Possession">
  <data key="d0">Possession</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Possession is the central legal concept across these articles, encompassing acquisition, transmission, loss, protection, and effects such as acquisitive prescription.&lt;SEP&gt;Possession is a legal state of control over property that does not result from acts done by mere permission or tolerance, and may be acquired through an intermediary or a legal representative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bare Owner">
  <data key="d0">Bare Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The owner of the property subject to a usufruct, whose ratification of a usufructuary's lease allows it to survive beyond the usufruct period.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bare Owner is the owner of bare property who holds the right of preemption in case of a sale of all or part of the usufruct attached to the bare property, or in case of a sale of the hekr right.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-Owner in Common">
  <data key="d0">Co-Owner in Common</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Co-Owner in Common is a joint owner of property held in common who holds the right of preemption in case of a sale of a part of the common property to a third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neighboring Owner">
  <data key="d0">Neighboring Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Neighboring Owner is the owner of adjoining property who holds the right of preemption in cases involving buildings, building land, rights of servitude, or land adjoining on two sides with a value of at least half the value of the land sold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Representative">
  <data key="d0">Legal Representative</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Legal Representative is the person authorized to consent on behalf of a life assured who is under legal incapacity, required for the validity of a life insurance contract on such person.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal representative is a person authorized to acquire possession on behalf of a person lacking discretion.&lt;SEP&gt;A Legal Representative is a person who may acquire possession on behalf of a person lacking discretion through legal representation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Servitude">
  <data key="d0">Right of Servitude</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Right of Servitude is a legal right over neighboring land that qualifies the Neighboring Owner for preemption when the sold land enjoys a servitude over the neighbor's land or vice versa, as described in Article 936.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intermediary">
  <data key="d0">Intermediary</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An intermediary is a person who exercises possession in the name of the possessor and is obliged to obey the possessor's instructions.&lt;SEP&gt;An Intermediary is a person through whom possession may be exercised, provided the intermediary acts in the name of the possessor and maintains a relationship obliging compliance with the possessor's instructions regarding that possession, as stated in Article 951.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Person Lacking Discretion">
  <data key="d0">Person Lacking Discretion</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A Person Lacking Discretion is an individual who may acquire possession only through the intervention of a Legal Representative, as stated in Article 950.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Liability Arising from Things">
  <data key="d0">Liability Arising from Things</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal section covering the responsibility of persons in charge of animals, buildings, and things requiring special care for damage caused by them, as established in Articles 176–178.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enrichment Without Just Cause">
  <data key="d0">Enrichment Without Just Cause</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal section establishing that a person who enriches himself without just cause to the detriment of another is liable to compensate for the loss sustained, even if the profit later disappears. Governed by Articles 179–180.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payment Not Due">
  <data key="d0">Payment Not Due</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A subsection dealing with the restitution of payments made when no obligation existed, including rules on good faith, bad faith, legal capacity, and prescription, as governed by Articles 181–187.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Voluntary Agency">
  <data key="d0">Voluntary Agency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A subsection addressing situations where a person knowingly assumes management of an urgent business of another person without being bound to do so, governed by Articles 188–197.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Effects of Obligations">
  <data key="d0">Effects of Obligations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A chapter dealing with the enforceability of obligations against the debtor and the distinction between civil and natural obligations, governed by Articles 199–202.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Civil Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legally enforceable obligation for which a natural obligation may constitute a valid cause.&lt;SEP&gt;An enforceable obligation against the debtor, which may arise from a natural obligation as its valid cause.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 176">
  <data key="d0">Article 176</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes that a person in charge of an animal, even if not its owner, is liable for any harm done by the animal, even if it strays or escapes, unless the accident was due to a cause beyond control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 177">
  <data key="d0">Article 177</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes liability of a person in charge of a building for damage caused by its collapse, even if partial, unless the accident did not result from negligent maintenance, age, or defect; also entitles endangered persons to seek court authorization for preventive measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 178">
  <data key="d0">Article 178</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes liability of a person in charge of things requiring special care or of machines for damage caused by them, unless the damage was due to a cause beyond control, subject to special legal provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 180">
  <data key="d0">Article 180</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Sets the prescription period for enrichment claims at three years from when the injured party knew of the right and fifteen years from when the right first arose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 181">
  <data key="d0">Article 181</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes that a person who receives what is not owed must return it, except when the payor knew he was under no obligation to pay, unless he was legally incapable or paid under duress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 182">
  <data key="d0">Article 182</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Allows recovery of payment not due if made in performance of an obligation whose cause had not materialized or had ceased to exist.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 183">
  <data key="d0">Article 183</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Permits restitution of premature payments if the payor was unaware that payment was not yet due; the creditor may limit restitution to profit gained from the premature payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 184">
  <data key="d0">Article 184</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Bars restitution of payment made by a third party if the creditor in good faith gave up the document of title, security, or allowed the claim to prescribe; the real debtor must indemnify the third party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 185">
  <data key="d0">Article 185</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Distinguishes between good faith recipients who must return only what they received, and bad faith recipients who must also return interest and profits gained or negligently forfeited from the date of payment or bad faith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 186">
  <data key="d0">Article 186</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Limits the liability of a recipient lacking legal capacity to the extent of his enrichment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 187">
  <data key="d0">Article 187</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Sets the prescription period for restitution claims at three years from when the payor knew of the right and fifteen years from when the right arose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 188">
  <data key="d0">Article 188</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines voluntary agency as a situation where a person knowingly and voluntarily assumes management of an urgent business of another person on that person's behalf without being bound to do so.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 189">
  <data key="d0">Article 189</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Extends voluntary agency to situations where a person manages another's affairs while managing his own, due to an inseparable connection between the two businesses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 190">
  <data key="d0">Article 190</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Provides that the rules of mandate apply when the person for whom the voluntary agent acts ratifies the agent's act.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 191">
  <data key="d0">Article 191</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Imposes a duty on the voluntary agent to continue the work commenced until the principal can manage it and to inform the principal of the intervention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 192">
  <data key="d0">Article 192</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Requires a voluntary agent to exercise the care of a reasonable person and holds him responsible for mistakes; the judge may reduce damages; delegates are jointly and severally responsible.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 193">
  <data key="d0">Article 193</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Binds the voluntary agent by the same obligations as a mandatary regarding restitution of what was received and rendering of accounts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 194">
  <data key="d0">Article 194</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Provides that upon the death of a voluntary agent, his heirs are bound by the same obligations as those of a mandatary under Article 717; upon the death of the principal, the agent remains bound to the heirs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 195">
  <data key="d0">Article 195</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Deems the voluntary agent a representative of the principal if he exercised reasonable care; the principal must carry out obligations, indemnify the agent, reimburse expenses with interest, and compensate for loss, though the agent is not entitled to remuneration unless the work falls within his profession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 196">
  <data key="d0">Article 196</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Limits the liability of a legally incapable voluntary agent to the extent of his enrichment, unless the liability results from an unlawful act; the principal remains fully responsible regardless of capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 197">
  <data key="d0">Article 197</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Sets the prescription period for voluntary agency claims at three years from when each party knew of his right and fifteen years from when the right arose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 198">
  <data key="d0">Article 198</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Provides that obligations arising directly from the law are governed by the legal provisions that created them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 199">
  <data key="d0">Article 199</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Establishes that an obligation is enforceable against the debtor, but a natural obligation cannot be enforced.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 200">
  <data key="d0">Article 200</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Empowers the judge to determine whether a natural obligation exists in the absence of a legal provision, provided it is not contrary to public policy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 201">
  <data key="d0">Article 201</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Prevents a debtor from claiming restitution of what was voluntarily given to discharge a natural obligation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 202">
  <data key="d0">Article 202</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Provides that a natural obligation may constitute a valid cause for a civil obligation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Person in Charge of Animal">
  <data key="d0">Person in Charge of Animal</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who has custody or supervision of an animal and is liable for harm caused by it even if not the owner, unless the harm resulted from a cause beyond control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Person in Charge of Building">
  <data key="d0">Person in Charge of Building</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who has custody or supervision of a building and is liable for damage caused by its collapse, even if partial, unless it did not result from negligent maintenance, age, or defect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Person in Charge of Things">
  <data key="d0">Person in Charge of Things</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who supervises things requiring special care or machines and is liable for damage caused by them unless the damage was due to a cause beyond control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Voluntary Agent">
  <data key="d0">Voluntary Agent</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who knowingly and voluntarily assumes management of the urgent business of another person without being legally bound to do so, owing duties of care, continuation, and notification.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payor">
  <data key="d0">Payor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who makes a payment, who may claim restitution if the payment was not due, was for an unmaterialized obligation, or was made prematurely without knowledge.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Law">
  <data key="d0">The Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Section V of the legal text, providing that obligations which arise directly and solely in consequence of the law are governed by the provisions of the law giving rise to such obligations, as established in Article 198.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Delegate">
  <data key="d0">Delegate</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A third party to whom a voluntary agent delegates the whole or part of the work he has assumed, and whose acts the voluntary agent is responsible for, without prejudice to the principal's right of direct remedy against the delegate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machine">
  <data key="d0">Machine</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanical device whose supervisor is liable for damage caused by it unless the damage was due to a cause beyond control, as established in Article 178.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compromise">
  <data key="d0">Compromise</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal agreement that can only be established by a written document or an official proces-verbal, which terminates disputes and extinguishes rights and claims finally renounced by the parties.&lt;SEP&gt;A contract by which two parties put an end to an existing dispute or prevent an anticipated dispute through the mutual surrender of part of their respective claims, requiring written documentation and legal capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Management">
  <data key="d0">Right of Management</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A limited legal authority that restricts the holder from entering into leases exceeding three years without the consent of the competent authority.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compromise Nullity">
  <data key="d0">Compromise Nullity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal invalidity of a compromise, which is indivisible so that nullity of one part involves nullity of the whole contract, unless the parties agreed the parts are independent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter II: Contracts Relating to the Use of a Thing">
  <data key="d0">Chapter II: Contracts Relating to the Use of a Thing</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A chapter of the civil code that covers contracts for the use of a thing, including leases generally.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section I: Leases">
  <data key="d0">Section I: Leases</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A section within Chapter II that provides the general legal provisions governing leases, including their elements and effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Competent Authority">
  <data key="d0">Competent Authority</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The authority whose consent is required for a person holding only a right of management to enter into a lease exceeding three years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Declaratory Effect">
  <data key="d0">Declaratory Effect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal effect of a compromise whereby it establishes the rights it covers as declaratory rather than constitutive, and this effect is limited solely to the disputed rights.&lt;SEP&gt;The legal effect of a compromise on the rights it covers, which is declaratory in nature and limited specifically to litigious rights, as stated in Article 554.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Renunciation">
  <data key="d0">Renunciation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The act of giving up rights in a compromise, the wording of which must be strictly interpreted and applies only to rights that form the precise object of the dispute settled by the compromise.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1145">
  <data key="d0">Article 1145</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1145 establishes that sums due to a vendor of a movable for price and accessories are secured by a privilege over the movable sold, enforceable as long as the movable preserves its identity, subject to third-party good faith rights and commercial provisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1146">
  <data key="d0">Article 1146</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1146 provides that co-owners who have partitioned a movable have a privilege over this movable for their respective remedies against each other resulting from partition and for repayment of any difference reverting to them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1147">
  <data key="d0">Article 1147</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1147 establishes that the price and accessories due to a vendor of an immovable are secured by a privilege over the immovable sold, which must be inscribed and its rank is fixed by the date of inscription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 1149">
  <data key="d0">Article 1149</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 1149 provides that co-owners who have partitioned an immovable have a privilege over it for their respective remedies against each other resulting from partition, including the right to claim payment of any difference, and such privilege must be inscribed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hotel Proprietor's Privilege">
  <data key="d0">Hotel Proprietor's Privilege</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal privilege securing sums due to hotel proprietors over effects brought by travellers, having the same rank as a lessor's privilege, enforceable even on effects not owned by the traveller provided they are not lost or stolen property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d0">Vendor's Privilege Over Movable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal privilege securing sums due to a vendor of movable property over the thing sold, ranking below other movable privileges but enforceable against lessors and hotel proprietors if they had knowledge of the privilege at the time the movable was brought onto their premises.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vendor's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d0">Vendor's Privilege Over Immovable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal privilege securing sums due to a vendor of an immovable over the property sold, requiring inscription notwithstanding transcription of the sale, with rank fixed by the date of inscription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d0">Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Movable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal privilege held by co-owners over partitioned movables for remedies against each other resulting from partition and for repayment of differences, having the same rank as a vendor's privilege.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d0">Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Immovable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal privilege held by co-owners over partitioned immovables for remedies and payment of differences, requiring inscription with rank fixed by the date of inscription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractor's And Architect's Privilege">
  <data key="d0">Contractor's And Architect's Privilege</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal privilege securing sums due to contractors and architects over buildings or works they erected, reconstructed, repaired, or maintained, limited to the increase in value resulting from such works, requiring inscription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lessor's Privilege">
  <data key="d0">Lessor's Privilege</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal privilege held by a lessor over leased property, having the same rank as a hotel proprietor's privilege.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Party In Good Faith">
  <data key="d0">Third Party In Good Faith</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who has acquired for valuable consideration a real right over property sold during a seller's last illness; the provisions restricting such sales do not apply to the prejudice of such third parties.&lt;SEP&gt;A third party who acquires rights over property without knowledge of existing privileges, whose acquired rights are protected against enforcement of such privileges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Privileges Over Immovables">
  <data key="d0">Special Privileges Over Immovables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A category of legal privileges applicable to immovable property, encompassing the vendor's privilege, the contractor's and architect's privilege, and the co-partitioner's privilege over immovables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Effects">
  <data key="d0">Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The personal belongings brought by a traveller to a hotel or its annexes, which serve as the subject of the hotel proprietor's privilege to secure sums due for accommodation, food, and expenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buildings Or Other Works">
  <data key="d0">Buildings Or Other Works</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical structures or constructions that are the subject of the contractor's and architect's privilege, including buildings erected, reconstructed, repaired, or maintained by contractors and architects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Minor Possessing Discretion">
  <data key="d0">Minor Possessing Discretion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A minor possessing discretion is a person whose contracts and dispositions of property are valid when wholly to their advantage, void when wholly to their disadvantage, and voidable when simultaneously profitable and detrimental, as defined in Article 111.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interdiction">
  <data key="d0">Interdiction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interdiction is a court-ordered legal restriction pronounced on persons suffering from insanity, mental derangement, imbecility, or prodigality, which may also be raised by the court in accordance with prescribed rules and procedures, as stated in Article 113.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insanity">
  <data key="d0">Insanity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insanity is a mental condition that, when combined with a registered sentence of interdiction, renders acts entered into by the affected person null, as described in Article 114.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495957</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mental Derangement">
  <data key="d0">Mental Derangement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mental derangement is a psychological condition that serves as grounds for court-ordered interdiction and nullification of acts performed after registration of the interdiction sentence, as per Article 114.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Imbecility">
  <data key="d0">Imbecility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Imbecility is a condition for which the court may pronounce interdiction; acts by a person interdicted for imbecility are governed by the same provisions as those for minors possessing discretion, according to Article 115.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prodigality">
  <data key="d0">Prodigality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prodigality is a condition of reckless spending for which the court may pronounce interdiction; acts after registration follow the same rules as for minors possessing discretion, as stated in Article 115.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judicial Adviser">
  <data key="d0">Judicial Adviser</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A judicial adviser is a person appointed by the court to assist a deaf and dumb, deaf and blind, or blind and dumb person who cannot express their will, in connection with acts necessary for their interests, as established in Article 117.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Guardian">
  <data key="d0">Natural Guardian</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A natural guardian is a person whose acts on behalf of those under their guardianship are valid within the limits provided by law, as stated in Article 118.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Guardian">
  <data key="d0">Legal Guardian</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal guardian is a person whose acts on behalf of those under their guardianship are valid within the limits provided by law, as stated in Article 118.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curator">
  <data key="d0">Curator</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A curator is a legal representative whose acts are valid within the limits provided by law, as stated in Article 118.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Essential Mistake">
  <data key="d0">Essential Mistake</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An essential mistake is a mistake of such gravity that, had it not been committed, the mistaken party would not have concluded the contract, as defined in Article 121; it may relate to the quality of the thing or the identity or qualities of the contracting party.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mistake of Law">
  <data key="d0">Mistake of Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mistake in law is an error regarding legal provisions that may entail the voidability of a contract if it fulfills the same elements as a mistake of fact, unless the law provides otherwise, as per Article 122.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mistake of Fact">
  <data key="d0">Mistake of Fact</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mistake of fact is an error regarding factual circumstances that, when it meets the criteria of essentiality, may serve as grounds for avoidance of a contract, as referenced in Articles 120 through 122.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object of Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Object of Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The object of an obligation is what a debtor must deliver or perform; it may be a future thing but cannot be impossible in itself, and must be determinate as to its kind and quantity, as defined in Articles 131 through 133.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Void Act">
  <data key="d0">Void Act</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A void act is a legal act that has no legal effect, such as acts by a minor lacking discretion, acts by insane persons after registration of interdiction, contracts with impossible objects, or agreements regarding the succession of a living person.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Voidable Act">
  <data key="d0">Voidable Act</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A voidable act is a legal act that may be annulled at the request of an interested party, including dispositions by a minor possessing discretion that are both profitable and detrimental, acts performed without a required judicial adviser, and contracts affected by mistake, fraud, duress, or lesion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil Code">
  <data key="d0">Civil Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Civil Code is the body of law containing Articles 109 through 134 that governs legal capacity, defects of consent, and the object of obligations in contractual matters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mistakes of Calculation and Clerical Mistakes">
  <data key="d0">Mistakes of Calculation and Clerical Mistakes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mistakes of calculation and clerical mistakes are errors that do not affect the validity of a contract but must be corrected, as provided in Article 123 of the Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Succession of a Living Person">
  <data key="d0">Succession of a Living Person</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An agreement with regard to the succession of a living person is void, even if the person consents, except in cases provided for by law, as stated in Article 131 of the Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Avoidance of Contract">
  <data key="d0">Avoidance of Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Avoidance of contract is a legal remedy whereby a contracting party may demand the nullification of a contract on grounds including legal incapacity, essential mistake, mistake of law, fraudulent misrepresentation, duress, or lesion, as referenced across Articles 119 through 129 of the Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Provisions for Lesion">
  <data key="d0">Special Provisions for Lesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Special provisions for lesion are legal rules applicable to certain contracts and rates of interest that take precedence over the general lesion provisions of Article 129, as stated in Article 130.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Specific Performance">
  <data key="d0">Specific Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal remedy in Section I of the code that compels a debtor to perform an obligation exactly as specified, provided such performance is possible and not excessively onerous for the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compensation in Lieu of Performance">
  <data key="d0">Compensation in Lieu of Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal remedy in Section II of the code, awarded when specific performance is impossible, covering losses suffered and lost profits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Real Right">
  <data key="d0">Real Right</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A property right, including ownership, that may be transferred through obligation, subject to transcription rules. An obligation to transfer a real right includes the duty of delivery and preservation of the article.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moral Prejudice">
  <data key="d0">Moral Prejudice</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-material harm for which damages may be awarded. The right to compensation for moral prejudice is not transferable to third parties unless fixed by agreement or subject to legal proceedings. Awards are limited to spouses and relatives up to the second degree for grief caused by death of a victim.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gross Negligence">
  <data key="d0">Gross Negligence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A severe degree of carelessness for which a debtor always remains liable. A debtor cannot be contractually discharged from liability for gross negligence, though the debtor may stipulate non-liability for gross negligence of persons employed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Penalty">
  <data key="d0">Penalty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A monetary sanction imposed on a debtor who abstains from performing an obligation when specific performance is impracticable unless performed by the debtor personally. The judge may increase the penalty if insufficient to compel performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contractual Obligation">
  <data key="d0">Contractual Obligation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An obligation arising from a contract, where a debtor who has not committed fraud or gross negligence is liable only for damages that could have been normally foreseen at the time of entering into the contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code De Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Code De Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The procedural law referenced in Article 219 governing the manner in which formal summons may be served by post.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thief">
  <data key="d0">Thief</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person who steals. The thief bears the risk of loss of the stolen thing regardless of how it perishes, as stated in Article 207.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agreed Damages">
  <data key="d0">Agreed Damages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Damages fixed in advance by the parties either in the contract or in a subsequent agreement, as provided in Articles 223 and 224. Agreed damages are not due if the debtor proves that the creditor suffered no loss. The judge may reduce the amount of agreed damages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Of Commodities">
  <data key="d0">Sale Of Commodities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal concept involving the sale of commodities or movable effects where a term is agreed for payment and delivery; the sale is ipso facto dissolved without summons if the price is not paid upon due date, subject to contrary agreement and at the vendor's option.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale With Right Of Redemption">
  <data key="d0">Sale With Right Of Redemption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A form of sale in which the vendor reserves the right to take back the thing sold within a fixed time; such a sale is declared void under Article 465.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Of A Thing Belonging To Another">
  <data key="d0">Sale Of A Thing Belonging To Another</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A form of sale where a person sells a definite and ascertained thing of which he is not the owner; the purchaser may demand annulment of the sale, and the sale cannot have any effect against the true owner even if the purchaser ratifies the contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Of Litigious Rights">
  <data key="d0">Sale Of Litigious Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A form of sale involving the assignment of a litigious right, defined as a right that is the subject of a court action or serious controversy; the debtor may extinguish the assigned right by paying the assignee the actual price paid plus expenses and interest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Court Officials">
  <data key="d0">Court Officials</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Members of the magistracy, Parquet, lawyers, Greffiers, and bailiffs who are prohibited from purchasing litigious rights within the jurisdiction of the court in which they serve, under penalty of nullity of the sale.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lawyers">
  <data key="d0">Lawyers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal professionals who are prohibited under Article 472 from entering into agreements with their clients regarding litigious rights they have undertaken to defend, under penalty of nullity of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale Of An Inheritance">
  <data key="d0">Sale Of An Inheritance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A form of sale where a person sells an inheritance without giving particulars thereof; the seller only warrants that he is an heir unless otherwise agreed, and transfer of rights requires fulfillment of necessary formalities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale During Last Illness">
  <data key="d0">Sale During Last Illness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sale made by a person during his last illness to an heir or non-heir at a price inferior to the value of the thing sold; it is valid against heirs only if the difference between value and price does not exceed one third of the inheritance, with provisions for ratification or payment to make up two thirds.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale By Representative To Himself">
  <data key="d0">Sale By Representative To Himself</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A prohibited form of sale where a person representing another by agreement, law, or court order purchases property entrusted to him for sale, unless authorized by a court order; such sales are governed by Articles 479 through 481.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brokers And Experts">
  <data key="d0">Brokers And Experts</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Professionals who are prohibited from purchasing goods entrusted to them for sale or appraisal, either in their own name or through intermediaries, under Article 480.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495958</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exchange">
  <data key="d0">Exchange</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract by which contracting parties mutually bind themselves to transfer to each other by way of exchange the ownership of a thing other than money; the provisions governing sale apply to exchange as far as the nature of exchange allows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Annulment Of Sale">
  <data key="d0">Annulment Of Sale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal remedy available to a purchaser who was unaware that the thing sold did not belong to the vendor, entitling the purchaser to claim damages even if the vendor acted in good faith, as provided under Article 468.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Future Property Gift">
  <data key="d0">Future Property Gift</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A gift of future property is declared void under Article 492, meaning property not yet owned by the donor at the time of the gift cannot be validly gifted.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Promise Of Gift">
  <data key="d0">Promise Of Gift</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A promise to make a gift that is not binding unless made by an authentic document, as provided under Article 490.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Annuity">
  <data key="d0">Annuity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A perpetual periodic prestation consisting of a sum of money or fixed quantity of fungibles, redeemable at the debtor's will, governed by rules on interest-bearing loans when made for valuable consideration.&lt;SEP&gt;Annuity is a periodic financial payment settled on the life of a person, payable upon occurrence of a specified event or maturity date.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assured">
  <data key="d0">Assured</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Assured is the person who contracts the insurance, pays the premium, and may nominate beneficiaries; the Assured may also surrender or reduce the policy under certain conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Life Assured">
  <data key="d0">Life Assured</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Life Assured is the person whose life is the subject of the life insurance contract; the insurer's obligations depend on events such as the death or survival of this person.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insurance Contract">
  <data key="d0">Insurance Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Insurance Contract is an agreement whereby the insurer undertakes to pay the assured or beneficiary a sum of money or annuity upon occurrence of a specified risk, in consideration of a premium or other pecuniary payment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Policy of Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Policy of Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Policy of Insurance is the document evidencing the insurance contract, containing general and special conditions, including those relating to reduction, surrender, and forfeiture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Life Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Life Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Life Insurance is a class of insurance in which sums become due upon the occurrence of the event insured against or on a stipulated maturity date, without the need to establish that any loss was suffered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insurance Reserve">
  <data key="d0">Insurance Reserve</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Insurance Reserve is a financial reserve associated with a life insurance policy, used in calculating reduced sums assured and amounts payable upon surrender or suicide of the life assured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Suicide">
  <data key="d0">Suicide</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Suicide is an event affecting the insurer's obligation under a life insurance contract; the insurer is generally released from payment unless suicide resulted from illness causing loss of control or occurred after two years from the contract date.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Section III: Contracts of Insurance">
  <data key="d0">Section III: Contracts of Insurance</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Section III: Contracts of Insurance is the part of the Code containing general provisions and specific rules governing insurance contracts, including life insurance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Code">
  <data key="d0">The Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Code is the legal instrument containing statutory provisions on insurance contracts, annuities, and related matters, with Articles 746 through 764 addressing insurance law.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lawful Economic Interest">
  <data key="d0">Lawful Economic Interest</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lawful Economic Interest is any legitimate economic interest a person may have in the non-occurrence of a specified risk, and it constitutes the insurable subject matter under an insurance contract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Void Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Void Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Void Conditions are specific types of clauses in an insurance policy that are legally unenforceable, including forfeiture clauses for breach of law, unreasonable delay-related forfeiture clauses, unclear printed conditions relating to nullity, embedded arbitration clauses, and arbitrary clauses unrelated to the insured event.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Limitation Period">
  <data key="d0">Limitation Period</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Limitation Period is a three-year statutory period after which actions arising out of insurance contracts become barred, running from the date of the occurrence giving rise to the action, with exceptions for concealment or late discovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Paid-Up Policy">
  <data key="d0">Paid-Up Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Paid-Up Policy is a converted insurance policy with a reduced sum assured, obtained by the assured after payment of at least three yearly premiums, in lieu of the original policy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indemnification">
  <data key="d0">Indemnification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Indemnification is the principle under which the insurer is only bound to compensate the assured for actual loss arising from the occurrence of the insured risk, limited to the amount insured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Age Misstatement">
  <data key="d0">Age Misstatement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Age Misstatement refers to false declarations or errors regarding the age of the life assured, which do not invalidate the insurance but may result in a proportional reduction of the sum assured if the premium paid was lower than required.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attempted Homicide">
  <data key="d0">Attempted Homicide</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Attempted Homicide is an unsuccessful deliberate attempt by the beneficiary to cause the death of the life assured, which grants the assured the right to substitute the beneficiary with another person.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 950">
  <data key="d0">Article 950</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 950 provides that a person lacking discretion may acquire possession through the intervention of a legal representative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 951">
  <data key="d0">Article 951</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 951 regulates possession by intermediary, requiring the intermediary to act in the name of the possessor and obey instructions; it also establishes presumptions about possession on one's own behalf.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 952">
  <data key="d0">Article 952</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 952 states that possession is transmitted by mutual agreement between possessor and another, without requiring actual delivery, provided the transferee can assume control of the right.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 953">
  <data key="d0">Article 953</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 953 allows transmission of possession without actual delivery if the possessor continues possession on behalf of a successor in title or the successor continues for his own account.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 954">
  <data key="d0">Article 954</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 954 equates the handing over of documents relating to goods with delivery of the goods themselves; if documents and goods go to different good-faith recipients, the recipient of the goods has preference.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 955">
  <data key="d0">Article 955</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 955 provides that possession is transmitted with all its features to a universal successor in title, and a special successor may add the original possessor's possession to his own for legal effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 956">
  <data key="d0">Article 956</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 956 provides that possession ceases when the possessor abandons actual control or loses it in any other way.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 957">
  <data key="d0">Article 957</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 957 states that temporary obstacles do not terminate possession, but possession ceases if the obstacle continues for a full year due to a new possession against the possessor's will.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 958">
  <data key="d0">Article 958</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 958 grants a person who loses possession of an immovable the right to claim reinstatement within one year; secret loss starts the period from discovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 959">
  <data key="d0">Article 959</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 959 provides that a person who possessed for less than a year may recover possession if the dispossessor lacks a better possession; possession by violence allows restitution within one year in all cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 960">
  <data key="d0">Article 960</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 960 allows a dispossessed person to take proceedings for recovery of possession even against a good-faith possessor of the property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 961">
  <data key="d0">Article 961</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 961 grants a possessor of an immovable for a full year the right to take proceedings for discontinuance of disturbance within the following year.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 962">
  <data key="d0">Article 962</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 962 allows a possessor of an immovable for a full year to apply to the Judge for suspension of threatening new works, and the Judge may order a guarantee in either outcome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 963">
  <data key="d0">Article 963</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 963 provides that when several persons claim possession of the same right, the person with actual possession is provisionally presumed possessor unless acquisition was wrongful.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 964">
  <data key="d0">Article 964</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 964 establishes the presumption that the possessor of a right is its rightful possessor until the contrary is proved.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 965">
  <data key="d0">Article 965</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 965 defines good faith in possession, stating that a possessor unaware of infringing another's right is in good faith; good faith of a juristic person depends on its representative; good faith is always presumed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 966">
  <data key="d0">Article 966</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 966 provides that good faith ceases when the possessor becomes aware of infringement or when defects are notified in legal proceedings; usurpation by violence is deemed bad faith.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 967">
  <data key="d0">Article 967</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 967 states that possession retains the character it had at the time of acquisition, subject to proof to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 968">
  <data key="d0">Article 968</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 968 establishes acquisitive prescription, allowing a possessor of a movable or immovable without ownership to acquire title after fifteen years of uninterrupted possession.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 969">
  <data key="d0">Article 969</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 969 reduces the acquisitive prescription period to five years for possession in good faith and by virtue of a just title over an immovable; a just title must be duly transcribed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 970">
  <data key="d0">Article 970</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 970 requires thirty-three years of possession for acquiring wakf property and hereditary rights by prescription; state and public entity property cannot be acquired by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 971">
  <data key="d0">Article 971</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 971 establishes the presumption that present possession existed during the intervening time if proven at an ascertained previous time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 972">
  <data key="d0">Article 972</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 972 prohibits acquiring by prescription contrary to one's title but allows it if the nature of possession changes by act of a third party or adverse claim against the owner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 973">
  <data key="d0">Article 973</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 973 makes rules of extinctive prescription applicable to acquisitive prescription regarding calculation, suspension, interruption, and renunciation, subject to compatibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acquisitive Prescription">
  <data key="d0">Acquisitive Prescription</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acquisitive prescription is the legal mechanism by which prolonged uninterrupted possession leads to acquisition of ownership or real rights, with periods of five, fifteen, or thirty-three years depending on circumstances.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal mechanism by which a person acquires ownership of property through continuous possession over a specified period of time, subject to rules on suspension, interruption, and calculation of the period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Just Title">
  <data key="d0">Just Title</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A just title is a duly transcribed document of title emanating from a person who is not the owner, required for the shortened five-year acquisitive prescription period.&lt;SEP&gt;A lawful basis or reason for acquiring possession, which, together with good faith, enables a possessor to acquire ownership of movables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Successor In Title">
  <data key="d0">Successor In Title</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A successor in title is a person who receives possession from a prior possessor, either universally or under a special title, and may add prior possession to his own.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three Possessory Actions">
  <data key="d0">Three Possessory Actions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The three possessory actions are legal remedies for the protection of possession, including reinstatement of possession, discontinuance of disturbance, and prevention of threatened disturbance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 974">
  <data key="d0">Article 974</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 974 provides that whatever the duration of acquisitive prescription, it is suspended under certain conditions; the article appears truncated in the source text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carrier">
  <data key="d0">Carrier</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A carrier is a person to whom goods are entrusted, and documents issued in respect of such goods are equivalent to delivery of the goods themselves under Article 954.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Competent Minister">
  <data key="d0">Competent Minister</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Competent Minister is the authority empowered under Article 970 to administratively remove encroachments on state property and public entity property.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hereditary Rights">
  <data key="d0">Hereditary Rights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hereditary rights are a category of rights that can only be acquired by prescription after thirty-three years of possession, as prescribed by Article 970.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Violence">
  <data key="d0">Violence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Violence is a means of dispossession that constitutes bad faith under Article 966 and entitles the dispossessed possessor to restitution within one year in all cases under Article 959.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disturbance">
  <data key="d0">Disturbance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Disturbance is an interference with the possession of an immovable, against which a possessor of at least one year may seek legal proceedings for discontinuance under Article 961.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Liquidator">
  <data key="d0">Liquidator</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person appointed by the majority of partners or by the judge to carry out the liquidation of partnership property, restricted from undertaking new business except as necessary to complete prior transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dissolution">
  <data key="d0">Dissolution</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal termination of a partnership, triggered by unanimous partner agreement, court order for non-performance, or a partner's retirement, after which liquidation is required.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Liquidation">
  <data key="d0">Liquidation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal process of winding up partnership affairs involving payment of creditors, settling outstanding debts, and distributing remaining assets among partners according to their capital contributions and profit shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partnership Property">
  <data key="d0">Partnership Property</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The assets of the partnership that are subject to liquidation and partition among all partners after satisfaction of creditors and deduction of outstanding obligations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loan for Consumption">
  <data key="d0">Loan for Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contract by which the lender undertakes to transfer ownership of a sum of money or other fungible to the borrower, who must return an equal thing in amount, kind, and quality at the end of the loan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Borrower">
  <data key="d0">Borrower</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The party to a loan for consumption who receives ownership of money or fungibles and is obligated to return an equivalent, pay agreed interest, and may terminate the contract with six months' notice after six months from the loan date.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Redemption">
  <data key="d0">Redemption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The right of the debtor to extinguish an annuity by repaying the capital sum, exercisable at any time subject to notice requirements and a maximum deferral period of fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dispute">
  <data key="d0">Dispute</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A conflict or anticipated conflict between parties that may be resolved through compromise by the mutual surrender of claims, extinguishing the rights and claims that have been renounced.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retirement">
  <data key="d0">Retirement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The right of a partner to withdraw from the partnership by giving previous notice to co-partners, provided the retirement is free of fraudulent intent and not at an unsuitable time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exclusion">
  <data key="d0">Exclusion</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The legal process by which a partner may be expelled from the partnership by court order when that partner's presence has given rise to objections to extending the partnership or when that partner's actions constitute grounds for dissolution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loan for Use">
  <data key="d0">Loan for Use</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A type of loan referenced in Article 540 whose provisions apply when a loan made without valuable consideration results in dispossession of the loaned thing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hidden Defect">
  <data key="d0">Hidden Defect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A defect in the loaned thing not apparent at the time of the loan, which triggers different remedies depending on whether the loan is for valuable consideration or gratuitous, and whether the lender deliberately concealed it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Securities">
  <data key="d0">Securities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Collateral or guarantees promised by the debtor to the creditor in the context of an annuity, the disappearance of which or failure to supply can compel the debtor to redeem.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sale">
  <data key="d0">Sale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal concept referenced in Article 540 whose provisions apply when a loan made for valuable consideration results in dispossession of the loaned thing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bearer Warrant">
  <data key="d0">Bearer Warrant</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A negotiable document transferable by delivery, which may be acquired by a possessor in good faith or subject to recovery claims if lost or stolen.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Usufruct">
  <data key="d0">Right of Usufruct</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal right granting a person the use and enjoyment of another's property and its fruits, which may be acquired by legal disposition, preemption, or prescription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Use">
  <data key="d0">Right of Use</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A limited real right permitting a person to use another's property, classified alongside the right of usufruct and the right of occupation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Right of Occupation">
  <data key="d0">Right of Occupation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A real right allowing a person to occupy or reside in a property, classified alongside the right of usufruct and the right of use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Natural Fruits">
  <data key="d0">Natural Fruits</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Products naturally generated by property, deemed collected from the moment of their separation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Industrial Fruits">
  <data key="d0">Industrial Fruits</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Products generated through human labor on property, deemed collected from the moment of separation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Fruits">
  <data key="d0">Legal Fruits</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Revenue or income derived from property through legal relationships, deemed collected day by day.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Necessary Expenditure">
  <data key="d0">Necessary Expenditure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Essential costs incurred for the preservation of property, which the owner is obligated to reimburse to the possessor upon restitution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Advantageous Expenditure">
  <data key="d0">Advantageous Expenditure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Costs that enhance the value of property, governed by the provisions of Articles 924 and 925 regarding reimbursement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Luxurious Expenditure">
  <data key="d0">Luxurious Expenditure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-essential ornamental costs incurred on property, for which the possessor cannot claim repayment but may remove the works if the property is restored to its original condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standing Crops">
  <data key="d0">Standing Crops</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Crops growing on land subject to a usufruct at the time of its termination, for which the usufructuary or heirs may retain the land until harvest upon payment of rent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Suspension of Prescription">
  <data key="d0">Suspension of Prescription</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The halting of the running of the acquisitive prescription period, which occurs whenever a qualifying cause for suspension exists, regardless of the length of the prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interruption of Prescription">
  <data key="d0">Interruption of Prescription</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The breaking of the acquisitive prescription period, occurring when the possessor abandons or loses possession even by the act of a third party, unless possession is recovered or legal proceedings are initiated within one year.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Charges">
  <data key="d0">Normal Charges</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Regular recurring costs and maintenance expenses in respect of property subject to a usufruct, which the usufructuary is obligated to bear during the continuance of the usufruct.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abnormal Expenses">
  <data key="d0">Abnormal Expenses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Extraordinary costs and heavy repairs not arising from any fault of the usufructuary, which the owner is obligated to bear, with the usufructuary required to pay interest on amounts expended by the owner.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Funds">
  <data key="d0">Public Funds</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Government securities in which the proceeds from the sale of movable property subject to a usufruct are invested when no security is given, with the income thereof paid to the usufructuary.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heir">
  <data key="d0">Heir</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A successor who may inherit the right to retain possession of land with standing crops until harvest after the death of the usufructuary, subject to payment of rent for that period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Disposition">
  <data key="d0">Legal Disposition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A legal act by which the right of usufruct may be acquired or created.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article 717">
  <data key="d0">Article 717</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d2">Article 194 references Article 717 to determine the specific obligations of the heirs of a voluntary agent, applying the same rules as those for the heirs of a mandatary.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<edge source="Life Assurance" target="Insurer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insurer provides life assurance coverage and is bound to pay the sum assured, but is not entitled to subrogation against persons causing the insured event.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual obligation,coverage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Assurance" target="Table of Rates">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Life assurance may be rendered void if the true age of the life assured exceeds the limit specified in the table of rates.</data>
  <data key="d9">age limit,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Assurance" target="Beneficiary">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The beneficiary is designated to receive the sum assured under life assurance, and the insurer cannot be subrogated to the beneficiary's rights against persons causing the insured event.</data>
  <data key="d9">entitlement,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Fire Insurance" target="Insurer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insurer under fire insurance is liable for all fire-related damage including salvage measures damage, loss during fire, and damage from defects in the insured thing, and is entitled to subrogation against the author of the damaging act.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual obligation,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Fire Insurance" target="Theft">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insurer under fire insurance is not liable for loss or disappearance of insured things during a fire if the insurer can establish that the loss was caused by theft.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,liability limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Egypt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Suretyship provisions require the surety to be a solvent person residing in Egypt, or alternatively the debtor may provide adequate real security.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal requirement,residence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Commercial Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Suretyship entered into in respect of a commercial debt is deemed a civil act even if the surety is a trader, except for guarantees arising from negotiable instruments.</data>
  <data key="d9">civil act,classification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Surety">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The surety is the central party in the suretyship contract, bound to perform the obligation if the debtor defaults, and must be solvent and reside in Egypt.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual role,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Joint and Several Liability">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Joint and several liability applies to sureties either when stipulated in the contract or by operation of law in judicial and legal suretyship, eliminating the surety's right to demand distrainment of the debtor's property first.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal concept,liability structure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Real Security">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When both real security and suretyship exist for the same debt, the real security must be realized before the surety's property can be seized.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt guarantee,priority of execution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Novation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Suretyship is only transferred to the new obligation with the consent of the sureties and joint and several co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Original Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Suretyship provides real, personal, or joint and several guarantees for the original obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">co-debtor,guarantee</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suretyship" target="Article 358">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 358 requires consent of sureties and co-debtors for transferring suretyship to the new obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurer" target="Insured">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insurer is liable to compensate the insured for covered events, including unintentional fault and force majeure, but not for deliberate or fraudulent damage caused by the insured.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,coverage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurer" target="Sum Assured">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insurer is obligated to pay the sum assured, which may be reduced proportionally if the agreed premium was lower than the correct premium based on the true age of the life assured.</data>
  <data key="d9">adjustment,payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurer" target="Subrogation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insurer has the right of subrogation in fire insurance to pursue claims against third parties causing the damage, but this right does not apply in life assurance.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal right,limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurer" target="Judicial Deposit">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the insured thing has been placed under judicial deposit and the insurer is duly notified, the insurer is prohibited from paying any sum due to the insured.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal notification,payment restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurer" target="Assured">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Insurer is released from the obligation to pay the Sum Assured if the Life Assured commits suicide, unless the suicide was caused by illness depriving the Life Assured of control or occurred after two years from the contract date.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract release,suicide</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurer" target="Beneficiary">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Insurer is obligated to pay the Sum Assured or Annuity to the Beneficiary upon occurrence of the event insured against or on the maturity date.</data>
  <data key="d9">insurance benefit,payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurer" target="Indemnification">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Insurer's obligation under Indemnification is limited to compensating the Assured only for actual loss arising from the insured event, up to the amount insured.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,loss limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insured" target="Premium">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insured is responsible for paying the agreed premium, and incorrect particulars affecting the premium result in proportional adjustments to the sum assured.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insured" target="Pledge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the insured thing is encumbered with a pledge, the rights under the pledge transfer to the insurance compensation due to the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation transfer,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insured" target="Mortgage">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the insured thing is encumbered with a mortgage, the rights under the mortgage transfer to the insurance compensation due to the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation transfer,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insured" target="Force Majeure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The insured is covered for damage resulting from fortuitous events of force majeure, for which the insurer is held liable.</data>
  <data key="d9">coverage,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insured" target="Salvage Measures">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Damage caused to insured things by salvage measures taken during a fire is included in the insurer's liability to the insured.</data>
  <data key="d9">damage coverage,protective action</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">4.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Surety guarantees the debt to the Creditor and is released when the Debtor's tender is accepted or declared valid and the Debtor retracts with the Creditor's acceptance.&lt;SEP&gt;The surety undertakes to the creditor to fulfil the debtor's obligation if the debtor fails to do so, and the creditor must first proceed against the debtor before pursuing the surety.&lt;SEP&gt;The creditor may only take proceedings against the surety after first proceeding against the debtor and exhausting the debtor's property, unless the surety is jointly and severally liable.&lt;SEP&gt;The creditor is bound to hand over to the surety all documents necessary for the right of action upon discharge of the debt, and must comply with formalities for transferring any immovable property security.</data>
  <data key="d9">document surrender,guarantee,guarantee enforcement,legal proceedings,security,security transfer,undertaking</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The surety guarantees the debtor's obligation, is discharged simultaneously with the debtor, and may revoke the guarantee for future debts if no duration was fixed and the debt has not yet been created.&lt;SEP&gt;A surety must notify the debtor before paying the debt; failure to do so results in forfeiture of the surety's right of action against the debtor if the debtor has already paid or has grounds to have the debt declared void or extinguished.</data>
  <data key="d9">discharge,forfeiture of rights,guarantee,notice requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Future Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The surety may enter into a guarantee for a future debt if the amount is fixed beforehand, and may revoke the guarantee at any time if no duration was fixed and the guaranteed debt has not yet been created.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee limit,revocation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Accessories of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In the absence of an express agreement, suretyship extends to the accessories of the debt, including expenses of the first demand for payment and expenses incurred after notice to the surety.</data>
  <data key="d9">coverage extension,default scope</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Subrogation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A surety who has paid the debt is subrogated to all of the creditor's rights against the debtor; if only part of the debt is paid, subrogation applies only after the creditor has recovered the full amount from the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt recovery,legal right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Sub-Surety">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A sub-surety guarantees the principal surety's obligation, and the creditor may not proceed against the sub-surety before taking action against the principal surety unless both are jointly and severally liable.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee of surety,secondary liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A surety who has paid the debt is subrogated to all of the creditor's rights against the debtor and may recover the capital, interest, and expenses from the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment,recovery,subrogation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Lessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor benefits from the surety's guarantee for the lessee's obligations, but the suretyship does not transfer to a tacitly renewed lease without the surety's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,guarantee</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Purge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Every surety of an inscribed debt has the right to apply for the sale of the property that a third party holder wishes to purge.</data>
  <data key="d9">opposition,sale application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Surety" target="Assignment of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A surety, whether real or personal, does not remain bound to the creditor in an assignment of debt unless he has agreed to the assignment.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,guarantee</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Grantor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the Grantor fails to fulfil his obligation, the Beneficiary may demand due performance of the contract or, if the contract is for valuable consideration, apply for its resiliation together with damages.&lt;SEP&gt;The Grantor is obligated to pay periodic Life Annuity payments to the Beneficiary, and failure to perform entitles the Beneficiary to demand performance or contract resiliation.</data>
  <data key="d9">annuity payment,contract,contractual breach,obligation,remedies</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Stipulation for Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stipulation for a third party gives the beneficiary a direct right against the promisor and the ability to demand performance.</data>
  <data key="d9">confers rights,direct claim</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Stipulator">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The stipulator may revoke the stipulation in favor of the beneficiary before the beneficiary claims the benefit, substitute a new beneficiary, or retain the benefit for himself.&lt;SEP&gt;The stipulator stipulates an obligation for the benefit of a third party and may revoke the stipulation before the beneficiary accepts it.</data>
  <data key="d9">creates benefit,revocation,revocation right,stipulation,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Promisor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The promisor undertakes to perform an obligation for the benefit of the beneficiary and may set up against the beneficiary the defenses arising out of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">defenses,obligation performance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Wakf Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Beneficiary is entitled to benefit from wakf property but cannot independently grant a lease without authorization from the Nazir, Judge, or Constituent.</data>
  <data key="d9">entitlement,restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Constituent of Wakf">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Constituent of Wakf may delegate leasing authority to the Beneficiary if designated as the sole beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d9">delegation,empowerment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Article 629">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 629 prohibits payment of rent to the Beneficiary without the consent of the Nazir.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment restriction,rent collection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Policy of Insurance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Assured may nominate the Beneficiary in the Policy of Insurance, or reserve the right to nominate persons at a later date.</data>
  <data key="d9">benefit entitlement,nomination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Attempted Homicide">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If a Beneficiary commits Attempted Homicide against the Life Assured, the Assured has the right to substitute that Beneficiary with another person, even if the Beneficiary had already accepted the insurance.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary substitution,intentional harm</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Beneficiary" target="Assured">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the Beneficiary deliberately causes or attempts to cause the death of the Life Assured, the Assured may substitute the Beneficiary with another person even after the Beneficiary has accepted the insurance.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary substitution,intentional harm</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Premium" target="Assured">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Assured pays the Premium to the Insurer as consideration for the insurance coverage.</data>
  <data key="d9">consideration,financial obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sum Assured" target="Age Misstatement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Age Misstatement results in a proportional reduction of the Sum Assured corresponding to the ratio between the premium agreed upon and the premium that should have been paid based on the true age.</data>
  <data key="d9">premium adjustment,proportional reduction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Legal Capacity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Legal capacity provisions apply to all persons who fulfill the conditions set by Egyptian legislative provisions.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,personal status</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Marriage">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If one spouse is Egyptian at the time of marriage, Egyptian law alone applies to the marriage's conditions and effects.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,territorial application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Inheritance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Inheritance and wills are governed by the national law of the deceased or testator at the time of death, with Egyptian law applying in certain circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,succession law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Contractual Obligations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Contractual obligations are governed by the law of the common domicile of contracting parties or the law of the place of contract conclusion, with Egyptian law applying for immovable contracts in Egypt.</data>
  <data key="d9">choice of law,legal governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Non-Contractual Obligations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Non-contractual obligations arising from torts are governed by the law of the place where the act occurred, with Egyptian law applying exceptions for acts lawful in Egypt but unlawful abroad.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,tort law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge determines the applicable law for persons of unknown or plural nationality, with Egyptian law applying when a person holds Egyptian nationality alongside foreign nationalities.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,legal determination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Foreign Juristic Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If a foreign juristic person carries on its principal activities in Egypt, Egyptian law governs its legal status regardless of where its principal seat of management is located.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,territorial application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Alimony">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Alimony obligations between relatives are governed by the national law of the debtor, with Egyptian law applying when the debtor is Egyptian.</data>
  <data key="d9">family obligation,legal governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Guardianship">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Guardianship and protective systems for persons without legal capacity are governed by the national law of the person to be protected.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,personal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Tort">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acts occurring abroad that are considered lawful in Egypt are not subject to foreign law even if the act is considered unlawful in the country where it occurred.</data>
  <data key="d9">public policy,territorial exception</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Egyptian Nationality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Egyptian nationality is governed by a special law applicable in Egypt.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal status,nationality</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Public Policy">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Public policy in Egypt serves as a legal standard for refusing the application of foreign law provisions contrary to it.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal standard,public order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Private International Law">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a foreign law is applicable in Egypt, only its internal provisions apply, excluding those relating to private international law.</data>
  <data key="d9">foreign law,legal exclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egypt" target="Morality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Morality in Egypt serves as a legal ground for refusing to apply foreign law provisions contrary to it.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal standard,public order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Right of Retention">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of retention follows preservation rules applicable to pledges, including the duty to preserve the thing and account for its fruits.</data>
  <data key="d9">preservation rules,security law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Possessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The possessor exercising the right of retention must preserve the thing in accordance with the rules applicable to pledge.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal analogy,preservation duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Mortgage">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The provisions relating to mortgage, including those on loss, deterioration, and transfer of creditor rights, apply equally to pledge.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicable provisions,legal equivalence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="The Thing Pledged">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledge constitutes a real right over the thing pledged, allowing the creditor to retain it until repayment and obtain payment from its sale in preference to other creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">real right,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Only items capable of being sold independently by public auction may be the object of a pledge, establishing a fundamental eligibility requirement for pledged property.</data>
  <data key="d9">eligibility,sale requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor is the beneficiary of the pledge, which constitutes a real right in favor of the creditor to secure the repayment of debt, granting rights of retention and preference over other creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual benefit,security right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Debtor who leaves a Pledge with the creditor as security for a debt is deemed to have tacitly acknowledged the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">security,tacit acknowledgment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Prescription Interruption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Pledge left with the creditor constitutes a tacit acknowledgment that triggers Prescription Interruption.</data>
  <data key="d9">acknowledgment,interruption trigger</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge" target="Privileged Right">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Privileged Rights are extinguished in the same way and in accordance with the same rules as a Pledge, as stated in Article 1136.</data>
  <data key="d9">analogous application,extinguishment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Immovable Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgage can only be constituted on immovable property that is marketable, capable of being sold by public auction, and specifically described.</data>
  <data key="d9">constitution,encumbrance,real right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Mortgagor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgagor grants the mortgage and must be the owner of the mortgaged property with legal capacity to dispose of it, bearing the costs of the authentic document unless otherwise agreed.&lt;SEP&gt;The mortgagor grants the mortgage over their property and is the guarantor of its effectiveness.</data>
  <data key="d9">creation,grant,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Mortgagee Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgagee creditor benefits from the mortgage, acquiring a real right of preference over ordinary and subordinate creditors for payment of the claim from the proceeds of the mortgaged property.</data>
  <data key="d9">benefit,creditor right,preference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Authentic Document">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgage can only be validly constituted by an authentic document, without which the mortgage is not legally effective.</data>
  <data key="d9">formal requirement,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgaged property must be capable of being sold by public auction, ensuring the mortgagee creditor can enforce the right through sale proceeds.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,marketability,sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Servitude">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgage extends to the accessories of the mortgaged property, including servitudes, improvements, and other works benefiting the owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">accessory,extension,immovable appurtenance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Article 1148">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1148 grants a privilege for sums due to contractors or architects that is preserved without prejudice when a mortgage extends to accessories of the mortgaged property.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractor priority,preserved right,privilege</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgage remains valid in favor of a mortgagee creditor who acted in good faith at the time of its conclusion, even if the mortgagor's title is later annulled or abolished.</data>
  <data key="d9">protection of creditor,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Fruits And Revenues">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">From the date of transcription of the formal summons to pay, the fruits and revenues of the mortgaged property are assimilated to the immovable and distributed in the same manner as the property price.</data>
  <data key="d9">assimilation,distribution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Formal Summons To Pay">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The transcription of the formal summons to pay triggers the assimilation of the mortgaged property's fruits and revenues to the immovable for distribution purposes.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,trigger event</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Future Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgage on future property or property in expectancy is void and cannot be validly constituted.</data>
  <data key="d9">invalidity,void contract</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgage encumbers the mortgaged property, with every part of the property securing the whole debt and each part of the debt secured by the whole property.&lt;SEP&gt;The Mortgage is a legal encumbrance on the Mortgaged Property serving as security for a debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">encumbrance,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Mortgagee">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgagee holds the mortgage and may enforce it by taking expropriation proceedings or assigning rank to another creditor.&lt;SEP&gt;The mortgagee holds the mortgage right and may enforce it through expropriation and sale of the mortgaged property.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,enforcement,holds</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgage secures the debt owed by the debtor and cannot be separated from it in terms of validity and extinction.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt security,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Inscription">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgage ranks from the date of its inscription, and the inscription has the effect of automatically collocating costs and interest with the mortgage debt.&lt;SEP&gt;Inscription of the mortgage deed or judgment establishes its legal effectiveness and enforceability against third parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">ranking,registration,validation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Code of Procedure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Code of Procedure governs the delays and formalities for expropriation and sale of the mortgaged property by the creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,procedural governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Co-owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A co-owner may grant a mortgage on their undivided share or a divided part of the immovable property held in common.</data>
  <data key="d9">grant,shared ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Partition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Partition of co-owned property may result in the transfer of the mortgage, with its degree of priority, to a portion of newly attributed property of equivalent value.</data>
  <data key="d9">reallocation,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Pactum Commissorium">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A pactum commissorium, which allows the creditor to acquire the mortgaged property at a fixed price upon non-payment or sell it without legal formalities, is void under mortgage law.</data>
  <data key="d9">prohibition,void agreement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Mortgage Deed">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgage deed is the formal instrument constituting the mortgage and must specify the amount of the secured debt or its maximum attainable amount.</data>
  <data key="d9">amount fixation,documentation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Bankruptcy">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Bankruptcy provisions create an exception to the general rule that a mortgage is effective against third parties only if inscribed before they acquire real rights on the property.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,priority rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party holder acquires the mortgaged property without personal liability for the debt and may purge the property of all mortgages under prescribed procedures.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,purge rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The inscription, renewal, radiation, and annulment of radiation of mortgages are governed by the Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Section III: Extinguishment of the Mortgage">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section III governs the extinguishment of the Mortgage, which occurs when the secured debt is extinguished, purge formalities are completed, or compulsory sale proceeds are deposited.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal termination,statutory provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Judgment Charge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judgment charge is governed by the same provisions as a mortgage, especially regarding inscription, renewal, radiation, indivisibility, effect, and extinguishment.</data>
  <data key="d9">governing provisions,legal equivalence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgage" target="Privileged Right">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Provisions relating to Mortgages apply to Privileged Rights over immovable property where not incompatible with their nature, and privileges are extinguished in the same manner as mortgages, as stated in Articles 1134, 1135, and 1136.</data>
  <data key="d9">analogous application,legal analogy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subrogation" target="Joint and Several Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A joint and several debtor who pays the full debt may exercise subrogation by bringing the creditor's action against other co-debtors, but recovery is limited to each co-debtor's respective share.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt recovery,right of action</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subrogation" target="Inscription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Legal or contractual subrogation into a right secured by an inscription is enforceable against third parties only if inscribed in the margin of the original inscription.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability,registration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subrogation" target="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder is subrogated into the rights of creditors discharged by payment, including guarantees furnished by the Debtor but excluding those furnished by other parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor rights,legal substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subrogation" target="Article 494">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 494 establishes that in a case of dispossession, the donee is subrogated into the rights and actions of the donor.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal right,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subrogation" target="Donee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Through subrogation, the donee acquires the rights and actions of the donor in cases of dispossession of the gifted thing.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal substitution,rights transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subrogation" target="Article 326">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 326 enumerates the specific cases in which a third party who makes payment is subrogated into the rights of the creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">enumeration of cases,legal basis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Subrogation" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Subrogation substitutes the paying third party into the rights of the creditor to the amount of sums paid, with all attributes, accessories, securities, and defenses attached to the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal effect,substitution of rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Bankruptcy" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a debtor becomes bankrupt, the creditor is bound to prove the debt in the bankruptcy proceedings under penalty of losing the remedy against the surety.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt proof,legal proceeding</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495981</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Bankruptcy" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Declaration of bankruptcy or insolvency causes the debtor to forfeit the benefit of the term on obligations, as stated in Article 273.&lt;SEP&gt;The debtor's bankruptcy or insolvency constitutes grounds for compelling the debtor to redeem the annuity.</data>
  <data key="d9">forced redemption,forfeiture,insolvency,legal consequence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Commercial Debt" target="Negotiable Instruments">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Suretyship resulting from backing signatures or endorsements on negotiable instruments is always deemed a commercial act, creating an exception to the general rule that suretyship for a commercial debt is a civil act.</data>
  <data key="d9">commercial classification,exception</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495961</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="Contract of Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Contract of Service may be dissolved on account of Force Majeure that prevents the worker from continuing work, subject to rules laid down in special laws.</data>
  <data key="d9">dissolution,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="Unlawful Act">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Force majeure and other causes beyond a person's control, such as unforeseen circumstances or the fault of the victim or a third party, exempt that person from liability for injury.</data>
  <data key="d9">beyond control,exemption,liability defense</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Force majeure events may entitle the Lessee to full or partial rent remission, reduction, or relief from lease obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">excuse of performance,rent relief</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="Amodiation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In amodiation, losses from force majeure are borne equally by both the Lessor and Lessee, with no recourse between them.</data>
  <data key="d9">risk allocation,shared loss</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="The Thing Pledged">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgor is responsible for loss or deterioration of the thing pledged when caused by force majeure or by the pledgor's own negligence.</data>
  <data key="d9">deterioration,liability,loss</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="Article 673">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 673 allows the concessionaire to repudiate responsibility for service interruptions by proving they were caused by force majeure or fortuitous events.</data>
  <data key="d9">liability exemption,unforeseeable event</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="Fortuitous Event">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Fortuitous Event is distinguished from Force Majeure as a separate legal ground for the concessionaire to repudiate responsibility for abnormal service interruptions.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal distinction,liability exemption</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Force Majeure" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor is not liable for damages if the impossibility of performance arose from force majeure beyond the debtor's control, unless the debtor agreed to bear such liability.</data>
  <data key="d9">liability exemption,unforeseen events</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Real Security" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a debtor undertakes to offer a surety, the debtor may alternatively provide adequate real security instead of producing a solvent surety.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative security,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495981</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Retention" target="Article 246">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 246 establishes the right of retention and its conditions, including the possessor's right to withhold return of a thing.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,right establishment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Retention" target="Possessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The possessor may exercise the right of retention over a thing on which necessary or useful expenditure has been incurred, refusing to return it until repayment.</data>
  <data key="d9">lawful withholding,security rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Retention" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of retention allows a person to refrain from performing obligations to a creditor until the creditor performs connected reciprocal obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;When a debt is secured by a right of retention on a movable, the creditor must surrender such security to the surety upon discharge of the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional performance,security rights,security transfer,surrender obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Retention" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of retention allows withholding performance from a debtor who has not fulfilled connected obligations or provided adequate security.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation enforcement,security rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Retention" target="Vendor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Vendor is entitled to exercise the Right of Retention to withhold delivery of the thing sold until the Purchaser pays the price due, as established in Article 459.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal right,payment security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Retention" target="Article 460">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 460 allocates the risk of loss during the Vendor's exercise of the Right of Retention to the Purchaser, unless the loss is caused by the Vendor's own act.</data>
  <data key="d9">risk allocation,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Deposit" target="Code of Procedure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judicial Deposit must be made in the manner prescribed by the Code of Procedure.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,procedural law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Deposit" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court authorizes the Judicial Deposit upon application by the Debtor when the Creditor refuses payment or other justified circumstances exist.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,judicial order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Deposit" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Debtor may resort to Judicial Deposit when the Creditor refuses payment, is unknown, incapable, or when the debt is disputed between several persons.</data>
  <data key="d9">discharge,safekeeping</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Deposit" target="Actual Tender">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Actual Tender must be followed by a Judicial Deposit or equivalent measure for it to be considered equivalent to payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">equivalence to payment,sequence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Deposit" target="Pledgor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgor has the right to demand that the thing pledged be placed in judicial deposit if the pledgee mismanages the pledged property, is guilty of bad management, or commits gross negligence.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal remedy,mismanagement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Depositor" target="Depository">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Depositor must pay agreed remuneration upon termination of the deposit and reimburse the Depository for preservation expenses and indemnify against losses resulting from the deposit.&lt;SEP&gt;The Depositor entrusts property to the Depository for safekeeping under a deposit contract, and the Depository must safeguard and return the thing deposited.</data>
  <data key="d9">custody,deposit,expenses,indemnification,obligation,remuneration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Depository" target="Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Depository is bound to return the deposited thing to the Owner upon demand, unless the deposit term was fixed for the Depository's benefit.</data>
  <data key="d9">restitution,return</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Deposit" target="Contract of Loan for Consumption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Deposit converts into a Contract of Loan for Consumption when the deposited object is consumable and the Depository is authorized to use it.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,consumable goods,conversion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heir of Depository" target="Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Heir of Depository who sells the deposited thing in good faith is liable to refund the price received or assign rights against the Purchaser to the Owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,liability,sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heir of Depository" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Heir of Depository may sell the deposited thing to the Purchaser in good faith, creating rights that may be assigned to the Owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">alienation,sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Boundary Marks">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Every owner has the right to compel neighbors to place boundary marks on adjoining properties.</data>
  <data key="d9">delimitation,legal right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Neighbor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The cost of placing boundary marks is shared between the owner and the neighbor.</data>
  <data key="d9">boundary delimitation,cost sharing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Party Wall">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An owner of a party wall may use it for its intended purpose, heighten it for good reason, and must bear the cost of heightening and maintenance of the heightened part.</data>
  <data key="d9">structural modification,usage rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Right of Way">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An owner whose land is cut off from a public road may claim a right of way over neighboring property subject to fair compensation.</data>
  <data key="d9">access rights,easement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Person in Charge of Animal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person in charge of an animal may be different from the owner, yet still bears liability for harm caused by the animal.</data>
  <data key="d9">custody,distinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Person in Charge of Building">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person in charge of a building may be different from the owner, yet still bears liability for damage caused by its collapse.</data>
  <data key="d9">custody,distinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Sale Of A Thing Belonging To Another">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The owner's ratification of a sale made by a non-owner makes the contract binding on the owner and valid as regards the purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal validity,ratification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Possessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The possessor must return the property to the owner, while the owner is obligated to reimburse necessary expenditures and may choose to retain improvements made by the possessor.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,expenditure,restitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Necessary Expenditure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The owner to whom property is restituted must pay the possessor all necessary expenditures incurred.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,reimbursement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Advantageous Expenditure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Reimbursement of advantageous expenditures is governed by the provisions of Articles 924 and 925.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provisions,reimbursement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Luxurious Expenditure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The owner is not required to reimburse luxurious expenditures, though the possessor may remove the works unless the owner elects to keep them upon payment of their break-up value.</data>
  <data key="d9">no reimbursement,removal rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Usufructuary">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The usufructuary must use the property according to its intended purpose, observe good management, and the owner may object to unlawful or unsuitable use and demand security.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligations,preservation,use</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Owner" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may, at the request of the owner, determine the method of repayment of expenses, including ordering periodical installments with necessary security.</data>
  <data key="d9">installments,repayment method</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor" target="Traveller">
  <data key="d7">4.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Hotel Proprietors are responsible for safeguarding the effects brought by Travellers, with liability limited to L.E. 50 for valuables unless gross negligence or refusal to accept custody is proven.&lt;SEP&gt;The Traveller must inform the Hotel Proprietor of theft, loss, or damage without delay, and legal action prescribes six months after leaving the establishment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hotel Proprietor has a privilege over the Traveller's effects brought into the hotel for unpaid accommodation, provisions, and expenses, as established in Article 1144.&lt;SEP&gt;The hotel proprietor is owed sums by the traveller for accommodation, food, and expenses incurred on the traveller's account.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt,hotel charges,liability,limitation,notice,prescription,privilege over effects,safekeeping,service relationship,time limit</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor" target="Hotel Staff">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Hotel Proprietor bears full liability for loss or damage caused by the gross negligence of Hotel Staff, removing the L.E. 50 limitation on compensation.</data>
  <data key="d9">negligence,vicarious liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor" target="Lessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Hotel Proprietor's privilege holds the same rank as the Lessor's privilege, and if they conflict, the earlier in date prevails, as stated in Article 1144.</data>
  <data key="d9">equal ranking,privilege comparison</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor" target="Holder in Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Hotel Proprietor is deemed to be a Holder in Good Faith of effects brought into the hotel by travellers for purposes of privilege enforcement, as established in Article 1133.</data>
  <data key="d9">deemed status,legal fiction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor" target="Hotel Proprietor's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The hotel proprietor is the holder and beneficiary of the hotel proprietor's privilege over the traveller's effects.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor's privilege over movable operates against the hotel proprietor if it can be proved the hotel proprietor had knowledge of the privilege when the movable was brought into the hotel.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability,knowledge requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Receiver" target="Judicial Custody">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Receiver is entrusted under Judicial Custody to preserve, manage, and return property subject to litigation, keeping regular accounts and rendering annual reports.</data>
  <data key="d9">accountability,management,safeguarding</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Receiver" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge appoints the Receiver when interested parties cannot agree, and may order the Receiver's books to be stamped by the Court.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,authority,oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Receiver" target="Interested Parties">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Receiver must render annual accounts of receipts and expenditure to Interested Parties, may not appoint one of them as substitute without the others' consent, and must not perform non-administrative acts without their unanimous consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">accountability,consent,reporting</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Receiver" target="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder may be appointed as receiver if applying to the Judge Des Referes during expropriation proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,property management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Receiver" target="Judge Des Referes">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge Des Referes has the authority to appoint a Receiver against whom expropriation proceedings may be taken when a party with interest requests expedition of the sale.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,property management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Custody" target="Wakf Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Judicial Custody may be ordered over Wakf Property when the Nazir's office is vacant, disputed, or subject to removal, or when the Wakf is indebted or a beneficiary is insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d9">administration,judicial order,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Custody" target="Nazir">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Judicial Custody may be triggered by the vacancy of the Nazir, litigation between co-nazirs, removal proceedings, or wrongful administration by the Nazir, and ends upon appointment of a new Nazir.</data>
  <data key="d9">oversight,removal,vacancy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Custody" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may order Judicial Custody of immovable property or things intended to remain in place at the Debtor's request.</data>
  <data key="d9">court order,safekeeping</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Custody" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Creditor who has reasonable grounds to apprehend misappropriation of movables charged with a privilege in his favor may apply to have them placed in Judicial Custody, as provided in Article 1133.</data>
  <data key="d9">asset protection,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Creditors">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Creditors' rights against the Wakf are safeguarded through judicial custody when threatened by the Nazir's wrongful administration or bad faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">rights protection,wrongful administration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Wakf Property">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Nazir has the exclusive authority to administer, lease, and collect rent for wakf property.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nazir is the administrator of wakf property and is authorized to lease it, subject to the three-year limit without judicial authorization.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nazir is responsible for administering and managing the Wakf Property, and the quality of that administration determines whether judicial custody is imposed.</data>
  <data key="d9">administration,leasing authority,management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge provides authorization for the Nazir to lease wakf property for periods exceeding three years.&lt;SEP&gt;The Nazir must obtain the judge's authority to lease wakf property for a period exceeding three years.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,judicial oversight,oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Article 630">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 630 prohibits the Nazir from leasing wakf property to himself while permitting leases to ascendants and descendants at current market rent.</data>
  <data key="d9">conflict of interest,self-dealing prohibition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Article 633">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 633 limits the Nazir's authority to lease wakf property to three years without judicial authorization from the Judge.</data>
  <data key="d9">duration limit,judicial oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Article 629">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 629 confirms the Nazir as the exclusive person entitled to receive rent for wakf property and prohibits payment to the Beneficiary without the Nazir's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusive authority,rent collection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Founder (Waqif)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the Nazir is also the founder, the Nazir may lease wakf property for more than three years without the judge's authority, subject to a succeeding Nazir's right to reduce the term.</data>
  <data key="d9">enhanced authority,role overlap</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Sole Beneficiary">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the Nazir is also the sole beneficiary, the Nazir may lease wakf property beyond three years without judicial authorization.</data>
  <data key="d9">enhanced authority,role overlap</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nazir" target="Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Nazir, as administrator of wakf property, leases it to the Lessee subject to judicial oversight and the three-year lease limit.</data>
  <data key="d9">leasing relationship,wakf tenancy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf Property" target="Servitude">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A servitude established for the benefit of a wakf property is subject to an extended thirty-three-year non-user period for extinguishment, compared to the standard fifteen-year period.</data>
  <data key="d9">extended prescription,non-user period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf Property" target="Buried Treasure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Treasure discovered on wakf property belongs specifically to the founder of the wakf or to his heirs rather than the property owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">discovery,ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf Property" target="Article 631">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 631 renders leases of Wakf Property invalid when the rent is grossly inadequate, with an exception for sole beneficiaries with administrative power.</data>
  <data key="d9">lease validity,rent adequacy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf Property" target="Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Lessee leases wakf property and is bound to make up the rent to match similar properties if the agreed rent is grossly inadequate, under penalty of resiliation.</data>
  <data key="d9">lease obligation,rent adjustment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf Property" target="Acquisitive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Wakf property and hereditary rights require thirty-three years of possession for acquisition by prescription, a longer period than standard.</data>
  <data key="d9">extended period,special rule</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gaming and Betting" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Gaming and Betting agreements are void, but the Judge has authority to reduce excessive stakes in sports bets and to allow exceptions for legally authorized lotteries.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,exception,voidability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gaming and Betting" target="Aleatory Contracts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Gaming and Betting is a subcategory of Aleatory Contracts, governed as Section I of Chapter IV.</data>
  <data key="d9">category,inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Annuity" target="Will">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Life Annuity obligation may be created by Will as an alternative to creation by contract, allowing testamentary establishment of periodic payment commitments.</data>
  <data key="d9">creation,testamentary disposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Annuity" target="Contract of Gift">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Life Annuity settled gratuitously must satisfy both the writing requirement and the special formal requirements applicable to Contracts of Gift under the law.</data>
  <data key="d9">form requirements,gratuitous settlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Annuity" target="Aleatory Contracts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Life Annuity is a subcategory of Aleatory Contracts, governed as Section II of Chapter IV.</data>
  <data key="d9">category,inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Insurance" target="Aleatory Contracts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Contract of Insurance is a subcategory of Aleatory Contracts, governed as Section III of Chapter IV.</data>
  <data key="d9">category,inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e39f1cce9ca69678644a7e31968b2821</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditors" target="Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Creditors hold claims against the Estate for unpaid debts and are entitled to payment from estate assets, with or without security on estate property.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt claims,secured interests</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditors" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court arranges adequate security for Creditors on estate property and ensures their approval is obtained for non-auction sales when the estate is insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d9">protection,security arrangement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditors" target="Administrator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator discharges estate debts to Creditors using estate funds, proceeds from asset sales, and suspends payments if the estate is insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt settlement,payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will" target="Inalienability Clause">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A will may contain an inalienability clause restricting the disposition of property, valid only if based on a legitimate reason and limited to a reasonable duration.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal restriction,testamentary disposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will" target="Inheritance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Wills are a form of post-mortem disposition governed by the national law of the testator at the time of death, with special rules for the form of the will.</data>
  <data key="d9">post-mortem disposition,succession law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Will may divide the property of the estate among Heirs, setting out each heir's share, with any excess over the hereditary share deemed a legacy.</data>
  <data key="d9">bequest,testamentary division</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will" target="Deceased">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Will is a testamentary disposition made by the Deceased, dividing estate property among heirs and becoming irrevocable upon the death of the testator.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorship,testamentary disposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will" target="Administrator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator executes the provisions of the Will after the settlement of all estate debts.</data>
  <data key="d9">execution,testamentary compliance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will" target="Right of Usufruct">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of usufruct may be bequeathed by will to successive persons alive at the time of the bequest or to an unborn child.</data>
  <data key="d9">bequest,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufruct" target="Right of User and Occupation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The rules governing usufruct apply to the Right of User and Occupation provided they are not incompatible with the nature of these two rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicable rules,legal hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufruct" target="Servitudes">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both servitudes and usufruct are property rights within the same legal code that limit or govern the use of real property.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,property rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufruct" target="Usufructuary">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The usufructuary is the holder of the usufruct right, which terminates upon the usufructuary's death and is subject to the conditions of the creating deed.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal entitlement,rights holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufruct" target="Article 993">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 993 governs the termination of usufruct and provisions regarding standing crops upon termination or death of the usufructuary.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,termination rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufruct" target="Article 994">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 994 governs the extinction of usufruct through loss of property and its transmission to substitute property.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinction by loss,legal governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufruct" target="Article 511">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 511 applies the provisions governing sale warranties to a partner's contribution consisting of a usufruct or other real right.</data>
  <data key="d9">property contribution,warranty provisions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Wakf">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Hekr may only be established on land that is constituted in wakf, and hekr rights are affected by changes in wakf status of the land.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal dependency,property requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Charie Court of First Instance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Hekr requires the permission of the Charie Court of First Instance and must be formalized by a deed drawn up by the President of the Court or a delegated official.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial oversight,legal authorization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Idjaratein">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Idjaratein is a specific type of hekr contract and is subject to the rules governing hekr, with modifications specified in its defining provision.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract subtype,legal applicability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Article 1008">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1008 governs the conditions under which the right of hekr terminates, including at the end of the fixed period or upon specific events.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,termination rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Grantee of Hekr">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The grantee of the hekr holds the right to dispose of it, owns constructions and plantations absolutely, and must maintain the land for exploitation.</data>
  <data key="d9">property rights,rights and obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Article 999">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 999 establishes the maximum duration of hekr at sixty years and provides default rules when no period is fixed.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,maximum duration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Article 1011">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1011 provides that the right of hekr is extinguished by non-user during fifteen years, or thirty-three years if constituted in wakf.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,legal governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Article 1012">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1012 restricts the establishment of hekr to wakf land from the date the law comes into force.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal restriction,wakf requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Heirs of Grantee of Hekr">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The heirs of the grantee may request maintenance of the hekr if the grantee dies before building or planting, as provided under Article 1008.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuation of rights,inheritance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Article 1004">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1004 governs minimum rent for hekr and the adjustment of rent based on changes in the rental value of similar lands.</data>
  <data key="d9">rent regulation,valuation adjustment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hekr" target="Bare Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In case of hekr, the Bare Owner holds preemption rights if the hekr right is sold, and the beneficiary of the hekr holds preemption rights if the bare property is sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal concept,preemption rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Idjaratein" target="Wakf">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Idjaratein is a contract by which a wakf creates a hekr on land containing buildings in need of repair.</data>
  <data key="d9">contracting party,property management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Kholou-el-intifaa" target="Wakf">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Kholou-el-intifaa is a lease contract granted by a wakf for an indefinite period, which the wakf may resiliate at any time with due notice and compensation.</data>
  <data key="d9">lease agreement,property management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitudes" target="Original Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Apparent servitudes may be created by the intention of the original owner when establishing an apparent distinction between two separate properties.</data>
  <data key="d9">creation of rights,property division</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitudes" target="State Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A servitude may be imposed on State property provided it is not incompatible with the use for which the property is intended.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal limitation,property rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitudes" target="Apparent Servitudes">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Apparent servitudes are a subcategory of servitudes that may be created by the intention of the original owner and are the only servitudes acquirable by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal classification,property rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitudes" target="Article 1018">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1018 provides that building restrictions imposed on a property constitute servitude rights for the benefit of other properties.</data>
  <data key="d9">building restrictions,legal governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitudes" target="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Servitudes and other real rights previously held by the Third Party Holder on the property before acquisition are re-vested in the Third Party Holder.</data>
  <data key="d9">re-vested rights,real rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf" target="Founder of Wakf">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The founder of a wakf may revoke the wakf or reduce its period, which affects the continuation of hekr on wakf land under Article 1008.</data>
  <data key="d9">establishment,revocation rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf" target="Preemption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Wakf is explicitly prohibited from exercising the right of preemption under Article 939.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusion,prohibition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wakf" target="Prodigality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The constitution of a wakf by a person under interdiction for prodigality is valid if authorized by the court.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,valid disposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Charie Court of First Instance" target="Article 1000">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1000 requires permission from the Charie Court of First Instance for the establishment of hekr.</data>
  <data key="d9">court authorization,legal procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Charie Court of First Instance" target="President of the Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The President of the Court is the official at the Charie Court of First Instance who draws up the hekr deed or delegates this task.</data>
  <data key="d9">delegation,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Preemption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Usufructuary holds the right of preemption in case of a sale of all or part of the bare property producing the usufruct.</data>
  <data key="d9">right holder,usufruct rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Lease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A usufructuary may grant leases that end when the usufruct is extinguished unless ratified by the bare owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">granting authority,property rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Bare Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A lease granted by a usufructuary requires ratification by the bare owner to survive beyond the extinguishment of the usufruct.</data>
  <data key="d9">property ownership,ratification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Right of Usufruct">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The usufructuary exercises the right of usufruct and is governed by the conditions of the deed creating the usufruct and applicable legal provisions.</data>
  <data key="d9">exercise of rights,obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Natural Fruits">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The usufructuary is entitled to the fruits of the property in proportion to the duration of the usufruct, with natural fruits deemed collected upon separation.</data>
  <data key="d9">collection,entitlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Legal Fruits">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The usufructuary acquires legal fruits day by day during the period of the usufruct.</data>
  <data key="d9">collection,entitlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may remove the property from the usufructuary and entrust it to a third party, or declare the usufruct extinguished, if the usufructuary persists in unlawful or improper use.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,removal,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Normal Charges">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The usufructuary is liable for all normal charges and maintenance expenses in respect of the property subject to the usufruct during the continuance of the usufruct.</data>
  <data key="d9">maintenance,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Abnormal Expenses">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The owner bears abnormal expenses and heavy repairs not caused by the usufructuary, but the usufructuary must pay interest on amounts expended; if the usufructuary advanced the cost, he is entitled to repayment of capital upon termination.</data>
  <data key="d9">capital repayment,interest</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Security">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The owner may demand security from the usufructuary if the owner's rights are endangered, and security is required when the property subject to usufruct is movable.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee,protection of rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Public Funds">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If no security is given for movable property subject to usufruct, the property is sold and proceeds invested in public funds with the income paid to the usufructuary.</data>
  <data key="d9">income allocation,investment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Usufructuary" target="Consumable Things">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A usufructuary who has provided security may use consumable things during the usufruct but must replace them when the usufruct terminates.</data>
  <data key="d9">replacement obligation,use</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Grantee of Hekr" target="Grantor of Hekr">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The grantee of the hekr must pay the agreed rent to the grantor, and the grantor may demand resiliation if rent is unpaid for three consecutive years.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual relationship,rental obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495962</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 179" target="Enrichment Without Just Cause">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 179 is the primary legal provision establishing the principle of enrichment without just cause and the obligation to compensate.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1000" target="Law Relating to the Publication of Real Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1000 requires that hekr deeds be published in accordance with the law relating to the publication of real rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal compliance,publication requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="President of the Court" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Judge may be delegated by the President of the Court to draw up the hekr deed.</data>
  <data key="d9">delegation,official duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="President of the Court" target="Notary">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A notary may be delegated by the President of the Court to draw up the hekr deed.</data>
  <data key="d9">delegation,official duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="President of the Court" target="Insolvency">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The President of the Court competent to declare insolvency may grant an alimentary allowance to the debtor from attached revenues.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor protection,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495965</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="President of the Court" target="Alimentary Allowance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The President of the Court has the power to grant an alimentary allowance to the debtor upon application when creditor revenues have been attached.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor relief,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495965</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="President of the Court" target="Judgment Charge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The President of the Court reviews applications, considers the debt amount and property value, and issues orders authorizing Judgment Charges, potentially restricting the charge to specific properties.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,judicial order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="State Property" target="Acquisitive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">State property and public entity property are excluded from acquisition by prescription entirely.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusion,immunity from prescription</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Apparent Servitudes" target="Rights of Way">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Rights of way are a type of apparent servitude that can be acquired by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal classification,property rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-039f2aa8acd475aa275e39916d71c1e0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Master (رب العمل)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker is employed by the Master under a contract of service, with both parties having reciprocal rights and obligations including performance of work, payment of salary, obedience of orders, and duty of care.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract,employment,obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Contract of Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker is bound by the Contract of Service to personally perform work, obey orders, preserve entrusted items, and safeguard trade secrets.</data>
  <data key="d9">employment,legal obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Non-Competition Agreement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker may be restricted by a Non-Competition Agreement from competing with the master after termination of the contract, provided the worker has attained majority and the restriction is limited as to time, place, and kind of work.</data>
  <data key="d9">post-employment,restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Invention">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker who discovers a new invention during service generally retains rights to it, unless the nature of the work requires inventive effort or the contract expressly assigns rights to the master.</data>
  <data key="d9">intellectual property,rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Trade Secrets">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker is obligated to safeguard Trade Secrets of the employer even after the end of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">confidentiality,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Compensation for Dismissal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker may be entitled to Compensation for Dismissal when the master's actions, such as vexatious treatment or breach of contract, effectively force the worker to terminate the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">remedy,unjustified termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Notice Period">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker must observe the Notice Period when terminating an indefinite contract; failure to do so results in an obligation to compensate the master for the whole or remaining notice period.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker (العامل)" target="Profit Sharing">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker may be entitled to Profit Sharing as part of or in lieu of agreed salary under the contract of service.</data>
  <data key="d9">entitlement,remuneration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master (رب العمل)" target="Non-Competition Agreement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master benefits from the Non-Competition Agreement to protect legitimate business interests, but cannot invoke it if the master himself terminated the contract without justification.</data>
  <data key="d9">legitimate interests,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master (رب العمل)" target="Contract of Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master is bound by the Contract of Service to pay salary, render accounts of profit-sharing, and fulfill obligations imposed by special laws.</data>
  <data key="d9">employment,legal obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master (رب العمل)" target="Salary">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master is obligated to pay the Worker's Salary at the time and place agreed upon in the contract or established by custom.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master (رب العمل)" target="Invention">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master has no automatic rights to a worker's invention, but may acquire rights if the worker's duties require inventive effort or if the contract expressly stipulates it, in which case the worker may claim special remuneration.</data>
  <data key="d9">intellectual property,rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master (رب العمل)" target="Notice Period">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master must observe the Notice Period when terminating an indefinite contract; failure to do so results in an obligation to compensate the worker for the whole or remaining notice period.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master (رب العمل)" target="Profit Sharing">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master is obligated to render the Worker an account of Profit Sharing amounts payable after each balance sheet and to supply information necessary for verification.</data>
  <data key="d9">accounting,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Service" target="Prescription Period">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Actions arising from a Contract of Service are subject to a one-year Prescription Period from termination, except for trade secrets-related actions and commission or profit-sharing claims.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal actions,limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Service" target="Article 674">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 674 provides the legal definition of a Contract of Service as an agreement where one party works under the control of another for remuneration.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract formation,legal definition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Service" target="Article 678">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 678 governs the permissible durations of a Contract of Service and the worker's right to terminate long-term contracts without compensation.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract duration,termination rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Service" target="Article 679">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 679 provides that a fixed-term Contract of Service terminates automatically upon expiration and is deemed renewed indefinitely if performance continues.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract renewal,fixed-term termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Service" target="Article 677">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 677 establishes that a Contract of Service does not require any special form unless law or administrative regulations provide otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d9">form requirements,legal formalities</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Service" target="Article 680">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 680 provides that a Contract of Service for specific work terminates upon completion and may be impliedly renewed if the work is renewable by nature and performance continues.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract termination,implied renewal,specific work</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Tips" target="Common Fund">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Tips given by customers are collected in a Common Fund for distribution to workers by or under the supervision of the employer.</data>
  <data key="d9">collection,distribution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Tips" target="Article 684">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 684 governs the conditions under which Tips are deemed to form part of the employee's salary, particularly in industries such as hotels, restaurants, cafes, and bars.</data>
  <data key="d9">industry custom,salary component</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Non-Competition Agreement" target="Penalty Clause">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Penalty Clause attached to a Non-Competition Agreement is void if so onerous as to pressure the worker, and this voidness extends to the entire non-competition condition.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,voidness</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Special Laws" target="Insurance Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Provisions relating to Insurance Contracts not mentioned in the Code are regulated by Special Laws.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal regulation,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Prescription Period" target="Warranty Against Defects">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Prescription Period limits the time within which the Purchaser may bring a warranty action for defects to one year from delivery of the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal procedure,time limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Prescription Period" target="Article 452">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 452 defines the Prescription Period as one year from delivery for warranty actions and prevents the vendor from invoking it when fraudulent concealment is proven.</data>
  <data key="d9">statutory governance,time limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Prescription Period" target="Fraudulent Concealment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraudulent Concealment prevents the Vendor from invoking the one-year Prescription Period as a defense against warranty claims by the Purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">fraud,legal exception</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandate (الوكالة)" target="Mandatary (الوكيل)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Mandatary is bound by the Mandate to perform a judicial act and, under a general mandate, is only authorized to perform Acts of Management.</data>
  <data key="d9">authority,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Mandator (الموكل)">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Mandatary undertakes to perform a judicial act on behalf of the Mandator under a Mandate contract.&lt;SEP&gt;The mandator appoints the mandatary to perform acts on their behalf, and the mandatary is bound to execute the mandate within its defined limits, inform the mandator of progress, and render accounts.&lt;SEP&gt;The mandator may revoke or restrict the mandate at any time, but must indemnify the mandatary for loss from untimely or unjustified revocation of a remunerated mandate.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,authority,contract,indemnification,obligation,representation,restriction,revocation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495963</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Acts of Management">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under a general mandate without specification, the Mandatary is only authorized to perform Acts of Management such as leases of up to three years, preservation, maintenance, recovery of rights, and payment of debts.&lt;SEP&gt;Under a general mandate, the mandatary is authorized only to perform acts of management such as granting leases, preservation, maintenance, recovery of rights, and discharge of debts.</data>
  <data key="d9">authority,authorization,limitation,scope of power</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Special Mandate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A special mandate is required for the mandatary to perform acts beyond management, such as sale, mortgage, gifts, compromise, arbitration, and court representation.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,legal requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Substitute (نائب الوكيل)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mandatary may nominate a substitute to perform the mandate; liability depends on whether delegation was authorized, with unauthorized delegation making the mandatary fully liable.</data>
  <data key="d9">delegation,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Third Party (الغير)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mandatary deals with third parties on behalf of the mandator, and the relationship is governed by Articles 104 to 107 on representation.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal dealings,representation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Heirs (ورثة)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon the death of the mandatary, the heirs are obligated to inform the mandator and take protective measures if they have legal capacity and knowledge of the mandate.</data>
  <data key="d9">post-mortem obligation,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Joint and Several Liability (التضامن)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When several mandataries are appointed by the same document without authorization to act severally, they must act jointly and are jointly and severally liable if the mandate is indivisible or if damage sustained by the mandator results from their common fault.</data>
  <data key="d9">collective responsibility,multiple agents</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Reasonable Person (الرجل المعتاد)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In a remunerated mandate, the mandatary must always exercise the diligence of a reasonable person in performing the mandate; in a gratuitous mandate, the standard is the care given to own affairs without exceeding the reasonable person threshold.</data>
  <data key="d9">duty of diligence,standard of care</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandatary (الوكيل)" target="Renunciation of Mandate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mandatary may renounce the mandate at any time by notification to the mandator, but must indemnify for loss if the renunciation is untimely or unjustified in a remunerated mandate, and cannot renounce a mandate in the interest of a third party without serious reasons.</data>
  <data key="d9">indemnification,notification,resignation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandator (الموكل)" target="Third Party (الغير)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a mandate is granted in the interest of a third party, the mandator cannot revoke or restrict it without the third party's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,interest,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandator (الموكل)" target="Judge (القاضى)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The agreed remuneration for a mandate is subject to assessment by the Judge, unless it has been voluntarily paid after performance.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial oversight,remuneration assessment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandator (الموكل)" target="Articles 104 to 107">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Articles 104 to 107 regarding representation apply to the legal relationship between the mandator and third parties dealing with the mandatary.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,representation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandator (الموكل)" target="Revocation of Mandate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mandator may revoke or restrict the mandate at any time regardless of contrary agreement, but must indemnify the mandatary for loss from untimely or unjustified revocation of a remunerated mandate, and cannot revoke a mandate in the interest of a third party without consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">indemnification,restriction,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandator (الموكل)" target="Joint and Several Liability (التضامن)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When several persons appoint a sole mandatary for a common purpose, they are jointly and severally liable to the mandatary regarding the consequences of performing the mandate.</data>
  <data key="d9">collective responsibility,multiple principals</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acts of Management" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acts of management carried out by the creditor retain their validity notwithstanding the realization of the resolutory condition, as stated in Article 269.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor actions,validity preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acts of Management" target="Minor Possessing Discretion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A minor possessing discretion who has attained the age of eighteen and has been authorized to take possession of his property may validly perform acts of management within the limits of the law, as provided in Article 112.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,property management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acts of Management" target="Prodigality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person placed under interdiction for prodigality who has been authorized to take possession of his property may validly carry out acts of management within the limits provided by law, as stated in Article 116.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,property management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Profit Sharing" target="Balance Sheet">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master's obligation to render a Profit Sharing account to the Worker is triggered after each Balance Sheet is prepared.</data>
  <data key="d9">accounting,verification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Profit Sharing" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge may designate a trustworthy person to receive information necessary to verify the accuracy of the Master's Profit Sharing account.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial oversight,verification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-deb378e6503c5c768bb0d28eca216bb6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Simulated Contract" target="Ostensible Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A simulated contract involves the creation of an ostensible contract as the visible layer concealing the genuine agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract law,legal simulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Simulated Contract" target="Hidden Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A simulated contract conceals a hidden (genuine) contract that represents the true agreement between parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract law,legal simulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Simulated Contract" target="Article 244">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 244 allows creditors and good faith successors to challenge a simulated contract by invoking the hidden contract and establishing the simulation by any means.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 244 governs the rights of creditors and successors in good faith to use the hidden contract in simulated contract arrangements.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor protection,hidden contract,legal provision,regulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Simulated Contract" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Good faith is a prerequisite for creditors and particular successors to invoke the hidden contract in simulated contract situations.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor protection,legal condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Ostensible Contract" target="Hidden Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The ostensible contract covers the hidden contract; in conflicts of interest, parties relying on the ostensible contract have preference over those relying on the hidden contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract duality,preference rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hidden Contract" target="Article 245">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 245 establishes that the hidden (genuine) contract binds the contracting parties and their universal successors.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding effect,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hidden Contract" target="Particular Successors in Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Particular successors in title in good faith may avail themselves of the hidden contract and establish the simulation of the prejudicial ostensible contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract law,successor rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hidden Contract" target="Universal Successors in Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The hidden (genuine) contract binds universal successors in title together with the original contracting parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding effect,successor rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495965</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Court of First Instance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court of First Instance is the judicial body empowered to declare insolvency by judgment and oversee related proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,legal declaration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Cairo Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Cairo Court maintains a centralized general register of all insolvency declarations and marginal entries transmitted by local courts.</data>
  <data key="d9">central registry,record keeping</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Misappropriation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Misappropriation is a penal consequence for debtors who commit fraud in connection with insolvency proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d9">insolvency abuse,penal offense</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Article 255">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 255 specifies that insolvency declaration renders all debts immediately due, with provisions for judicial extension of time.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt acceleration,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Article 262">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 262 provides that insolvency automatically ceases five years after the registration of the insolvency judgment.</data>
  <data key="d9">automatic termination,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Insolvency is a legal state declared when a debtor's assets are insufficient to pay due debts, making the debtor subject to court supervision and penalties.&lt;SEP&gt;The debtor may be declared insolvent, which accelerates debts and affects the debtor's obligations, but the debtor may reclaim rescheduled debts after cessation of insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d9">financial condition,legal status</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Insolvency affects creditor rights by accelerating debts, limiting debtor dispositions, and permitting individual proceedings against the debtor.&lt;SEP&gt;Cessation of insolvency does not prevent creditors from attacking acts of the debtor or exercising the debtor's rights in accordance with Articles 235 to 243.&lt;SEP&gt;The creditor is responsible to the surety for the debtor's insolvency if the creditor fails to take the necessary proceedings in due time after the surety has indicated the debtor's property.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor liability,creditor rights,debt enforcement,debtor insolvency,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Lessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor may claim resiliation of the lease due to the lessee's insolvency unless adequate securities are provided within a reasonable time.</data>
  <data key="d9">resiliation,security demand</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Joint and Several Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a joint and several debtor becomes insolvent, the insolvent debtor's share is borne pro rata by the co-debtor who effected payment and all other solvent co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">burden sharing,financial inability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Co-debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The share of an insolvent co-debtor is distributed among solvent co-debtors and the paying co-debtor pro rata to their respective shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">insolvent share,pro rata contribution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Release of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a creditor releases a co-debtor from all liability, the creditor bears the released co-debtor's contribution in the share of insolvent co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor burden,released debtor contribution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Greffier of the Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Greffier of the Court records the judgment ending the state of insolvency in the margin of the registration on the day the judgment is delivered.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal procedure,recording</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insolvency" target="Article 239">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 239 allocates the burden of proof regarding insolvency: the creditor establishes the debts and the debtor must prove assets equal or exceed liabilities.</data>
  <data key="d9">asset-liability test,burden of proof</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court of First Instance" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court of First Instance in the district of the debtor's domicile has jurisdiction over insolvency proceedings affecting the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor oversight,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court of First Instance" target="Greffier">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The greffier serves as the registrar of the Court of First Instance, responsible for registering insolvency suits and recording judgments.</data>
  <data key="d9">administrative duty,court officer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court of First Instance" target="Article 250">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 250 designates the Court of First Instance in the debtor's domicile district as the court that declares insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d9">jurisdiction,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court of First Instance" target="Summary Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Summary Court refers disputes outside its competence to the Court of First Instance and fixes a date for appearance.</data>
  <data key="d9">competence,judicial referral</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court of First Instance" target="Abandonment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Declarations of abandonment of mortgaged property are submitted to the Registrar of the competent Court of First Instance.</data>
  <data key="d9">declaration filing,jurisdiction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Minister of Justice" target="Cairo Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Minister of Justice issues regulations (Arrêté) governing the organization and operation of the general register maintained by the Cairo Court.</data>
  <data key="d9">general register,regulatory authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Minister of Justice" target="Egyptian Civil Code">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Minister of Justice is tasked with executing and implementing the Egyptian Civil Code starting from October 15, 1949.</data>
  <data key="d9">execution,implementation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Cairo Court" target="Greffier">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The greffier is required to send copies of insolvency registrations and marginal entries to the Cairo Court for the general register.</data>
  <data key="d9">central filing,record transmission</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Cairo Court" target="Article 253">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 253 requires the greffier to send copies of insolvency registrations and entries to the Cairo Court for the general register.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,registration duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Cairo Court" target="Registrar of the Cairo Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Registrar of the Cairo Court serves within the Cairo Court and makes registration entries on its behalf.</data>
  <data key="d9">affiliation,judicial administration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Greffier" target="Judgment Charge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Greffier is responsible for notifying the debtor of the Judgment Charge order, endorsing it on the judgment copy, and informing the court that rendered the original judgment on the same day.</data>
  <data key="d9">administrative duty,notification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1119" target="Article 247">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 247 refers to Article 1119 for the judicial sale procedure applicable to perishable or deteriorating retained things.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1119" target="Pledge Of A Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1119 authorizes the sale of a pledged movable in danger of perishing or deteriorating, upon application to the Judge.</data>
  <data key="d9">endangered pledged thing,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 244" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 244 conditions the right of creditors and particular successors to use the hidden contract upon their being in good faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition precedent,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495981</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 245" target="Universal Successors in Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 245 provides that the genuine contract binds universal successors in title along with the contracting parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding effect,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 253" target="Article 261">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 261 refers to the registration provided for in Article 253 for recording the judgment ending the state of insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 258" target="Caisse du Tribunal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 258 requires the purchaser to deposit the normal price at the Caisse du Tribunal for distribution among creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">fund deposit,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 259" target="Alimentary Allowance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 259 authorizes the President of the Court to grant an alimentary allowance to the debtor from attached revenues.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor relief,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 260" target="Misappropriation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 260 defines the two cases in which a debtor is liable to penalties for misappropriation related to insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,penal consequences</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 261" target="Article 263">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 261 references Article 263 regarding the restoration of original payment terms for debts after insolvency ceases.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495964</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Misappropriation" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Misappropriation penalties apply to debtors who willfully become insolvent or conceal property in fraud of creditor rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">fraudulent conduct,penal offense</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Misappropriation" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An Heir who fraudulently appropriates a part of the assets of the estate is liable to the penalties for Misappropriation under Article 889.</data>
  <data key="d9">fraudulent appropriation,penalty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possessor" target="Possession">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The possessor exercises actual control over a right or thing and is the central subject of the legal rules on possession.</data>
  <data key="d9">actual control,rights and obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possessor" target="Acquisitive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acquisitive prescription depends on the possessor maintaining continuous possession; it is interrupted if the possessor abandons or loses possession.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition of ownership,possession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possessor" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A possessor in good faith who holds a movable or bearer warrant by virtue of a just title becomes the owner thereof; good faith also limits the possessor's liability for loss and entitles the possessor to collect fruits.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,liability,ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possessor" target="Bad Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A possessor in bad faith is liable for all fruits collected or failed to be collected from the moment of becoming aware, and is responsible for loss or deterioration of the thing even if fortuitous.</data>
  <data key="d9">fruits,liability,responsibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possessor" target="Just Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Possession itself serves as a presumption of just title and good faith unless proven otherwise; just title is required along with good faith for acquiring ownership of movables.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,ownership,presumption</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possessor" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party who purchased stolen or lost property in good faith from a market or merchant may demand reimbursement of the purchase price from the person claiming restitution.</data>
  <data key="d9">good faith purchase,recovery,restitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Caisse du Tribunal" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Caisse du Tribunal holds the proceeds from debtor property dispositions at normal price for distribution to creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">asset distribution,financial depository</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Caisse du Tribunal" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Caisse du Tribunal serves as the depository for funds to be distributed to creditors in accordance with the procedure for distribution.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor payment,financial depository</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Caisse du Tribunal" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Proceeds from a Public Auction are deposited into the Caisse du Tribunal, the court treasury, by order of the judge.</data>
  <data key="d9">court treasury,deposit of proceeds</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Alimentary Allowance" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The alimentary allowance is a payment granted to the debtor from attached revenues to cover living expenses during insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor relief,living expenses</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Alimentary Allowance" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs who were supported by the deceased are entitled to receive an Alimentary Allowance from estate assets pending the final winding up, deductible from their inheritance shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">estate distribution,financial support</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Alimentary Allowance" target="Juge de Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Juge de Service authorizes the payment of Alimentary Allowances to supported heirs and resolves any disputes arising in connection with such allowances.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,dispute resolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Obligation" target="Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract creates obligations that bind the parties; the object of an obligation must be determinate as to kind and quantity, otherwise the contract is void.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding duty,creation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Obligation" target="Novation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Novation replaces the original Obligation with a new one, requiring both obligations to be free from grounds of nullity.</data>
  <data key="d9">new obligation,replacement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Obligation" target="Giving in Payment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Giving in Payment extinguishes the Obligation when the Creditor accepts a substitute prestation, with provisions of sale and payment applying accordingly.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt discharge,extinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Obligation" target="Extinctive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Extinctive Prescription extinguishes the Obligation after the applicable period has elapsed, leaving only a natural obligation upon the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,legal effect</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Public Policy">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract is void if its object or consideration is contrary to public policy.</data>
  <data key="d9">invalidation,legal principle</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Morality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract is void if its object or consideration is contrary to morality.</data>
  <data key="d9">invalidation,legal standard</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract must be performed in accordance with its contents and in compliance with the requirements of good faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">honesty,performance standard</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract does not create obligations binding upon third parties but may create rights in their favor.</data>
  <data key="d9">non-binding obligations,rights creation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Universal Successor in Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The effects of a contract apply to the parties and their universal successors in title, subject to the rules relating to successions.</data>
  <data key="d9">rights transfer,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Particular Successor in Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Obligations and personal rights created by contracts relating to property are transferred to particular successors together with the property when they constitute essential elements.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligations,property transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Equity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract binds the contracting parties not only to its expressed conditions but also to everything which, according to law, usage, and equity, is deemed a necessary sequel to the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">fairness,performance standard</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Contract Interpretation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Contract interpretation is the process of ascertaining the common intention of the parties when the contract wording requires construction, going beyond the literal meaning of the words.</data>
  <data key="d9">construction,intention ascertainment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Contracting Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The effects of a contract apply to the contracting parties, binding them to its contents and to everything deemed a necessary sequel according to law, usage, and equity.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding effect,mutual obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Bilateral Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A bilateral contract is a specific type of contract in which both parties are bound by correlative obligations, and the non-performance by one party may justify the other party's abstention from performance.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract type,correlative obligations,synallagmatic</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Inalienability Clause">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract may contain an inalienability clause restricting the disposition of property, valid only if based on a legitimate reason and limited to a reasonable duration.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal restriction,property disposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Consent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Consent through the exchange of concordant intentions is a fundamental element required for the creation of a contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">element,formation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Offer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An offer is a necessary component of contract formation, made by an offeror to another party.</data>
  <data key="d9">formation,proposal</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Acceptance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acceptance of an offer completes the formation of a contract, provided it matches the offer without restriction or modification.</data>
  <data key="d9">agreement,formation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Representative">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A representative may conclude a contract on behalf of a principal within the limits of his authority.</data>
  <data key="d9">agency,formation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Legal Capacity">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Legal capacity is a prerequisite for a person to validly conclude a contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal validity,prerequisite,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Earnest Money">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Earnest money is paid at the time of contract conclusion and indicates the right of either party to withdraw, subject to forfeiture or double repayment.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee,withdrawal right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Promise To Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A promise to contract is an agreement to enter into a contract in the future, requiring all essential points and timeframe to be specified to be binding.</data>
  <data key="d9">future obligation,prerequisite</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A public auction is a method of forming a contract of sale, concluded only when the final bid is accepted.</data>
  <data key="d9">conclusion,sale method</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court resolves disputes over details of contracts by applying the nature of the transaction, law, custom, and equity.</data>
  <data key="d9">adjudication,dispute resolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Declaration Of Intention">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A declaration of intention is the means by which parties express their concordant intentions to form a contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">expression of will,formation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Sources Of Obligations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Contracts are identified as one of the sources from which obligations arise, forming Section I of Chapter I on sources of obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,source</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Ownership of movables and immovables and other real rights are transferred by contract when the contract refers to an object belonging to the person disposing of it.</data>
  <data key="d9">real rights,transfer mechanism</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Natural Guardian">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acts by a natural guardian are valid within the limits provided by law when concluding contracts on behalf of those under guardianship.</data>
  <data key="d9">representation,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Essential Mistake">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A party who committed an essential mistake may demand avoidance of the contract if the other party committed the same mistake, had knowledge thereof, or could have easily detected it.</data>
  <data key="d9">avoidance,defect of consent</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Fraudulent Misrepresentation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract may be declared void on the grounds of fraudulent misrepresentation when artifices of such gravity were practiced that the other party would not have concluded the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">avoidance,defect of consent</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Duress">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract is voidable as a result of duress when a party has contracted under the stress of justifiable fear unlawfully instilled by the other party.</data>
  <data key="d9">avoidance,defect of consent</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Lesion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A judge may annul a contract or reduce obligations when lesion is established due to exploitation of levity of character or unbridled passion, provided proceedings commence within one year.</data>
  <data key="d9">annulment,unfairness</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Object of Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The object of an obligation must be possible, determinate as to kind and quantity, and not contrary to law for the contract to be valid.</data>
  <data key="d9">essential element,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Mistakes of Calculation and Clerical Mistakes">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Mistakes of calculation and clerical mistakes do not affect the validity of a contract but must be corrected, as established in Article 123.</data>
  <data key="d9">correction,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract" target="Civil Code">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Civil Code governs the formation and validity of contracts, including provisions on defects of consent and the object of obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Consideration" target="Nullity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract is void when an obligation is assumed without consideration or with consideration contrary to public policy or morality.</data>
  <data key="d9">validity requirement,void contract</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nullity" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court may order nullity on its own initiative when a contract is void.</data>
  <data key="d9">court-ordered,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nullity" target="Avoidance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Avoidance applies to voidable contracts through a designated party's right, while nullity applies to void contracts and may be invoked by any interested person or ordered by the court.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract invalidation,legal distinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Avoidance" target="Ratification">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Ratification, whether express or implied, extinguishes the right to avoidance and is retroactive to the date of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,retroactive confirmation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Avoidance" target="Mistake">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Mistake serves as grounds for avoidance of a contract, with a three-year prescription period from the date of discovery.</data>
  <data key="d9">error,grounds for avoidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Avoidance" target="Fraudulent Misrepresentation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraudulent misrepresentation serves as grounds for avoidance of a contract, with a three-year prescription period from the date of discovery.</data>
  <data key="d9">deception,grounds for avoidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Avoidance" target="Duress">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Duress serves as grounds for avoidance of a contract, with a three-year prescription period from the date the duress ceases.</data>
  <data key="d9">coercion,grounds for avoidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Avoidance" target="Legal Incapacity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Legal incapacity is a ground for avoidance of a contract, with the prescription period running from the date of cessation of such incapacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity,grounds for avoidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Adhesion" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may modify leonine conditions or relieve the adhering party of the obligation to perform them in a contract of adhesion.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial review,modification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Adhesion" target="Leonine Condition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Leonine conditions are unfair or oppressive terms found in contracts of adhesion that may be modified by the judge in accordance with equity.</data>
  <data key="d9">oppressive conditions,unfair terms</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Adhesion" target="Equity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Leonine conditions in a contract of adhesion may be modified by the judge or the adhering party may be relieved of them in accordance with the principles of equity.</data>
  <data key="d9">fairness,modification standard</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Policy" target="Morality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both morality and public policy in Egypt serve as grounds for refusing the application of foreign law provisions under Article 28.</data>
  <data key="d9">foreign law exclusion,legal standard</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Policy" target="Natural Obligation">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">No natural obligation can exist that is contrary to public policy.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural obligation cannot exist if it is contrary to public policy, as established in Article 200.</data>
  <data key="d9">compliance,constraint,legal limitation,validity constraint</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Policy" target="Compromise">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A compromise cannot be made on matters touching the status of individuals or public policy, though proprietary interests arising from personal status or penal offences may be compromised.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal limitation,personal status</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Incapacity" target="Fraudulent Misrepresentation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person under legal incapacity who demands avoidance of a contract remains liable for damages if fraudulent methods were employed to conceal the incapacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">concealment,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Incapacity" target="Avoidance of Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person under legal incapacity may demand avoidance of the contract, subject to liability for damages if fraudulent methods were used to conceal the incapacity, as stated in Article 119.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity defect,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Fraudulent Misrepresentation" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraudulent misrepresentation practiced by a third party only entitles the victim to demand avoidance of the contract if the other contracting party was aware or should necessarily have been aware of the fraud, as specified in Article 126.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional avoidance,third-party fraud</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Fraudulent Misrepresentation" target="Avoidance of Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract may be subject to avoidance on the grounds of fraudulent misrepresentation when artifices of such gravity were practiced that the other party would not have concluded the contract, as per Article 125.</data>
  <data key="d9">defect of consent,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Duress" target="Payor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A payor who made a payment under duress may claim restitution even if he knew he was not obligated to pay, as provided in Article 181.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,restitution right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Duress" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Duress practiced by a third party only entitles the victim to demand avoidance of the contract if the other contracting party had or should necessarily have had knowledge of the duress, as specified in Article 128.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional avoidance,third-party duress</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Duress" target="Avoidance of Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A contract is subject to avoidance when a party has contracted under the stress of justifiable fear unlawfully instilled by the other party, as per Article 127.</data>
  <data key="d9">defect of consent,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stipulator" target="Promisor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The promisor undertakes to perform the obligation that the stipulator has stipulated for the benefit of a third party, and the revocation of the stipulation does not automatically liberate the promisor vis-a-vis the stipulator.</data>
  <data key="d9">stipulation,undertaking</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Stipulator" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Revocation of a third-party stipulation does not liberate the debtor vis-a-vis the stipulator unless there is an express or implied agreement to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract,obligation,revocation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Interpretation" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In cases of doubt during contract interpretation, the construction shall be in favor of the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">doubt resolution,favorable construction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Reinstatement" target="Damages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When reinstatement of parties to their pre-contract position is impossible after a contract is void or annulled, damages equivalent to the loss may be awarded as an alternative remedy.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative remedy,compensation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge determines the extent of damages in light of circumstances and may order payment by installments, periodical payments, restoration of the original position, or performance of a specific act.&lt;SEP&gt;The judge fixes the amount of damages if not predetermined in a contract or by law, taking into account the prejudice suffered by the creditor and the unjustifiable attitude of the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">assessment,compensation method,judicial authority,judicial discretion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Unlawful Act">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Every unlawful act causing injury imposes an obligation to make reparation through damages upon the person who committed it.&lt;SEP&gt;Any contractual clause discharging a person from responsibility for unlawful acts is void, and formal summons is not required when damages arise from an unlawful act.</data>
  <data key="d9">liability,reparation,tort,void clause</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Penal Offence">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a damages claim arises from a penal offence, the civil action for damages is only prescribed when the penal action itself is prescribed.</data>
  <data key="d9">civil action,criminal proceedings,prescription</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Necessity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person who causes injury under necessity is only liable for equitable damages as determined by the judge, rather than full reparation.</data>
  <data key="d9">defense,equitable compensation,injury avoidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Joint and Several Liability">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When several persons are responsible for an injury, they are jointly and severally liable for damages, shared equally unless the judge assigns individual shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">equal division,multiple tortfeasors,shared responsibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Security">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When damages are ordered to be paid by installments or as a periodical payment, the debtor may be required to provide security to ensure payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee,installment payment,judicial order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Restoration">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon the demand of the victim, the judge may order restoration of the original position as an alternative to monetary damages for reparation of an unlawful act.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation method,original position,victim demand</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Lessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is liable to pay damages to the lessee for losses caused by defects, disturbances, or government acts imputable to the lessor.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor is liable for damages upon non-performance or delayed performance of obligations, unless the impossibility arose from a cause beyond the debtor's control.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Moral Prejudice">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Damages include compensation for moral prejudice, subject to restrictions on transferability and eligibility limited to spouses and relatives up to the second degree.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,harm type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Contractual Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">For contractual obligations, a debtor not guilty of fraud or gross negligence is liable only for damages that could have been normally foreseen at the time of contracting.</data>
  <data key="d9">foreseeability,limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may reduce or refuse damages if the creditor, by the creditor's own fault, contributed to or increased the loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">contributory negligence,fault</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Thief">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The thief is responsible for the risk of loss of a stolen thing regardless of how the thing perishes, as stated in Article 207.</data>
  <data key="d9">responsibility,risk of loss</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Damages" target="Agreed Damages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Agreed damages are a form of damages fixed in advance by the parties, subject to the provisions of Articles 215 to 220.</data>
  <data key="d9">advance fixation,subcategory</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Property Owner" target="Right of Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of ownership grants the property owner exclusive rights to use, enjoy, and dispose of property within the limits of the law, including all fruits, products, and accessories.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusive control,legal right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Property Owner" target="Neighbor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A property owner must not exercise rights excessively to the detriment of a neighbor, and both share obligations for boundary demarcation and may share canal and drainage costs.</data>
  <data key="d9">nuisance,property rights,shared obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Property Owner" target="Land">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A property owner's ownership of land extends to everything above and below the surface as far as it can be usefully enjoyed, and the surface ownership may be separated from what is above or below by law or agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership extent,surface and subsurface rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Property Owner" target="Fair Compensation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">No property owner may be deprived of property except in cases provided by law and upon payment of fair compensation.</data>
  <data key="d9">deprivation of property,just compensation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Right of Way">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An owner whose land is cut off from a public road has a right of way over neighboring land, provided fair compensation is paid and the passage causes the least possible damage.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,legal easement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Private Canal or Drain">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Neighboring owners may use a private canal or drain for irrigation or drainage after the owner has satisfied their own needs, and must contribute to construction and maintenance costs in proportion to the benefiting land area.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost contribution,shared usage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Party Wall">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A neighbor who did not contribute to heightening expenses may become a co-proprietor of the heightened part by paying half the cost and half the value of any additional ground area.</data>
  <data key="d9">co-proprietorship acquisition,heightening</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Direct View">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A neighbor is not entitled to have a direct view over a neighbor's property at a distance of less than one meter.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal restriction,minimum distance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Oblique View">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A neighbor is not entitled to have an oblique view over a neighbor's property at a distance of less than fifty centimeters.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal restriction,minimum distance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Light Shaft Opening">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">No distance requirement applies to a light shaft opening intended only for air and light without providing a view over the neighbor's property.</data>
  <data key="d9">air and light,exemption</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Public Road">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The prohibition on having an oblique view over a neighbor's property ceases to have effect if the oblique view is simultaneously a direct view over a public road.</data>
  <data key="d9">proximity,view exception</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighbor" target="Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A neighbor who has acquired by prescription the right to a direct view at less than one meter over a neighbor's property prevents that neighbor from building within one meter along the building where the view was opened.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquired rights,long-term use</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Ownership" target="Right of Usufruct">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of usufruct is a real right derived from the right of ownership, classified under Chapter II of the legal text.</data>
  <data key="d9">derived rights,property law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Ownership" target="Right of Use">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of use is a real right derived from the right of ownership, classified alongside usufruct and occupation.</data>
  <data key="d9">derived rights,property law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Ownership" target="Right of Occupation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of occupation is a real right derived from the right of ownership, classified alongside usufruct and use.</data>
  <data key="d9">derived rights,property law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Way" target="Public Road">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An owner whose land is cut off from or lacks adequate access to a public road is entitled to a right of way over neighboring land, subject to fair compensation and the condition of least possible damage.</data>
  <data key="d9">access easement,landlocked property</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Way" target="Fair Compensation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The exercise of a right of way is subject to payment of fair compensation to the neighbor whose land is crossed.</data>
  <data key="d9">easement condition,payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Private Canal or Drain" target="Land">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When damage is caused to land by a canal or drain crossing it, the landowner has the right to claim adequate compensation for the damage done.</data>
  <data key="d9">damage compensation,water passage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egyptian Civil Code" target="National Courts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Egyptian Civil Code repealed the previous civil law applied before National Courts and replaced it with a new comprehensive civil code.</data>
  <data key="d9">repeal,replacement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egyptian Civil Code" target="Mixed Courts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Egyptian Civil Code repealed the previous civil law applied before Mixed Courts and replaced it with a new comprehensive civil code.</data>
  <data key="d9">repeal,replacement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egyptian Civil Code" target="Qasr El-Qubba">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Egyptian Civil Code was issued at Qasr El-Qubba on July 16, 1948.</data>
  <data key="d9">issuance location</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egyptian Civil Code" target="Private International Law">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Private International Law principles apply as a supplementary framework for conflicts of law not specifically addressed in the Egyptian Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d9">conflict resolution,supplementary law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egyptian Civil Code" target="Official Gazette">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Egyptian Civil Code was ordered to be published in the Official Gazette as a legal requirement for its promulgation and enforcement.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal promulgation,publication</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egyptian Civil Code" target="International Convention">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">International Conventions in force in Egypt take precedence over the conflict-of-law provisions of the Egyptian Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal hierarchy,supersession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Egyptian Civil Code" target="State with Multiple Legal Systems">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the applicable law is that of a state with multiple legal systems, the internal law of that state determines which system applies, as provided by the Egyptian Civil Code.</data>
  <data key="d9">conflict resolution,legal pluralism</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debt" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor holds the right to recover the debt through legal proceedings against the debtor and, under certain conditions, against the surety.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt recovery,legal proceedings</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Bilateral Contract" target="Rescission">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In bilateral contracts, if one party fails to perform his obligation, the other party may demand rescission after serving a formal summons; correlative obligations are extinguished upon impossibility of performance.</data>
  <data key="d9">correlative obligations,dissolution,non-performance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Rescission" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may grant additional time to the debtor, reject rescission when the unperformed part is of little importance, or award damages upon rescission.</data>
  <data key="d9">discretion,grace period,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Formal Summons" target="Vendor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Formal Summons must be served by the Vendor or the Purchaser to the prospective preemptor to initiate the preemption notification period.</data>
  <data key="d9">notification,procedural requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Formal Summons" target="Code De Procedure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The formal summons may be served by post in the manner provided for in the Code de Procedure.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Formal Summons" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Damages are not due unless the debtor has been formally summoned, subject to exceptions specified in Article 220.</data>
  <data key="d9">default,notification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Unlawful Act" target="Victim">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The victim suffers injury from an unlawful act and may claim damages within three years of knowing the injury and the responsible person, or fifteen years from the act.</data>
  <data key="d9">claim,injury,prescriptive period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Unlawful Act" target="Legitimate Defense">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Causing injury in legitimate defense of one's person or property exempts the person from liability, provided the defensive measures do not exceed what is necessary.</data>
  <data key="d9">exemption,justification,proportionality</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Unlawful Act" target="Public Official">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A public official is exempt from liability for an unlawful act causing injury if performed under a superior's obligatory order with reasonable belief in its lawfulness and due care.</data>
  <data key="d9">immunity,lawful belief,superior orders</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Unlawful Act" target="Legal Capacity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The limitation of liability based on lack of legal capacity does not apply when the liability of a voluntary agent results from an unlawful act.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,liability override</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supervisor" target="Minor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The supervisor is liable for injuries caused by the minor's unlawful acts; supervision of a minor transfers to schoolteachers or craft supervisors while under their care.</data>
  <data key="d9">duty of care,supervision,vicarious liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supervisor" target="Right of Redress">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A supervisor held liable for the unlawful act of a supervised person has a right of redress against that person to the extent of their responsibility for the injury.</data>
  <data key="d9">claim for compensation,recourse,vicarious liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Minor" target="Legal Capacity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A minor has limited or no legal capacity depending on age and discretion.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity limitation,legal status</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Minor" target="Domicile">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The domicile of a minor is the domicile of the legal representative, though a minor over eighteen may have a special domicile.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal representation,residence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Servant">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The master is liable for damage caused by the servant's unlawful acts performed in the course of or as a result of employment, based on the master's actual powers of supervision and control.</data>
  <data key="d9">employment,supervision,vicarious liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Right of Redress">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A master held liable for the unlawful act of a servant has a right of redress against the servant to the extent of the servant's responsibility for the injury.</data>
  <data key="d9">claim for compensation,recourse,vicarious liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Sub-contractor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sub-contractors and Workmen have a direct right of action against the Master to the extent of sums due by the Master to the main Contractor at the time the action is commenced.</data>
  <data key="d9">direct right of action,payment claim</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Workmen">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Workmen working for a Contractor or Sub-contractor have direct rights of action against the Master and enjoy priority of payment over assignees of the Contractor.</data>
  <data key="d9">direct right of action,privilege and priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Contractor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Contractor executes works and places them at the Master's disposal; the Master is obligated to take delivery and pay the price upon delivery unless custom or agreement provides otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual relationship,delivery and payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Architect">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Architect provides the Master with design plans, specifications, and supervision of work, and is entitled to separate fees for each; fees are fixed according to prevailing custom if not specified in the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">fees,professional services</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Contract For Work">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master may terminate the Contract for Work at any time before completion, provided compensation is paid to the Contractor for expenses, completed work, and lost profit.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation obligation,termination rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Contractor's Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the Contractor's personal skill was not a consideration, the Contractor's Heirs may continue the Contract for Work if they offer sufficient guarantees; otherwise, the Master may resiliate the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract continuation,guarantee requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Contractor's Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the contract is dissolved by the death of the Contractor, the Master is bound to pay the Contractor's Estate the value of the work already done and expenses incurred, to the extent of the benefit derived, and may demand delivery of prepared materials and commenced plans against payment of a fair price.</data>
  <data key="d9">dissolution by death,payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Unit Price Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under a Unit Price Contract, the Master may rescind the contract and stop the work without delay if the estimated excess in price is considerable, provided the Master pays the Contractor for work done according to contract terms.</data>
  <data key="d9">price excess,rescission right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Lump Sum Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under a Lump Sum Contract, the Master may authorize modifications or additions to the plan and agree on a price for them with the Contractor; such agreement must be in writing unless the principal contract was verbal.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,modification approval</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master" target="Materials">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Master bears the loss of materials supplied by the Master when works are destroyed by a fortuitous event before delivery, or when destruction is caused by the Master's fault or a defect in materials supplied by the Master.</data>
  <data key="d9">loss bearing,supply responsibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Animal Keeper" target="Animal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The animal keeper is strictly liable for harm caused by the animal even if it strays or escapes, unless the harm resulted from a cause beyond the keeper's control.</data>
  <data key="d9">custody,harm,strict liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-65dbd9e16c67e80f2fa0f1f2dcb7d767</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Animal" target="Person in Charge of Animal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The person in charge of an animal bears liability for any harm the animal causes, even if the animal strays or escapes.</data>
  <data key="d9">custody,liability for harm</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Security" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor may demand additional security if the debtor diminishes the original security, and may ask for security before the expiration of a suspensive term if there is reasonable fear of the debtor's bankruptcy or insolvency, as stated in Articles 273 and 274.</data>
  <data key="d9">collateral demand,creditor protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Security" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor must not diminish the special security given to the creditor by his own act, and failure to provide promised security causes the debtor to forfeit the benefit of the term, as stated in Article 273.</data>
  <data key="d9">collateral obligation,forfeiture</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Substitute (نائب الوكيل)" target="Joint and Several Liability (التضامن)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a mandatary appoints an unauthorized substitute, both the mandatary and the substitute are jointly and severally responsible for the acts performed.</data>
  <data key="d9">shared liability,unauthorized delegation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Deposit (الوديعة)" target="Depository (المودع عنده)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The depository is bound by the deposit contract to take delivery of the thing, keep it in safe custody, and return it in kind.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual duty,safekeeping</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Depository (المودع عنده)" target="Depositor (المودع)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The depository receives a thing from the depositor for safe custody and must return it upon request, with varying standards of care depending on whether the deposit is gratuitous or remunerated.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual obligation,custody,return</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495983</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Depository (المودع عنده)" target="Reasonable Person (الرجل المعتاد)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In a remunerated deposit, the depository must exercise the diligence of a reasonable person in the custody of the deposited thing; in a gratuitous deposit, the standard is the care given to own affairs without exceeding the reasonable person threshold.</data>
  <data key="d9">duty of diligence,standard of care</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b9b58ee7946b0a36ba314ba68f421633</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Servient Tenement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A servitude imposes restrictions and obligations on the servient tenement, whose owner may not diminish the use of the servitude or make it more inconvenient.</data>
  <data key="d9">burden,obligation,restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Dominant Tenement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A servitude is established for the benefit of the dominant tenement, entitling its owner to use and preserve the right in the least harmful manner possible.</data>
  <data key="d9">benefit,legal right,use and preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may grant damages instead of material redress for breaches of servitude restrictions when it considers there are sufficient reasons for doing so.</data>
  <data key="d9">adjudication,damages,redress</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Co-Owners In Common">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners in common of a dominant tenement share servitude rights; use of the servitude by one co-owner interrupts prescription in favor of all other co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">interruption,prescription,shared rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Deed Of Creation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Servitudes are governed by the rules laid down in the deed by which they are created, as established in Article 1019.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,source of rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Local Custom">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Local custom serves as one of the governing sources for servitudes alongside the deed of creation and statutory provisions.</data>
  <data key="d9">customary law,governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A servitude right can be extinguished through prescription by non-user, and the manner of its exercise may also be modified by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,modification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Non-User">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A servitude is extinguished by non-user for a period of fifteen years generally, or thirty-three years if created for the benefit of wakf property.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,lapse of right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Servitude" target="Article 445">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Servitude is the subject of the presumption in Article 445 that the vendor need not warrant an apparent or disclosed servitude.</data>
  <data key="d9">disclosure presumption,statutory subject</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Dominant Tenement" target="Servient Tenement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The dominant tenement benefits from a servitude that imposes a burden on the servient tenement, creating a legally recognized relationship of subordination between the two properties.</data>
  <data key="d9">burden and benefit,servitude,subordination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagor" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgagor may be a person other than the debtor; in such case only the mortgaged property may be proceeded against and the mortgagor cannot demand sale of the debtor's property first.&lt;SEP&gt;The mortgagor may be the debtor himself or a third party who consents to the mortgage of his property in the interests of the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity,debt security,identity,legal distinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagor" target="Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgagor may dispose of and manage the mortgaged property and collect its fruits, but any disposal does not affect the right of the mortgagee.</data>
  <data key="d9">disposition,management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagor" target="Abandonment of Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgagor who is not the debtor may avoid proceedings by abandoning the mortgaged property according to the procedures laid down for abandonment by a third party holder.</data>
  <data key="d9">avoidance,surrender</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagor" target="Inscription">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgagor bears the cost of the inscription, its renewal, and its radiation unless an agreement provides otherwise.&lt;SEP&gt;The mortgagor bears the cost of inscription, its renewal, and its radiation unless otherwise agreed between the parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost,cost bearing,financial obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Lesion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Lesion is assessed by determining the value of the immovable property at the time of sale to ascertain whether the loss exceeds one fifth of its value.</data>
  <data key="d9">property type,valuation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Real Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Possession, ownership, and real rights over immovable property are governed by the law of the place where the immovable is situated.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance by location,property law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Contractual Obligations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Contracts relating to immovable property are governed by the law of the place where the immovable is situated, regardless of other choice-of-law rules.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract law,lex situs</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Movable Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Property is classified as either immovable or movable, with immovables being fixed things and movables being all other things.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal distinction,property classification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Preemption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Preemption applies specifically to the sale of immovable property, allowing qualified persons to substitute themselves in place of the purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal right,property sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights governs the transfer of ownership and other real rights over immovable property, requiring publication of relevant acts and instruments.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,transfer requirements</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Ownership of immovable property is transferred by contract subject to the rules of the Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">property rights,real rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Preemptor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Preemptor is bound to compensate the Purchaser for buildings or plantations constructed on the Immovable Property before notification of preemption, and may request removal of those constructed after notification.</data>
  <data key="d9">buildings,compensation,plantations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Article 1147">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1147 establishes the vendor's privilege applicable to immovable property that has been sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,legal subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Article 1148">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1148 establishes the contractor's and architect's privilege applicable to immovable property in the form of buildings or other works.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,legal subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immovable Property" target="Article 1149">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1149 establishes the co-partitioner's privilege applicable to immovable property that has been partitioned among co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,legal subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Authentic Document" target="Donor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The donor must execute a gift of immovable property through an authentic document under penalty of nullity, though movable gifts may be completed by delivery alone.</data>
  <data key="d9">formal requirement,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Book IV: Accessory Rights Or Real Securities" target="Chapter I: Mortgages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Book IV contains Chapter I, which specifically governs mortgages as part of the broader framework of accessory real rights and real securities.</data>
  <data key="d9">code structure,hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Chapter I: Mortgages" target="Section I: The Constitution Of Mortgages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Chapter I on Mortgages contains Section I, which specifically governs the constitution and formation of mortgages.</data>
  <data key="d9">code structure,hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-388e4df953c05363795bb5989efc84e1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1148" target="Contractor's And Architect's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1148 establishes the contractor's and architect's privilege over buildings or works to secure sums due for construction, repair, or maintenance.</data>
  <data key="d9">establishment,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Prescription" target="Joint and Several Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Prescription extinguishing the debt as to one joint and several debtor benefits other co-debtors only to the extent of that debtor's share, and interruption or suspension as to one debtor cannot be invoked against others.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinction,partial benefit,time limit</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Prescription" target="Gregorian Calendar">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Prescription periods are calculated according to the Gregorian Calendar unless expressly provided otherwise by law.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal period,time calculation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Prescription" target="Advance Proof">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Advance proof is governed by the law in force at the time it was or should have been established, similar to how new prescription provisions apply from their effective date.</data>
  <data key="d9">evidence law,temporal application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Prescription" target="Restitution">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Claims for restitution are subject to prescription periods of three years from knowledge and fifteen years from when the right arose.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal claim,time limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Future Property" target="Article 492">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 492 declares that a gift of future property is void and has no legal effect.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal prohibition,voidability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Lessee">
  <data key="d7">5.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor undertakes to enable the lessee to enjoy the leased property for a certain time in return for a fixed rent under the lease contract.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessor and lessee are bound by the agreement of lease, with the lessor granting enjoyment of the property and the lessee paying rent, maintaining the property, and complying with agreed terms.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessor grants the lease to the lessee and is entitled to rent, restituted property, and compensation for unlawful retention, while the lessee is obligated to maintain and return the property and pay rent.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lessor grants leases to the Lessee, creating reciprocal rights and obligations including rent payment, property maintenance, and compensation upon early termination.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lessor has a privilege over the Lessee's attachable movables and crops on the leased property for rent and other sums due under the lease, enforceable for up to two years, as governed by Article 1143.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual obligation,lease agreement,lease relationship,mutual obligations,obligations,privilege enforcement,reciprocal obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495984</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Improvements">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is bound to reimburse the lessee for improvements that increase the value of the property unless made without the lessor's knowledge or despite objection.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,reimbursement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="New Owner">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor's ownership of the leased property may be transferred to a new owner, who is bound by the lease only if it has an established date prior to the transfer.&lt;SEP&gt;If the new owner cannot prove that the lessee knew of the transfer of ownership at the time advance rent was paid, the new owner has only a remedy against the lessor for those payments.</data>
  <data key="d9">advance rent liability,lease binding,ownership transfer,remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Compensation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is entitled to receive compensation from the lessee for unlawful retention or damage, and must pay compensation to the lessee for early termination or eviction.</data>
  <data key="d9">damage recovery,payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Tacit Renewal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor's knowledge of and failure to object to the lessee's continued use results in tacit renewal of the lease for an indefinite duration.</data>
  <data key="d9">implied agreement,indefinite lease</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may grant the lessor time for settlement when the lessor chooses to keep improvements and pay the lessee, and may maintain a lease despite sub-letting prohibitions.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,time for settlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser must furnish adequate security and ensure that the lessor suffers no real prejudice for the court to maintain the lease despite a sub-letting prohibition.</data>
  <data key="d9">no prejudice requirement,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Movables">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor holds rights of retention and recovery over movables on the leased property, which cannot be exercised if the movables were removed to meet the lessee's professional requirements or if remaining movables sufficiently cover the rent.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessor has a lien on all attachable movables stocking the leased property as security for amounts due under the lease, with the right to object to their removal and to recover them from any possessor even in good faith, subject to the possessor's rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">lien,recovery,recovery rights,retention,retention rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Sub-Lessee">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The sub-lessee is directly answerable to the lessor for amounts owed to the principal lessee from the time a summons is served, and cannot set up advance payments against the lessor unless made before the summons in accordance with custom or a formal agreement.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lessor's privilege extends over movables and crops belonging to the Sub-Lessee if sub-letting was expressly prohibited; if not prohibited, the privilege is limited to amounts due by the Sub-Lessee to the principal Lessee, as stated in Article 1143.</data>
  <data key="d9">direct answerability,privilege enforcement,rent payment,sub-letting</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Transfer of Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor's ownership of the leased property may be transferred voluntarily or by force to a third party, and the lease is only binding on the new owner if it has an established date prior to the transfer.</data>
  <data key="d9">lease enforceability,voluntary or forced transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Notice of Evacuation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor may give notice of evacuation to the lessee when the lessor has a personal need for the property, provided such right was agreed upon and notice is given in accordance with Article 563.</data>
  <data key="d9">personal need,termination right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Cattle and Agricultural Implements">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Lessor owns cattle and agricultural implements that are only included in an agricultural lease if expressly specified.</data>
  <data key="d9">lease inclusion,ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Article 610">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 610 limits the Lessor's obligation to deliver cattle and agricultural implements on agricultural land to cases where they are expressly included in the lease.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,property delivery</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Article 613">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 613 requires the Lessor's consent before the Lessee may make any substantial change in the established method of cultivating agricultural land.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent requirement,cultivation control</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Article 627">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 627 obligates the Lessor to reimburse the Lessee or Heirs of Lessee for expenditures on unripened crops and pay equitable compensation for work done when amodiation ceases before its term.</data>
  <data key="d9">early termination,reimbursement obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Leased Property">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is bound to deliver the leased property in suitable condition, maintain it, and carry out all necessary repairs during the lease.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessor leases the property to the lessee and warrants it against defects and disturbances that may diminish its enjoyment.</data>
  <data key="d9">delivery,leasing,maintenance obligation,ownership,warranty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Rent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is entitled to receive rent and has a lien on movables in the leased property as security for all amounts due.</data>
  <data key="d9">collection,entitlement,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Warranty Against Disturbance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is obligated to warrant the lessee against disturbances from the lessor, the lessor's servants, other lessees, or successors in title.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal obligation,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Warranty Against Defects">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor warrants the leased property against defects that prevent or appreciably diminish enjoyment, except for defects customarily tolerated or known to the lessee.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal obligation,property condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Lien">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor has a lien on all attachable movables stocking the leased property as security for amounts due under the lease, with the right to object to removal and to recover removed items.</data>
  <data key="d9">retention,security right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Servants of the Lessor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is responsible for the acts of the lessor's servants that cause disturbance or damage to the lessee's enjoyment of the property.</data>
  <data key="d9">agency,responsibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Successor in Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor warrants the lessee against lawful claims made by any successor in title of the lessor.</data>
  <data key="d9">succession,warranty coverage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Agreement of Lease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor is a party to the agreement of lease, bound by its terms including warranties and obligations regarding the leased property.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual party,obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Other Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor warrants the primary lessee against disturbances or damage based on lawful claims by any other lessee, extending the lessor's warranty beyond the lessor's own acts.</data>
  <data key="d9">disturbance,warranty coverage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Fraud">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the lessor fraudulently conceals the cause of a warranty against disturbance or defects, any agreement excluding or limiting such warranty is void.</data>
  <data key="d9">fraudulent concealment,void agreement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Holder in Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Lessor is deemed to be a Holder in Good Faith of furniture used in the leased premises for purposes of privilege enforcement, as established in Articles 1133 and 1143.</data>
  <data key="d9">deemed status,legal fiction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Lessor's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessor holds a lessor's privilege over leased property.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessor" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor's privilege over movable operates against the lessor if it can be proved the lessor had knowledge of the privilege when the movable was brought onto the leased property.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability,knowledge requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Leased Property">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee has the right to enjoy the use of the leased property and must not be disturbed by the lessor in such enjoyment.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessee is entitled to use and enjoy the leased property and must restitute it upon expiration of the lease, preserving it with the care of a reasonable person.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessee uses and must restitute the leased property upon expiration in the condition it was received, subject to permissible wear.</data>
  <data key="d9">enjoyment,maintenance,restitution,use,use right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Sub-Lessee">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may sublet the property to a sub-lessee, and the sub-lessee becomes directly answerable to the lessor for amounts owed after summons.&lt;SEP&gt;The Sub-Lessee rents from the principal Lessee, and the amount due by the Sub-Lessee to the Lessee determines the scope of the Lessor's privilege when sub-letting was not prohibited, as stated in Article 1143.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt obligation,direct liability,sub-lease arrangement,sub-lease relationship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Assignee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may assign the lease to an assignee and remains guarantor for the assignee's obligations unless the lessor formally accepts the assignment.</data>
  <data key="d9">assignment,guarantee</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="New Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The new owner may evict the lessee only after providing proper notice under Article 563 and paying compensation, unless the lease is binding on the new owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,eviction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Improvements">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may erect buildings, plant trees, or make other improvements on the leased property that increase its value and may claim reimbursement.</data>
  <data key="d9">construction,value increase</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Rent">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee is obligated to pay rent to the lessor, which may consist of money or any other prestation.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessee is obligated to pay rent at agreed times or at times established by local custom, with payment made at the lessee's domicile unless otherwise agreed.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessee is obligated to pay rent to the lessor for the use of the leased property.</data>
  <data key="d9">financial duty,lease consideration,payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Heirs of Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon the death of the lessee, the heirs may claim termination of the lease if the burden has become too heavy, provided the claim is made within six months.</data>
  <data key="d9">inheritance,termination claim</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Notice of Evacuation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee must comply with notice of evacuation given by the lessor or new owner, and the lease is not deemed renewed if notice has been given.</data>
  <data key="d9">compliance,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Industrial or Commercial Establishment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may create an industrial or commercial establishment on the leased immovable property, and compelling circumstances may require the lessee to sell such establishment.</data>
  <data key="d9">business operation,creation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Proces Verbal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If no proces verbal or inventory was drawn at the time of delivery, the lessee is presumed to have received the leased property in good condition, subject to proof to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal evidence,presumption of condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Resiliation of Lease">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may demand resiliation of the lease if the leased property is unfit for use, poses a health danger, or is destroyed.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessee may claim resiliation of the lease when deprived of enjoyment due to third-party claims, government acts, trespass, or defects in the property.&lt;SEP&gt;The lessee may claim resiliation of the lease if not authorized to assign the lease or sublet the property, upon payment of equitable compensation.</data>
  <data key="d9">claim,equitable compensation,legal remedy,remedy,termination right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Agricultural Land">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Lessee is obligated to cultivate agricultural land in accordance with normal agricultural use and maintain the land in a good state of production.</data>
  <data key="d9">cultivation,maintenance obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Cattle and Agricultural Implements">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Lessee must properly care for and maintain cattle and agricultural implements belonging to the Lessor when included in the lease.</data>
  <data key="d9">care,maintenance obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Article 611">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 611 obligates the Lessee to properly care for and maintain cattle and agricultural implements belonging to the Lessor in accordance with customary use.</data>
  <data key="d9">care obligation,maintenance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Article 617">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 617 grants the Lessee the right to remain on leased land until the harvest ripens upon payment of proportional rent when the harvest is unripe at lease end through no fault of the Lessee.</data>
  <data key="d9">right to remain,unripe harvest</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Article 623">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 623 requires the Lessee in amodiation to exercise the same degree of care in cultivation and crop preservation as in personal affairs, and holds the Lessee responsible for land deterioration unless reasonable care is proven.</data>
  <data key="d9">responsibility,standard of care</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Incoming Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An outgoing Lessee must allow the Incoming Lessee to prepare and sow the land before vacating, provided the Incoming Lessee sustains no injury thereby.</data>
  <data key="d9">preparation rights,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Article 625">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 625 prohibits the Lessee in amodiation from assigning the lease or subletting the land without the Lessor's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">assignment restriction,subletting prohibition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Article 632">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 632 obligates the Lessee of wakf property to supplement inadequate rent to current market levels under penalty of contract resiliation.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract penalty,rent supplementation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Reduction of Rent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may claim a reduction of rent when the enjoyment of the leased property is appreciably diminished by defects, disturbances, or government authority acts.</data>
  <data key="d9">remedy,rent adjustment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Lessee's Repairs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee is bound to carry out lessee's repairs in accordance with general usage, unless otherwise agreed in the lease.</data>
  <data key="d9">general usage,maintenance obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Fire">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee is responsible for fire damage to the leased property unless the cause is proven not imputable to the lessee; when multiple lessees occupy the building, responsibility is proportional to the part occupied unless the fire originated in one specific lessee's part.</data>
  <data key="d9">damage responsibility,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Movables">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee of certain property types must stock the leased property with movables of sufficient value to secure rent for two years or the lease period if shorter, unless rent is paid in advance or other guarantees are provided.</data>
  <data key="d9">security obligation,stocking requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Alteration">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may not make alterations to the leased property without the lessor's permission unless no damage is occasioned to the lessor; unauthorized alterations may compel reinstatement and compensation.</data>
  <data key="d9">permission requirement,restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Restitution">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee is bound upon expiration of the lease to return the leased property; unlawful retention results in compensation liability based on the rental value and harm to the lessor.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,return of property</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A lessee who in good faith first enters into possession of the leased property or transcribes the lease before another lessee takes possession will have preference over competing lessees.</data>
  <data key="d9">preference,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Custom">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In the absence of express agreement, the lessee must comply with local custom regarding rent payment times, tolerated defects, and lessee's repairs, as established by the practice of the place where the property is situated.</data>
  <data key="d9">compliance,general usage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Agreement of Lease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee is a party to the agreement of lease, deriving rights such as enjoyment of the property and obligations such as rent payment and proper use.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual party,obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Compensation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee is entitled to claim compensation when the leased property is unfit for use, its usefulness is diminished, or the lessor fails to fulfill maintenance obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">damage claim,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lessee" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lessee may seek authorization from the court to carry out repairs and deduct the costs from rent when the lessor delays performance of maintenance obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authorization,repair enforcement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assignee" target="Assignor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The assignor transfers a right to the assignee, warrants the existence of the right, and may be subject to a right of recourse in warranty by the assignee.</data>
  <data key="d9">assignment,recourse,warranty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assignee" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor may raise against the assignee the defenses that were available against the assignor at the time the assignment became effective, as well as defenses arising from the contract of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d9">defenses,legal rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs of Lessee" target="Article 627">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 627 grants Heirs of Lessee the right to claim reimbursement for expenditures on unripened crops or to continue cultivation until harvest when amodiation ends due to the Lessee's death.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuation rights,reimbursement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs of Lessee" target="Agricultural Land">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs of Lessee may continue cultivation of Agricultural Land until crops ripen when amodiation is dissolved by the Lessee's death, provided they can maintain proper cultivation.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuation of cultivation,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Leased Property" target="Government Authority">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Lawful acts by a government authority may appreciably diminish the enjoyment of the leased property, entitling the lessee to remedies.</data>
  <data key="d9">lawful act,regulatory impact</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Leased Property" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party may claim rights over the leased property or commit trespass, triggering the lessee's obligation to notify the lessor and the right to take legal action.</data>
  <data key="d9">interference,rights claim,trespass</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Leased Property" target="Other Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When several lessees rent the same property, the one who without fraud first enters into possession has preference; if none has grounds for preference, each may only claim damages for rights not enjoyed.</data>
  <data key="d9">co-tenancy,competition,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7e205ea7c997286b5be1331358b8dcb1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lease Contract" target="Article 563">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lease contract is governed by the notice periods prescribed in Article 563 for termination, evacuation, and renewal-related procedures.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal reference,notice periods</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation" target="Joint and Several Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A joint and several debtor cannot invoke compensation between the creditor and another co-debtor except to the extent of that other co-debtor's share.</data>
  <data key="d9">mutual debts,set-off limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation" target="Registered Creditors">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Registered Creditors retain their rights when Compensation for building destruction is allocated for reconstruction costs, ensuring their registered claims are not prejudiced.</data>
  <data key="d9">priority claims,rights preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation" target="Building">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Compensation arising from the destruction of a Building shall be set aside for the costs of reconstruction as decided by the Syndicate of Owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">destruction recovery,reconstruction funding</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation" target="Article 868">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 868 provides that Compensation due on account of building destruction shall be set aside for reconstruction costs without prejudice to registered creditors' rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">allocation,reconstruction funding</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Notice of Evacuation" target="Lease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A lease without a fixed term may be terminated by either party through notice of evacuation, with notice periods varying according to the type of property.</data>
  <data key="d9">notice period,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Tacit Renewal" target="Real Securities">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Real securities supplied under the original lease are transferred to the tacitly renewed lease, subject to real property publication rules.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuity of guarantee,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 563" target="Article 608">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 608 requires compliance with the notice periods set forth in Article 563 for lease termination.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,notice requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Real Securities" target="Novation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Real securities guaranteeing the original obligation are not automatically transferred to the new obligation unless provided by law, agreement, or the intention of the parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Real Securities" target="Original Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Real securities guarantee the performance of the original obligation and may be transferred to the new obligation under specific conditions.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee,performance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Real Securities" target="Article 357">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 357 sets the conditions for transferring real securities to the new obligation depending on the type of novation.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,transfer conditions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Party Wall" target="Co-owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-proprietors share repair and reconstruction costs of a party wall in proportion to their respective shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">co-proprietorship,repair obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inalienability Clause" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An inalienability clause may be deemed valid if its purpose is to protect a lawful interest of a third party, and the reasonable duration may extend for the life of that third party.</data>
  <data key="d9">protected interest,validity basis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint Ownership" target="Co-owner">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Joint ownership involves two or more co-owners holding undivided shares in the same property, with shares presumed equal unless proven otherwise.&lt;SEP&gt;Every co-owner may take necessary preservation measures for the commonly held property even without the consent of other co-owners.&lt;SEP&gt;All co-owners bear management, preservation, tax, and other costs of common property proportionally to their shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost bearing,preservation,property ownership,proportional obligation,shared rights,unilateral action</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint Ownership" target="Manager">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A manager, whether appointed by majority or by the Court, administers the commonly held property and may establish binding management rules.</data>
  <data key="d9">administration,management authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint Ownership" target="Co-Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A co-owner is a participant in joint ownership, holding an undivided share in property held in common.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal status,ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint Ownership" target="Partition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Partition is the legal process through which joint ownership of property is terminated by dividing it among co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal process,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint Ownership" target="Family Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Family joint ownership is a specific form of joint ownership created by written agreement among family members sharing a common occupation or interest.</data>
  <data key="d9">family arrangement,specialized ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint Ownership" target="Obligatory Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Obligatory joint ownership is a form of joint ownership in which partition cannot be demanded because the property's purpose requires it to remain undivided.</data>
  <data key="d9">mandatory status,permanent indivision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owner" target="Majority Decision">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Decisions on ordinary management taken by a majority of co-owners calculated on the basis of share value are binding on all co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">management,voting rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owner" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may take necessary measures upon application of any co-owner when there is no majority, and dissenting co-owners may challenge decisions before the Court within two months of notification.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute resolution,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owner" target="Acquirer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If a co-owner alienates a specific part of common property that does not fall within the co-owner's share upon partition, the acquirer's right transfers to the part that devolves on the co-owner, or the acquirer may demand cancellation if unaware of the co-owner's lack of ownership of that specific part.</data>
  <data key="d9">partition adjustment,property transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owner" target="Partition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Partition divides commonly held property among co-owners into individual portions, and the rights of an acquirer of a specific part are adjusted according to the portion that devolves upon the disposing co-owner after partition.</data>
  <data key="d9">property division,share allocation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owner" target="Common Movable Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A co-owner has the right to recover a common share in movable property or a collection of property before partition, as provided in Article 833.</data>
  <data key="d9">pre-partition claim,recovery right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owner" target="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners who partitioned a movable hold a privilege over it to secure their remedies against each other.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owner" target="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners who partitioned an immovable hold a privilege over it to secure their remedies and payment of differences.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Manager" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may appoint a manager to manage property held in common when co-owners fail to reach a majority decision.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,property management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Manager" target="Co-owners">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners who own the majority in value of shares appoint and may discharge the Manager.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,discharge</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Manager" target="Family Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Manager is appointed by co-owners holding the majority in value of shares to administer Family Joint Ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Manager" target="Partnership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Managers direct the affairs of the partnership and their managerial powers cease upon the dissolution of the partnership.</data>
  <data key="d9">authority,management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Manager" target="Liquidator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Managers are deemed to be liquidators in relation to third parties until a formal liquidator is appointed.</data>
  <data key="d9">succession,temporary authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 816" target="Article 818">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 818 provides an exception allowing an owner to compel assignment of part of a wall or land only in the case provided for in Article 816.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,legal cross-reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 823" target="Article 824">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 824 declares alienations void only when the inalienability clause is valid in accordance with the provisions of Article 823.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal dependency,validity condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 832" target="Co-Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 832 governs the right of co-owners holding at least three-quarters of common property to decide on alienation.</data>
  <data key="d9">alienation,majority decision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Establishments Injurious to Neighbors" target="Regulations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Factories, wells, steam engines, and all establishments injurious to neighbors must be constructed at distances and subject to conditions laid down by regulations.</data>
  <data key="d9">construction requirements,legal compliance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-98373fb133ca4e39a319d3605e4b523f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partition" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge fixes by order on petition the portion of property to which the mortgage is transferred following partition.</data>
  <data key="d9">allocation,judicial determination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partition" target="Co-Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Every co-owner has the right to demand partition of property held in common unless bound by law or agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal right,property division</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partition" target="Provisional Partition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Provisional partition is a temporary form of partition that may convert into a final partition after fifteen years of continuous possession.</data>
  <data key="d9">property division,temporary arrangement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partition" target="Expert">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The expert proceeds with the composition of separate parts and valuation of property as part of the partition process.</data>
  <data key="d9">division,property valuation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partition" target="Personal Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Personal creditors have the right to oppose a partition and must be joined before an action for partition is introduced, with the partition being without effect against opposing creditors if they are excluded.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor opposition,procedural rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partition" target="Article 845">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 845 permits rescission of a partition by agreement if a co-partitioner proves injury exceeding one fifth of his share, provided the action is brought within one year.</data>
  <data key="d9">injury threshold,rescission</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Solidarity" target="Joint and Several Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Solidarity establishes the legal framework governing the rights of joint and several creditors, including proportional or equal division of received payments.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor rights,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Solidarity" target="Joint and Several Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Solidarity establishes the legal principle under which each joint and several debtor is liable for the entire debt, and payment by one liberates all others.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor liability,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Solidarity" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under solidarity, joint and several creditors may take proceedings jointly or severally against the debtor, and amounts received are divided proportionally among all creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt collection,joint rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Solidarity" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under solidarity between debtors, payment by one debtor liberates all other debtors from the obligation, as stated in Article 284.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt performance,joint liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Creditor" target="Assignment of a Right">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Assignment of a right allows a creditor to transfer his claim to a third party, including all associated warranties, securities, privileges, and accrued interest, without requiring the debtor's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">claim assignment,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Creditor" target="Assignor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The assignor in an assignment of a right is the original creditor who transfers his claim to a third party.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor as transferor,role identity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Co-debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A joint and several debtor who pays the full debt has a right of recourse against each co-debtor for their respective share, and shares of insolvent co-debtors are borne pro rata by solvent co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">recourse,shared liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Novation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Novation between a creditor and one joint and several debtor releases other co-debtors unless the creditor expressly reserves his rights against them.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt release,obligation modification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Merger">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Merger in the person of a creditor and one joint and several debtor does not extinguish the debt as regards other co-debtors except to the extent of the merged share.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt extinguishment,partial effect</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Release of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Release of debt granted to one joint and several debtor does not release other co-debtors unless the creditor expressly declares such, and the creditor may claim the remaining balance from other co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">discharge,partial release</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Indivisibility">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under indivisibility, each debtor in an indivisible obligation is liable for the debt in full, and a debtor who pays has recourse against others for their shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">full liability,obligation type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Transaction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A transaction between a creditor and one joint and several debtor benefits other co-debtors if it involves remission of the debt, but is binding on others only if they consent when it creates or increases obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">benefit and consent,settlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Formal Demand">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A formal demand directed by the creditor at one joint and several debtor has no effect against other co-debtors, but a formal demand by a co-debtor against the creditor benefits all co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">limited effect,procedural act</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Acknowledgement of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An acknowledgement of debt by one joint and several debtor does not bind the other co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">non-binding effect,recognition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Oath">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An oath refused or tendered by one joint and several debtor does not prejudice the other co-debtors, but an oath taken by a co-debtor at the creditor's request benefits all co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">benefit and prejudice,judicial proceeding</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Joint and Several Debtor" target="Judgment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A judgment against one joint and several debtor has no effect against other co-debtors, but a judgment in favor of one co-debtor benefits all others unless based on a ground personal to that co-debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">court decision,selective effect</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-debtor" target="Article 298">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 298 provides that the share of an insolvent co-debtor is borne pro rata by the paying co-debtor and all other solvent co-debtors.</data>
  <data key="d9">contribution,insolvency</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-debtor" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Co-debtor shares responsibility for the debt with the Debtor and is not freed if the Debtor retracts a tender before acceptance or valid judgment.</data>
  <data key="d9">joint liability,shared obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Novation may be effected by a change of the Creditor when the Creditor, Debtor, and a third party agree that the third party shall be the new Creditor.&lt;SEP&gt;Novation may be effected by a change of the creditor, requiring agreement among the creditor, debtor, and third party.</data>
  <data key="d9">change of creditor,obligation change,party substitution,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party may become the new debtor or new creditor through novation with the consent of the original parties.&lt;SEP&gt;The Third Party participates in Novation by agreeing to substitute the original Debtor or by becoming the new Creditor through tripartite agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,participation,party substitution,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Voidable Contract">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Novation is only valid in the context of a voidable contract if the new obligation is intended to confirm the contract and replace the original obligation.&lt;SEP&gt;Novation of an obligation arising from a Voidable Contract is only valid if the new obligation is intended to ratify and replace the original voidable contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">confirmation,ratification,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Novation may be effected by a change of the Debtor when a third party agrees to take the place of the original Debtor, releasing the original Debtor from the obligation.&lt;SEP&gt;Novation may be effected by a change of the debtor, substituting a third party as the new debtor with the creditor's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">change of debtor,obligation change,party substitution,release</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Original Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Novation extinguishes the original obligation with all its accessories and substitutes a new obligation in its place.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinction,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="New Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Novation creates a new obligation to replace the extinguished original obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">creation,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Current Account">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Mere entry of a debt in a current account does not constitute novation; only fixing and agreeing the balance does.</data>
  <data key="d9">application,limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Promissory Note">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Subscription of a promissory note in respect of a pre-existing debt does not by itself constitute novation.</data>
  <data key="d9">distinction,exclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Delegation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Delegation constitutes novation by change of debtor when the parties agree to substitute a new obligation for the original obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal mechanism,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Article 353">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 353 establishes the conditions under which novation is valid, requiring both obligations to be free from nullity.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Article 354">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 354 provides that novation is not presumed and must be expressly agreed or clearly inferred from circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,presumption</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Novation" target="Article 356">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 356 defines the effects of novation on the original obligation, its accessories, and associated securities.</data>
  <data key="d9">effects,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Merger" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Merger extinguishes the debt when the creditor and debtor roles merge in a single person, with retroactive revival if the cause disappears.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinction,unification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Merger" target="Article 370">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 370 defines merger and its effects on the extinction and potential revival of debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">definition,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Merger" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Merger occurs when the qualities of creditor and debtor in the same debt unite in the same person, extinguishing the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinction,unification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Indivisibility" target="Article 300">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 300 provides the statutory definition of indivisibility, specifying when an obligation cannot be divided by nature or by the parties' intention.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal definition,statutory provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assignment of a Right" target="Article 303">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 303 is the statutory basis for the assignment of a right, establishing the conditions under which a creditor may transfer his claim to a third party.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal basis,statutory provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assignment of a Right" target="Assignor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The assignor is the party who transfers a right in the assignment, warranting the existence of the right in cases of valuable consideration but not warranting the debtor's solvency unless specifically stipulated.</data>
  <data key="d9">role,transfer of claim</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assignment of a Right" target="Attachment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Attachment limits the assignability of a right; a right can only be assigned to the extent that it is capable of being attached.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal constraint,transferability limit</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Transmission of an Obligation" target="Chapter IV">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Chapter IV organizes the legal provisions governing the transmission of obligations, including the assignment of rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">code organization,legal structure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Chapter IV" target="Section I">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section I is a subdivision of Chapter IV specifically addressing the legal rules on the assignment of a creditor's right to a third party.&lt;SEP&gt;Section I, titled "General Provisions," falls under Chapter IV on Privileged Rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal topic,structural hierarchy,structural organization,subdivision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-caf40c15d50ed811bc93db1880154564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495986</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Chapter IV" target="Privileged Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Chapter IV, titled "Privileged Rights," introduces the concept of priority rights established by law.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal topic,structural organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section I" target="Chapter III">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section I, titled "Elements of a Pledge," is a subsection of Chapter III, which covers "Rights Derived from the Right of Ownership Pledge."</data>
  <data key="d9">legal section,structural hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assignor" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The assignor does not warrant the solvency of the debtor unless specifically stipulated, and such warranty only applies at the time of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,warranty of solvency</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assignor" target="Warranty of Solvency">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The assignor does not warrant the solvency of the debtor unless such warranty is specifically stipulated, and if provided, it is limited to the time of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d9">limitation of liability,specific stipulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Attachment" target="Assignment of Right">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An attachment served upon the debtor before the assignment becomes effective against third parties makes the assignment equivalent to an attachment, and the debt may be divided pro rata between distrainers and the assignee.</data>
  <data key="d9">effectiveness against third parties,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment" target="Promise To Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a party refuses to honor a promise to contract and legal proceedings enforce the promise, a final judgment substitutes for and replaces the contract itself.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagee" target="Right of Preference">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Right of Preference entitles mortgagees to be paid before unsecured creditors from the proceeds of the mortgaged property.&lt;SEP&gt;The mortgagee exercises the right of preference to be paid before ordinary creditors from the proceeds of the mortgaged property.</data>
  <data key="d9">entitlement,legal privilege,payment,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagee" target="Lease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A lease entered into by the mortgagor has limited enforceability against the mortgagee, requiring an established date prior to the formal summons to pay.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability limitation,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagee" target="Assignment of Rent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An assignment of rent in advance has limited enforceability against the mortgagee, subject to established date and transcription requirements similar to those governing leases.</data>
  <data key="d9">advance payment,enforceability limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagee" target="Unsecured Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Mortgagees are paid before unsecured creditors out of the proceeds of sale of the mortgaged property, in accordance with the Right of Preference.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment order,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagee" target="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mortgagee may, upon maturity of the debt, take expropriation proceedings against the third party holder unless the third party holder chooses to pay the debt, redeem the mortgage, or abandon the property.&lt;SEP&gt;The Third Party Holder must pay inscribed debts or face expropriation proceedings initiated by the Mortgagee, and the Mortgagee must summon the Third Party Holder before initiating expropriation.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt payment,enforcement,expropriation,legal obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagee" target="Formal Summons to Pay">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The transcription of a formal summons to pay by one creditor benefits all other creditors of the mortgaged property.</data>
  <data key="d9">benefit,procedural effect</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgagee" target="Expropriation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Mortgagee may initiate expropriation proceedings against the Third Party Holder in accordance with the Code of Procedure after summoning the holder to pay or abandon the property.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,legal action</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgaged Property" target="Right of Tracing">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of tracing allows the mortgage to follow into substituted assets such as compensation, insurance, or expropriation payments when the mortgaged property perishes or deteriorates.</data>
  <data key="d9">substitution,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgaged Property" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge may order the cessation of acts that may result in loss or deterioration of the mortgaged property and adopt necessary protective measures.</data>
  <data key="d9">injunction,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgaged Property" target="Formal Summons to Pay">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">From the date of transcription of the formal summons to pay, the fruits and revenues of the mortgaged property are assimilated to the immovable and distributed like the price of the property.</data>
  <data key="d9">assimilation,trigger</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgaged Property" target="Rules of Accession">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The rules of accession determine the compensation owed by the landowner who retains constructions erected by a third party, which compensation becomes subject to the mortgage under Article 1038.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgaged Property" target="Expropriation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Expropriation is the legal process by which the mortgagee may compel the forced sale of the mortgaged property upon summons to the debtor to pay, governed by the Code of Procedure.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,forced sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgaged Property" target="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder holds the mortgaged property with specific rights including purge, abandonment, auction participation, and obligations including restitution of fruits and liability for deterioration.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership,rights and obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mortgaged Property" target="Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Mortgaged Property may be sold by public auction as part of expropriation proceedings, extinguishing mortgage rights upon deposit or payment of the purchase price.</data>
  <data key="d9">compulsory expropriation,forced sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscription" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The mortgage becomes effective against third parties only if inscribed before they acquire real rights on the property.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscription" target="Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Law Regulating the Publication of Real Rights governs the procedures for inscription, renewal, radiation, and annulment of registration of real rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,registration procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscription" target="Rank Assignment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The rank of a mortgage is determined from the date of its inscription, establishing the order of payment among mortgagees.</data>
  <data key="d9">priority,temporal order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscription" target="Privileged Right">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The provisions relating to Inscription, including its effects, renewal, and radiation, are applicable to Privileged Rights over immovable property in so far as they are not incompatible with the nature of those rights, as stated in Article 1134.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal formalities,registration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscription" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor's privilege over immovable must be inscribed and its rank is fixed by the date of inscription.</data>
  <data key="d9">rank determination,registration requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscription" target="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The co-partitioner's privilege over immovable must be inscribed and its rank is fixed by the date of inscription.</data>
  <data key="d9">rank determination,registration requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscription" target="Contractor's And Architect's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contractor's and architect's privilege must be inscribed and its rank is fixed by the date of inscription.</data>
  <data key="d9">rank determination,registration requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Preference" target="Ordinary Creditors">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Mortgagees exercise their right of preference to be paid from the proceeds of the mortgaged property before ordinary creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment order,priority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495987</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Preference" target="Article 234">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 234 establishes that all creditors are equal in the security of the debtor's property, subject to any right of preference acquired in accordance with law.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor equality,priority rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Tracing" target="Expropriation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of tracing allows the mortgage to follow into expropriation payments for public utility when the mortgaged property is subject to compulsory acquisition.</data>
  <data key="d9">substitution,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-185a4d5a4d8c2ab4a1aeb2eacd09fc19</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Tracing" target="General Privileges">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">General Privileges, even when extending over immovables, do not confer a Right of Tracing into the hands of subsequent holders, as established in Article 1134.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal limitation,property tracking</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Code of Procedure" target="Article 841">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 841 requires that property which cannot be divided in kind be sold in the manner laid down by the Code of Procedure.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal reference,sale procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Code of Procedure" target="Expropriation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Code of Procedure governs the expropriation proceedings that may be taken against the owner, third party holder, or receiver.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,procedural law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Formal Summons to Pay" target="Third Party Holder">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder's declaration of abandonment must be entered in the margin of the transcription of the Formal Summons to Pay.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal notification,procedural requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lease" target="Amodiation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Amodiation is governed by the general rules of lease, subject to specific provisions regarding produce sharing and shared loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,subtype</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lease" target="Right of Management">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person holding only a right of management cannot enter into a lease exceeding three years without the consent of the competent authority.</data>
  <data key="d9">duration restriction,legal limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Expropriation" target="Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Auction is the mechanism through which expropriation is effected, resulting in the transfer of ownership and extinguishment of mortgage rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">compulsory sale,legal process</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 842" target="Co-Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 842 grants personal creditors of any co-owner the right to oppose partition proceedings without their intervention.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor rights,opposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 843" target="Co-Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 843 deems each co-partitioner to have been the owner of the part falling to him from the date he became co-owner in common.</data>
  <data key="d9">deemed ownership,retroactive title</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 844" target="Warranty Against Eviction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 844 establishes the warranty among co-partitioners against eviction or interference caused by reasons predating the partition, with proportional liability.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,mutual indemnity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 846" target="Provisional Partition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 846 establishes the rules for provisional partition including its maximum duration of five years and conversion to final partition after fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d9">duration,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 848" target="Contract of Lease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 848 provides that provisional partition is governed by the provisions of the law relating to contracts of lease insofar as they are not incompatible with its nature.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,legal analogy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 849" target="Summary Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 849 empowers the Summary Judge to order a provisional partition upon the application of one co-owner when co-owners cannot reach agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,provisional partition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 850" target="Obligatory Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 850 prohibits co-owners from demanding partition when the purpose of the common property requires it to remain in obligatory joint ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">permanent indivision,prohibition of partition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 850" target="Family Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 850 establishes the principle that co-owners cannot demand partition when the property is intended to always remain in common, which underpins Family Joint Ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal foundation,partition restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 851" target="Family Joint Ownership">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 851 sets forth the legal basis for creating family joint ownership through written agreement among family members with a common occupation or interest.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 851 establishes the legal basis for creating Family Joint Ownership from inherited or other property by family members with a common occupation or interest.</data>
  <data key="d9">creation,creation provisions,family agreement,legal basis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-Owner" target="Right of Repurchase">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A co-owner may exercise the right of repurchase to buy back an undivided share sold by another co-owner to a third person.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal right,share recovery</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-Owner" target="Provisional Partition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners enter into provisional partition by allotting each other the enjoyment of divided parts equal to their shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">enjoyment rights,temporary allotment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-Owner" target="Warranty Against Eviction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-partitioners are bound to warrant each other against eviction or interference due to causes existing before the partition.</data>
  <data key="d9">indemnity,mutual obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-Owner" target="Right of Action">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A dissenting co-owner has a right of action before the Court within two months of notification of an alienation decision.</data>
  <data key="d9">dissent,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-Owner" target="Summary Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A co-owner who wishes to withdraw from joint ownership may summon other co-owners before the Summary Court when agreement on partition cannot be reached.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute resolution,legal proceedings</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-Owner" target="Personal Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Personal creditors of a co-owner may oppose partition in kind or sale by auction without their intervention, and such opposition compels all co-owners to join them in every stage of the proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor rights,opposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family Joint Ownership" target="Co-owners">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Family Joint Ownership is created and held by co-owners who are members of the same family with a common occupation or interest.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal arrangement,ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family Joint Ownership" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court has authority to authorize early withdrawal of shares from Family Joint Ownership and to discharge its manager.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute resolution,judicial oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family Joint Ownership" target="Article 852">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 852 limits the duration of Family Joint Ownership to fifteen years and provides mechanisms for early withdrawal.</data>
  <data key="d9">duration,withdrawal rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family Joint Ownership" target="Joint Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Family Joint Ownership is governed by the rules of Joint Property as stated in Article 855.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family Joint Ownership" target="Mandate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Family Joint Ownership is governed by the rules of Mandate as stated in Article 855.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family Joint Ownership" target="Non-family Member">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Non-family Member may only become a partner in Family Joint Ownership if he and all co-owners consent thereto, per Article 853.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent requirement,restricted entry</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Summary Court" target="Expert">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Summary Court may delegate one or more experts to value and divide common property into separate parts.</data>
  <data key="d9">court appointment,valuation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e2bf229fbd3c4efddfb708c780a85c16</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Summary Court" target="Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Estate notices must be posted on the notice board of the Summary Court within whose jurisdiction the deceased was domiciled at the date of death.</data>
  <data key="d9">jurisdiction,notice posting</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Expert" target="Administrator of an Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator may engage an Expert to assist in preparing the inventory and estimating the value of estate assets as permitted under Article 888.</data>
  <data key="d9">assistance,valuation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Expert" target="Administrator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator may employ an Expert for the preparation of the inventory and the estimation of the value of the estate property.</data>
  <data key="d9">employment,valuation assistance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Representative" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Representative is authorized to receive payment on behalf of the Creditor, and payment to the Representative is deemed valid as if made to the Creditor personally.</data>
  <data key="d9">agency,authorization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Representative" target="Principal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A representative acts on behalf of a principal, and rights and obligations from contracts concluded within authority are binding upon the principal.</data>
  <data key="d9">agency,representation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Representative" target="Mandatory">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mandatory is a specific type of representative who acts in accordance with the principal's precise instructions.</data>
  <data key="d9">instruction-based,type of</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Representative" target="Self-Dealing">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A representative is prohibited from contracting with himself in the name of the principal without authorization, to prevent conflicts of interest.</data>
  <data key="d9">conflict of interest,prohibition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Representative" target="Undisclosed Representation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a representative fails to disclose representative status at the time of contracting, the contract's effect on the principal depends on the third party's knowledge or indifference.</data>
  <data key="d9">disclosure failure,third party</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Payment may be made by the debtor, his representative, or a third party, but is only valid if the payer is the owner of that with which he has paid and has the capacity to dispose of it.&lt;SEP&gt;The Debtor is required to make payment as soon as the obligation is definitely created, using the very thing due, unless otherwise agreed or provided by law.</data>
  <data key="d9">fulfillment,obligation,performance,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment" target="Giving in Payment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Giving in Payment takes the place of Payment when the Creditor accepts another prestation in settlement of the right due.</data>
  <data key="d9">equivalent to payment,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment" target="Section I: Payment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section I: Payment encompasses the legal framework governing the parties to payment, conditions of validity, subrogation upon payment, and the proper recipient of payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,section coverage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Receipt" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Debtor has the right to demand a Receipt for partial payment and annotation on the document of title upon making any payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">proof of payment,right to demand</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495988</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Document of Title of Debt" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Debtor has the right to demand restitution or cancellation of the Document of Title of Debt upon full payment, or a written declaration of its loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">cancellation,restitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Giving in Payment" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Debtor may satisfy the obligation through Giving in Payment by providing the Creditor with a different prestation than what was originally due, provided the Creditor accepts it.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt discharge,substitution of performance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Delegation" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Delegation liberates the original debtor from the obligation to the creditor, provided the new debtor's obligation is valid and the new debtor is not insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d9">liberation,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Delegation" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In delegation, a third party undertakes to pay the creditor in the debtor's stead, with the creditor's acceptance.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment,undertaking</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Delegation" target="Article 359">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 359 defines delegation and its requirements.</data>
  <data key="d9">definition,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Delegation" target="Article 360">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 360 provides that delegation may constitute novation by change of debtor when parties agree to substitute obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,novation by delegation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Delegation" target="Right Of Recourse">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of recourse arises in the context of delegation when the new debtor's obligation towards the original debtor is void or contestable.</data>
  <data key="d9">new debtor,redress</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Summons" target="Interest">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon service of a Summons on the Creditor, Interest on the debt ceases to run.</data>
  <data key="d9">cessation,financial charge</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Summons" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Summons is served on the Creditor, after which the Creditor becomes responsible for loss or deterioration of the thing due.</data>
  <data key="d9">formal notice,legal process</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Interest" target="Loan for Consumption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Interest is the agreed charge associated with a loan for consumption, payable by the borrower as it falls due, and its absence means the loan is deemed gratuitous.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual obligation,financial terms</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Actual Tender" target="Final Judgment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Actual Tender is recognized as valid if confirmed by a Final Judgment, at which point the Debtor's retraction releases co-debtors and sureties.</data>
  <data key="d9">court recognition,validation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Actual Tender" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Debtor makes the Actual Tender of the thing due and may retract it as long as it has not been accepted by the Creditor or recognized as valid by a Final Judgment.</data>
  <data key="d9">offer,performance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Expenses" target="Principal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When an insufficient payment is made, Expenses are deducted first, then interest, and lastly the Principal of the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">imputation order,payment allocation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Expenses" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Debtor bears the Expenses in connection with payment unless an agreement or legal provision provides otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d9">burden of payment,cost allocation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Principal" target="Self-Dealing">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A principal may authorize or subsequently ratify a contract where the representative engaged in self-dealing, overriding the general prohibition.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,ratification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Principal" target="Undisclosed Representation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The principal is only bound by or benefits from a contract involving undisclosed representation if the third party necessarily knew of the representation or was indifferent to the identity of the contracting party.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding effect,knowledge</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Principal" target="Extinction Of Representation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When both the representative and the third party were unaware that the representation had ended, the effects of the contract revert to the principal or his successor in title.</data>
  <data key="d9">reversion,unawareness</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Principal" target="Voluntary Agent">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The principal is bound to carry out obligations entered into by the voluntary agent, indemnify him, reimburse expenses with interest, and compensate for loss.&lt;SEP&gt;The voluntary agent manages the principal's urgent business and owes duties of care, continuation, and notification to the principal.</data>
  <data key="d9">duty of care,indemnification,reimbursement,representation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Principal" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon the death of the principal, the voluntary agent remains bound by the same obligations to the heirs as he was to the deceased principal.</data>
  <data key="d9">continued obligation,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Former Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The third party holder has a claim for reimbursement against the former owner of the mortgaged property for all amounts paid to satisfy creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">property acquisition,reimbursement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Purge">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The third party holder may initiate the purge process to clear the property of all inscriptions by serving formal summons on inscribed creditors and offering to pay inscribed debts up to the valued amount.&lt;SEP&gt;The Third Party Holder may choose to purge the mortgaged property of all inscriptions by paying the sum at which the property is valued to creditors or depositing it at the Caisse of the Court.</data>
  <data key="d9">clearance,legal procedure,legal process,property clearance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Purchaser by Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser by auction is liable to refund the disposed third party holder for costs of the deed, its transcription, and summons served.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost liability,refund</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Inscribed Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The third party holder must serve formal summons on inscribed creditors when purging the property and may be compelled by inscribed creditors to pay debts if the amount owed is sufficient.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment obligation,procedural notice</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party holder may pay the secured debt and its accessories upon maturity and is subrogated into the rights of the satisfied creditor, with a claim for reimbursement against the debtor.&lt;SEP&gt;The Third Party Holder has a right of action against the Debtor for sums paid in excess of what is due under the title deed and is subrogated into the rights of creditors discharged.</data>
  <data key="d9">excess payment,payment,subrogation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder must pay creditors whose rank entitles them to payment, and is subrogated into the rights of creditors discharged by payment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Third Party Holder is personally liable towards creditors for any deterioration caused to the mortgaged property by negligence.</data>
  <data key="d9">deterioration,payment obligation,personal liability,subrogation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Abandonment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder may abandon the mortgaged property by submitting a declaration to the Registrar of the Court of First Instance and notifying the creditor conducting expropriation within five days.</data>
  <data key="d9">declaration,legal procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder may participate in the auction on condition of not offering a price lower than the sum still owed on the property.</data>
  <data key="d9">bidding condition,participation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Preceding Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder has a right of action for warranty against the Preceding Owner to the extent a successor in title has against the person from whom property was acquired.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal recourse,warranty claim</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Caisse of the Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder may deposit sums for property valuation, auction prices, or debt payments at the Caisse of the Court as an alternative to direct payment to creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">fund deposit,payment mechanism</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Title Deed">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder transcribes the Title Deed to establish ownership and is deemed owner by virtue of the original Title Deed upon acquiring the property at auction.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership proof,transcription</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Fruits of the Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Third Party Holder must restitute Fruits of the Property from the date of being summoned to pay or abandon, or from a new summons if proceedings are abandoned for three years.</data>
  <data key="d9">liability,restitution obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Third Party Holder in Good Faith is protected in rights acquired during the interval between the extinguishment and revival of a mortgage.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal protection,rights preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party Holder" target="Judgment of Adjudication">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a person other than the Third Party Holder acquires the property at auction, that person's right is derived from the Third Party Holder by virtue of the Judgment of Adjudication.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal derivation,property transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inscribed Creditor" target="Purge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Every inscribed creditor has the right to apply for the sale of the property being purged within thirty days of the last formal summons, potentially leading to a public auction.</data>
  <data key="d9">opposition,sale application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purge" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court receives deposits from the third party holder or applicant to cover costs and satisfies creditors, and the conditions of sale are filed before the Court.</data>
  <data key="d9">deposit,judicial oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Applicant" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The applicant must deposit at the Caisse of the Court a sum sufficient to cover the cost of the sale by auction and has no right to a refund if the auction does not yield a higher price than the third party holder's offer.</data>
  <data key="d9">deposit,financial obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compulsory Expropriation" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When an application is made for the sale of the property, the formalities laid down for compulsory expropriation must be followed in conducting the public auction.</data>
  <data key="d9">formalities,procedural requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-afa3ee7e2c4d684befc62e7fa3041d30</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Greffier of the Court" target="Registrar of the Cairo Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Greffier sends a copy of the judgment ending insolvency to the Registrar of the Cairo Court for the purpose of making a similar registration entry.</data>
  <data key="d9">communication,legal procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Conditional Obligation" target="Suspensive Condition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A suspensive condition is a form of conditional obligation where the obligation does not become executory until the condition is realized, as defined in Articles 267 and 268.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition type,legal classification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Conditional Obligation" target="Resolutory Condition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A resolutory condition is a form of conditional obligation where the obligation is extinguished upon realization of the condition, as defined in Article 269.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition type,legal classification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Conditional Obligation" target="Retroactive Effect">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The fulfillment of a condition in a conditional obligation is effective retroactively to the day the obligation was contracted, unless the will of the parties or the nature of the contract indicates otherwise, as stated in Article 270.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition fulfillment,retroactivity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suspensive Condition" target="Resolutory Condition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A suspensive condition determines the existence of an obligation, while a resolutory condition determines its extinction; both are governed by rules on void conditions in Article 266.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition types,legal contrast</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Suspensive Condition" target="Sale Upon Trial">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A sale upon trial is deemed to have been made subject to a suspensive condition of acceptance of the thing sold, unless the agreement or circumstances indicate a resolutory condition applies.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional sale,legal structure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Resolutory Condition" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon realization of the resolutory condition, the creditor must restitute what has been received and is liable in damages if restitution is impossible due to a cause for which the creditor is responsible.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation extinguishment,restitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Resolutory Condition" target="Sale Upon Trial">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A sale upon trial may alternatively be made subject to a resolutory condition instead of a suspensive condition, as determined by the agreement or circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative condition,conditional sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Time Clause" target="Retroactive Effect">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The extinction of an obligation at the end of a resolutory term has no retroactive effect, as stated in Article 274, distinguishing it from the retroactive effect of resolutory conditions.</data>
  <data key="d9">retroactivity,term expiration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Time Clause" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Before the expiration of a suspensive term, the creditor may take measures to protect his rights and demand security if he reasonably fears the debtor's bankruptcy or insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d9">protective measures,security demand</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Time Clause" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor benefits from the term of an obligation, but forfeits this benefit if declared bankrupt or insolvent, diminishes security given to the creditor, or fails to supply promised security, as stated in Article 273.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation timing,term benefit</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Alternative Obligation" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor has the right of election in alternative obligations and is freed entirely upon performance of one of the multiple prestations, as stated in Article 275.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation performance,option right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Facultative Obligation" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In a facultative obligation, the debtor may free himself by performing a substitute prestation in place of the principal prestation promised.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation performance,substitution right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Retroactive Effect" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A condition will not have retroactive effect if the execution of the obligation becomes impossible before fulfillment of the condition on account of a cause independent of the debtor and for which the debtor is not responsible, as stated in Article 270.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,retroactivity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Restitution" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon realization of a resolutory condition, the creditor must restitute what has been received and is liable in damages if restitution is impossible due to a cause for which the creditor is responsible, as stated in Article 269.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition realization,obligation to return</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Restitution" target="Legal Capacity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">&gt;A recipient lacking legal capacity is liable for restitution only to the extent of his enrichment from the undue payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity limitation,enrichment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Protective Measures" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor may take protective measures to safeguard his rights before the realization of a suspensive condition or before the expiration of a suspensive term, as provided in Articles 268 and 274.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal action,safeguarding rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">6.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Vendor transfers ownership of the thing sold to the Purchaser, and must allow the Purchaser sufficient acquaintance and trial of the thing where applicable.&lt;SEP&gt;The vendor and purchaser may agree to assign the mortgage debt in connection with the sale of mortgaged immovable property, requiring creditor ratification or refusal within six months of notification.&lt;SEP&gt;The vendor and purchaser are the two primary parties to a sale contract, with the vendor obligated to deliver the thing sold and the purchaser obligated to pay the price and bear costs of the deed.&lt;SEP&gt;The vendor is bound to the purchaser to transfer ownership, deliver the thing sold, and provide warranty against disturbance and eviction.&lt;SEP&gt;The Vendor and the Purchaser are the two contracting parties in the sale contract, with the Vendor obligated to deliver the thing sold and provide warranties, and the Purchaser obligated to pay the price and inspect the thing sold.&lt;SEP&gt;The Vendor sells property to the Purchaser, and both must be notified of any intention to exercise preemption within fifteen days of a formal summons.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual parties,debt assignment,mortgage,obligations,property transfer,reciprocal obligations,sale,sale contract,sale transaction,transfer of ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Obligations Of The Vendor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor is subject to the obligations of transfer, delivery, and warranty as prescribed by law.</data>
  <data key="d9">compliance,legal duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Legally Incapable Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor may be required to supplement the price when selling an immovable belonging to a legally incapable person with lesion exceeding one fifth.</data>
  <data key="d9">lesion protection,price supplement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Ownership Transfer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor is responsible for effecting the transfer of ownership of the thing sold to the purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,property conveyance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Delivery">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor is bound to deliver the thing sold to the purchaser in its proper state, including accessories and permanent appurtenances.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual obligation,transfer of possession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Warranty Against Disturbance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor guarantees the purchaser against disturbance in enjoyment of the thing sold, whether caused by the vendor or a third party.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal guarantee,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Revendication Action">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor must join as co-defendant or take the purchaser's place in a revendication action upon receiving proper notice.</data>
  <data key="d9">defense obligation,legal intervention</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Deficiency And Excess">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor is liable for deficiencies and entitled to a supplement of the price for excess quantities in the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">price supplement,quantity liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor's liability under the warranty of eviction may be affected by whether the vendor acted in good or bad faith, particularly regarding compensation for the purchaser's expenses.</data>
  <data key="d9">knowledge,warranty obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Sale Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Sale Contract binds the Vendor to transfer ownership of a thing or propriety right to the Purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding obligation,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Fraud">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraud may be committed by the Vendor in a sale contract by misrepresenting the thing sold to the Purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual misconduct,deception</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Warranty Against Dispossession">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Vendor is bound by the Warranty Against Dispossession to protect the Purchaser against loss of ownership to a person with superior title, and remains liable for dispossession resulting from the Vendor's own acts even if warranty is excluded.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee,legal obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Warranty Against Defects">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Vendor is liable under the Warranty Against Defects for any defects in the thing sold diminishing its value or usefulness at the time of delivery, even if the Vendor was unaware of their existence.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal obligation,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="The Thing Sold">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Vendor sells and must deliver the Thing Sold to the Purchaser, bearing warranty responsibility for its qualities and freedom from defects at the time of delivery.</data>
  <data key="d9">delivery,sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Bad Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Vendor may be found in Bad Faith when knowingly failing to disclose defects or third-party rights, resulting in enhanced liability including responsibility for superfluous expenditures.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal state,misconduct</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Preemptor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Preemptor assumes the Purchaser's obligations vis-a-vis the Vendor and cannot benefit from payment delays without the Vendor's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligations,payment,rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor of movable property holds a privilege over the movable sold to secure the price and accessories.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor of immovable property holds a privilege over the immovable sold to secure the price and accessories.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Sale Of An Inheritance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A vendor who sells an inheritance without particulars only warrants that he is an heir and must reimburse the purchaser for debts encashed or property sold from the inheritance.</data>
  <data key="d9">inheritance rights,warranty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Sale During Last Illness">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A vendor who sells during his last illness at a price below value is subject to the one-third rule, where the sale is only valid against heirs if the difference does not exceed one third of the inheritance.</data>
  <data key="d9">one-third rule,restricted sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor" target="Sale With Right Of Redemption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the vendor reserves the right to take back the thing sold within a fixed time at the time of sale, the sale is declared void under Article 465.</data>
  <data key="d9">reserved right,void transaction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Upon Trial" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser has the right to accept or reject the thing sold during a trial period, with silence after the agreed time constituting acceptance.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional acceptance,trial period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Subject To Tasting" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser may accept the thing sold based on tasting and must declare acceptance within the time fixed by agreement or custom.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional acceptance,tasting</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Price Determination" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser is affected by the method of price determination, which may rely on the market price when no specific price is fixed.</data>
  <data key="d9">market reference,price agreement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Price Determination" target="Contracting Parties">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contracting parties may fix the price for the thing sold or, if no price is fixed, the sale is not void if they intended to adopt the current trade price or their usual dealing price.</data>
  <data key="d9">price agreement,trade price</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lesion" target="Supplement Of Price Action">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The supplement of price action is a remedy available when lesion exceeds one fifth of the value of an immovable sold by a legally incapable person.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal remedy,price adjustment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lesion" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An action for supplement of price on grounds of lesion does not lie in respect of sales by public auction conducted in accordance with the law.</data>
  <data key="d9">exemption,legal procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lesion" target="Legally Incapable Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A legally incapable person is protected against lesion exceeding one fifth of the value of their immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d9">disadvantage,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lesion" target="Special Provisions for Lesion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The general provisions on lesion in Article 129 are subject to special legal provisions relating to lesion in certain contracts and to rates of interest, as stated in Article 130.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal hierarchy,specific rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lesion" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court, upon request of the prejudiced party, may annul a contract or reduce obligations in cases of lesion.</data>
  <data key="d9">annulment,judicial remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lesion" target="Avoidance of Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Avoidance of contract may be granted in cases of lesion when obligations are out of proportion to advantages due to exploitation of levity or passion, provided proceedings commence within one year, as per Article 129.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal remedy,unfairness</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supplement Of Price Action" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The supplement of price action does not operate to the prejudice of third parties in good faith who have acquired a real right on the immovable sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">good faith protection,real rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Ownership Transfer" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser acquires ownership of the thing sold, which may be subject to conditions such as full payment in a credit sale.</data>
  <data key="d9">property acquisition,rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Credit Sale" target="Installment Payment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In a credit sale with installment payments, the vendor may stipulate that ownership transfer is subject to integral payment of all installments.</data>
  <data key="d9">deferred payment,ownership condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Credit Sale" target="Contract Of Lease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The provisions governing credit sales, including ownership transfer conditions and installment payment rules, apply even if the contracting parties describe their contract as a contract of lease.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal characterization,substance over form</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Installment Payment" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge has the authority to reduce agreed damages in installment payment contracts according to circumstances, as referenced in Article 224.</data>
  <data key="d9">damages reduction,judicial discretion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Installment Payment" target="Contracting Parties">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contracting parties may agree on installment payments, including terms for the vendor retaining part of the price as damages upon cancellation.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual agreement,payment terms</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Deficiency And Excess" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser has the right to demand price reduction or contract cancellation when there is a deficiency in the quantity of the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">price adjustment,quantity dispute</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Perishment Before Delivery" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the thing sold perishes before delivery due to a cause beyond the vendor's control, the purchaser is entitled to dissolution of the sale and a refund of the price.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract dissolution,refund</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Deterioration Before Delivery" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser has the option to cancel the sale or uphold it at a reduced price if the thing sold deteriorates before delivery.</data>
  <data key="d9">cancellation option,price reduction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Disturbance" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party holding a right over the thing sold may cause disturbance to the purchaser, triggering the vendor's warranty obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">disturbance source,legal claim</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Revendication Action" target="Code Of Procedure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Code of Procedure governs the vendor's obligation to intervene in a revendication action brought against the purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal process,procedural law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Of Eviction" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser is entitled to compensation under the warranty of eviction, including refund of amounts paid to avoid dispossession along with legal interest and expenses.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Code Of Procedure" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Code of Procedure governs the recourse available against Court orders admitting claims related to inventory disputes.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal recourse,procedural governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 430" target="Article 224">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 430 paragraph 2 references Article 224 as the legal basis for the judge to reduce agreed damages in installment payment contracts.</data>
  <data key="d9">damages reduction,legal cross-reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fd28c11e235e6cd3a8e0ed791403ed14</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 440" target="Article 443">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 443 references Article 440 regarding the Purchaser's obligation to notify the Vendor of the revendication action as a condition for recovering certain litigation costs.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,statutory linkage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 224" target="The Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 224 empowers the judge to reduce agreed damages if the amount is grossly exaggerated or the principal obligation was partially performed.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,reduction of damages</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 224" target="Contractual Damages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 224 provides that contractual damages are not due if the debtor proves the creditor suffered no loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditions,non-entitlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owners" target="Common Parts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners must participate in the costs of preservation, maintenance, management, and reconstruction of Common Parts in proportion to the value of their shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost sharing,maintenance obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owners" target="Syndicate of Owners">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners in a building may form a Syndicate of Owners and are bound by its decisions taken by majority based on share value.</data>
  <data key="d9">collective decision-making,membership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owners" target="Non-family Member">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Co-owners must unanimously consent for a Non-family Member to acquire or be assigned a share of a co-owner in Family Joint Ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent requirement,share acquisition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-owners" target="The Syndicate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Syndicate decides on reconstruction of destroyed buildings and provides loans to co-owners secured by a privileged charge on their property.</data>
  <data key="d9">financial assistance,governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Ownership of Storeys in Buildings" target="Common Parts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Ownership of Storeys in Buildings designates Common Parts as indivisible portions co-owned proportionally by all building owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">property law,shared ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Ownership of Storeys in Buildings" target="Article 856">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 856 defines the legal framework for Ownership of Storeys in Buildings, establishing that different owners are co-owners of the ground and common parts.</data>
  <data key="d9">co-ownership rules,legal definition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Building" target="Common Parts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Building contains Common Parts including foundations, main walls, entrances, yards, roofs, lifts, passages, and pipes intended for common use by all owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">co-ownership,shared infrastructure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Building" target="Syndicate of Owners">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Building divided into storeys or apartments may be collectively managed by a Syndicate of Owners formed by the individual owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">collective management,governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Building" target="Inner Walls">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Inner Walls separating two apartments in a Building are owned as party property by the owners of those two apartments.</data>
  <data key="d9">party property,shared ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Building" target="Person in Charge of Building">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The person in charge of a building bears liability for damage caused by its collapse, even if partial, unless due to negligent maintenance, age, or defect.</data>
  <data key="d9">custody,liability for collapse</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Common Parts" target="Title Deeds">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Title Deeds may contain provisions that override the default legal rules regarding co-ownership and designation of Common Parts in a building.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual override,legal provisions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Common Parts" target="Article 858">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 858 obliges every owner to participate in the costs of preservation, maintenance, management, and reconstruction of Common Parts in proportion to the value of their share.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost obligation,maintenance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Syndicate of Owners" target="Representative of the Syndicate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Syndicate of Owners appoints a Representative of the Syndicate to carry out its decisions and manage common parts.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,execution of decisions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Syndicate of Owners" target="Article 864">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 864 grants the Syndicate of Owners the right to manage common parts through binding majority decisions when no management rules exist.</data>
  <data key="d9">decision-making authority,governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Syndicate of Owners" target="Collective Insurance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Syndicate of Owners may take out Collective Insurance against risks to the building or to co-owners jointly, with the prescribed majority consent per Article 865.</data>
  <data key="d9">insurance authorization,risk management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Representative of the Syndicate" target="President of the Court of First Instance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The President of the Court of First Instance may appoint or discharge the Representative of the Syndicate when the required majority of owners is not obtained.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial appointment,oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Representative of the Syndicate" target="Article 866">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 866 governs the appointment, duties, and court representation authority of the Representative of the Syndicate.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,duties</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Upper Storey" target="Lower Storey">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The owner of the Lower Storey is obligated to execute repairs to prevent the Upper Storey from falling, and the owner of the Upper Storey may not heighten the building so as to injure the Lower Storey.</data>
  <data key="d9">repair obligations,structural dependency</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495991</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Upper Storey" target="Article 860">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 860 grants the owner of the Upper Storey the right to rebuild the lower storey at the lower owner's cost and to withhold occupancy until repayment.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost recovery,rebuilding rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lower Storey" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may order the sale of the Lower Storey if its owner refuses to execute necessary repairs to prevent the Upper Storey from falling.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial enforcement,sale order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lower Storey" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge may order the sale of the Lower Storey or the execution of urgent repairs if its owner refuses to carry out necessary structural works.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial enforcement,repair orders</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 853" target="Non-family Member">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 853 restricts disposal of co-owner shares to a Non-family Member without the unanimous consent of all co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">share disposal restriction,unanimous consent</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 855" target="Joint Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 855 references Joint Property rules as supplementary law governing Family Joint Ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">governing law,legal reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 855" target="Mandate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 855 references Mandate rules as supplementary law governing Family Joint Ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">governing law,legal reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 859" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 859 grants the Judge authority to order the sale of a lower storey or urgent repairs when the lower storey owner refuses necessary maintenance.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 865" target="Collective Insurance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 865 authorizes the syndicate to take out Collective Insurance against risks to the building or co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,risk management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 868" target="Registered Creditors">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 868 preserves the rights of Registered Creditors when compensation for building destruction is allocated for reconstruction.</data>
  <data key="d9">priority protection,rights preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ad7032046527ea8a21f07927c9bceaa9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495992</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mandate" target="Voluntary Agent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The rules of mandate apply to a voluntary agent when the principal ratifies the agent's act, and the voluntary agent is bound by the same obligations as a mandatary regarding restitution and accounting.</data>
  <data key="d9">analogous legal framework,ratification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Registered Creditors" target="The Syndicate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Syndicate must allocate reconstruction compensation without prejudice to the rights of registered creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,rights preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Registered Creditors" target="Administrator of an Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator must publish notices calling on creditors to submit claims within three months and may only face proceedings from creditors through the administrator after appointment.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt settlement,notice</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Registered Creditors" target="Purchaser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Registered Creditors retain their rights of preference on the portion of the sale price of the property that reverts to the Purchaser, even after preemption is exercised.</data>
  <data key="d9">preference rights,secured claims</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Creditor who has obtained an enforceable judgment may apply for a Judgment Charge on the Debtor's immovable property as security for principal, interest, and costs.&lt;SEP&gt;The creditor may apply for a judgment charge to secure the debt owed by the debtor and has the same rights as a mortgagee once the charge is granted.</data>
  <data key="d9">application,legal right,security interest,security right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="Chapter II: Judgment Charges upon Immovable Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Chapter II governs the constitution and procedures for obtaining a Judgment Charge on immovable property, including eligibility requirements and application procedures.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,statutory framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor may lodge an appeal against the order authorizing a judgment charge, either before the judge who issued it or before the Court of First Instance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Debtor's specific immovable properties may be encumbered by a Judgment Charge as security for the debt owed to the Creditor, but no Judgment Charge may be taken after the Debtor's death on property in the estate.</data>
  <data key="d9">appeal,legal challenge,legal obligation,property encumbrance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="Enforceable Judgment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An Enforceable Judgment is a required legal basis for obtaining a Judgment Charge on immovable property, and certain types of judgments such as those confirming a compromise or agreement are acceptable, while judgments as to validity of a signature are not.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal prerequisite,statutory requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="Section I: The Constitution of a Judgment Charge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section I governs the creation, eligibility requirements, and procedural steps for establishing a Judgment Charge on immovable property, including the application process and judicial authorization.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,statutory framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="President Of The Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The President of the Court has the authority to grant or reject an application for a judgment charge.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,order</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="Court Of First Instance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court of First Instance serves as the appellate body for both the debtor and creditor to challenge orders related to the judgment charge.</data>
  <data key="d9">appellate review,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judgment Charge" target="Greffier Of The Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Greffier of the Court must notify the debtor of the judgment charge order and endorse it on the authenticated copy of the judgment or on the certificate.</data>
  <data key="d9">endorsement,notification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Enforceable Judgment" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Creditor must obtain an Enforceable Judgment rendered on the merits condemning the Debtor to a liquidated amount before obtaining a Judgment Charge.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal basis,prerequisite</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arbitration Award" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Creditor cannot obtain a Judgment Charge based on an Arbitration Award until the award has been made enforceable.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability,legal limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Foreign Court" target="Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Creditor cannot obtain a Judgment Charge based on a judgment rendered by a Foreign Court until that judgment has been made enforceable.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability,legal limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Donor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The donor is the party who makes the gift and is bound by obligations regarding delivery, warranty, and revocation.</data>
  <data key="d9">grantor,legal obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Donee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The donee is the party who receives the gift and acquires rights to delivery, warranty protection, and obligations regarding consideration.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal rights,recipient</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Valuable Consideration">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Valuable consideration may be attached to a gift, affecting the donor's warranty obligations and the donee's performance requirements.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,conditional gift</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Article 491">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 491 governs gifts of specific things not belonging to the donor by reference to Articles 466 and 467.</data>
  <data key="d9">governing provision,legal rule</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Article 492">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 492 declares that a gift of future property is void.</data>
  <data key="d9">future property,voidability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Article 372">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 372 states that the substantive rules governing gifts apply to the release of an obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">governing rules,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Release Of Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The substantive rules governing gifts apply to the release of an obligation as provided by Article 372.</data>
  <data key="d9">governing rules,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gift" target="Sale Of A Thing Belonging To Another">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under Article 491, a gift of a definite and specific thing not belonging to the donor is governed by the same provisions as the sale of a thing belonging to another, specifically Articles 466 and 467.</data>
  <data key="d9">analogous provisions,non-owned property</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Revocation of Gift">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The donor may initiate revocation of the gift either with the donee's consent or by court application based on reasonable grounds.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal grounds,revocation rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Article 493">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 493 obligates the donor to deliver the gifted thing to the donee when the donee has not taken possession.</data>
  <data key="d9">delivery obligation,legal duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Donee">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The donor transfers property to the donee without consideration, and may impose specific obligations on the donee; the donee must accept the gift for it to be complete.&lt;SEP&gt;The donor is obligated to deliver the gifted thing to the donee and has limited warranty obligations for dispossession and defects.</data>
  <data key="d9">delivery obligation,gift contract,obligations,warranty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Article 494">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 494 defines the conditions under which the donor warrants against dispossession of the gifted thing.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispossession,warranty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Article 495">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 495 addresses the donor's liability for defects in the gifted thing, particularly when defects are intentionally hidden.</data>
  <data key="d9">defect warranty,liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Article 496">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 496 limits the donor's liability exclusively to intentional acts or gross negligence, excluding liability for minor faults.</data>
  <data key="d9">fault standard,liability limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Future Property Gift">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A donor cannot validly gift future property as such a gift is declared void, and if the gift is of a specific thing not belonging to the donor, it is governed by the same rules as sale of a thing belonging to another.</data>
  <data key="d9">property limitation,void transaction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donor" target="Promise Of Gift">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A promise by a donor to make a gift is not binding unless it is made by an authentic document, as stated in Article 490.</data>
  <data key="d9">binding commitment,formal requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donee" target="Revocation of Gift">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The donee may consent to revocation or contest it, and is liable for restitution of fruits from the date of agreement or legal proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,restitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Donee" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge awards equitable compensation to the donee for prejudice suffered when the donor intentionally concealed the cause of dispossession.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,equitable remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 491" target="Articles 466 and 467">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 491 directs that gifts of specific things not belonging to the donor are governed by the provisions of Articles 466 and 467.</data>
  <data key="d9">governing provisions,legal reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 494" target="Dispossession">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 494 governs the donor's warranty obligations against dispossession of the gifted thing, including liability for intentional concealment and limits based on valuable consideration.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal protection,warranty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Revocation of Gift" target="Article 500">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 500 establishes the conditions under which a gift may be revoked, requiring either donee consent or court authorization.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,revocation procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Revocation of Gift" target="Article 501">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 501 enumerates specific reasonable grounds for revocation including ingratitude, financial inability, and birth of a child.</data>
  <data key="d9">ingratitude,reasonable grounds</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Revocation of Gift" target="Article 502">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 502 lists the obstacles that prevent revocation of a gift, such as inherent value increase, death of a party, and alienation.</data>
  <data key="d9">limitations,obstacles</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Revocation of Gift" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court has the authority to permit revocation of a gift when the donee does not consent and the donor has reasonable grounds.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,legal process</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 501" target="Alimony">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 501 establishes that the donor's inability to meet a legally binding alimony obligation constitutes a reasonable ground for revocation of a gift.</data>
  <data key="d9">financial obligation,revocation grounds</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 502" target="Spouse">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 502 provides that a gift made by one spouse to another cannot be revoked, even after the dissolution of the marriage.</data>
  <data key="d9">marital gift,revocation obstacle</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 503" target="Fruits">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 503 provides that upon revocation, the donee is liable for restitution of fruits only from the date of agreement on revocation or commencement of legal proceedings.</data>
  <data key="d9">restitution,revocation effects</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Partner">
  <data key="d7">4.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Partners are personally liable for Partnership debts not covered by Partnership assets, each in proportion to his share in the losses, and any agreement relieving a Partner from such liability is void.&lt;SEP&gt;A Partner is a member of the Partnership who contributes capital, services, or other assets and is entitled to a share of profits while being liable for losses and partnership debts.&lt;SEP&gt;Partners contribute property, services, or other assets to a partnership and share in its profits or losses.&lt;SEP&gt;Partners are members of the partnership with mutual obligations, the right to request dissolution, and entitlement to share in assets or losses upon liquidation.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual participation,contribution,debt liability,liability,membership,obligations,personal liability,rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Partnership Deed">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The partnership deed is the written instrument required to establish a partnership, and must be executed in writing under penalty of nullity.</data>
  <data key="d9">formation document,legal requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Juristic Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A partnership is deemed a juristic person upon constitution, acquiring legal personality enforceable against third parties after publication formalities.</data>
  <data key="d9">corporate status,legal personality</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Article 505">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 505 defines partnership as a contract involving contributions of property or services for sharing profits or losses.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual elements,legal definition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Third parties interact with the partnership and may invoke its juristic personality even when publication formalities have not been completed, as provided under Article 506.</data>
  <data key="d9">external relationship,legal standing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Managing Partner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Managing Partner is entrusted with the day-to-day management and acts of disposition of the Partnership, acting within its objects and subject to fiduciary duties.</data>
  <data key="d9">authority,fiduciary duty,management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="The Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may order the dissolution of the Partnership upon demand of any Partner for non-performance of obligations or for reasons not attributable to the partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">dissolution,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Article 526">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 526 governs the termination of a Partnership upon expiration of its term or achievement of its object, and provides for automatic year-by-year extension if operations continue.</data>
  <data key="d9">duration,extension,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Article 528">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 528 provides for termination of the Partnership upon death, interdiction, insolvency, or bankruptcy of a Partner, with provisions for continuation with heirs or remaining partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuation,death,insolvency,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Article 529">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 529 permits termination of the Partnership by retirement of a Partner with proper notice or by unanimous agreement of all Partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">retirement,unanimous dissolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs of a deceased partner may continue in the Partnership by agreement, even as minors, and are entitled to the deceased partner's share valued at the date of death, paid in money.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuation after death,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Non-Partner Manager">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Non-Partner Manager may manage the Partnership but, unlike a managing partner appointed under the Partnership Deed, may be discharged from his post at any time.</data>
  <data key="d9">dischargeable,management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Article 527">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 527 provides that the Partnership ends upon total or substantial loss of its capital, and is dissolved if a specific thing pledged as contribution perishes before delivery.</data>
  <data key="d9">capital loss,dissolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court has the authority to order the dissolution of a partnership upon the application of any partner for non-performance or other serious reasons.</data>
  <data key="d9">dissolution,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 508">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 508 presumes partners' contributions to be equal and to consist of ownership of property unless otherwise agreed.</data>
  <data key="d9">equal contributions,ownership presumption</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495993</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 510">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 510 makes a partner liable for interest on unpaid monetary contributions from the due date, plus compensation for any loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">interest liability,unpaid contribution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 511">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 511 applies sale or lease warranty provisions to a partner's property contributions, depending on whether ownership or use is contributed.</data>
  <data key="d9">property contribution,warranty provisions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 512">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 512 requires a partner contributing services to perform those services and account for resulting profits, without obligating transfer of patents.</data>
  <data key="d9">profit accounting,service contribution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 513">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 513 governs a Partner whose contribution consists of debts due by third parties, requiring recovery of those debts to extinguish his obligation to the Partnership.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 513 states that a partner contributing debts due by third parties is only discharged upon recovery of those debts and is liable for damages if unpaid at maturity.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt contribution,obligation,recovery obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Partnership Deed">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Partnership Deed defines each Partner's share in the profits and losses of the Partnership, with statutory default rules applying in the absence of such provisions.</data>
  <data key="d9">agreement,loss sharing,profit sharing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Personal Creditors">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Personal Creditors of a Partner may only claim payment from the Partner's profit share during the continuance of the Partnership and from the Partner's share in assets after liquidation.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor rights,debt recovery</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 514">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 514 establishes the default proportional rule for a Partner's share in profits and losses based on capital contribution when the Partnership Deed is silent.</data>
  <data key="d9">capital contribution,loss sharing,profit sharing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 523">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 523 makes a Partner personally liable for Partnership debts not covered by Partnership assets and voids any agreement relieving a Partner from such liability.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt liability,personal liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 519">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 519 grants non-managing Partners the right to personally examine the Partnership Books and Documents, and renders any agreement restricting this right void.</data>
  <data key="d9">examination rights,transparency</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 520">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 520 provides that each Partner is deemed authorized by the other Partners to manage the Partnership in the absence of any special provision on management.</data>
  <data key="d9">deemed authorization,default management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 521">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 521 obliges each Partner to abstain from activities prejudicial to the Partnership and to exercise the same standard of care as for his own interests, with a higher standard for remunerated managers.</data>
  <data key="d9">abstention,duty of care,fiduciary duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 522">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 522 makes a Partner liable for interest on Partnership money taken or retained and entitles a Partner to interest on private funds advanced to the Partnership in good faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">interest entitlement,interest liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Article 524">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 524 provides that when a Partner becomes insolvent, his share of Partnership debts is apportioned among the remaining Partners in proportion to their respective shares in the losses.</data>
  <data key="d9">apportionment,insolvency</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Partnership Capital">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Partner's share in profits and losses of the Partnership is proportional to his contribution to the Partnership Capital when the Partnership Deed does not fix respective shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">contribution,proportional sharing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Retirement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A partner may retire from the partnership by giving previous notice to co-partners, provided the retirement is free of fraudulent intent and occurs at a suitable time.</data>
  <data key="d9">notice,partnership rights,withdrawal</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partner" target="Exclusion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A partner may apply to the Court for the exclusion of another partner whose presence has caused objections or whose actions provide grounds for dissolution, while the partnership continues among the remaining partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">court application,expulsion,partnership continuity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 506" target="Juristic Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 506 establishes that a partnership acquires juristic personality upon constitution, with publication formalities required for recognition by third parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal recognition,third parties</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 506" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 506 requires completion of publication formalities before a partnership's juristic personality can be invoked against third parties, while allowing third parties to invoke it even without such formalities.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal recognition,publication formalities</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Legal Capacity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A juristic person has legal capacity within the limits fixed by its constitution or established by law.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity,legal rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="The State">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The State is classified as a juristic person under Egyptian law.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal entity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Wakfs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Wakfs are classified as juristic persons under Egyptian law.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal entity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Commercial And Civil Corporations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Commercial and civil corporations are classified as juristic persons under Egyptian law.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal entity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Religious Groups And Communities">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Religious groups and communities recognized by the state are classified as juristic persons.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal entity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Associations And Foundations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Associations and foundations created in accordance with the Civil Code are classified as juristic persons.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal entity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Domicile">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A juristic person has its own domicile, which is the place where its seat of management is situated.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal status,residence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Provinces (Mudirias)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Provinces (mudirias) are classified as juristic persons under Egyptian law in accordance with conditions fixed by law.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal entity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juristic Person" target="Article 965">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 965 provides that when the possessor is a juristic person, the good or bad faith of its representative is what determines the entity's legal position.</data>
  <data key="d9">good faith determination,representative intent</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership Deed" target="Article 507">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 507 requires the partnership deed to be in writing, prescribing nullity for non-compliance and limiting the effect of nullity between partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">nullity,writing requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-57a6161f06af628f254589c2ce62b0d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership Deed" target="Managing Partner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Managing Partner may be appointed by a special clause in the Partnership Deed, granting authority to act notwithstanding objections by other partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership Deed" target="Article 515">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 515 renders the Partnership Deed void if it provides that a Partner shall not participate in profits or losses of the Partnership.</data>
  <data key="d9">non-participation,validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partnership Deed" target="Liquidation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The liquidation and partition of partnership property are carried out in the manner specified in the partnership deed, with statutory rules applying when the deed is silent.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,procedural rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Custom" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge applies custom as a source of law when no applicable legislative provision exists.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial decision-making,legal hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Islamic Sharia" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge applies principles of Islamic Sharia when neither legislative provisions nor custom are available.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial decision-making,legal hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Natural Justice" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge applies principles of natural justice when legislative provisions, custom, and Islamic Sharia principles are all absent.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial decision-making,legal hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Natural Guardianship">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Natural guardianship applies to persons deprived of full or partial legal capacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Legal Guardianship">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Legal guardianship applies to persons deprived of full or partial legal capacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Curatorship">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Curatorship applies to persons deprived of full or partial legal capacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Personal Liberty">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Personal liberty is an inalienable right that cannot be renounced, forming a foundation of legal capacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">fundamental rights,inalienability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Person Under Legal Disability">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person under legal disability has limited or no legal capacity and is governed by rules of guardianship or curatorship.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity limitation,legal status</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Minor Lacking Discretion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A minor lacking discretion has no legal capacity to dispose of property, and all such acts are deemed void.</data>
  <data key="d9">incapacity,voidness</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Discerning Minor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A discerning minor has limited legal capacity: purely beneficial acts are valid, harmful acts are void, and mixed acts are voidable for the minor's benefit.</data>
  <data key="d9">limited capacity,voidability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Civil Code">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Civil Code establishes the rules governing legal capacity and the ability to conclude contracts.</data>
  <data key="d9">governance,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Capacity" target="Compromise">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Parties to a compromise must have legal capacity to dispose for valuable consideration of the rights that are the objects of the compromise.</data>
  <data key="d9">capacity requirement,contract validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Marriage" target="Divorce">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Divorce and separation are governed by the national law of the husband at the time of legal proceedings, distinguishing them from the general effects of marriage.</data>
  <data key="d9">family law,legal dissolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Real Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Possession, ownership, and real rights over movable property are governed by the law of the place where the movable was situated at the time of the relevant legal event.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance by location,property law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-51653f52168ac08cb3725623b6116442</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Consumable Things">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Consumable things are a subcategory of property whose utility consists in their consumption or disposal.</data>
  <data key="d9">consumption,property classification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Fungibles">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fungibles are a subcategory of property that can be replaced by one another in payment and are estimated by number, measure, volume, or weight.</data>
  <data key="d9">interchangeability,property classification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Rights In Non-Material Things">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Rights in respect of non-material things are a category of intangible rights regulated by special laws.</data>
  <data key="d9">intangible rights,special regulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Ownership">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Ownership of movable property is transferred by contract upon identification and may be subject to court-determined equity rules when mingled with other movables.</data>
  <data key="d9">property rights,real rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court decides matters in accordance with the rules of equity when movables belonging to different owners become mingled and cannot be separated without deterioration.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute resolution,equity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Article 1144">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1144 establishes privileges applicable to movable property in the form of effects brought by a traveller to a hotel.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,legal subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Article 1145">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1145 establishes the vendor's privilege applicable to movable property that has been sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,legal subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Article 1146">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1146 establishes the co-partitioner's privilege applicable to movable property that has been partitioned among co-owners.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,legal subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movable Property" target="Bearer Warrant">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both movable property and bearer warrants can be acquired by a possessor in good faith by virtue of a just title, and are subject to recovery claims if lost or stolen.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition by prescription,possession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 223" target="Contractual Damages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 223 establishes that parties may fix the amount of contractual damages in advance through contract or subsequent agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,pre-fixing damages</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 225" target="Fraud">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 225 permits the creditor to claim damages exceeding the contractually fixed amount when the debtor has committed fraud or gross negligence.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,increased damages</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 226" target="Moratory Interest">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 226 establishes the legal rates of moratory interest at four percent for civil matters and five percent for commercial matters.</data>
  <data key="d9">interest rates,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 227" target="Moratory Interest">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 227 caps the agreed rate of moratory interest at seven percent and treats excess commissions as disguised interest subject to reduction.</data>
  <data key="d9">agreement,maximum rate</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 227" target="Disguised Interest">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 227 defines disguised interest as any commission or consideration that, combined with agreed interest, exceeds the seven percent maximum, and subjects it to reduction.</data>
  <data key="d9">concealed charges,maximum interest</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">12.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The (Creditor, Debtor) relation describes the fundamental legal and financial relationship in which a Debtor owes an obligation or debt to a Creditor, who is entitled to receive payment or performance from the Debtor. The Debtor is obligated to fulfill the debt to the Creditor or the Creditor's representative, and this relationship is governed by rules on enforceability, restitution, and premature payment. The Creditor may refuse performance by a person other than the Debtor when the contract or the nature of the obligation requires personal performance.

The Creditor possesses a broad range of rights and remedies to protect and enforce the obligation. The Creditor may take protective measures, demand security, and initiate legal proceedings against the Debtor to recover the debt. Through an enforceable judgment, the Creditor may obtain judgment charges on the Debtor's immovable property, and both parties hold mutual rights to appeal orders related to such judgment charges before the Court of First Instance. The Creditor may also apply for judicial custody of the Debtor's movables charged with a privilege when there are reasonable grounds to fear misappropriation, as provided under Article 1133. When a surety is involved, the Creditor must first take proceedings against the Debtor before pursuing the surety and must prove the debt in bankruptcy to preserve rights against the surety.

On the Debtor's side, the Debtor must provide any promised securities to the Creditor and may be compelled to redeem an annuity upon failure to pay for two consecutive years or upon insolvency. The Debtor may also be subject to insolvency proceedings initiated by the Creditor. However, the Debtor retains certain rights, including the ability to interrupt the running of prescription by expressly or tacitly acknowledging the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt obligation,debt relationship,default,financial obligation,financial relationship,judgment,judicial protection,legal entitlement,legal proceedings,legal rights,obligation,payment,proceedings,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-216d3be312ab83530941a80552f52fd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7f532f3b01d9714eacf3e7b72f70dee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a30022d60171abe488b40b8463f0d09d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495981</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party who pays off a debt is subrogated into the rights of the creditor who has been paid off, acquiring all attributes, securities, and defenses of the debt.&lt;SEP&gt;The Third Party agrees with the Creditor to take the place of the original Debtor, releasing the original Debtor without the original Debtor's consent being necessary.&lt;SEP&gt;A creditor who receives payment from a third party may by agreement with the third party subrogate such third party into his rights even without the debtor's consent, provided the agreement is made at the time of payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">agreement,consent,debt assumption,payment,subrogation,subrogation agreement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor may apply to the judge to fix terms for the debtor's election, demand additional security, and take proceedings to enforce obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,legal recourse</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Creditor must be in Good Faith to obtain a Judgment Charge over the Debtor's immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d9">eligibility requirement,legal condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f71fe7de933a9312a43fa3b37e07559a</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495994</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Assignment of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An assignment of debt is not effective against the creditor unless ratified by the creditor, and the creditor's silence after notification and a reasonable period constitutes refusal.</data>
  <data key="d9">effectiveness,ratification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Person Assuming the Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An assignment of debt may also take place by a direct agreement between the creditor and the person assuming the debt, providing that such person replaces the original debtor in his obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt assumption,direct agreement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Lender">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor who has borrowed money from the lender may subrogate the lender into the rights of the creditor who received the payment, even without the creditor's consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">loan for payment,subrogation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Holder of Mortgaged Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A holder of a mortgaged property who pays all the mortgage debt is subrogated into the rights of the creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">full payment,subrogation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Extinctive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Extinctive Prescription limits the Creditor's ability to enforce claims after the prescribed period has elapsed.</data>
  <data key="d9">claim restriction,limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Release Of Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Release of obligation is effected by the creditor voluntarily discharging the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,voluntary discharge</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Compensation (Set Off)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Compensation extinguishes both debts between the debtor and creditor to the extent of the smaller debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,mutual debt</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Assignment Of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor assigns the creditor's right to a third party through assignment of debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">assignment,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Payor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The payor who makes a payment not due may claim restitution from the creditor, subject to exceptions based on good faith and legal capacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">restitution,undue payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Specific Performance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A creditor may demand specific performance, apply to the court for enforcement orders, and in cases of urgency carry out the obligation at the debtor's expense without a court order.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Annuity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor is entitled to receive annuity payments and may compel redemption if the debtor defaults, fails to provide securities, or becomes insolvent.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,entitlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditor" target="Securities">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor is entitled to receive and retain the securities promised by the debtor as protection for the annuity obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">collateral entitlement,creditor protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 228" target="Moratory Interest">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 228 establishes that moratory interest is due without requiring the creditor to prove loss resulting from the delay.</data>
  <data key="d9">no proof of loss required</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 230" target="Caisse Du Tribunal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 230 provides that the Caisse du Tribunal may be bound to pay interest as a result of deposit of the price of expropriated property.</data>
  <data key="d9">deposit,interest payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 230" target="Article 1111">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1111 subjects accrued interest secured by the pledge to the provisions of Article 230.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,interest limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section III" target="Article 234">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 234 is the opening provision of Section III governing the means of realizing and securing the rights of creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,structural organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section III" target="Extinguishment Of A Pledge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section III, titled "Extinguishment of a Pledge," encompasses the legal provisions governing termination of pledge rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal topic,structural organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court may maintain the lease in force for the purchaser of an industrial or commercial establishment despite a prohibition on sub-letting, provided the purchaser furnishes adequate security and the lessor suffers no real prejudice.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authorization,lease maintenance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-387cddeaa4a9e7d2617ee1f26ca2f811</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495985</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Sale Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Sale Contract entitles the Purchaser to acquire ownership of a thing in consideration of a price in money.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,consideration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Fraud">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraud proven against the Vendor entitles the Purchaser to claim annulment of the sale even when the contract states the Purchaser is acquainted with the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">annulment,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Warranty Against Dispossession">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Purchaser is the beneficiary of the Warranty Against Dispossession, entitled to claim compensation, refunds, and legal interest when the thing sold is lost to a person with superior title.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Warranty Against Defects">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Purchaser is the beneficiary of the Warranty Against Defects, entitled to bring a warranty action for defects discovered in the thing sold, provided timely notice is given to the Vendor.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="The Thing Sold">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Purchaser buys and receives the Thing Sold, and must inspect it upon delivery and give timely notice of any defects to preserve warranty rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">inspection,purchase,receipt</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Legal Interest">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Purchaser is entitled to Legal Interest on amounts recovered from the Vendor in warranty claims, accruing from the time of dispossession or from the date of formal summons.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,financial entitlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Article 449">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 449 imposes on the Purchaser the obligation to inspect the thing sold upon delivery and give timely notice of defects to the vendor.</data>
  <data key="d9">inspection,legal obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Article 456">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 456 establishes the Purchaser's obligation to pay the price at the place of delivery or at the Purchaser's domicile if payment is deferred.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment obligation,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Article 457">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 457 grants the Purchaser the right to retain the price when disturbed by a third party or when a defect is discovered, subject to the vendor's right to demand payment upon supplying security.</data>
  <data key="d9">price retention,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Dissolution of Sale">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Purchaser may seek Dissolution of Sale as a remedy when the loss from dispossession or defect is sufficiently severe, or when the price is unpaid in commodity sales.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract termination,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Preemptor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Preemptor is substituted for the Purchaser in all rights and obligations vis-a-vis the Vendor upon exercising the right of preemption.</data>
  <data key="d9">rights and obligations,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Sale Of A Thing Belonging To Another">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser may demand annulment of a sale when the thing sold does not belong to the vendor, and may claim damages even if the vendor acted in good faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">annulment,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Purchaser" target="Sale Of An Inheritance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The purchaser of an inheritance must reimburse the vendor for debts of the inheritance paid by the vendor and must fulfill necessary formalities for the transfer of rights to be effective against third parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">formalities,reimbursement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 234" target="The Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 234 provides that all of the debtor's property serves as security for the debtor's debts to creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor's property,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 235" target="The Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 235 grants the creditor the right to exercise the debtor's rights of action to prevent or remedy insolvency.</data>
  <data key="d9">exercise of rights,subrogation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 236" target="The Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 236 deems the creditor acting as the debtor's representative, with proceeds from exercising rights falling into the debtor's patrimonium.</data>
  <data key="d9">proceeds,representation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 237" target="Paulian Action">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 237 establishes the Paulian action, allowing creditors to demand that prejudicial acts of alienation by the debtor be declared void.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor protection,voiding acts</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 238" target="Paulian Action">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 238 sets the conditions for the Paulian action, requiring proof of fraud for acts with valuable consideration and allowing invalidation of gratuitous acts without proof of fraud.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditions,fraud</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 238" target="Fraud">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 238 requires proof of the debtor's fraud and the other party's knowledge of that fraud as conditions for invalidating acts for valuable consideration under the Paulian action.</data>
  <data key="d9">Paulian action,fraudulent intent</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 241" target="Caisse Du Tribunal">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 241 allows a transferee to escape creditor action by depositing the normal price at the Caisse du Tribunal.</data>
  <data key="d9">deposit of price,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 242" target="Fraud">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 242 provides that fraud consisting solely of giving one creditor unjustified preference over another results only in the loss of that advantage by the preferred creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">penalty,unjustified preference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 243" target="Paulian Action">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 243 sets the prescription periods for the Paulian action at three years from knowledge and fifteen years absolute.</data>
  <data key="d9">limitation period,prescription</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="The Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may reduce damages or refuse interest based on the debtor's circumstances including partial performance or creditor bad faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial oversight,obligation enforcement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="The Creditor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge is empowered to protect creditor rights by awarding compensation, reducing interest, and declaring prejudicial acts void.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute resolution,judicial protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="Testamentary Executor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge must confirm the appointment of a testamentary executor made by the deceased before the executor may act.</data>
  <data key="d9">confirmation,oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="Administrator of an Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge appoints administrators, may discharge and replace them for adequate reasons, and requires periodic accountings of administration.</data>
  <data key="d9">accountability,appointment,discharge</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="Pledgee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge authorizes the pledgee to sell or appropriate the pledged thing upon failure of payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,judicial authorization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="Pledgor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge may authorize the pledgor to sell the pledged thing at an advantageous price even before debt maturity.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authorization,sale permission</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Public auction is a sale method authorized by the Judge for disposing of pledged property in cases of deterioration, danger of loss, or debtor default.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforcement,judicial authorization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Judge" target="Pledged Thing">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge authorizes the sale, disposition, or appropriation of the pledged thing in cases of danger, deterioration, or default.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,sale approval</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Creditor" target="The Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The creditor is owed obligations by the debtor and is granted various legal protections including interest, damages, and the right to challenge prejudicial acts.</data>
  <data key="d9">obligation,rights and duties</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Creditor" target="Bad Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A creditor who acts in bad faith by unjustifiably prolonging litigation may face reduction or denial of interest as a judicial penalty under Article 229.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial penalty,prolonged litigation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Debtor" target="Fraud">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor may commit fraud through acts of alienation intended to diminish assets or increase obligations to the prejudice of creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">deceptive conduct,legal consequence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Debtor" target="Bad Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor who acts in bad faith causing loss exceeding moratory interest may be liable for supplementary damages in addition to interest under Article 231.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor misconduct,supplementary damages</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-44d4f4013c73026f1d71efd0ee65bdc0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Fraud" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor always remains liable for fraud and cannot be contractually discharged from liability arising from personal fraud.</data>
  <data key="d9">misconduct,perpetual liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Bad Faith" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Good faith and bad faith are contrasting legal states that determine the extent of a recipient's obligation to return undue payments and profits.</data>
  <data key="d9">contrasting states,legal standard</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Bad Faith" target="Possession">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Bad faith, including usurpation by violence, negatively affects the legal character and consequences of possession.</data>
  <data key="d9">character of possession,legal disadvantage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Bad Faith" target="Violence">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Usurpation of possession by violence is automatically deemed to constitute bad faith, negatively affecting the usurper's legal position.</data>
  <data key="d9">deemed bad faith,legal consequence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The State" target="Uncultivated Land">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The State owns uncultivated land without an owner and regulates its appropriation through licensing in accordance with regulations.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership,regulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The State" target="Provinces (Mudirias)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Provinces (mudirias) are administrative divisions of the state recognized as juristic persons under Egyptian law.</data>
  <data key="d9">administrative division,governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mohammedan Law" target="Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Mohammedan Law governs the establishment of heirs, their hereditary shares, and the devolution of estate property.</data>
  <data key="d9">inheritance,legal governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Court" target="Administrator of an Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court appoints administrators, oversees the winding up process, and authorizes remuneration commensurate with duties performed.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,remuneration,supervision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495995</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Court" target="Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court orders necessary urgent measures for the preservation of estate property, including placing property under seal and depositing valuables.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial oversight,preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Court" target="The Greffier">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Greffier records court orders regarding the appointment of administrators and confirmation of testamentary executors in a public register with alphabetical indexes.</data>
  <data key="d9">record-keeping,registration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Court" target="Article 530">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 530 empowers The Court to order dissolution of the Partnership for non-performance by a Partner or for reasons not attributable to the Partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial dissolution,non-performance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator takes possession of estate property upon appointment, preserves and administers it, and proceeds with the winding up under court control.</data>
  <data key="d9">administration,possession,winding up</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator must obtain authorization to pay alimentary allowances to heirs supported by the deceased, deductible from their inheritance shares.</data>
  <data key="d9">alimentary allowance,debt recovery</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="The Deceased">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator must immediately pay burial and funeral expenses of the deceased from estate assets in accordance with the deceased's social standing.</data>
  <data key="d9">funeral expenses,obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="Testamentary Executor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The rules applicable to an administrator of an estate, including responsibilities and liabilities, apply equally to a testamentary executor.</data>
  <data key="d9">equivalent role,legal equivalence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="Ministere Public">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Ministere Public can request the discharge and replacement of an estate administrator for adequate reasons.</data>
  <data key="d9">discharge request,oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="Estate Winding Up">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Estate Winding Up is carried out by the Administrator who must preserve assets, pay debts, publish notices, file statements, and ultimately distribute the remaining property to heirs.</data>
  <data key="d9">process execution,responsibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="Debtors of the Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator must publish a notice calling on Debtors of the Estate to submit particulars of their debts within three months of the last publication.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt notification,estate settlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator of an Estate" target="Statement of Assets and Liabilities">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator must file the Statement of Assets and Liabilities with the Court registry within four months of appointment and inform all interested parties by registered letter.</data>
  <data key="d9">estate documentation,filing obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Testamentary Executor" target="The Deceased">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Testamentary Executor is appointed by the deceased to manage the estate subject to judicial confirmation.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,testamentary instruction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Greffier" target="Public Register">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Greffier maintains the Public Register by entering court orders regarding the appointment of administrators and confirmation of testamentary executors, recording deceased persons' names alphabetically.</data>
  <data key="d9">maintenance,record-keeping</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Juge de Service" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Juge de Service authorizes alimentary allowances to heirs who were supported by the deceased and resolves any disputes arising regarding such allowances.</data>
  <data key="d9">alimentary allowance,dispute resolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inheritance Certificate" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs must receive an Inheritance Certificate before disposing of estate assets, recovering estate debts, or setting off personal debts against estate debts.</data>
  <data key="d9">asset disposal,prerequisite</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Omdah" target="Estate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Omdah's main residence door serves as an official posting location for estate notices calling on creditors and debtors to submit their claims.</data>
  <data key="d9">administrative role,public notice</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate" target="Heirs">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs are entitled to receive the residue of the Estate after settlement of all liabilities, in proportion to their respective shares in the inheritance.&lt;SEP&gt;Heirs are entitled to inherit shares of the estate after all debts and obligations have been settled through the winding up process.</data>
  <data key="d9">entitlement,inheritance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate" target="The Deceased">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Estate comprises the property and obligations left by the deceased, subject to administration and distribution under court supervision.</data>
  <data key="d9">origin,property ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate" target="Administrator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator is responsible for managing, inventorying, and administering the Estate, including recovering claims, paying debts, and distributing remaining assets.</data>
  <data key="d9">administration,management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Movable and immovable property of the Estate is sold by Public Auction in accordance with rules for forced sales, unless all heirs agree to an alternative method.</data>
  <data key="d9">asset liquidation,forced sale</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate" target="Deceased">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Estate comprises all property, assets, rights, and liabilities that belonged to the Deceased at the time of death.</data>
  <data key="d9">origin,ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Public Register">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The entry of an administrator's appointment in the Public Register has the same effect vis-à-vis third parties dealing with Heirs in connection with immovable property belonging to the estate as the entry provided for in Article 914.</data>
  <data key="d9">property rights,third-party protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Article 528">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 528 permits the Partnership to continue with the Heirs of a deceased partner by agreement, with the Heirs entitled to the deceased partner's share valued at the date of the triggering event.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuation,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Court">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court issues inheritance certificates to Heirs upon production of an Elam Charei, resolves disputes over inventory and division, and orders the sale or allocation of family papers and sentimental articles.</data>
  <data key="d9">certification,dispute resolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Deceased">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs are the legal successors of the Deceased, entitled to inherit property and obligated to report known debts and claims of the estate.</data>
  <data key="d9">inheritance,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Administrator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator delivers estate property to the Heirs, proceeds with amicable or judicial division, and provides interim delivery of items not needed for winding up the estate.</data>
  <data key="d9">delivery,distribution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Public Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs are always entitled to take part in the Public Auction of estate movable and immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d9">bidding rights,participation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Voluntary Agent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon the death of a voluntary agent, his heirs are bound by the same obligations as those of a mandatary's heirs under Article 717.</data>
  <data key="d9">inherited obligation,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Article 717">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 194 references Article 717 to determine the specific obligations of the heirs of a voluntary agent, applying the same rules as those for the heirs of a mandatary.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,inherited obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs" target="Sale During Last Illness">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs are protected in sales made during last illness; if the difference between the value and price exceeds one third of the inheritance, the sale is only valid against heirs if they ratify it or if the purchaser pays the amount necessary to make up the two thirds.</data>
  <data key="d9">inheritance protection,ratification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movables Without an Owner" target="Acquisition by Appropriation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acquisition by Appropriation is the legal method by which a person who takes possession of a movable without an owner, with the intention of appropriation, acquires ownership of it.</data>
  <data key="d9">method,ownership acquisition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Movables Without an Owner" target="Wild Animals">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Wild Animals are deemed to have no owner as long as they remain at liberty, falling under the category of ownerless property.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal status</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wild Animals" target="Domestic Animals">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Domestic Animals are distinguished from Wild Animals in that they are not automatically considered ownerless when at liberty, though a tamed animal that loses the habit of returning to its designated place becomes ownerless again.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal distinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Special Regulations" target="Antiquities">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Rights to Antiquities are governed by Special Regulations as specified in Article 873.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,regulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-167d3617b2a011b102a6a4698ea987b0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495996</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Cattle and Agricultural Implements" target="Article 622">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 622 provides that cattle and agricultural implements belonging to the Lessor on the land at the time of agreement are automatically included in an amodiation lease.</data>
  <data key="d9">amodiation,automatic inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Amodiation" target="Article 620">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 620 establishes that general lease conditions apply to Amodiation in the absence of contrary agreement or custom.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicability,governing law</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Amodiation" target="Article 621">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 621 sets the default duration of Amodiation to one yearly rotation of crops when no term is fixed by the parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">crop rotation,default duration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 609" target="Article 365">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 609 requires compliance with the notice periods set forth in Article 365 for lease termination by officials and employees.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,notice requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 633" target="Three-Year Lease Limit">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 633 establishes the three-year maximum lease period for wakf property without judicial authorization.</data>
  <data key="d9">lease restriction,legal basis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 365" target="Compensation (Set Off)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 365 provides that compensation takes place only when invoked by the interested party, extinguishes both debts to the extent of the smaller amount, and cannot be renounced before the right arises.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,invocation,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 618" target="Incoming Lessee">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 618 protects the Incoming Lessee's right to prepare and sow the land by prohibiting the outgoing Lessee from diminishing or retarding enjoyment of the land.</data>
  <data key="d9">continuity of use,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-e12a49355a81a50aa510c658916d11d8</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgor" target="Pledgee">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgor delivers the thing pledged to the pledgee as security for a debt; the pledgee must preserve and manage it with reasonable care and restitute it upon repayment.&lt;SEP&gt;The pledgee, as creditor, holds the pledge granted by the pledgor, as debtor, and may enforce it upon default while the pledgor may seek restitution or advantageous sale.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual relationship,creditor-debtor relationship,pledge enforcement,pledge obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgor" target="The Thing Pledged">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgor is bound to deliver the thing pledged, guarantees its integrity and efficacy, and must not diminish its value or prevent the pledgee from exercising contractual rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">delivery,guarantee</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgor" target="Contract Of Pledge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgor is the debtor who grants the contract of pledge as security for a debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor obligations,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgor" target="Debtor Of A Pledged Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a pledged debt falls due prematurely, the debtor must discharge it jointly to the pledgee and pledgor, and both must cooperate on investment and creation of a new pledge, as per Article 1128.</data>
  <data key="d9">cooperation,joint obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgee" target="The Thing Pledged">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgee has the right to retain the thing pledged against all persons, must manage it with the care of a reasonable person, and may not derive gratuitous advantage from it.</data>
  <data key="d9">management,preservation,retention</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgee" target="Fruits And Revenue">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgee must invest and manage the thing pledged to produce all possible fruits and revenue, and must apply the net revenue in reduction of the secured debt following a prescribed order of imputation.</data>
  <data key="d9">application of income,debt reduction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgee" target="Contract Of Pledge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledgee is the creditor who holds and benefits from the contract of pledge, with rights of enforcement and obligations of preservation.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor rights,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgee" target="Debtor Of A Pledged Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor of a pledged debt may raise against the pledgee defenses regarding the validity of the secured debt and defenses against the original creditor, as governed by Article 1127.</data>
  <data key="d9">defenses,legal standing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledgee" target="Pledged Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Pledgee may collect the Pledged Debt up to the amount due, demand its sale, or request its allocation if unpaid when both debts fall due, as governed by Article 1129.</data>
  <data key="d9">collection,security right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Thing Pledged" target="Third Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party chosen by the contracting parties may hold the thing pledged to make the pledge valid as against other third parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">custody,third-party rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3e1cec3036487db165cfc64219e07fe0</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495997</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person Assuming the Debt" target="Original Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The original debtor cannot call upon the person assuming the debt to make payment to the creditor so long as the original debtor has not discharged his own obligation to the person assuming the debt under the contract of assignment.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition precedent,payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 318" target="Article 321">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 321 provides that the provisions of Article 318 regarding transmission of warranties and surety obligations are applicable when assignment of debt is made between the creditor and the person assuming the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicable provisions,cross-reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 320" target="Article 321">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 321 provides that the provisions of Article 320 regarding defenses available to the person assuming the debt are applicable when assignment of debt is made between the creditor and the person assuming the debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicable provisions,cross-reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lender" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor who has borrowed money from the lender to pay off a debt may subrogate the lender into the rights of the creditor who received the payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">borrowing,subrogation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lender" target="Borrower">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lender transfers ownership of money or fungibles to the borrower, who is obligated to return an equivalent thing at the end of the loan and pay agreed interest.</data>
  <data key="d9">loan contract,obligation,transfer of ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lender" target="Hidden Defect">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lender who deliberately hides defects in the loaned thing is liable to either repair the defect or replace the defective thing, even in a gratuitous loan.</data>
  <data key="d9">defect liability,disclosure obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may grant the Debtor one or more reasonable delays for performance of the obligation in exceptional cases where no serious prejudice is caused to the Creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">delay,grace period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495990</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Administrator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator files the inventory with the Court, seeks Court authorization for debt payments, and acts under Court authority in estate matters.</data>
  <data key="d9">authorization,reporting</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Inventory">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court investigates disputes regarding the accuracy of the Inventory and admits or rejects claims by order within thirty days of the inventory filing notice.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute adjudication,oversight</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Elam Charei">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An heir must produce an Elam Charei to the Court in order to obtain a certificate establishing rights in the inheritance and the extent of the heir's share.</data>
  <data key="d9">certification prerequisite,documentation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Family Papers">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court orders either the sale or allocation of Family Papers to one of the heirs, with or without deduction from the heir's share, in the absence of agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">allocation,dispute resolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Agricultural Industrial Or Commercial Enterprise">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court ensures that an Agricultural, Industrial or Commercial Enterprise constituting a distinct economic unity is allotted as a whole to the most capable heir.</data>
  <data key="d9">allocation,economic unity preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Contract For Work">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court has authority to reduce compensation for lost profit, grant a price increase, or order the resiliation of the Contract for Work in cases of economic imbalance caused by exceptional unforeseen events.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract adjustment,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Preemption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court handles actions in preemption, which must be introduced before the district court where the property is situated and enrolled on the Court list as a matter of urgency.</data>
  <data key="d9">adjudication,legal procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court considers the Good Faith of each party when deciding matters related to mingled movables in accordance with the rules of equity.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute resolution,equitable consideration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Person in Charge of Building">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An endangered person may obtain a court order to take preventive measures against building danger at the owner's cost if the owner fails to act.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authorization,preventive measures</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Article 185">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The filing of a claim in court triggers the obligation to restitute interest and profits from the date of the claim, even for a good faith recipient.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial proceeding,trigger event</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Minor Possessing Discretion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acts of a minor possessing discretion may be ratified by the minor upon attaining majority, by the guardian, or by the court.</data>
  <data key="d9">majority,ratification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Judicial Adviser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court may appoint a judicial adviser to assist persons who are deaf and dumb, deaf and blind, or blind and dumb in acts necessary for their interests.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,assistance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Interdiction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court has the authority to pronounce or raise interdictions on persons suffering from specified conditions.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,pronouncement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Exclusion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court has the authority to order the exclusion of a partner upon application by another partner, allowing the partnership to continue among the remaining partners.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,partner removal</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court" target="Retirement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Court may authorize a partner's retirement from a fixed-duration partnership when the partner provides adequate reasons for the application.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authorization,partner withdrawal</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Receipt in Discharge" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person who produces to the debtor a receipt in discharge issued by the creditor is deemed qualified to receive payment, unless it has been agreed that payment shall be made to the creditor in person.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment receipt,proof of authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract of Assignment" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor may raise defenses arising from the contract of assignment against the assignee, in addition to defenses available against the assignor.</data>
  <data key="d9">defenses,legal rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Managing Partner" target="Article 516">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 516 governs the appointment, authority, and removal of a Managing Partner, distinguishing between appointments in the Partnership Deed and subsequent appointments.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,authority,removal</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Managing Partner" target="Article 517">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 517 regulates situations where multiple Managing Partners are appointed, establishing rules for individual action, objection, and majority decision-making.</data>
  <data key="d9">decision-making,multiple management,voting</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Personal Creditors" target="Article 525">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 525 restricts Personal Creditors from claiming against a Partner's capital share during the Partnership but permits protective attachment before liquidation.</data>
  <data key="d9">attachment,creditor rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495998</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Personal Creditors" target="Partnership Liquidation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Personal Creditors may enforce their claims against their debtor Partner's share in Partnership assets only after Partnership Liquidation and payment of Partnership debts.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt recovery,post-liquidation claims</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 516" target="Non-Partner Manager">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 516 provides that Non-Partner Managers are always dischargeable from their management post, unlike managing partners appointed by the Partnership Deed.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,discharge</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2ca3d37a2b6a416649d044c8f474249f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495999</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Auction" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Debtor may sell perishable or costly-to-store items by Public Auction with the permission of the judge and deposit the proceeds in the Caisse du Tribunal.</data>
  <data key="d9">court-authorized sale,perishable goods</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Auction" target="Forced Sales">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Public Auction of estate property is conducted in accordance with the manner and delays laid down for Forced Sales.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal compliance,procedural framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Auction" target="Preemption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Preemption cannot be exercised if the sale of the immovable property is conducted by Public Auction in accordance with legally prescribed procedures.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusion,sale method</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Defects" target="Judicial Sale">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Judicial Sales are exempt from the Warranty Against Defects, meaning the vendor in such sales is not liable for defects in the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusion,legal exception</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Defects" target="Administrative Sale by Auction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Administrative Sales by Auction are exempt from the Warranty Against Defects, meaning the vendor in such sales is not liable for defects in the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusion,legal exception</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Defects" target="Fraudulent Concealment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraudulent Concealment by the Vendor of defects in the thing sold renders void any clause restricting or abolishing the Warranty Against Defects.</data>
  <data key="d9">invalidation,legal consequence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Defects" target="Article 448">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 448 creates an exception to the Warranty Against Defects, exempting the vendor from liability for defects that are customarily tolerated.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Defects" target="Article 451">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 451 confirms that the Warranty Against Defects action survives even if the thing sold has perished by any cause.</data>
  <data key="d9">statutory governance,survival of action</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Defects" target="Article 453">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 453 governs the contractual modification of the Warranty Against Defects, permitting increase, restriction, or abolition, while voiding restrictive clauses in cases of fraudulent concealment.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual modification,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Defects" target="Notice of Defect">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Notice of Defect is a procedural prerequisite for the Purchaser to preserve rights under the Warranty Against Defects, and failure to provide it results in deemed acceptance of the thing sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">condition precedent,procedural prerequisite</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator" target="Inventory">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator prepares and files the Inventory with the Registry of the Court within four months of appointment, recording all claims and debts of the estate.</data>
  <data key="d9">filing,preparation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator may apply to the Judge for an extension of time for filing the inventory if justified by circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d9">extension request,judicial approval</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrator" target="Legacies">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrator proceeds with the payment of Legacies and other charges after the discharge of the debts of the estate.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment,testamentary charges</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Inventory" target="Interested Party">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An Interested Party must be informed of the filing of the Inventory by registered letter and may dispute its accuracy by petition to the Court within thirty days.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute,notification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-cd0cfdf46c326cc398cf2f5e175a148f</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Extinctive Prescription benefits the Debtor by extinguishing obligations, but the Debtor cannot renounce it before acquiring the right to invoke it.</data>
  <data key="d9">defense,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Periodic Payments">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Periodic Payments are subject to a five-year extinctive prescription period, even if the debt is admitted by the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">five-year term,prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Professional Remuneration">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Professional Remuneration claims are subject to a five-year extinctive prescription period from the time prestations were made.</data>
  <data key="d9">five-year term,prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Taxes and Dues">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Taxes and Dues are subject to a three-year extinctive prescription period running from the end of the year for which they are due.</data>
  <data key="d9">prescription period,three-year term</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Prescription Interruption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Prescription Interruption halts the current extinctive prescription period and commences a new one of the same duration from the time the interrupting act ceases.</data>
  <data key="d9">interruption,restart</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Prescription Suspension">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Prescription Suspension prevents the running of extinctive prescription when a legal or moral bar prevents the creditor from claiming his right.</data>
  <data key="d9">halt,suspension</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge cannot invoke extinctive prescription on his own initiative; it must be invoked by the debtor, his creditors, or any interested party.</data>
  <data key="d9">invocation,judicial role</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="One-Year Prescription Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">One-Year Prescription Rights are a specific category under Extinctive Prescription with a one-year term applicable to claims of merchants, manufacturers, hotel and restaurant proprietors, workmen, servants, and wage earners.</data>
  <data key="d9">one-year term,prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Written Document">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a right is established by a Written Document, the Extinctive Prescription period is extended from the original shorter term to fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d9">extension,fifteen-year term</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Prescription Commencement">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Prescription Commencement defines the starting point from which the Extinctive Prescription period begins to run for different types of obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">starting point,temporal scope</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Prescription Calculation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Prescription Calculation provides the method for computing the duration of Extinctive Prescription periods, specifying that periods are measured in days with the first day excluded.</data>
  <data key="d9">computation method,time measurement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Original Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Extinctive prescription extinguishes the original obligation through the passage of a prescribed time period.</data>
  <data key="d9">prescription,time-based extinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Article 374">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 374 sets the general fifteen-year term for extinctive prescription.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Article 375">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 375 prescribes a five-year prescription period for periodic rights such as rent, interest, wages, and pensions.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,periodic rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Periodic Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Periodic rights are subject to a shortened five-year extinctive prescription period under Article 375.</data>
  <data key="d9">periodic payments,time limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Article 366">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 366 governs the interaction between extinctive prescription and compensation, allowing compensation if the prescription period had not expired when compensation became possible.</data>
  <data key="d9">interaction,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinctive Prescription" target="Acquisitive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Rules of extinctive prescription apply to acquisitive prescription regarding calculation of periods, suspension, interruption, and renunciation.&lt;SEP&gt;The rules governing extinctive prescription, including calculation of period, suspension, interruption, renunciation, and modification, apply to acquisitive prescription insofar as they are not incompatible with its nature.</data>
  <data key="d9">analogy,applicable rules,legal applicability,procedural rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Contract" target="Sale by Sample">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sale by Sample is a specific form of Sale Contract requiring the thing sold to conform to the sample provided.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract type,variation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Contract" target="Sale upon Trial">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sale upon Trial is a specific form of Sale Contract deemed subject to a suspensive condition of acceptance by the purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional sale,contract type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Contract" target="Sale Subject to Tasting">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sale Subject to Tasting is a specific form of Sale Contract where the sale is only concluded upon the purchaser's declaration of acceptance.</data>
  <data key="d9">conditional sale,contract type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Contract" target="Book II: Specific Contracts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Book II: Specific Contracts governs the Sale Contract and other named contracts within the civil code.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal codification,scope</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Contract" target="Article 462">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 462 assigns the costs of the Sale Contract, including stamp duties and transcription fees, to the Purchaser in the absence of contrary agreement or usage.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost allocation,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="State" target="Taxes and Dues">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Taxes and Dues are financial obligations owed to the State, with a specific three-year prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d9">financial obligation,owed to</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496000</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="State" target="Sea">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Land uncovered by the sea belongs to the State, and no one may encroach upon the sea shore except to restore property boundaries covered by the sea.</data>
  <data key="d9">land acquisition,state ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="State" target="Public Domain">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The public domain includes immovable and movable property owned by the State and allocated for public utility purposes.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership,public utility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Professional Remuneration" target="Written Document">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If a right to Professional Remuneration is established by a Written Document, its prescription period extends to fifteen years.</data>
  <data key="d9">documentation,prescription extension</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Natural Obligation" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Natural Obligation remains upon the Debtor even after the original legal obligation has been extinguished by prescription.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal effect,residual duty</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Natural Obligation" target="Civil Obligation">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A natural obligation may constitute a valid cause for a civil obligation.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural obligation is not enforceable but may serve as a valid cause for a civil obligation, which is enforceable against the debtor.</data>
  <data key="d9">cause,enforceability,legal basis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Natural Obligation" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge decides, in the absence of legal provision, whether a natural obligation exists.&lt;SEP&gt;The judge determines whether a natural obligation exists in the absence of a legal provision, provided it is not contrary to public policy.</data>
  <data key="d9">determination,judicial discretion,legal authority,legal determination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="One-Year Prescription Rights" target="Oath of Payment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Oath of Payment is a mandatory procedural condition for any person invoking the One-Year Prescription Rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">procedural condition,proof</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Oath of Payment" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge directs the Oath of Payment of his own accord in cases where the one-year prescription is invoked.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial direction,procedural requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Oath of Payment" target="Heirs and Guardians">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs and Guardians of a deceased debtor may be required to take the Oath of Payment, declaring whether they know of the debt or its payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">procedural duty,successor obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">3.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party who pays off the debt has a remedy against the debtor up to the amount paid, though the debtor may contest the claim if he had any interest in opposing the payment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Debtor may procure the Creditor's consent to substitute the Debtor with a Third Party who consents to be the new Debtor.&lt;SEP&gt;A creditor may refuse payment tendered by a third party if the debtor opposes it and has informed the creditor of his opposition.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,opposition,payment,refusal of payment,remedy,substitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-324a040f11b0c3c65beffd71f78f256b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a11205fa1d01480b8d8ec820b20881b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495989</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party" target="Compensation (Set Off)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Compensation cannot take place to the detriment of rights acquired by third parties, including attaching creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">limitation,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party" target="Assignment Of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A third party becomes the assignee of the debt through assignment by the creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">assignee,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Renunciation of Prescription" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Renunciation of Prescription governs the Debtor's ability to waive the defense of prescription, which is prohibited before the right is acquired but permitted afterwards.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal right,waiver</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Written Document" target="Compromise">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A compromise can only be established and proved by a written document or by an official proces-verbal.</data>
  <data key="d9">form requirement,proof of contract</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs and Guardians" target="Debtor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs and Guardians succeed the Debtor's position in relation to the oath requirement upon the Debtor's death.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal succession,representation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5bd0ee678232358e2574db52e78f6b18</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 670" target="Principle of Equality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 670 enshrines the Principle of Equality requiring the concessionaire to treat consumers equally in services and rates.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,regulatory principle</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 670" target="Fair Competition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 670 protects Fair Competition by rendering the concessionaire liable for compensation when discrimination disturbs the natural balance of competition.</data>
  <data key="d9">anti-discrimination,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 671" target="Concessionaire of Public Utility Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 671 obliges the Concessionaire of Public Utility Service to apply the rates set by public authority without deviation.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal obligation,rate enforcement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 672" target="Consumer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 672 protects the Consumer by entitling recovery of excess payments due to rate irregularities or mistakes.</data>
  <data key="d9">consumer protection,rate correction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 673" target="Strike">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 673 classifies a strike as a fortuitous event if the concessionaire proves it occurred without fault and could not be avoided.</data>
  <data key="d9">fortuitous event,liability exemption</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 673" target="Fortuitous Event">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 673 permits the concessionaire to repudiate responsibility for service interruptions caused by a fortuitous event that could not have been foreseen or avoided by vigilant management.</data>
  <data key="d9">liability exemption,unforeseeable event</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 674" target="Section II: Contracts of Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 674 is contained within Section II: Contracts of Service, which governs employment relationships.</data>
  <data key="d9">codification,section composition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 675" target="Section II: Contracts of Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 675 is contained within Section II: Contracts of Service and limits its provisions by reference to special service laws.</data>
  <data key="d9">codification,section composition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 675" target="Special Laws Relating to Service">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 675 subordinates the provisions of Section II to Special Laws Relating to Service, which take precedence in cases of express or implied inconsistency.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal hierarchy,precedence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may reduce the debtor's excessively onerous contractual obligations when exceptional and unpredictable events make performance threaten the debtor with exorbitant loss.&lt;SEP&gt;The judge fixes reasonable times for the debtor's performance, determines the object of obligations when the debtor fails to elect, and adjudicates disputes between debtor and creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">hardship relief,judicial oversight,legal determination,obligation reduction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-3a8c1a0ea5746b3d491108307acaad91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c925e3c43dc116878af8cdc5a0aabdc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779495982</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Privileged Right">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Privileged Rights are enforceable against the property of the Debtor, granting preference to certain creditors over others.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal burden,property encumbrance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="General Privileges">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">General Privileges extend to all movable and immovable property of the Debtor, as stated in Article 1132.</data>
  <data key="d9">property coverage,scope of application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Special Privileges">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Special Privileges are limited to a specific movable or immovable property of the Debtor, as stated in Article 1132.</data>
  <data key="d9">limited scope,specific property</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Compensation (Set Off)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor has the right to invoke compensation to set off mutual debts with the creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt extinguishment,right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Release Of Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Release of obligation liberates the debtor upon the debtor becoming aware of the release, unless the debtor refuses.</data>
  <data key="d9">discharge,liberation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Impossibility Of Performance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Impossibility of performance extinguishes the obligation if the debtor proves that performance became impossible due to causes beyond the debtor's control.</data>
  <data key="d9">burden of proof,extinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Assignment Of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor's right to compensation is affected by assignment of debt depending on whether the debtor consented without reserve or merely received notification.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,notification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Object of Obligation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor is obligated to perform or deliver the object of the obligation and must supply an article of average quality when the degree of quality is not specified, as per Article 133.</data>
  <data key="d9">performance obligation,quality standard</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Specific Performance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor shall be compelled to specifically perform an obligation if such performance is possible, upon being formally summoned.</data>
  <data key="d9">compulsion,enforcement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Gross Negligence">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor always remains liable for gross negligence and cannot be contractually discharged from such liability.</data>
  <data key="d9">misconduct,perpetual liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Penalty">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A penalty may be imposed on a debtor who abstains from performing an obligation when specific performance is impracticable unless performed personally.</data>
  <data key="d9">deterrence,enforcement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Agreed Damages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A debtor is not liable for agreed damages if the debtor proves that the creditor suffered no loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">exemption,proof of no loss</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Sale Of Litigious Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor against whom a litigious right has been assigned may extinguish that right by paying the assignee the actual price paid plus expenses and interest accrued from the date of payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,payment right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Annuity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor is obligated to make periodic annuity payments and possesses the right to redeem the annuity at any time, subject to notice and deferral restrictions.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment obligation,redemption right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Debtor" target="Securities">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The debtor must supply the creditor with the promised securities, and failure to do so or the disappearance of securities without replacement compels the debtor to redeem the annuity.</data>
  <data key="d9">collateral obligation,creditor protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 676" target="Canvasser">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 676 extends contract of service provisions to apply to the relationship between employers and canvassers.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract scope,legal applicability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 676" target="Assurance Agent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 676 extends contract of service provisions to the relationship between masters and assurance agents.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract scope,legal applicability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 681" target="Worker">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 681 establishes the presumption that the Worker performs services for remuneration when such services are not customarily gratuitous or fall within the Worker's profession.</data>
  <data key="d9">presumption of remuneration,professional services</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 682" target="Judge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 682 authorizes the Judge to fix the salary in accordance with equity when no applicable rates or customs exist for determining remuneration.</data>
  <data key="d9">equity,judicial authority,salary determination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 683" target="Worker">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 683 defines the sums forming an integral part of the Worker's salary for computing the attachable portion, including commissions, allowances, and gratuities.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal protection,salary components</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 684" target="Worker">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 684 provides that tips may form part of the Worker's salary in certain industries when subject to regulation or collected in a common fund.</data>
  <data key="d9">salary determination,tips</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 685" target="Worker">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 685 imposes on the Worker the obligation to perform work personally with reasonable care and to obey lawful employer orders.</data>
  <data key="d9">performance duties,worker obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 685" target="Employer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 685 obliges the Worker to obey the Employer's orders related to the agreed work provided such orders do not violate the contract, law, or public morals.</data>
  <data key="d9">authority,lawful orders,worker obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Concessionaire of Public Utility Service" target="Consumer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Concessionaire of Public Utility Service is bound to observe strict equality between consumers in the provision of services and rates charged.</data>
  <data key="d9">equality,legal obligation,service provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Concessionaire of Public Utility Service" target="Public Authority">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Concessionaire of Public Utility Service must apply the rates laid down by the Public Authority, which have force of law.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal compliance,rate regulation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Consumer" target="Concessionaire">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Concessionaire is obligated to provide services to Consumers in a normal manner at specified rates, and when enjoying a monopoly must observe strict equality among all Consumers.</data>
  <data key="d9">equality obligation,service provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Worker" target="Employer">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Worker undertakes to perform work under the supervision or control of the Employer in exchange for remuneration under a contract of service.</data>
  <data key="d9">employment relationship,remuneration,supervision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Employer" target="Commercial Representative">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Commercial Representative works under the orders and supervision of the Employer and may be remunerated on a commission basis.</data>
  <data key="d9">agency,commission,supervision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496001</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Employer" target="Commercial Traveller">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Commercial Traveller works under employer supervision and retains post-employment commission rights on orders resulting from prior efforts.</data>
  <data key="d9">agency,commission,supervision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Employer" target="Assurance Agent">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Assurance Agent works under the orders and supervision of the Employer and is subject to contract of service provisions even if remunerated on commission.</data>
  <data key="d9">agency,contract of service,supervision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-11baf9c7614ada0e460df61b91f7c7f1</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Of Pledge" target="Pledge Of An Immovable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Pledge of an immovable (antichresis) is a specific type of contract of pledge involving immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal classification,type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Of Pledge" target="Pledge Of A Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Pledge of a movable is a specific type of contract of pledge involving movable property.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal classification,type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Of Pledge" target="Pledge Of Debts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Pledge of debts is a specific type of contract of pledge involving debt instruments.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal classification,type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Of Pledge" target="Extinguishment Of A Pledge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Extinguishment of a pledge terminates the rights arising from the contract of pledge, as governed by Articles 1112 and 1113.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal effect,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Of Pledge" target="Article 1111">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1111 defines the scope of obligations secured by a contract of pledge, including capital, expenses, compensation, costs, and interest.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal definition,scope</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Of Pledge" target="Secured Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The secured debt is the obligation guaranteed by the contract of pledge; its extinguishment terminates the pledge and its revival revives the pledge.</data>
  <data key="d9">guarantee,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract Of Pledge" target="Pledged Thing">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The pledged thing is the property given as collateral under the contract of pledge, subject to preservation, enforcement, and transfer rules.</data>
  <data key="d9">collateral,security</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinguishment Of A Pledge" target="Article 1112">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1112 governs the extinguishment of a right of pledge upon extinguishment of the secured debt and its revival.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinguishment Of A Pledge" target="Article 1113">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1113 enumerates the specific causes of extinguishment of a pledge, including renunciation, union of rights, and loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">causes of termination,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Extinguishment Of A Pledge" target="Third Parties In Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Rights legally acquired by third parties in good faith during the interval between extinguishment and revival of a pledge are protected, as stated in Article 1112.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquired rights,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of An Immovable" target="Article 1114">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1114 requires inscription of the pledge contract for a pledge of an immovable to be valid against third parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">inscription,legal requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of An Immovable" target="Section IV">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section IV, titled "Certain Kinds of Pledge," includes provisions on pledges of immovables.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal topic,structural organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of An Immovable" target="Article 1115">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1115 permits the pledgee of an immovable to lease it to the pledgor while maintaining validity against third parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">lease rights,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of An Immovable" target="Article 1116">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1116 imposes maintenance, preservation, and tax payment obligations on the pledgee of an immovable.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,maintenance obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of A Movable" target="Article 1117">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1117 requires a written contract with an established date for a pledge of a movable to be valid against third parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal requirement,written contract</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of A Movable" target="Section IV">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section IV, titled "Certain Kinds of Pledge," includes provisions on pledges of movables.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal topic,structural organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of A Movable" target="Bearer Securities">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The possession rules governing bearer securities apply to the pledge of movables, as stated in Article 1118.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal application,possession rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of A Movable" target="Material Movables">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The possession rules governing material movables apply to the pledge of movables, as stated in Article 1118.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal application,possession rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of A Movable" target="Third Parties In Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Third parties in good faith may assert rights acquired over a pledged movable even after the date of the pledge, as protected under Article 1118.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquired rights,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of A Movable" target="Article 1118">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1118 applies the possession rules of material movables and bearer securities to the pledge of movables, protecting good faith parties.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,possession rules</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Good Faith" target="Essential Mistake">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A party who committed a mistake cannot take advantage of it in a manner contrary to the principles of good faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">fairness,limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Good Faith" target="Acquisitive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Possession in good faith combined with a just title reduces the acquisitive prescription period for immovable property to five years.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal advantage,reduced prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Article 1123">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1123 requires notification to or acceptance by the debtor for a pledge of debts to be valid.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal requirement,notification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Section IV">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section IV, titled "Certain Kinds of Pledge," includes provisions on pledges of debts.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal topic,structural organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Nominative Bonds">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Nominative bonds may be pledged through the special transfer procedure as a form of pledge of debts, as stated in Article 1124.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal procedure,pledgeable instrument</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Bonds Payable To Order">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Bonds payable to order may be pledged through the special transfer procedure as a form of pledge of debts, without need for notification, as stated in Article 1124.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal procedure,pledgeable instrument</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Article 1124">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1124 prescribes the special procedure for pledging nominative bonds and bonds payable to order as debt pledges.</data>
  <data key="d9">bond pledging procedure,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Article 1125">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1125 prohibits the pledge of debts that cannot be assigned or attached.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,prohibition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Article 1126">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1126 governs the pledgee's right to collect interest and periodic payments on pledged debts and the obligation to protect the pledged debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">interest collection,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Article 1127">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1127 allows the debtor of a pledged debt to raise defenses against the pledgee.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor defenses,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Article 1128">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1128 governs the situation where a pledged debt falls due before the secured debt, requiring joint discharge and cooperation.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,premature maturity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pledge Of Debts" target="Article 1129">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1129 permits the unpaid pledgee to collect from the pledged debt when both debts fall due.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt collection,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1121" target="Article 1129">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1129 directs the pledgee to Article 1121, paragraph 2 for the procedures governing the sale or allocation of the pledged debt.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 1129 references Article 1121 paragraph 2 for the procedure of selling or allocating a pledged debt.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,enforcement procedure,legal reference,procedural guidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1122" target="Institutions Authorized To Lend Money On Pledge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1122 provides that special provisions governing institutions authorized to lend money on pledge take precedence over the general pledge provisions.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal precedence,special provisions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1123" target="Article 305">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1123 references Article 305 for the notification or acceptance procedure governing debt pledges.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,procedural requirement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-b47dd95d0cd89cb486e24e3bc8e4bbe9</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496002</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Dispossession" target="Fraudulent Concealment">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraudulent Concealment by the Vendor of third-party rights renders void any clause restricting or abolishing the Warranty Against Dispossession.</data>
  <data key="d9">invalidation,legal consequence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496003</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Dispossession" target="Article 445">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 445 governs the modification of the Warranty Against Dispossession, allowing contracting parties to increase, restrict, or abolish it by special agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">modification,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Warranty Against Dispossession" target="Article 446">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 446 establishes that the Warranty Against Dispossession cannot be excluded for dispossessions resulting from the vendor's own acts, regardless of any contractual clause to the contrary.</data>
  <data key="d9">exception,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Total Dispossession" target="Article 443">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Total Dispossession is governed by Article 443, which enumerates the specific claims available to the Purchaser including the value of the thing sold, legal interest, and litigation costs.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,statutory provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Partial Dispossession" target="Article 444">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Partial Dispossession is governed by Article 444, which defines the conditions under which the Purchaser may claim full or limited remedies.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,statutory provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Fraudulent Concealment" target="Article 446">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraudulent Concealment by the Vendor of third-party rights triggers continued liability under Article 446 even when warranty exclusion clauses are in place.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal consequence,liability preservation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Fraudulent Concealment" target="Article 453">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Fraudulent Concealment of defects renders void any clause abolishing or restricting the warranty under Article 453.</data>
  <data key="d9">invalidation,void clause</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Interest" target="Article 458">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 458 limits the Vendor's entitlement to Legal Interest on the price to cases where the Purchaser has been placed in default by formal summons or the thing sold is productive and has been delivered.</data>
  <data key="d9">entitlement conditions,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 443" target="Article 444">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 444 references Article 443 by allowing the Purchaser in partial dispossession cases to claim the same sums provided for in Article 443 when the loss is sufficiently severe.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,statutory linkage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 443" target="Action of Revendication">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The costs of the Action of Revendication are recoverable by the Purchaser under Article 443, except for costs the Purchaser could have avoided by notifying the vendor under Article 440.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost recovery,legal action</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 443" target="Dissolution of Sale">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Dissolution of Sale is mentioned in Article 443 as an alternative legal basis for the Purchaser's action against the Vendor, distinct from warranty claims for dispossession.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative basis,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 443" target="Annulment of Sale">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Annulment of Sale is mentioned in Article 443 as an alternative legal basis for the Purchaser's action against the Vendor, alongside Dissolution of Sale.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative basis,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 444" target="Article 450">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 450 directs the Purchaser to bring a warranty action in accordance with Article 444 when timely notice of a defect has been given.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,statutory linkage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 459" target="Article 273">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 459 references Article 273 regarding the loss of the benefit of the payment term by the Purchaser, which triggers the Vendor's extended right of retention.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,statutory linkage</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 449" target="Notice of Defect">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 449 establishes the procedural requirements for Notice of Defect, including the reasonable time frame for visible defects and immediate notice for hidden defects.</data>
  <data key="d9">procedural requirement,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 455" target="Proper Working Warranty">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 455 defines the Proper Working Warranty, establishing specific notice and action deadlines of one month and six months respectively for defects appearing during the guaranteed period.</data>
  <data key="d9">specific warranty,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 461" target="Dissolution of Sale">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 461 provides for the automatic Dissolution of Sale without summons in sales of commodities or movable effects when the price is not paid on the due date.</data>
  <data key="d9">automatic dissolution,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 461" target="Commodities and Movable Effects">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 461 applies special dissolution rules to sales of Commodities and Movable Effects, permitting automatic rescission if the price is unpaid.</data>
  <data key="d9">special sale rules,statutory governance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-bcfe60a9d613098f00d2002c6c559476</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judge" target="Penalty">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may increase the penalty amount if insufficient to compel the debtor to perform.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,modification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judge" target="Agreed Damages">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge may reduce the amount of agreed damages, as indicated in Article 224.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial modification,reduction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judge" target="Three Possessory Actions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Judge has the authority to order suspension or continuance of threatening works and may require adequate guarantees as part of possessory protection.</data>
  <data key="d9">judicial authority,ordering guarantees</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judge" target="Liquidator">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The judge appoints a liquidator when partners fail to agree on the appointment, or when the partnership is declared void.</data>
  <data key="d9">appointment,judicial authority</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Loan for Use (Ariyah)" target="Lender (Mu'ir)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lender is a party to the loan for use contract, obligated to deliver the thing lent and reimburse necessary preservation expenses.</data>
  <data key="d9">contracting party,obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Loan for Use (Ariyah)" target="Borrower (Musta'ir)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The borrower is a party to the loan for use contract, obligated to use the thing properly, maintain it, and restitute it at the end of the loan.</data>
  <data key="d9">contracting party,obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lender (Mu'ir)" target="Borrower (Musta'ir)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The lender delivers a non-consumable thing to the borrower without valuable consideration under a loan for use contract, and the borrower must restitute it after use.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual relationship,loan for use</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496004</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract for Work (Muqawalah)" target="Contractor (Muqawil)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contractor is a party to the contract for work, obligated to perform the work properly and warrant the quality of materials supplied.</data>
  <data key="d9">contracting party,performance obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contract for Work (Muqawalah)" target="Master of the Work (Rabb al-Amal)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The master is a party to the contract for work, obligated to pay remuneration and receive the completed work.</data>
  <data key="d9">contracting party,payment obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor (Muqawil)" target="Master of the Work (Rabb al-Amal)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contractor undertakes to perform work for the master in consideration of remuneration under a contract for work.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual relationship,work and remuneration</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor (Muqawil)" target="Architect (Muhandis Mi'mari)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The architect and contractor are jointly and severally liable for ten years for total or partial demolition of constructions or defects endangering structural solidity.</data>
  <data key="d9">construction warranty,joint liability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor (Muqawil)" target="Subcontractor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contractor may have rights of recourse against subcontractors, as referenced in Article 651 which excludes subcontractors from its scope.</data>
  <data key="d9">right of recourse,subcontracting</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor (Muqawil)" target="Article 655">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 655 applies once the contractor has completed the work and placed it at the disposal of the master, triggering the master's obligation to accept delivery.</data>
  <data key="d9">completion of work,placement at disposal</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master of the Work (Rabb al-Amal)" target="Article 655">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 655 obligates the master to take delivery of the completed work promptly, and deems it delivered if the master refuses without lawful cause after formal notice.</data>
  <data key="d9">acceptance of work,delivery obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Master of the Work (Rabb al-Amal)" target="Architect (Muhandis Mi'mari)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The architect provides design plans to the master and may supervise execution, bearing liability for defects arising from the plans or from supervision.</data>
  <data key="d9">design liability,professional services</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Architect (Muhandis Mi'mari)" target="Article 653">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 653 renders void any clause that seeks to exclude or restrict the architect's warranty liability.</data>
  <data key="d9">void clauses,warranty protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 650" target="Article 209">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 650 references Article 209 regarding the procedure for assigning work to a replacement contractor at the original contractor's cost.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,legal procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 651" target="Ten-Year Warranty (Decennial Liability)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 651 establishes the ten-year joint and several liability of the architect and contractor for structural defects and demolition of constructions.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal basis,liability period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 654" target="Ten-Year Warranty (Decennial Liability)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 654 prescribes a three-year limitation period for warranty actions from the date of destruction or discovery of defects.</data>
  <data key="d9">limitation of actions,prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section II: Loan for Use" target="Chapter III: Contracts for the Hire of Services">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section II on loan for use is a sub-section within the broader legal framework of Chapter III.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal organization,structural hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section I: Contracts for Work" target="Chapter III: Contracts for the Hire of Services">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section I on contracts for work is a sub-section within Chapter III covering contracts for the hire of services.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal organization,structural hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section I: Contracts for Work" target="Article 655">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 655 is part of Section I on Contracts for Work, governing the master's obligation to accept delivery under the Obligations of the Master sub-section.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,structural inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-446116d718087d8c93a8dc8bc8172aaa</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will (الوصية)" target="Mohammedan Law (الشريعة الإسلامية)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Wills are governed by the rules of Mohammedan Law and by statutory laws on wills, as established in Article 915.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,regulatory framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will (الوصية)" target="Illness Immediately Preceding Death (مرض الموت)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Any legal disposition made during a terminal illness with the object of gifting is deemed a testamentary disposition governed by the rules applicable to wills under Article 916.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal equivalence,testamentary disposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Will (الوصية)" target="Laws on Wills (القوانين الصادرة في شأنها)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Laws on Wills are statutory legislation that, alongside Mohammedan Law, govern the legal framework for wills as established in Article 915.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal governance,statutory framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Testator (الموصى)" target="Division by Disposition (Mortis Causa)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A division by disposition may always be revoked by the testator and becomes irrevocable only upon the testator's death.</data>
  <data key="d9">revocation,testamentary control</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Testator (الموصى)" target="Article 916">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 916 deems legal dispositions made by a person during a terminal illness with the object of gifting as testamentary dispositions.</data>
  <data key="d9">terminal illness,testamentary disposition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs (الورثة)" target="Division by Disposition (Mortis Causa)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Property not included in the division or divided parts of predeceased contingent heirs devolve in common on the heirs under the rules of inheritance.</data>
  <data key="d9">inheritance,property devolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs (الورثة)" target="Estate (التركة)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Heirs inherit property from the estate in accordance with the rules of inheritance, and may call for division of the estate when debts are unresolved.</data>
  <data key="d9">estate division,inheritance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Heirs (الورثة)" target="Article 917">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 917 deems a disposition to an heir as testamentary when the disposer retains possession and enjoyment of the property during his lifetime.</data>
  <data key="d9">disposition to heir,retained possession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate (التركة)" target="Creditors of the Estate (دائنو التركة)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Unsecured creditors of the estate may take action on alienated immovable property of the estate provided their claims are recorded by law.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor rights,debt settlement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate (التركة)" target="Article 913">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 913 allows any heir to call for division of the estate when estate debts are not included in the division or creditors do not agree.</data>
  <data key="d9">debt resolution,estate division</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Estate (التركة)" target="Debts of the Estate (ديون التركة)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Debts of the estate represent financial obligations that must be resolved during the winding-up process, and if not included in the division, any heir may call for a new division under Article 913.</data>
  <data key="d9">estate liability,financial obligations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Accession (الالتصاق)" target="Contract (العقد)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both contract and accession are recognized legal methods for acquiring ownership and real rights in property under the civil code.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal mechanisms,property acquisition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Accession to Immovable Property (الالتصاق بالعقار)" target="Land Owner (مالك الأرض)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">All buildings, plantations, and works on land are presumed to have been made by the land owner at the owner's expense and belong to the land owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership presumption,property rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Accession to Movable Property (الالتصاق بالمنقول)" target="Court (المحكمة)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When two movables of different owners become inseparably mingled, the court decides the matter according to rules of equity, considering damage, circumstances, and good faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispute resolution,equitable judgment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Alluvium" target="River">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Alluvium is formed gradually and imperceptibly by the river through natural sediment deposits.</data>
  <data key="d9">natural formation,sediment deposit</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496005</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Alluvium" target="Land Owner (مالك الأرض)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Alluvium formed by the river belongs to the riparian land owners under Article 918.</data>
  <data key="d9">property acquisition,riparian rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Alluvium" target="Riparian Owners (الملاك المجاورين)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Alluvium formed gradually and imperceptibly by the river belongs to the riparian owners under Article 918.</data>
  <data key="d9">natural formation,property acquisition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="River" target="Islands (الجزر)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Islands formed in a river's channel are a result of the river's natural processes, and their ownership is regulated by special laws under Article 921.</data>
  <data key="d9">land creation,natural formation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Creditors of the Estate (دائنو التركة)" target="Debts of the Estate (ديون التركة)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Creditors of the estate hold claims against the estate's debts and may enforce their rights on alienated immovable property if debts are not settled, as provided in Articles 913 and 914.</data>
  <data key="d9">creditor claims,debt enforcement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party (أجنبي)" target="Good Faith (حسن النية)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a third party carries out works on another's land in good faith, believing entitled to do so, the land owner's remedies are limited to payment of value rather than removal under Article 925.</data>
  <data key="d9">construction rights,legal protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party (أجنبي)" target="Land Owner (مالك الأرض)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When a third party carries out works on another's land, the land owner has varying rights to demand removal, retention, or compensation depending on the third party's knowledge and consent.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation,construction disputes</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party (أجنبي)" target="Light Constructions (المنشآت الصغيرة)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Light constructions such as sheds, shops, and shelters erected on another's land without permanent intent remain the property of the third party who erected them under Article 929.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership retention,temporary structures</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party (أجنبي)" target="Article 926">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 926 provides that when a third party carries out works with the land owner's permission, the land owner cannot demand removal and must pay the prescribed amounts to the third party.</data>
  <data key="d9">payment obligation,permitted works</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Land Owner (مالك الأرض)" target="Buildings, Plantations, and Works (بناء أو غراس أو منشآت)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">All buildings, plantations, and works existing on land are presumed to have been made by the land owner and to belong to the land owner under Article 922.</data>
  <data key="d9">land improvement,presumptive ownership</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Court (المحكمة)" target="Good Faith (حسن النية)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In disputes involving mingled movables, the court considers the good faith of each party alongside damage and circumstances when deciding according to rules of equity under Article 931.</data>
  <data key="d9">equitable judgment,judicial discretion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 913" target="Article 895">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 913 references Article 895 as the legal basis for any heir to call for division of the estate when debts are not settled with creditors.</data>
  <data key="d9">division procedure,statutory cross-reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Materials (المواد)" target="Buildings, Plantations, and Works (بناء أو غراس أو منشآت)">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Materials belonging to another person that are incorporated into buildings, plantations, or works may become the exclusive property of the land owner under Articles 923 and 930, with compensation rights for the material owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">construction components,ownership transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Materials (المواد)" target="Article 930">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 930 denies the material owner the right to claim restitution of materials used by a third party in constructions but grants compensation claims against both the third party and the land owner.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation rights,material restitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 927" target="Article 982">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 927 directs that the compensation provisions of Article 982 apply to the payment of compensation in the three preceding accession articles.</data>
  <data key="d9">compensation rules,cross-reference</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-df1a6e51cba8144f7df6e3933946186c</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Personality" target="Birth And Death Registers">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Birth and death, which mark the beginning and end of legal personality, are established by official registers.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal proof,official records</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Personality" target="Personality Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Personality rights are inherent in legal personality and a person may demand cessation of infringement and compensation for damage.</data>
  <data key="d9">inherent rights,personhood</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Domicile" target="Missing Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The domicile of a missing person is the domicile of the legal representative.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal representation,residence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Domicile" target="Trade Or Profession Domicile">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The place where a person exercises a trade or profession is considered a form of domicile for matters related to that trade or profession.</data>
  <data key="d9">commercial activity,residence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Domicile" target="Person Under Legal Disability">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The domicile of a person under legal disability is the domicile of the legal representative.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal representation,residence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Moslem Law" target="Missing Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Moslem law applies to missing persons in the absence of special laws.</data>
  <data key="d9">default law,legal application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Moslem Law" target="Absent Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Moslem law applies to absent persons in the absence of special laws.</data>
  <data key="d9">default law,legal application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family" target="Direct Lineal Relationship">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Direct lineal relationship defines one form of kinship within the family, existing between ascendants and descendants.</data>
  <data key="d9">family structure,kinship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496006</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family" target="Collateral Relationship">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Collateral relationship defines another form of kinship within the family, existing between persons sharing a common ascendant.</data>
  <data key="d9">family structure,kinship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Family" target="First Name And Family Name">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The family name is bestowed upon a person's children, establishing family identity through naming.</data>
  <data key="d9">family identity,naming</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="First Name And Family Name" target="Name Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Name rights protect the use of a person's first name and family name against unlawful dispute or unauthorized use by another.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal remedy,name protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Things Outside The Ambit Of Trade" target="Proprietary Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Things outside the ambit of trade by their nature or by law cannot be objects of proprietary rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal restriction,property classification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-f2ed29ae636e1222ff661f604af08b21</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Chapter IV: Privileged Rights" target="Section I: General Provisions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section I: General Provisions is a subdivision of Chapter IV: Privileged Rights, containing foundational rules governing privileges.</data>
  <data key="d9">hierarchical structure,legal code organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Chapter IV: Privileged Rights" target="Section II: Kinds of Privileges">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section II: Kinds of Privileges is a subdivision of Chapter IV: Privileged Rights, enumerating specific privileged rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">hierarchical structure,legal code organization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Privileged Right" target="Holder in Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Privileged Right cannot be set up against a Holder in Good Faith of a movable, providing protection to innocent possessors as established in Article 1133.</data>
  <data key="d9">enforceability limitation,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Privileged Right" target="State Treasury">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The State Treasury holds a privileged right for sums due for taxes, duties, and other dues, ranking prior to all other privileges and mortgages over immovables except costs of legal proceedings, as established in Articles 1134 and 1139.</data>
  <data key="d9">priority ranking,tax privilege</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Privileged Right" target="Costs of Legal Proceedings">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Costs of Legal Proceedings hold the highest-ranking privilege, payable in priority to any other claim whether privileged or secured by mortgage, as stated in Article 1138.</data>
  <data key="d9">highest priority,legal expense privilege</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="General Privileges" target="Special Privileges">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">General Privileges and Special Privileges are the two categories of privileged rights, distinguished by their scope of application to the debtor's property.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,legal distinction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section II: Kinds of Privileges" target="Article 1137">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1137 introduces Section II by providing that the rights enumerated in the following articles are privileged in addition to those established by special provisions of the law.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal enumeration,transitional provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-75e8b45f8a42802e1cda62772aac5a3b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496007</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1144" target="Hotel Proprietor's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1144 establishes the hotel proprietor's privilege over effects brought by a traveller to secure sums due for accommodation, food, and expenses.</data>
  <data key="d9">establishment,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation (Set Off)" target="Article 362">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 362 grants the debtor the right to compensation of mutual debts under specified conditions.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,right of compensation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation (Set Off)" target="Article 364">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 364 enumerates the exceptions where compensation does not apply.</data>
  <data key="d9">exceptions,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation (Set Off)" target="Article 363">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 363 permits compensation even when the places of payment of the two debts differ, provided the debtor compensates the creditor for any resulting loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,place of payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation (Set Off)" target="Article 366">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 366 allows compensation to proceed even after the prescription period has expired for a debt, provided the period had not expired when compensation first became possible.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,prescription interaction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation (Set Off)" target="Article 367">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 367 prohibits compensation to the detriment of rights acquired by third parties, including when a third party has seized property held by the debtor for the creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,third party protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation (Set Off)" target="Article 369">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 369 provides that a debtor who paid despite having a right to compensation cannot invoke securities to the prejudice of third parties unless unaware of that right.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,securities,waiver</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compensation (Set Off)" target="Article 368">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 368 limits the debtor's right to compensation after consenting to assignment of debt without reserve, while preserving it when the debtor has only been notified.</data>
  <data key="d9">assignment limitation,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Release Of Obligation" target="Article 371">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 371 provides the rules for voluntary release of the debtor by the creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,voluntary release</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Impossibility Of Performance" target="Article 373">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 373 establishes impossibility of performance as a ground for extinguishing obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">impossibility,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Current Account" target="Article 355">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 355 governs the relationship between current account entries and novation.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal provision,novation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496008</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 368" target="Assignment Of Debt">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 368 governs the effect of assignment of debt on the debtor's right to set up compensation, distinguishing between consented and merely notified assignments.</data>
  <data key="d9">assignment rules,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67005e57ac525a50d6ec74943784bd7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Domain" target="Public Juristic Persons">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The public domain includes immovable and movable property owned by public juristic persons and allocated for public utility purposes.</data>
  <data key="d9">ownership,public utility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Public Domain" target="Public Utility">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Property becomes part of the public domain when allocated for public utility purposes, and loses this status when the allocation for public utility ceases.</data>
  <data key="d9">allocation,purpose</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Offer" target="Offeror">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The offeror is the person who initiates an offer in the contract formation process.</data>
  <data key="d9">initiation,proposal</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Offer" target="Acceptance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acceptance is the response to an offer; if acceptance goes beyond, restricts, or modifies the offer, it is deemed a rejection comprising a new offer.</data>
  <data key="d9">agreement,response</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Offeror" target="Contract Of Adhesion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In a contract of adhesion, the offeror draws up standard conditions that are not subject to discussion by the accepting party.</data>
  <data key="d9">imposition,standard conditions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acceptance" target="Silence As Acceptance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Silence or failure to reply constitutes a form of acceptance in specific circumstances, such as when the offer relates to existing dealings or is solely in the offeree's interest.</data>
  <data key="d9">form of acceptance,legal equivalence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Minor Lacking Discretion" target="Void Act">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">All acts in law performed by a minor lacking discretion are deemed void.</data>
  <data key="d9">incapacity,nullity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Personal Rights" target="Obligations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Obligations and personal rights are treated as equivalent concepts in the civil law framework, both referring to legal duties and corresponding rights between persons.</data>
  <data key="d9">equivalence,legal framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-5deaa4278dff32d7c807ecaff56b6721</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496009</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Architect" target="Contractor">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both the Architect and the Contractor are bound by warranty obligations for the works; any clause tending to exclude or restrict this warranty is void.&lt;SEP&gt;Contractors and architects share the same privilege over buildings or other works for which they have been entrusted with erection, reconstruction, repair, or maintenance.</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,legal liability,shared privilege,warranty obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Architect" target="Contractor's And Architect's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The architect holds a privilege over buildings or works to secure sums due for design and construction-related work.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Sub-contractor">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Contractor may entrust the execution of all or part of the work to a Sub-contractor but remains responsible to the Master for the Sub-contractor's work.</data>
  <data key="d9">delegation of work,responsibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Workmen">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Workmen are employed by the Contractor in the execution of work and have rights of action against the main Contractor and the Master for sums due to them.</data>
  <data key="d9">employment,payment rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Warranty">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Contractor is bound by warranty obligations; warranty actions are prescribed after three years from the date of destruction of the works or discovery of defects.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal obligation,prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Unit Price Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under a Unit Price Contract, the Contractor is bound to notify the Master forthwith of any anticipated considerable excess over the estimated price and forfeits the right to recover excess expenses if notification is not given.</data>
  <data key="d9">cost overrun,notification obligation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Lump Sum Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under a Lump Sum Contract, the Contractor has no claim to an increase in price due to modifications, additions, or rises in material and labor costs, even if such increases render performance onerous.</data>
  <data key="d9">no increase claim,price limitation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Materials">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Contractor bears the loss of materials supplied by the Contractor when works are destroyed by a fortuitous event before delivery, and must indemnify the Master for materials supplied by the Master if destruction or deterioration is caused by the Contractor's fault.</data>
  <data key="d9">indemnification,loss bearing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Contract For Work">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Contract for Work is dissolved upon the death of the Contractor if personal skill was a factor, or if performance of the work becomes impossible.</data>
  <data key="d9">contract dissolution,death or impossibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor" target="Contractor's And Architect's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contractor holds a privilege over buildings or works to secure sums due for construction, reconstruction, repair, or maintenance work.</data>
  <data key="d9">beneficiary,holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Administrative Authority" target="Concessionaire">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Administrative Authority concludes a concession agreement with the Concessionaire entrusting the exploitation of a public utility service for a fixed period.</data>
  <data key="d9">concession agreement,public utility management</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Concessionaire" target="Monopoly">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the Concessionaire enjoys a Monopoly, whether de jure or de facto, the Concessionaire is bound to observe strict equality among all consumers in the provision of services and the collection of rates, and is prohibited from granting advantages to some consumers while refusing them to others.</data>
  <data key="d9">equality obligation,exclusive right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-abea989754099319029f914b70c57b4b</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Preemption" target="Bare Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Bare Owner holds the right of preemption in case of a sale of all or part of the usufruct attached to the bare property.</data>
  <data key="d9">property rights,right holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Preemption" target="Co-Owner in Common">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Co-Owner in Common holds the right of preemption when a part of the common property is sold to a third party.</data>
  <data key="d9">joint ownership,right holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Preemption" target="Neighboring Owner">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Neighboring Owner holds the right of preemption in cases involving buildings, building land, rights of servitude, or land adjoining on two sides.</data>
  <data key="d9">adjoining property,right holder</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Preemption" target="Right of Usufruct">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of usufruct may be acquired by preemption as one of the recognized methods of creation.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,creation of right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possession" target="Legal Representative">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A legal representative may acquire possession on behalf of a person lacking discretion.&lt;SEP&gt;A Legal Representative may acquire possession on behalf of a person lacking discretion through legal representation.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,acquisition on behalf of another,legal capacity,legal representation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496010</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possession" target="Intermediary">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An intermediary exercises possession in the name of the possessor and is obliged to follow instructions regarding such possession.&lt;SEP&gt;An Intermediary may exercise possession on behalf of the possessor provided the intermediary acts in the possessor's name and is bound to follow the possessor's instructions.</data>
  <data key="d9">indirect exercise,indirect possession,legal control,obligation to obey</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possession" target="Person Lacking Discretion">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Person Lacking Discretion may acquire possession only through the intervention of a Legal Representative acting on their behalf.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,legal capacity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possession" target="Acquisitive Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Continuous possession for a prescribed period leads to acquisition of ownership or real rights through acquisitive prescription.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition of ownership,legal effect</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possession" target="Successor In Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A successor in title receives possession from a prior possessor and may add the prior possessor's period to his own for legal effect.</data>
  <data key="d9">addition of possession periods,transmission</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Possession" target="Three Possessory Actions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The three possessory actions provide legal remedies to protect possession through reinstatement, discontinuance of disturbance, and prevention of threatened works.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal protection,remedies</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Neighboring Owner" target="Right of Servitude">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The existence of a Right of Servitude over neighboring land or in favor of neighboring land is one of the conditions that qualifies the Neighboring Owner to exercise the right of preemption.</data>
  <data key="d9">preemption eligibility,property rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-fed27b2f101964bdcb4efb60a029255e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Legal Representative" target="Life Assured">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the Life Assured is under legal incapacity, the Legal Representative must provide consent for the life insurance contract to be valid.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent,legal capacity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Liability Arising from Things" target="Article 176">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 176 is one of the legal provisions governing liability arising from things, specifically addressing liability for harm caused by animals.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Liability Arising from Things" target="Article 177">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 177 is one of the legal provisions governing liability arising from things, specifically addressing liability for damage caused by building collapse.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Liability Arising from Things" target="Article 178">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 178 is one of the legal provisions governing liability arising from things, specifically addressing liability for damage caused by things requiring special care or machines.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Enrichment Without Just Cause" target="Article 180">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 180 sets the prescription periods for claims arising from enrichment without just cause.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment Not Due" target="Article 181">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 181 establishes the core principle that undue payments must be returned and exceptions to this rule.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment Not Due" target="Article 182">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 182 allows recovery of payments made for obligations whose cause had not materialized or had ceased to exist.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment Not Due" target="Article 183">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 183 governs restitution of premature payments made by a payor unaware that the obligation was not yet due.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment Not Due" target="Article 184">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 184 limits restitution rights when a creditor in good faith has given up documents or security due to a third party's payment.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment Not Due" target="Article 185">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 185 distinguishes the obligations of good faith and bad faith recipients of undue payments.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment Not Due" target="Article 186">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 186 limits the liability of recipients lacking legal capacity to the extent of their enrichment.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Payment Not Due" target="Article 187">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 187 sets the prescription periods for restitution claims of payments unduly received.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 188">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 188 defines voluntary agency as the voluntary assumption of management of another's urgent business.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 189">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 189 extends voluntary agency to cases where the agent manages connected businesses.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 190">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 190 provides that rules of mandate apply upon ratification by the principal.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 191">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 191 imposes duties on the voluntary agent to continue work and inform the principal.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 192">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 192 sets the standard of care for voluntary agents and rules on delegation and joint liability.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 193">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 193 binds voluntary agents to the same obligations as mandataries regarding restitution and accounting.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 194">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 194 addresses the obligations of heirs upon the death of a voluntary agent or principal.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 195">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 195 establishes the representative status of the voluntary agent and the compensation obligations of the principal.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 196">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 196 limits liability of legally incapable voluntary agents while maintaining the principal's full responsibility.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agency" target="Article 197">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 197 sets the prescription periods for claims arising from voluntary agency.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Effects of Obligations" target="Article 199">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 199 establishes the enforceability of obligations and the exception for natural obligations.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496011</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Effects of Obligations" target="Article 200">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 200 empowers the judge to determine natural obligations in the absence of legal provisions.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Effects of Obligations" target="Article 201">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 201 prevents restitution of voluntary payments made to discharge a natural obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Effects of Obligations" target="Article 202">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 202 provides that a natural obligation may serve as valid cause for a civil obligation.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 194" target="Article 717">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 194 references Article 717 regarding the obligations of heirs of a mandatary, applying the same rules to the heirs of a voluntary agent.</data>
  <data key="d9">cross-reference,legal inheritance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 198" target="The Law">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 198 is the sole provision under Section V (The Law), establishing that obligations arising directly from the law are governed by the legal provisions that created them.</data>
  <data key="d9">governed by,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Person in Charge of Things" target="Machine">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The person in charge of a machine bears liability for damage caused by it, unless the damage was due to a cause beyond control.</data>
  <data key="d9">liability for damage,supervision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Voluntary Agent" target="Delegate">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A voluntary agent who delegates the whole or part of the work to a third party is responsible for the acts of the delegate, without prejudice to the principal's direct remedy against the delegate.</data>
  <data key="d9">delegation,responsibility</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-72190ee93a1a5ec60f7856bff9129dbe</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compromise" target="Compromise Nullity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A compromise is indivisible, meaning the nullity of one part involves the nullity of the whole contract, unless the parties agreed the parts are independent.</data>
  <data key="d9">indivisibility,legal invalidity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compromise" target="Declaratory Effect">
  <data key="d7">2.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A compromise has a declaratory effect on the rights it covers, meaning it establishes rather than creates rights, and this effect is limited exclusively to the disputed rights.&lt;SEP&gt;A compromise has a declaratory effect on the rights it covers, and this effect is limited specifically to litigious rights.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal effect,rights determination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compromise" target="Renunciation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Renunciation contained in a compromise must be strictly interpreted and applies only to rights that form the precise object of the dispute settled by the compromise.</data>
  <data key="d9">right extinguishment,strict interpretation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Compromise" target="Dispute">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Compromise terminates existing disputes or prevents anticipated disputes through the mutual surrender of claims, extinguishing the rights and claims that the parties have renounced.</data>
  <data key="d9">extinguishment,mutual surrender,resolution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Management" target="Competent Authority">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A person holding only a right of management must obtain the consent of the competent authority to enter into a lease exceeding three years.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent requirement,lease authorization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Chapter II: Contracts Relating to the Use of a Thing" target="Section I: Leases">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section I on Leases is part of Chapter II covering contracts relating to the use of a thing.</data>
  <data key="d9">hierarchy,legal structure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-1c840f4cf771d616ec902915fec410fc</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496012</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1145" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1145 establishes the vendor's privilege over movable property sold to secure the price and accessories due.</data>
  <data key="d9">establishment,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1146" target="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1146 establishes the co-partitioner's privilege over partitioned movables for remedies and repayment of differences.</data>
  <data key="d9">establishment,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1147" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1147 establishes the vendor's privilege over immovable property sold to secure the price and accessories due.</data>
  <data key="d9">establishment,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 1149" target="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Immovable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 1149 establishes the co-partitioner's privilege over partitioned immovables for remedies and payment of differences.</data>
  <data key="d9">establishment,legal provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor's Privilege" target="Lessor's Privilege">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The hotel proprietor's privilege has the same rank as the lessor's privilege; if both claims compete, the first in date has priority.</data>
  <data key="d9">priority,rank equivalence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor's Privilege" target="Third Party In Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Rights acquired by third parties in good faith are protected and limit the enforceability of the hotel proprietor's privilege on effects removed despite objection.</data>
  <data key="d9">limitation,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor's Privilege" target="Effects">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The traveller's effects serve as the collateral subject of the hotel proprietor's privilege, securing sums owed for accommodation and related expenses.</data>
  <data key="d9">security,subject of privilege</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hotel Proprietor's Privilege" target="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor's privilege over movable ranks below the hotel proprietor's privilege and other previously mentioned movable privileges unless the hotel proprietor or lessor had knowledge of the vendor's privilege at the relevant time.</data>
  <data key="d9">ranking,subordination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable" target="Third Party In Good Faith">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Rights acquired by third parties in good faith are protected and subject to the vendor's privilege over the movable sold.</data>
  <data key="d9">limitation,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor's Privilege Over Movable" target="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Movable">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The co-partitioner's privilege over movable has the same rank as the vendor's privilege over movable; if both compete, the first in date has priority.</data>
  <data key="d9">priority,rank equivalence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Vendor's Privilege Over Immovable" target="Special Privileges Over Immovables">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The vendor's privilege over immovable falls under the category of special privileges over immovables.</data>
  <data key="d9">categorization,inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496013</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Co-partitioner's Privilege Over Immovable" target="Special Privileges Over Immovables">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The co-partitioner's privilege over immovable falls under the category of special privileges over immovables.</data>
  <data key="d9">categorization,inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor's And Architect's Privilege" target="Special Privileges Over Immovables">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The contractor's and architect's privilege falls under the category of special privileges over immovables.</data>
  <data key="d9">categorization,inclusion</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Contractor's And Architect's Privilege" target="Buildings Or Other Works">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Buildings or other works are the subject of the contractor's and architect's privilege, securing sums due for their erection, reconstruction, repair, or maintenance.</data>
  <data key="d9">security,subject of privilege</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d8828f18209d105c6cf9cd5ef1db7a7d</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Third Party In Good Faith" target="Sale During Last Illness">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The restrictions on sales during last illness do not apply to the prejudice of a third party in good faith who has acquired a real right over the property for valuable consideration.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquired rights,protection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Minor Possessing Discretion" target="Voidable Act">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Dispositions by a minor possessing discretion that are simultaneously profitable and detrimental are voidable in the interest of the minor.</data>
  <data key="d9">annulment,partial validity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Minor Possessing Discretion" target="Imbecility">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acts entered into by a person under interdiction for imbecility are governed by the same provisions regulating acts performed by minors possessing discretion.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal equivalence,regulatory framework</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Interdiction" target="Insanity">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court pronounces interdiction on persons suffering from insanity in accordance with prescribed procedures.</data>
  <data key="d9">court order,legal restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Interdiction" target="Prodigality">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court pronounces interdiction on prodigals to restrict their legal capacity.</data>
  <data key="d9">court order,legal restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Interdiction" target="Imbecility">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The court pronounces interdiction on persons suffering from imbecility.</data>
  <data key="d9">court order,legal restriction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insanity" target="Void Act">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An act entered into by a person suffering from insanity after registration of the sentence of interdiction is null.</data>
  <data key="d9">nullity,registered interdiction</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Judicial Adviser" target="Voidable Act">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An act for which a judicial adviser has been ordered is voidable if performed without the adviser's assistance after registration of the decision.</data>
  <data key="d9">annulment,procedural defect</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Essential Mistake" target="Avoidance of Contract">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A party who committed an essential mistake may demand avoidance of the contract if the other party shared the mistake, knew of it, or could have easily detected it, as provided in Article 120.</data>
  <data key="d9">defect of consent,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mistake of Law" target="Mistake of Fact">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A mistake in law entails voidability of the contract if it fulfills the same elements as a mistake of fact.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal equivalence,voidability</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Void Act" target="Succession of a Living Person">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An agreement regarding the succession of a living person is void, even with consent, except in cases provided for by law, as stated in Article 131.</data>
  <data key="d9">inheritance restriction,nullity</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-a934e76c8be75aea27632cb59bb1eab2</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496014</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Specific Performance" target="Compensation in Lieu of Performance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When specific performance is impossible or too onerous, compensation in lieu of performance is the alternative remedy available to the creditor.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative remedy,legal hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Specific Performance" target="Real Right">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An obligation to transfer a real right may be enforced through specific performance, and includes the duty to deliver and preserve the article until delivery.</data>
  <data key="d9">property enforcement,transfer</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-c55f265501d290a4d7038eb81b48b01e</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Of Commodities" target="Exchange">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The provisions governing sale apply to exchange as far as the nature of exchange allows, with each exchanging party deemed both vendor and purchaser.</data>
  <data key="d9">analogous provisions,legal application</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Of Litigious Rights" target="Court Officials">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Court officials including judges, prosecutors, lawyers, clerks, and bailiffs are prohibited from purchasing litigious rights within the jurisdiction of their court under penalty of nullity.</data>
  <data key="d9">conflict of interest,prohibition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale Of Litigious Rights" target="Lawyers">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Lawyers are prohibited from entering into agreements with their clients regarding litigious rights they are defending, under penalty of nullity of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">professional conduct,prohibition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Sale By Representative To Himself" target="Brokers And Experts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Brokers and experts are prohibited from purchasing goods entrusted to them for sale or appraisal, similar to the prohibition on representatives purchasing property entrusted to them.</data>
  <data key="d9">prohibition,self-dealing</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-2afef25d5c248ee2d7f4012a3a1806de</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496015</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Annuity" target="Redemption">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Redemption is the mechanism by which the debtor extinguishes the annuity obligation, either by repaying the full capital or a sum whose interest at the legal rate equals the annuity amount.</data>
  <data key="d9">debtor's right,extinguishment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assured" target="Policy of Insurance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Assured may surrender, reduce, or convert the Policy of Insurance after payment of at least three yearly premiums, provided the event insured against is bound to occur.</data>
  <data key="d9">policy ownership,surrender rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assured" target="Life Assured">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When the Assured takes out insurance on the life of a third party (Life Assured), the Life Assured must consent in writing prior to the issue of the policy for the contract to be valid.</data>
  <data key="d9">consent requirement,insurance arrangement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Assured" target="Paid-Up Policy">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Assured may convert the original policy into a Paid-Up Policy with a reduced sum assured after payment of at least three yearly premiums, provided the event insured against is bound to occur.</data>
  <data key="d9">policy conversion,premium payment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Assured" target="Life Insurance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Life Insurance is contracted on the life of the Life Assured, with the insurer's obligations triggered by events such as the death or survival of that person.</data>
  <data key="d9">risk coverage,subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurance Contract" target="Policy of Insurance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Insurance Contract is documented by the Policy of Insurance, which contains the general and special conditions governing the agreement.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual evidence,documentation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurance Contract" target="Section III: Contracts of Insurance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Section III: Contracts of Insurance contains the general and specific legal provisions governing the formation, validity, and enforcement of Insurance Contracts.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal framework,statutory provision</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurance Contract" target="Lawful Economic Interest">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A Lawful Economic Interest is the required subject matter that may be covered under an Insurance Contract, ensuring the insured event involves a legitimate financial stake.</data>
  <data key="d9">insurable interest,subject matter</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Insurance Contract" target="Limitation Period">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Actions arising out of an Insurance Contract are barred by the Limitation Period of three years from the date of the occurrence giving rise to the claim.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal claims,statutory time bar</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Policy of Insurance" target="Suicide">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A clause in the Policy of Insurance requiring the insurer to pay even in case of voluntary and knowing suicide is only enforceable if suicide occurs after two years from the date of the contract.</data>
  <data key="d9">exclusion clause,policy conditions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Policy of Insurance" target="Void Conditions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Void Conditions are specific clauses within a Policy of Insurance that are legally unenforceable, including forfeiture clauses for minor breaches, unclear printed conditions, and arbitrary provisions.</data>
  <data key="d9">invalidity,unenforceable clauses</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Insurance" target="Suicide">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Under Life Insurance, the Insurer is generally released from the obligation to pay if the Life Assured commits suicide, unless suicide resulted from illness or occurred after two years from the contract date.</data>
  <data key="d9">contractual exclusion,obligation release</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Life Insurance" target="Insurance Reserve">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The Insurance Reserve is used in Life Insurance to determine the reduced Sum Assured and the amount payable to persons to whom the right reverts upon events such as policy reduction or surrender.</data>
  <data key="d9">financial calculation,reserve value</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-9932611f10b70b3f24d61761ccb1f7d6</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 954" target="Carrier">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 954 equates the handing over of documents issued in respect of goods entrusted to a carrier with the delivery of the goods themselves.</data>
  <data key="d9">document delivery,equivalent to goods delivery</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 958" target="Three Possessory Actions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 958 provides the possessory action for reinstatement of possession of an immovable within one year of loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">possessory action,reinstatement remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496016</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 959" target="Violence">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 959 provides that when loss of possession occurs by violence, the possessor may always claim restitution within one year regardless of other circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispossession by force,restitution</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 961" target="Three Possessory Actions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 961 provides the possessory action for discontinuance of disturbance after one year of possession.</data>
  <data key="d9">discontinuance of disturbance,possessory action</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 961" target="Disturbance">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 961 grants a possessor of an immovable for a full year the right to take proceedings for the discontinuance of a disturbance within the following year.</data>
  <data key="d9">discontinuance,legal remedy</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 962" target="Three Possessory Actions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 962 provides the possessory action for prevention of new works threatening possession.</data>
  <data key="d9">possessory action,prevention of threatened works</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 966" target="Violence">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 966 deems a person who has usurped possession of another by violence to have acted in bad faith.</data>
  <data key="d9">bad faith,usurpation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 970" target="Competent Minister">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 970 grants the Competent Minister the right to remove encroachments on state and public entity property administratively.</data>
  <data key="d9">administrative removal,encroachment</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Article 970" target="Hereditary Rights">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 970 requires thirty-three years of possession for acquiring hereditary rights by prescription, the same extended period applicable to wakf property.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition by prescription,extended prescription period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acquisitive Prescription" target="Just Title">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A just title is required alongside good faith to qualify for the shortened five-year acquisitive prescription for immovable property.</data>
  <data key="d9">requirement for five-year rule,shortened period</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acquisitive Prescription" target="Article 974">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Article 974 governs the suspension of acquisitive prescription regardless of its duration, supplementing the rules established in Article 973.</data>
  <data key="d9">procedural rule,suspension of prescription</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3ca1025f025aad9c1f9f42dbd8604eb</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acquisitive Prescription" target="Suspension of Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acquisitive prescription is suspended whenever a qualifying cause for suspension exists, regardless of the duration of the prescription period.</data>
  <data key="d9">calculation of period,suspension</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acquisitive Prescription" target="Interruption of Prescription">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Acquisitive prescription is interrupted if the possessor abandons or loses possession, but not if possession is recovered within one year or legal proceedings for recovery are initiated within that period.</data>
  <data key="d9">interruption,loss of possession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Liquidator" target="Partnership Property">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The liquidator is responsible for winding up and partitioning partnership property among partners after satisfying creditors and outstanding debts.</data>
  <data key="d9">asset management,distribution,liquidation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Dissolution" target="Liquidation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Dissolution of the partnership triggers the liquidation process, during which the partnership retains its juristic personality until liquidation is completed.</data>
  <data key="d9">legal process,sequence</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Loan for Consumption" target="Sale">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The provisions relating to sale apply to a loan for consumption made for valuable consideration in the event of dispossession of the loaned thing.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispossession,legal provisions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Loan for Consumption" target="Loan for Use">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The provisions relating to loan for use apply to a loan for consumption made without valuable consideration in the event of dispossession of the loaned thing.</data>
  <data key="d9">dispossession,legal provisions</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Borrower" target="Hidden Defect">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">When hidden defects appear in a loaned thing, the borrower's remedy depends on whether the loan is for consideration, is gratuitous, or whether the lender deliberately concealed the defect.</data>
  <data key="d9">consumer protection,defect liability,remedies</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d7ea290b6a89e52ab815c1f948ab88f5</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496017</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Usufruct" target="Standing Crops">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon termination of the usufruct, if standing crops exist, the land is left to the usufructuary or heirs until harvest upon payment of rent.</data>
  <data key="d9">harvest rights,termination</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Usufruct" target="Right of Use">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of use is classified alongside the right of usufruct and the right of occupation as a right derived from the right of ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,derived rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Usufruct" target="Right of Occupation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of occupation is classified alongside the right of usufruct and the right of use as a right derived from the right of ownership.</data>
  <data key="d9">classification,derived rights</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Usufruct" target="Legal Disposition">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The right of usufruct may be acquired by a legal disposition as one of the recognized methods of creation.</data>
  <data key="d9">acquisition,creation of right</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496018</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Right of Usufruct" target="Heir">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Upon the death of the usufructuary and termination of the usufruct, the heir may retain possession of land with standing crops until harvest, subject to payment of rent.</data>
  <data key="d9">standing crops,succession</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-40fa09ee0da5fe29862351638b61d0db</data>
  <data key="d11">/Volumes/Shared/NileUni/GenAI/LegalPolicy_LLM/EgyptianLaw.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1779496019</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
</graph></graphml>